window.CL_LESSONS = [{"id":"setup-welcome","title":"How to learn to code (properly)","level":"beginner","minutes":8,"summary":"What experienced developers actually know, how this site works, and the learning loop that gets you there.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-15-years-of-experience-really-means\">What &quot;15 years of experience&quot; really means</h2>\n<p>Experienced developers have not memorised every function. They look things up constantly. What they have is a set of habits and instincts:</p>\n<ul><li>They <strong>break big problems into small ones</strong> until each piece is obvious.</li><li>They <strong>read error messages</strong> calmly and fully, because the error usually says what is wrong.</li><li>They <strong>know where to look</strong>: official docs, the source code, the Network tab, the logs.</li><li>They <strong>build things</strong>, ship them, and learn from what breaks.</li><li>They use the <strong>same core tools every day</strong> (editor, terminal, Git, browser DevTools) so well that those tools disappear.</li></ul>\n<p>You cannot download years. You can copy the habits from day one, and that is what Code Ladder is built around.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-you-will-be-able-to-build\">What you will be able to build</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Track</th><th>You will be able to</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Foundations</td><td>Use the terminal, Git, packages and debugging tools like a professional</td></tr><tr><td>Web Development</td><td>Build fast, accessible, responsive websites and full-stack apps</td></tr><tr><td>Cryptography &amp; Blockchain</td><td>Understand hashes, keys and signatures, then write, test and secure smart contracts</td></tr><tr><td>Tokenomics</td><td>Design and stress-test token supply, vesting, liquidity and incentives</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-learning-loop\">The learning loop</h2>\n<p>Every lesson follows the same loop. Do not skip steps.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Read</strong> the lesson once without coding.</li><li><strong>Type</strong> every example yourself (no copy-paste) in the <a href=\"#playground\">Playground</a> or VS Code.</li><li><strong>Break it on purpose.</strong> Delete a bracket, misspell a name, and read the error you get.</li><li><strong>Pass the quiz.</strong> Passing marks the lesson complete.</li><li><strong>Build</strong> one of the level's <a href=\"#projects\">projects</a> without the lesson open.</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The 20-minute rule</p><p>When you get stuck, try for 20 focused minutes: read the error, search it, simplify the problem. Then ask for help. Never suffer for hours, and never ask after 30 seconds.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-ask-a-good-question\">How to ask a good question</h2>\n<p>Whether you ask a friend, a forum or an AI, include these four things. Writing them down often solves the problem before you send it.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>What I am trying to do</strong> (the goal, not just the step).</li><li><strong>What I expected</strong> to happen.</li><li><strong>What actually happened</strong>, with the exact error text.</li><li><strong>What I already tried.</strong></li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"a-realistic-timeline\">A realistic timeline</h2>\n<p>At one to two focused hours a day, most people can build and deploy solid websites within 4 to 6 months and write and test simple smart contracts within 8 to 10. The <a href=\"#roadmap\">12-month roadmap</a> lays it out. Consistency beats intensity: 45 minutes every day beats a 7-hour Sunday.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Using AI while you learn</p><p>AI assistants are excellent tutors. Ask them to explain an error, quiz you, or review code you wrote. Do not ask them to write projects you could not explain line by line. If you keep code you do not understand, you are borrowing skill you will have to repay later.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"What is the main thing that separates experienced developers from beginners?","options":["They have memorised the documentation","Habits: breaking problems down, reading errors, knowing where to look, shipping","They type faster"],"answer":1,"explain":"Seniors look things up all the time. The difference is how they approach problems and how well they use their tools."},{"q":"You have been stuck on a bug for 20 focused minutes. What should you do?","options":["Keep going alone for the rest of the day","Delete the project and start again","Ask for help, explaining the goal, what you expected, what happened and what you tried"],"answer":2,"explain":"The 20-minute rule balances struggling (which teaches you) with not wasting hours."},{"q":"Why type the examples instead of pasting them?","options":["Pasting is against the rules","Typing forces you to notice every detail, so it sticks","Pasted code does not run"],"answer":1,"explain":"Your brain processes code far more deeply when your hands write it."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Your very first line of code","file":"hello.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Every lesson has exercises like this one. Type the code in the editor, then press <strong>Check my code</strong> (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>). Wrong answers get a tip; after <strong>5 wrong tries in a row</strong> the answer appears as faded ghost text for you to type over.</p>\n<p>Type this exact line: <code>console.log('Hello, world!');</code></p>\n<p>It tells the computer to print the text <em>Hello, world!</em>. After it passes, look at the <strong>Output</strong> box.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"console.log('Hello, world!');","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*console","text":"Start with the word <code>console</code>, all lower case."},{"re":"console\\.log","text":"After <code>console</code> comes a dot and then <code>log</code>: <code>console.log</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(","text":"Open a round bracket straight after <code>log</code>, with no space: <code>console.log(</code>"},{"re":"['\"]Hello, world!['\"]","text":"The text goes inside quotes, with the comma and exclamation mark: <code>'Hello, world!'</code>"},{"re":"\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Close the round bracket at the end and finish with a semicolon: <code>);</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>console.log(...)</code> is a built-in instruction that prints whatever is inside the brackets. Text goes in quotes so the computer knows it is text, not more code. Almost every programmer started with this exact line.</p>"},{"title":"Make the computer do maths","file":"maths.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Print the answer to 7 times 6. In code, multiply is written with a star: <code>*</code>. Numbers do not need quotes.</p>\n<p>Type: <code>console.log(7 * 6);</code></p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"console.log(7 * 6);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"console\\.log\\(","text":"Start with <code>console.log(</code> exactly like the last exercise."},{"re":"7\\s*\\*\\s*6","text":"Inside the brackets: <code>7 * 6</code>. No quotes, because these are numbers."},{"re":"\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Close the bracket and add a semicolon: <code>);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>The computer works out <code>7 * 6</code> first, then prints the result: <strong>42</strong>. Without quotes, <code>7 * 6</code> is a calculation. With quotes, <code>'7 * 6'</code> would just be text and would print as it is.</p>"}],"text":"What 15 years of experience really means Experienced developers have not memorised every function. They look things up constantly. What they have is a set of habits and instincts: They break big problems into small ones until each piece is obvious. They read error messages calmly and fully, because the error usually says what is wrong. They know where to look : official docs, the source code, the Network tab, the logs. They build things , ship them, and learn from what breaks. They use the same core tools every day (editor, terminal, Git, browser DevTools) so well that those tools disappear. You cannot download years. You can copy the habits from day one, and that is what Code Ladder is built around. What you will be able to build Track You will be able to Foundations Use the terminal, Git, packages and debugging tools like a professional Web Development Build fast, accessible, responsive websites and full-stack apps Cryptography Blockchain Understand hashes, keys and signatures, then write, test and secure smart contracts Tokenomics Design and stress-test token supply, vesting, liquidity and incentives The learning loop Every lesson follows the same loop. Do not skip steps. Read the lesson once without coding. Type every example yourself (no copy-paste) in the Playground or VS Code. Break it on purpose. Delete a bracket, misspell a name, and read the error you get. Pass the quiz. Passing marks the lesson complete. Build one of the level's projects without the lesson open. The 20-minute rule When you get stuck, try for 20 focused minutes: read the error, search it, simplify the problem. Then ask for help. Never suffer for hours, and never ask after 30 seconds. How to ask a good question Whether you ask a friend, a forum or an AI, include these four things. Writing them down often solves the problem before you send it. What I am trying to do (the goal, not just the step). What I expected to happen. What actually happened , with the exact error text. What I already tried. A realistic timeline At one to two focused hours a day, most people can build and deploy solid websites within 4 to 6 months and write and test simple smart contracts within 8 to 10. The 12-month roadmap lays it out. Consistency beats intensity: 45 minutes every day beats a 7-hour Sunday. Using AI while you learn AI assistants are excellent tutors. Ask them to explain an error, quiz you, or review code you wrote. Do not ask them to write projects you could not explain line by line. If you keep code you do not understand, you are borrowing skill you will have to repay later.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-coding-basics","title":"\"Coding basics 1: integers, decimals, characters and strings\"","level":"beginner","minutes":35,"summary":"Every value in a program has a type. Learn integers, decimals (floats), characters, strings and booleans by typing real code.","html":"<h2 id=\"programs-are-instructions-plus-data\">Programs are instructions plus data</h2>\n<p>A program is a list of instructions that work on <strong>data</strong>: prices, names, quantities, true-or-false answers. Every piece of data has a <strong>type</strong>, and the type decides what you can do with it. You can multiply two numbers; you cannot multiply two names.</p>\n<p>These five types exist, under slightly different names, in almost every language:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it holds</th><th>JavaScript</th><th>Python</th><th>Java / C</th><th>Solidity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Integer</strong></td><td>Whole numbers: <code>12</code>, <code>-3</code>, <code>0</code></td><td><code>number</code> (or <code>bigint</code>)</td><td><code>int</code></td><td><code>int</code>, <code>long</code></td><td><code>uint256</code>, <code>int256</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Decimal (float)</strong></td><td>Numbers with a fractional part: <code>19.99</code></td><td><code>number</code></td><td><code>float</code></td><td><code>double</code>, <code>float</code></td><td>none (no decimals)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Character</strong></td><td>One letter or symbol: <code>'A'</code></td><td>a string of length 1</td><td>a string of length 1</td><td><code>char</code></td><td><code>bytes1</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>String</strong></td><td>Text: <code>'Amara Studio'</code></td><td><code>string</code></td><td><code>str</code></td><td><code>String</code> / <code>char[]</code></td><td><code>string</code></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Boolean</strong></td><td><code>true</code> or <code>false</code></td><td><code>boolean</code></td><td><code>bool</code> (<code>True</code>)</td><td><code>boolean</code> / <code>bool</code></td><td><code>bool</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>We will use <strong>JavaScript</strong> for the exercises, because it runs right here in your browser. Everything you learn transfers to other languages.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">How exercises work</p><p>Each exercise has an editor that works like VS Code. Type the code, then press <strong>Check my code</strong> (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd>). Wrong answers get a tip. After <strong>5 wrong tries in a row</strong>, the answer appears as faded <strong>ghost text</strong> and you type over it, character by character. <strong>Run it</strong> shows what your code prints.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"variables-naming-your-data\">Variables: naming your data</h2>\n<p>A <strong>variable</strong> is a labelled box that holds a value. In JavaScript you create one with <code>const</code> (it will not be reassigned) or <code>let</code> (it can change).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const brand = 'Amara Studio';   // a string\nlet stock = 12;                 // an integer\nconst price = 34.5;             // a decimal\nconst inStock = true;           // a boolean</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>console.log(...)</code> prints a value so you can see it. You will use it constantly.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"integers-whole-numbers\">Integers: whole numbers</h2>\n<p>Integers are counts: items in stock, people, days, votes. The maths operators:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>+</code></td><td>add</td><td><code>7 + 2</code></td><td><code>9</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>-</code></td><td>subtract</td><td><code>7 - 2</code></td><td><code>5</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>*</code></td><td>multiply</td><td><code>7 * 2</code></td><td><code>14</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>/</code></td><td>divide</td><td><code>7 / 2</code></td><td><code>3.5</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>%</code></td><td>remainder (modulo)</td><td><code>7 % 2</code></td><td><code>1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>**</code></td><td>power</td><td><code>7 ** 2</code></td><td><code>49</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>In JavaScript, <code>7 / 2</code> gives <code>3.5</code>. To get a whole-number answer, round <strong>down</strong> with <code>Math.floor(7 / 2)</code>, which gives <code>3</code>. (Python has a special operator for this: <code>7 // 2</code>.)</p>\n<p>The <strong>remainder</strong> operator <code>%</code> is more useful than it looks: <code>n % 2 === 0</code> tells you if a number is even, and it answers &quot;how many are left over?&quot;</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"decimals-floats-and-their-famous-trap\">Decimals (floats) and their famous trap</h2>\n<p>Numbers with a decimal point are called <strong>floating-point</strong> numbers, or <strong>floats</strong>. Computers store them in binary, which cannot represent some decimals exactly, just as 1/3 cannot be written exactly in decimal (0.3333...).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">console.log(0.1 + 0.2);   // 0.30000000000000004</code></pre></div>\n<p>That tiny error is why professionals <strong>never store money as a float</strong>. Store pence (or cents) as integers, and convert only for display. To show a decimal neatly, use <code>.toFixed(2)</code>, which rounds to 2 decimal places and returns a <strong>string</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"characters-letters-are-secretly-numbers\">Characters: letters are secretly numbers</h2>\n<p>A <strong>character</strong> (often called a <strong>char</strong>) is a single letter, digit, symbol or space: <code>'A'</code>, <code>'7'</code>, <code>'?'</code>, <code>' '</code>.</p>\n<p>Computers only store numbers, so every character has a number code. The standard is <strong>Unicode</strong> (its first 128 codes are the older <strong>ASCII</strong> table):</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Code</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>'0'</code> to <code>'9'</code></td><td>48 to 57</td></tr><tr><td><code>'A'</code> to <code>'Z'</code></td><td>65 to 90</td></tr><tr><td><code>'a'</code> to <code>'z'</code></td><td>97 to 122</td></tr><tr><td>space</td><td>32</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>So <code>'A'</code> is stored as 65 and <code>'a'</code> as 97. That is why sorting can put &quot;Zebra&quot; before &quot;apple&quot;: 90 comes before 97.</p>\n<p>Languages like Java and C have a real <code>char</code> type (<code>char grade = 'A';</code>). JavaScript and Python simply use a string of length 1. To go between characters and codes:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>JavaScript</th><th>Python</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Character to code</td><td><code>'A'.charCodeAt(0)</code> gives <code>65</code></td><td><code>ord('A')</code> gives <code>65</code></td></tr><tr><td>Code to character</td><td><code>String.fromCharCode(66)</code> gives <code>'B'</code></td><td><code>chr(66)</code> gives <code>'B'</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"3\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"strings-text\">Strings: text</h2>\n<p>A <strong>string</strong> is a sequence of characters. You can write it with single quotes <code>'...'</code>, double quotes <code>&quot;...&quot;</code> or backticks (called template literals, which let you insert values with <code>${}</code>).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const brand = 'amara studio';\nbrand.length;             // 12 (characters, including the space)\nbrand[0];                 // 'a' (position 0 is the first character)\nbrand.toUpperCase();      // 'AMARA STUDIO'\nbrand.includes('studio'); // true\nbrand.slice(0, 5);        // 'amara'\n`Welcome to ${brand}!`    // 'Welcome to amara studio!'</code></pre></div>\n<p>Positions start at <strong>0</strong>, not 1. That trips up every beginner once.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"4\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"booleans-true-or-false\">Booleans: true or false</h2>\n<p>A <strong>boolean</strong> is either <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>. Comparisons produce booleans, and booleans drive every decision a program makes.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">12 &gt; 5            // true\n12 === 12         // true  (=== means &quot;is exactly equal to&quot;)\n'12' === 12       // false (a string is not a number)\n12 !== 5          // true  (!== means &quot;is not equal to&quot;)</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"converting-between-types\">Converting between types</h2>\n<p>Data from forms, files and URLs always arrives as <strong>text</strong>, even when it looks like a number. <code>'42' + 1</code> gives <code>'421'</code> (joining text), not <code>43</code>. Convert first:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Conversion</th><th>JavaScript</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Text to number</td><td><code>Number('42')</code></td><td><code>42</code></td></tr><tr><td>Text to whole number, ignoring letters after it</td><td><code>parseInt('42px')</code></td><td><code>42</code></td></tr><tr><td>Number to text</td><td><code>String(42)</code></td><td><code>'42'</code></td></tr><tr><td>What type is this?</td><td><code>typeof 42</code></td><td><code>'number'</code></td></tr><tr><td>A failed conversion</td><td><code>Number('abc')</code></td><td><code>NaN</code> (&quot;Not a Number&quot;)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"5\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-same-ideas-in-python\">The same ideas in Python</h2>\n<p>You will meet Python for scripts, data and tokenomics models. The concepts are identical; only the spelling changes:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">stock = 12                 # int (no const or let in Python)\nprice = 34.5               # float\nletter = 'A'               # a string of length 1\nin_stock = True            # bool: capital T\nprint(ord(letter))         # 65\nprint(stock // 5)          # 2  (whole-number division)\nprint(int('42') + 1)       # 43</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"6\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which value is an integer?","options":["19.99","12","'12'"],"answer":1,"explain":"19.99 is a decimal (float) and '12' is a string because of the quotes."},{"q":"What does 17 % 5 give?","options":["3.4","2","3"],"answer":1,"explain":"% gives the remainder: 5 goes into 17 three times (15), with 2 left over."},{"q":"Why should you store £34.50 as 3450 pence instead of 34.5?","options":["Floats cannot store some decimals exactly, which causes rounding errors with money","Integers use more memory","JavaScript cannot show decimals"],"answer":0,"explain":"0.1 + 0.2 is 0.30000000000000004 in floating point. Whole pence are always exact."},{"q":"What is 'B'.charCodeAt(0)?","options":["65","66","98"],"answer":1,"explain":"Capital A is 65, so B is 66. Lower-case b is 98."},{"q":"A form field gives '5'. What does '5' + 1 produce in JavaScript?","options":["6","'51'","An error"],"answer":1,"explain":"With a string involved, + joins text. Convert with Number('5') first."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Store an integer","file":"stock.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Create a <strong>constant</strong> called <code>stock</code> that holds the integer <code>12</code>. Then print it with <code>console.log</code>.</p>\n<p>Two lines. No quotes around <code>12</code>: quotes would make it text, not a number.</p>","starter":"// Line 1: create the constant\n// Line 2: print it","html":"","solution":"const stock = 12;\nconsole.log(stock);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*const\\s","text":"Start the first line with the keyword <code>const</code>."},{"re":"const\\s+stock\\s*=","text":"Name the constant <code>stock</code>, followed by <code>=</code>."},{"re":"stock\\s*=\\s*12\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Give it the value <code>12</code> with no quotes."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*stock\\s*\\)","text":"Print it on the second line with <code>console.log(stock);</code>."}],"explain":"<p><code>const</code> creates a variable that cannot be reassigned. <code>stock</code> is its name. <code>=</code> stores the value <code>12</code> in it. <code>console.log(stock)</code> looks up the value in the box and prints it: <strong>12</strong>.</p>"},{"title":"Boxes and leftovers","file":"cupcakes.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>A bakery has <strong>40</strong> cupcakes and each box holds <strong>6</strong>. Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>boxes</code> equal to <code>Math.floor(40 / 6)</code> (the number of full boxes).</li><li>A constant <code>leftover</code> equal to <code>40 % 6</code> (the cupcakes that do not fit).</li><li>Print both: <code>console.log(boxes, leftover);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const boxes = Math.floor(40 / 6);\nconst leftover = 40 % 6;\nconsole.log(boxes, leftover);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+boxes\\s*=","text":"Line 1 starts with <code>const boxes =</code>."},{"re":"Math\\.floor\\(\\s*40\\s*\\/\\s*6\\s*\\)","text":"Use <code>Math.floor(40 / 6)</code>. Capital M, and a dot before <code>floor</code>."},{"re":"const\\s+leftover\\s*=\\s*40\\s*%\\s*6","text":"Line 2: <code>const leftover = 40 % 6;</code> The <code>%</code> sign gives the remainder."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*boxes\\s*,\\s*leftover\\s*\\)","text":"Print both values, separated by a comma, inside one <code>console.log(...)</code>."}],"explain":"<p>40 divided by 6 is 6.67. <code>Math.floor</code> rounds down to <strong>6</strong> full boxes. 6 boxes hold 36, so <code>40 % 6</code> is the <strong>4</strong> left over. The output is <code>6 4</code>.</p>"},{"title":"See the float trap yourself","file":"floats.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>total</code> equal to <code>0.1 + 0.2</code>.</li><li>Print <code>total</code>.</li><li>Print <code>total.toFixed(2)</code>.</li></ol>\n<p>Then press <strong>Run it</strong> and compare the two outputs.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const total = 0.1 + 0.2;\nconsole.log(total);\nconsole.log(total.toFixed(2));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+total\\s*=\\s*0\\.1\\s*\\+\\s*0\\.2","text":"Line 1: <code>const total = 0.1 + 0.2;</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*console\\.log\\(\\s*total\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Line 2 prints the raw value: <code>console.log(total);</code>"},{"re":"total\\.toFixed\\(\\s*2\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3 uses the method <code>toFixed(2)</code> on <code>total</code>: <code>total.toFixed(2)</code>."}],"explain":"<p>The first line of output is <code>0.30000000000000004</code>: a real floating-point rounding error. <code>toFixed(2)</code> rounds it for display to <code>&quot;0.30&quot;</code>. For money, store <code>30</code> pence instead and you never meet this problem.</p>"},{"title":"Characters as numbers","file":"chars.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>letter</code> holding the character <code>'A'</code>.</li><li>Print its code with <code>letter.charCodeAt(0)</code>.</li><li>Print the character for code <code>66</code> with <code>String.fromCharCode(66)</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const letter = 'A';\nconsole.log(letter.charCodeAt(0));\nconsole.log(String.fromCharCode(66));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+letter\\s*=\\s*['\"]A['\"]","text":"Line 1: <code>const letter = 'A';</code> with the A inside quotes (it is text)."},{"re":"letter\\.charCodeAt\\(\\s*0\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(letter.charCodeAt(0));</code> The <code>0</code> means &quot;the first character&quot;."},{"re":"String\\.fromCharCode\\(\\s*66\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(String.fromCharCode(66));</code> Capital S on <code>String</code>, capital C on <code>Char</code> and <code>Code</code>."}],"explain":"<p><code>charCodeAt(0)</code> reads the number stored for the character at position 0: <strong>65</strong>. <code>String.fromCharCode(66)</code> goes the other way and gives <strong>B</strong>. Every string you type is stored as numbers like these, which matters later for hashing and encryption.</p>"},{"title":"Work with a string","file":"brand.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>brand</code> holding <code>'amara studio'</code> (all lower case).</li><li>Print it in capitals with <code>brand.toUpperCase()</code>.</li><li>Print how many characters it has with <code>brand.length</code>.</li><li>Print its first character with <code>brand[0]</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const brand = 'amara studio';\nconsole.log(brand.toUpperCase());\nconsole.log(brand.length);\nconsole.log(brand[0]);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+brand\\s*=\\s*['\"]amara studio['\"]","text":"Line 1: <code>const brand = 'amara studio';</code>"},{"re":"brand\\.toUpperCase\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2 needs <code>brand.toUpperCase()</code> inside <code>console.log(...)</code>. It is a method, so it ends with <code>()</code>."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*brand\\.length\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(brand.length);</code> No brackets after <code>length</code>: it is a property, not a method."},{"re":"brand\\[\\s*0\\s*\\]","text":"Line 4: square brackets with the position: <code>brand[0]</code>."}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>AMARA STUDIO</code>, then <code>12</code>, then <code>a</code>. Methods like <code>toUpperCase()</code> do something and need <code>()</code>. Properties like <code>length</code> just hold a value. Square brackets pick a character by position, starting at 0.</p>"},{"title":"Turn text into a number","file":"convert.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>A form gives you the text <code>'42'</code>. Write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>input</code> holding the <strong>string</strong> <code>'42'</code>.</li><li>A constant <code>qty</code> equal to <code>Number(input)</code>.</li><li>Print <code>qty + 1</code>.</li><li>Print <code>typeof qty</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const input = '42';\nconst qty = Number(input);\nconsole.log(qty + 1);\nconsole.log(typeof qty);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+input\\s*=\\s*['\"]42['\"]","text":"Line 1: the value is text, so it needs quotes: <code>const input = '42';</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+qty\\s*=\\s*Number\\(\\s*input\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const qty = Number(input);</code> Capital N on <code>Number</code>."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*qty\\s*\\+\\s*1\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(qty + 1);</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*typeof\\s+qty\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>typeof</code> is a keyword, followed by a space and the variable: <code>console.log(typeof qty);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>43</code> and <code>number</code>. Without <code>Number(...)</code>, <code>input + 1</code> would have given <code>&quot;421&quot;</code>, because <code>+</code> joins text. Converting input before doing maths is a habit that prevents a whole family of bugs.</p>"},{"title":"Write the same thing in Python","file":"basics.py","lang":"python","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Python does not need <code>const</code>, <code>let</code> or semicolons. Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>stock = 12</code></li><li><code>letter = 'A'</code></li><li>Print the character code of <code>letter</code> with <code>print(ord(letter))</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"stock = 12\nletter = 'A'\nprint(ord(letter))","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^stock\\s*=\\s*12\\s*$","text":"Line 1: just <code>stock = 12</code>. No <code>const</code>, no semicolon."},{"re":"^letter\\s*=\\s*['\"]A['\"]\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>letter = 'A'</code>"},{"re":"print\\(\\s*ord\\(\\s*letter\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: Python prints with <code>print(...)</code>, and <code>ord(...)</code> gives a character's code: <code>print(ord(letter))</code>."}],"explain":"<p>This would print <strong>65</strong>, just like <code>charCodeAt(0)</code> in JavaScript. Python runs on your computer rather than in the browser, so there is no Run button here. Install Python with uv from the <a href=\"#toolkit\">Install everything</a> page and try it with <code>uv run python</code>.</p>"}],"text":"Programs are instructions plus data A program is a list of instructions that work on data : prices, names, quantities, true-or-false answers. Every piece of data has a type , and the type decides what you can do with it. You can multiply two numbers; you cannot multiply two names. These five types exist, under slightly different names, in almost every language: Type What it holds JavaScript Python Java / C Solidity Integer Whole numbers: 12 , -3 , 0 number (or bigint ) int int , long uint256 , int256 Decimal (float) Numbers with a fractional part: 19.99 number float double , float none (no decimals) Character One letter or symbol: 'A' a string of length 1 a string of length 1 char bytes1 String Text: 'Amara Studio' string str String / char[] string Boolean true or false boolean bool ( True ) boolean / bool bool We will use JavaScript for the exercises, because it runs right here in your browser. Everything you learn transfers to other languages. How exercises work Each exercise has an editor that works like VS Code. Type the code, then press Check my code (or Ctrl + Enter ). Wrong answers get a tip. After 5 wrong tries in a row , the answer appears as faded ghost text and you type over it, character by character. Run it shows what your code prints. Variables: naming your data A variable is a labelled box that holds a value. In JavaScript you create one with const (it will not be reassigned) or let (it can change). const brand = 'Amara Studio'; // a string let stock = 12; // an integer const price = 34.5; // a decimal const inStock = true; // a boolean console.log(...) prints a value so you can see it. You will use it constantly. Integers: whole numbers Integers are counts: items in stock, people, days, votes. The maths operators: Operator Meaning Example Result + add 7 + 2 9 - subtract 7 - 2 5 * multiply 7 * 2 14 / divide 7 / 2 3.5 % remainder (modulo) 7 % 2 1 ** power 7 ** 2 49 In JavaScript, 7 / 2 gives 3.5 . To get a whole-number answer, round down with Math.floor(7 / 2) , which gives 3 . (Python has a special operator for this: 7 // 2 .) The remainder operator % is more useful than it looks: n % 2 === 0 tells you if a number is even, and it answers how many are left over? Decimals (floats) and their famous trap Numbers with a decimal point are called floating-point numbers, or floats . Computers store them in binary, which cannot represent some decimals exactly, just as 1/3 cannot be written exactly in decimal (0.3333...). console.log(0.1 + 0.2); // 0.30000000000000004 That tiny error is why professionals never store money as a float . Store pence (or cents) as integers, and convert only for display. To show a decimal neatly, use .toFixed(2) , which rounds to 2 decimal places and returns a string . Characters: letters are secretly numbers A character (often called a char ) is a single letter, digit, symbol or space: 'A' , '7' , '?' , ' ' . Computers only store numbers, so every character has a number code. The standard is Unicode (its first 128 codes are the older ASCII table): Character Code '0' to '9' 48 to 57 'A' to 'Z' 65 to 90 'a' to 'z' 97 to 122 space 32 So 'A' is stored as 65 and 'a' as 97. That is why sorting can put Zebra before apple : 90 comes before 97. Languages like Java and C have a real char type ( char grade = 'A'; ). JavaScript and Python simply use a string of length 1. To go between characters and codes: JavaScript Python Character to code 'A'.charCodeAt(0) gives 65 ord('A') gives 65 Code to character String.fromCharCode(66) gives 'B' chr(66) gives 'B' Strings: text A string is a sequence of characters. You can write it with single quotes '...' , double quotes ... or backticks (called template literals, which let you insert values with ${} ). const brand = 'amara studio'; brand.length; // 12 (characters, including the space) brand[0]; // 'a' (position 0 is the first character) brand.toUpperCase(); // 'AMARA STUDIO' brand.includes('studio'); // true brand.slice(0, 5); // 'amara' `Welcome to ${brand}!` /","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-coding-basics-2","title":"\"Coding basics 2: decisions, loops and functions\"","level":"beginner","minutes":35,"summary":"The three ideas behind every program ever written. Make decisions with if, repeat work with loops, and package logic into functions.","html":"<h2 id=\"three-ideas-run-everything\">Three ideas run everything</h2>\n<p>Every program, from a to-do list to a blockchain, is built from three ideas on top of the data types you just learned:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Decisions</strong>: do this <em>if</em> something is true, otherwise do that.</li><li><strong>Loops</strong>: repeat something many times without writing it out many times.</li><li><strong>Functions</strong>: give a set of steps a name, so you can reuse it.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"thinking-before-typing-pseudocode\">Thinking before typing: pseudocode</h2>\n<p>Professionals often sketch logic in plain English first. This is called <strong>pseudocode</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">for each product in the basket:\n    add (price x quantity) to the total\nif the total is at least 5000 pence:\n    shipping is free\notherwise:\n    shipping costs 399 pence</code></pre></div>\n<p>Once the steps are clear, turning them into code is mostly spelling. When you feel stuck, write pseudocode first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"decisions-if-else-if-else\">Decisions: if, else if, else</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const stock = 3;\n\nif (stock === 0) {\n  console.log('Sold out');\n} else if (stock &lt; 5) {\n  console.log('Only a few left');\n} else {\n  console.log('In stock');\n}</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li>The condition goes in round brackets <code>( )</code>.</li><li>The code to run goes in curly braces <code>{ }</code>.</li><li>Only the <strong>first</strong> branch whose condition is true runs.</li><li>Indent the code inside braces by two spaces. The computer does not care, but humans do.</li></ul>\n<p>Combine conditions with <code>&amp;&amp;</code> (and), <code>||</code> (or) and <code>!</code> (not): <code>if (age &gt;= 18 &amp;&amp; hasId)</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"loops-repeat-without-rewriting\">Loops: repeat without rewriting</h2>\n<p>A <code>for</code> loop has three parts in its brackets, separated by semicolons:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 3; i++) {\n  console.log('Lap', i);\n}\n//  let i = 1   start: create a counter\n//  i &lt;= 3      keep going while this is true\n//  i++         after each lap, add 1 to i</code></pre></div>\n<p>Output: <code>Lap 1</code>, <code>Lap 2</code>, <code>Lap 3</code>.</p>\n<p>When you have a list, <code>for...of</code> gives you each item in turn, with no counter to manage:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const sizes = ['S', 'M', 'L'];\nfor (const size of sizes) {\n  console.log(size);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>And a <code>while</code> loop repeats as long as a condition stays true. Make sure something inside it eventually makes the condition false, or it runs forever (an <strong>infinite loop</strong>, which freezes the page).</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"functions-name-your-steps\">Functions: name your steps</h2>\n<p>A function packages steps under a name. It takes <strong>inputs</strong> (parameters) and usually <strong>returns</strong> an output.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">function priceWithVat(pence) {\n  return Math.round(pence * 1.2);\n}\n\npriceWithVat(1000);   // 1200\npriceWithVat(4500);   // 5400</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>function</code> keyword, then the name, then parameters in brackets.</li><li><code>return</code> sends a value back to whoever called the function, and ends it.</li><li>Defining a function does nothing on its own. <strong>Calling</strong> it, <code>priceWithVat(1000)</code>, runs it.</li></ul>\n<p>Good function names are verbs that say what they do: <code>calculateShipping</code>, <code>isEven</code>, <code>formatPrice</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"3\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-same-three-ideas-in-python\">The same three ideas in Python</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">total = 4200\nif total &gt;= 5000:\n    print('Free shipping')\nelse:\n    print('Shipping: 3.99')\n\nfor i in range(1, 6):     # 1 to 5\n    print(i)\n\ndef is_even(n):\n    return n % 2 == 0</code></pre></div>\n<p>Python uses a colon and <strong>indentation</strong> (4 spaces) instead of curly braces, so indentation is not just style there: it is the structure.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"4\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"In an if / else if / else chain, how many branches run?","options":["All of them whose condition is true","Only the first one whose condition is true","Always the else"],"answer":1,"explain":"Once a branch matches, the rest are skipped."},{"q":"What does this print? for (let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) { console.log(i); }","options":["1 2 3","0 1 2","0 1 2 3"],"answer":1,"explain":"It starts at 0 and stops when i &lt; 3 becomes false, so 3 is never printed."},{"q":"What does return do inside a function?","options":["Sends a value back to the caller and ends the function","Prints the value","Restarts the function"],"answer":0,"explain":"return hands a result back. console.log is what prints."},{"q":"What causes an infinite loop?","options":["A loop with more than 1,000 items","A loop whose condition never becomes false","Using let instead of const"],"answer":1,"explain":"If nothing inside the loop eventually makes its condition false, it never stops."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Free shipping or not?","file":"shipping.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write an <code>if</code> / <code>else</code> that decides shipping:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>total</code> equal to <code>4200</code> (pence).</li><li><code>if (total &gt;= 5000)</code> print <code>'Free shipping'</code>.</li><li>Otherwise (<code>else</code>) print <code>'Shipping: 3.99'</code>.</li></ol>\n<p>Use the usual layout: braces on the same line as <code>if</code> and <code>else</code>, and each <code>console.log</code> indented on its own line.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const total = 4200;\nif (total >= 5000) {\n  console.log('Free shipping');\n} else {\n  console.log('Shipping: 3.99');\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+total\\s*=\\s*4200","text":"Line 1: <code>const total = 4200;</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*total\\s*>=\\s*5000\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"The <code>if</code> line: <code>if (total &gt;= 5000) {</code> with the condition in round brackets and an opening curly brace."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*['\"]Free shipping['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Inside the <code>if</code>: <code>console.log('Free shipping');</code>"},{"re":"\\}\\s*else\\s*\\{","text":"Close the <code>if</code> block and open the else on the same line: <code>} else {</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*['\"]Shipping: 3\\.99['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Inside the <code>else</code>: <code>console.log('Shipping: 3.99');</code>"}],"explain":"<p>4200 is not at least 5000, so the condition is <code>false</code>, the first block is skipped and the <code>else</code> block runs: <strong>Shipping: 3.99</strong>. Change <code>4200</code> to <code>5200</code> and run it again to see the other branch.</p>"},{"title":"Count to five","file":"count.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write a <code>for</code> loop that prints the numbers 1 to 5:</p>\n<ol><li><code>for (let i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++) {</code></li><li>Inside it, indented: <code>console.log(i);</code></li><li>Close it with <code>}</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {\n  console.log(i);\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*for\\s*\\(","text":"Start with the keyword <code>for</code> followed by round brackets."},{"re":"let\\s+i\\s*=\\s*1\\s*;","text":"First part: <code>let i = 1;</code> (start counting at 1). Note the semicolon."},{"re":"i\\s*<=\\s*5\\s*;","text":"Second part: <code>i &lt;= 5;</code> (keep going while i is 5 or less)."},{"re":"i\\+\\+\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Third part: <code>i++</code> then close the round bracket and open a curly brace: <code>i++) {</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*i\\s*\\)","text":"Inside the loop: <code>console.log(i);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>The loop starts with <code>i</code> at 1, checks <code>i &lt;= 5</code>, runs the body, then adds 1. When <code>i</code> reaches 6 the check fails and the loop stops. Output: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.</p>"},{"title":"Add up a basket","file":"basket.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Use a <code>for...of</code> loop to total some prices:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const prices = [2000, 1500, 4500];</code></li><li><code>let total = 0;</code> (use <code>let</code>, because it will change)</li><li>A loop: <code>for (const price of prices) {</code></li><li>Inside it: <code>total = total + price;</code></li><li>Close the loop with <code>}</code></li><li>After the loop: <code>console.log(total);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const prices = [2000, 1500, 4500];\nlet total = 0;\nfor (const price of prices) {\n  total = total + price;\n}\nconsole.log(total);","also":["const prices = [2000, 1500, 4500];\nlet total = 0;\nfor (const price of prices) {\n  total += price;\n}\nconsole.log(total);"],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+prices\\s*=\\s*\\[\\s*2000\\s*,\\s*1500\\s*,\\s*4500\\s*\\]","text":"Line 1 creates the list in square brackets: <code>const prices = [2000, 1500, 4500];</code>"},{"re":"let\\s+total\\s*=\\s*0","text":"Line 2: <code>let total = 0;</code> It must be <code>let</code> because the loop changes it."},{"re":"for\\s*\\(\\s*const\\s+price\\s+of\\s+prices\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"The loop line: <code>for (const price of prices) {</code> (singular <code>price</code>, plural <code>prices</code>)."},{"re":"total\\s*(\\+=|=\\s*total\\s*\\+)\\s*price","text":"Inside the loop, add each price to the total: <code>total = total + price;</code>"},{"re":"\\}\\s*\\n\\s*console\\.log\\(\\s*total\\s*\\)","text":"Close the loop with <code>}</code>, then print the total after it, on its own line."}],"explain":"<p><code>total</code> starts at 0. The loop runs three times, adding 2000, then 1500, then 4500. Printing after the loop shows <strong>8000</strong> pence: £80.00. If you put the <code>console.log</code> inside the loop, it would print the running total three times.</p>"},{"title":"Write your first function","file":"even.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write a function that tells you whether a number is even, then use it:</p>\n<ol><li><code>function isEven(n) {</code></li><li>Inside it: <code>return n % 2 === 0;</code></li><li>Close it with <code>}</code></li><li>Print <code>isEven(10)</code>.</li><li>Print <code>isEven(7)</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function isEven(n) {\n  return n % 2 === 0;\n}\nconsole.log(isEven(10));\nconsole.log(isEven(7));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+isEven\\s*\\(\\s*n\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"The first line: <code>function isEven(n) {</code> (capital E in <code>isEven</code>)."},{"re":"return\\s+n\\s*%\\s*2\\s*===\\s*0","text":"Inside: <code>return n % 2 === 0;</code> A number is even when its remainder after dividing by 2 is 0."},{"re":"isEven\\(\\s*10\\s*\\)","text":"After the function, call it: <code>console.log(isEven(10));</code>"},{"re":"isEven\\(\\s*7\\s*\\)","text":"Then: <code>console.log(isEven(7));</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>10 % 2</code> is 0, so <code>0 === 0</code> is <code>true</code>. <code>7 % 2</code> is 1, so the result is <code>false</code>. One function, reused with any number. That is the whole point of functions.</p>"},{"title":"A Python loop","file":"loop.py","lang":"python","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a Python loop that prints 1 to 5. Two lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>for i in range(1, 6):</code></li><li>Indented by <strong>4 spaces</strong>: <code>print(i)</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"for i in range(1, 6):\n    print(i)","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^for\\s+i\\s+in\\s+range\\(\\s*1\\s*,\\s*6\\s*\\)\\s*:","text":"Line 1: <code>for i in range(1, 6):</code> ending with a colon. <code>range(1, 6)</code> stops just before 6."},{"re":"^ {4}print\\(\\s*i\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2 must start with exactly 4 spaces (press Tab once), then <code>print(i)</code>."}],"explain":"<p><code>range(1, 6)</code> produces 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. It never includes the end number, which is a classic source of off-by-one bugs. The indented line is the loop's body.</p>"}],"text":"Three ideas run everything Every program, from a to-do list to a blockchain, is built from three ideas on top of the data types you just learned: Decisions : do this if something is true, otherwise do that. Loops : repeat something many times without writing it out many times. Functions : give a set of steps a name, so you can reuse it. Thinking before typing: pseudocode Professionals often sketch logic in plain English first. This is called pseudocode : for each product in the basket: add (price x quantity) to the total if the total is at least 5000 pence: shipping is free otherwise: shipping costs 399 pence Once the steps are clear, turning them into code is mostly spelling. When you feel stuck, write pseudocode first. Decisions: if, else if, else const stock = 3; if (stock === 0) { console.log('Sold out'); } else if (stock 5) { console.log('Only a few left'); } else { console.log('In stock'); } The condition goes in round brackets ( ) . The code to run goes in curly braces { } . Only the first branch whose condition is true runs. Indent the code inside braces by two spaces. The computer does not care, but humans do. Combine conditions with (and), || (or) and ! (not): if (age = 18 hasId) . Loops: repeat without rewriting A for loop has three parts in its brackets, separated by semicolons: for (let i = 1; i = 3; i++) { console.log('Lap', i); } // let i = 1 start: create a counter // i = 3 keep going while this is true // i++ after each lap, add 1 to i Output: Lap 1 , Lap 2 , Lap 3 . When you have a list, for...of gives you each item in turn, with no counter to manage: const sizes = ['S', 'M', 'L']; for (const size of sizes) { console.log(size); } And a while loop repeats as long as a condition stays true. Make sure something inside it eventually makes the condition false, or it runs forever (an infinite loop , which freezes the page). Functions: name your steps A function packages steps under a name. It takes inputs (parameters) and usually returns an output. function priceWithVat(pence) { return Math.round(pence * 1.2); } priceWithVat(1000); // 1200 priceWithVat(4500); // 5400 function keyword, then the name, then parameters in brackets. return sends a value back to whoever called the function, and ends it. Defining a function does nothing on its own. Calling it, priceWithVat(1000) , runs it. Good function names are verbs that say what they do: calculateShipping , isEven , formatPrice . The same three ideas in Python total = 4200 if total = 5000: print('Free shipping') else: print('Shipping: 3.99') for i in range(1, 6): # 1 to 5 print(i) def is_even(n): return n % 2 == 0 Python uses a colon and indentation (4 spaces) instead of curly braces, so indentation is not just style there: it is the structure.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-how-web-works","title":"How the web works","level":"beginner","minutes":12,"summary":"Browsers, servers, URLs, DNS and HTTP, explained with a single click on a link.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-happens-when-you-visit-a-website\">What happens when you visit a website</h2>\n<p>You type <code>https://shop.example.com/products?colour=red</code> and press Enter. In under a second:</p>\n<ol><li>Your <strong>browser</strong> (the client) asks <strong>DNS</strong> which IP address <code>shop.example.com</code> lives at. DNS is the internet's phone book.</li><li>The browser opens a secure connection (the <strong>S</strong> in HTTPS means encrypted with TLS) to the <strong>server</strong> at that address.</li><li>It sends an <strong>HTTP request</strong>: &quot;GET /products?colour=red please&quot;.</li><li>The server runs some code, maybe reads a database, and sends back an <strong>HTTP response</strong>: a status code plus some HTML.</li><li>The browser reads the HTML, then requests every CSS file, JavaScript file and image it mentions.</li><li>It builds the page and runs the JavaScript, which might fetch even more data.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"anatomy-of-a-url\">Anatomy of a URL</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Example</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Protocol</td><td><code>https://</code></td><td>How to talk (encrypted HTTP)</td></tr><tr><td>Domain</td><td><code>shop.example.com</code></td><td>Which server (via DNS)</td></tr><tr><td>Path</td><td><code>/products</code></td><td>Which resource on that server</td></tr><tr><td>Query string</td><td><code>?colour=red</code></td><td>Extra options, as key=value pairs</td></tr><tr><td>Fragment</td><td><code>#reviews</code></td><td>A spot within the page (never sent to the server)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"http-methods-and-status-codes\">HTTP methods and status codes</h2>\n<p>Requests use a <strong>method</strong> to say what they want:</p>\n<ul><li><code>GET</code>: read something (load a page, fetch products)</li><li><code>POST</code>: create something (submit an order)</li><li><code>PUT</code> / <code>PATCH</code>: update something</li><li><code>DELETE</code>: remove something</li></ul>\n<p>Responses carry a <strong>status code</strong>. Learn these by heart:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>You will see it when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>200</td><td>OK</td><td>Everything worked</td></tr><tr><td>301 / 302</td><td>Redirect</td><td>A page moved</td></tr><tr><td>400</td><td>Bad request</td><td>You sent invalid data</td></tr><tr><td>401 / 403</td><td>Not logged in / not allowed</td><td>Auth problems</td></tr><tr><td>404</td><td>Not found</td><td>Wrong URL</td></tr><tr><td>500</td><td>Server error</td><td>The server's code crashed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-three-languages-of-the-browser\">The three languages of the browser</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>HTML</strong> is the structure and content: headings, paragraphs, images, buttons.</li><li><strong>CSS</strong> is the presentation: colours, fonts, layout, animation.</li><li><strong>JavaScript</strong> is the behaviour: respond to clicks, fetch data, update the page.</li></ul>\n<p>Think of a house: HTML is the walls and rooms, CSS is the paint and furniture, JavaScript is the electricity and plumbing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"front-end-back-end-and-where-blockchain-fits\">Front end, back end, and where blockchain fits</h2>\n<ul><li>The <strong>front end</strong> runs in the user's browser. Anyone can view its code (right-click, View Source).</li><li>The <strong>back end</strong> runs on servers you control: databases, payments, secrets. Users cannot see it.</li><li>A <strong>blockchain</strong> is like a shared back end that nobody owns. Smart contracts are programs stored on it, and your front end talks to them through the user's wallet.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-try\"><p class=\"callout-title\">See it for yourself</p><p>Open any website, press <kbd>F12</kbd> (or right-click and choose Inspect), go to the <strong>Network</strong> tab and refresh. Every line is one HTTP request. Click one to see its method, status code, headers and response.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"What does DNS do?","options":["Encrypts your connection","Turns a domain name into the IP address of a server","Stores the website's HTML"],"answer":1,"explain":"DNS is the internet's phone book: names in, addresses out."},{"q":"A page loads but shows &quot;404&quot;. What happened?","options":["The server crashed","You are not logged in","The server could not find anything at that URL"],"answer":2,"explain":"404 means Not Found. 500 would mean the server crashed, 401 or 403 would be an auth problem."},{"q":"Which part of the browser stack controls behaviour, like what happens when you click &quot;Add to bag&quot;?","options":["HTML","CSS","JavaScript"],"answer":2,"explain":"HTML is structure, CSS is presentation, JavaScript is behaviour."},{"q":"Why must API keys and database passwords live on the back end, never in front-end code?","options":["Anyone can read front-end code in their browser","Front-end code runs too slowly","Browsers delete secrets automatically"],"answer":0,"explain":"Everything sent to the browser can be inspected. Secrets belong on servers."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Ask a DNS server for an address","file":"dns.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Step 1 above is a <strong>DNS lookup</strong>: turning a name into an IP address. You can do one yourself. Google runs a public DNS service you can ask over HTTPS, and it answers in JSON. Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const res = await fetch('https://dns.google/resolve?name=example.com&amp;type=A');</code></li><li><code>const data = await res.json();</code></li><li><code>console.log(data.Answer[0].data);</code></li></ol>\n<p><code>fetch</code> sends an HTTP request, and <code>await</code> means &quot;wait for the reply&quot;. Live requests work when you run Code Ladder on your own computer; the hosted preview may block them.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const res = await fetch('https://dns.google/resolve?name=example.com&type=A');\nconst data = await res.json();\nconsole.log(data.Answer[0].data);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+res\\s*=\\s*await\\s+fetch\\(","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const res = await fetch(</code>"},{"re":"dns\\.google/resolve\\?name=example\\.com&type=A","text":"The address, in quotes, is <code>'https://dns.google/resolve?name=example.com&amp;type=A'</code>. Check the <code>?</code> and the <code>&amp;</code>."},{"re":"const\\s+data\\s*=\\s*await\\s+res\\.json\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const data = await res.json();</code> turns the reply into data you can read."},{"re":"data\\.Answer\\[\\s*0\\s*\\]\\.data","text":"Line 3: the IP address is at <code>data.Answer[0].data</code>. Capital A on <code>Answer</code>."}],"explain":"<p><code>fetch</code> sends an HTTP GET request asking: what is the <strong>A record</strong> (IPv4 address) for example.com? The JSON reply has an <code>Answer</code> list, and the first answer's <code>data</code> is the IP address. Your browser does this lookup automatically, in milliseconds, for every new domain you visit.</p>"},{"title":"Take a URL apart","file":"url.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>JavaScript can split any URL into the parts in the table above. Write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const url = new URL('https://shop.example.com/products?colour=red');</code></li><li>Print <code>url.hostname</code></li><li>Print <code>url.pathname</code></li><li>Print <code>url.searchParams.get('colour')</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const url = new URL('https://shop.example.com/products?colour=red');\nconsole.log(url.hostname);\nconsole.log(url.pathname);\nconsole.log(url.searchParams.get('colour'));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+url\\s*=\\s*new\\s+URL\\(","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const url = new URL(</code> (URL in capitals)."},{"re":"shop\\.example\\.com/products\\?colour=red","text":"The URL in quotes: <code>'https://shop.example.com/products?colour=red'</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*url\\.hostname\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(url.hostname);</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*url\\.pathname\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(url.pathname);</code>"},{"re":"url\\.searchParams\\.get\\(\\s*['\"]colour['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(url.searchParams.get('colour'));</code> Capital P in <code>searchParams</code>."}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>shop.example.com</code> (the domain DNS looks up), <code>/products</code> (the path), and <code>red</code> (from the query string). Every web framework does this splitting for you behind the scenes.</p>"}],"text":"What happens when you visit a website You type https://shop.example.com/products?colour=red and press Enter. In under a second: Your browser (the client) asks DNS which IP address shop.example.com lives at. DNS is the internet's phone book. The browser opens a secure connection (the S in HTTPS means encrypted with TLS) to the server at that address. It sends an HTTP request : GET /products?colour=red please . The server runs some code, maybe reads a database, and sends back an HTTP response : a status code plus some HTML. The browser reads the HTML, then requests every CSS file, JavaScript file and image it mentions. It builds the page and runs the JavaScript, which might fetch even more data. Anatomy of a URL Part Example Meaning Protocol https:// How to talk (encrypted HTTP) Domain shop.example.com Which server (via DNS) Path /products Which resource on that server Query string ?colour=red Extra options, as key=value pairs Fragment #reviews A spot within the page (never sent to the server) HTTP methods and status codes Requests use a method to say what they want: GET : read something (load a page, fetch products) POST : create something (submit an order) PUT / PATCH : update something DELETE : remove something Responses carry a status code . Learn these by heart: Code Meaning You will see it when 200 OK Everything worked 301 / 302 Redirect A page moved 400 Bad request You sent invalid data 401 / 403 Not logged in / not allowed Auth problems 404 Not found Wrong URL 500 Server error The server's code crashed The three languages of the browser HTML is the structure and content: headings, paragraphs, images, buttons. CSS is the presentation: colours, fonts, layout, animation. JavaScript is the behaviour: respond to clicks, fetch data, update the page. Think of a house: HTML is the walls and rooms, CSS is the paint and furniture, JavaScript is the electricity and plumbing. Front end, back end, and where blockchain fits The front end runs in the user's browser. Anyone can view its code (right-click, View Source). The back end runs on servers you control: databases, payments, secrets. Users cannot see it. A blockchain is like a shared back end that nobody owns. Smart contracts are programs stored on it, and your front end talks to them through the user's wallet. See it for yourself Open any website, press F12 (or right-click and choose Inspect), go to the Network tab and refresh. Every line is one HTTP request. Click one to see its method, status code, headers and response.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-terminal","title":"The terminal is your superpower","level":"beginner","minutes":15,"summary":"Move around, create and inspect files, and run programs by typing, the way every developer works.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-bother-with-a-black-box-of-text\">Why bother with a black box of text?</h2>\n<p>Almost every developer tool (Git, Node, npm, Foundry, deployment tools) is used from the terminal. It is also faster than clicking once you know a dozen commands, and it lets you automate anything.</p>\n<p>Open it: on Mac press <kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd> and type <em>Terminal</em>. On Windows open <em>Terminal</em> (PowerShell), or Ubuntu if you installed WSL. Inside VS Code press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>`</kbd>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-dozen-commands-you-need\">The dozen commands you need</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>macOS / Linux / WSL</th><th>Windows PowerShell</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Where am I?</td><td><code>pwd</code></td><td><code>pwd</code></td></tr><tr><td>List files</td><td><code>ls -la</code></td><td><code>ls</code></td></tr><tr><td>Go into a folder</td><td><code>cd projects</code></td><td><code>cd projects</code></td></tr><tr><td>Go up one level</td><td><code>cd ..</code></td><td><code>cd ..</code></td></tr><tr><td>Go home</td><td><code>cd ~</code></td><td><code>cd ~</code></td></tr><tr><td>Make a folder</td><td><code>mkdir my-site</code></td><td><code>mkdir my-site</code></td></tr><tr><td>Make an empty file</td><td><code>touch index.html</code></td><td><code>ni index.html</code></td></tr><tr><td>Show a file</td><td><code>cat notes.txt</code></td><td><code>cat notes.txt</code></td></tr><tr><td>Copy</td><td><code>cp a.txt b.txt</code></td><td><code>cp a.txt b.txt</code></td></tr><tr><td>Move or rename</td><td><code>mv old.txt new.txt</code></td><td><code>mv old.txt new.txt</code></td></tr><tr><td>Delete a file</td><td><code>rm file.txt</code></td><td><code>rm file.txt</code></td></tr><tr><td>Clear the screen</td><td><code>clear</code> or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>L</kbd></td><td><code>cls</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"paths-absolute-and-relative\">Paths: absolute and relative</h2>\n<p>A <strong>path</strong> is an address for a file.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Absolute</strong> paths start from the root: <code>/Users/ada/projects/my-site</code> (Mac) or <code>C:\\Users\\ada\\projects</code> (Windows).</li><li><strong>Relative</strong> paths start from where you are: <code>projects/my-site</code>.</li><li><code>.</code> means &quot;this folder&quot;, <code>..</code> means &quot;the parent folder&quot;, <code>~</code> means &quot;my home folder&quot;.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"your-first-session\">Your first session</h2>\n<p>Type these one at a time, reading the output after each:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">cd ~\nmkdir -p code/hello-terminal\ncd code/hello-terminal\npwd\necho &quot;Hello from the terminal&quot; &gt; hello.txt\nls -la\ncat hello.txt\ncode .</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>echo &quot;...&quot; &gt; file</code> writes text into a file. <code>code .</code> opens the current folder in VS Code (see the Install guide if that command is missing).</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"commands-flags-and-arguments\">Commands, flags and arguments</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">ls -la ~/code</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>ls</code> is the <strong>command</strong>.</li><li><code>-la</code> are <strong>flags</strong> that change its behaviour (<code>-l</code> long format, <code>-a</code> include hidden files).</li><li><code>~/code</code> is the <strong>argument</strong>: what to act on.</li></ul>\n<p>Forgot how a command works? Try <code>ls --help</code>, <code>man ls</code> (press <code>q</code> to quit), or <code>tldr ls</code> if you installed tldr.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">rm does not use the Recycle Bin</p><p>Files deleted with <code>rm</code> are gone. <code>rm -rf folder</code> deletes a folder and everything inside without asking. Never run it with a path you are not 100% sure about, and never paste commands from strangers.</p></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Speed habits from day one</p><p>Press <kbd>Tab</kbd> to autocomplete names. Press <kbd>Up</kbd> to reuse the last command. Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd> to search your history. Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd> to stop a running program.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"You are in /Users/ada/code/site. Where does <code>cd ..</code> take you?","options":["/Users/ada","/Users/ada/code","Your home folder"],"answer":1,"explain":"<code>..</code> is the parent folder, one level up."},{"q":"What does the <code>-a</code> in <code>ls -la</code> do?","options":["Sorts alphabetically","Includes hidden files (names starting with a dot, like .gitignore)","Shows all folders on the computer"],"answer":1,"explain":"Flags modify a command. Hidden files matter a lot in development: .git, .env, .gitignore."},{"q":"Which command is dangerous to run without double-checking the path?","options":["pwd","ls -la","rm -rf"],"answer":2,"explain":"rm -rf deletes everything in the path permanently, with no confirmation."},{"q":"A dev server is running in your terminal and you want to stop it. What do you press?","options":["Ctrl+C","Ctrl+Z then Enter","Escape"],"answer":0,"explain":"Ctrl+C sends an interrupt that stops the running program."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Make a project folder","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Type the two commands that create a folder called <code>my-site</code> and then move into it. This editor checks your commands; run them for real in your own terminal afterwards.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"mkdir my-site\ncd my-site","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*mkdir\\s","text":"Line 1 uses <code>mkdir</code> (&quot;make directory&quot;)."},{"re":"mkdir\\s+my-site","text":"Name the folder <code>my-site</code>, with a hyphen and no spaces."},{"re":"^\\s*cd\\s+my-site\\s*$","text":"Line 2 moves into it with <code>cd</code> (&quot;change directory&quot;): <code>cd my-site</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>mkdir my-site</code> creates the folder. <code>cd my-site</code> moves you inside it, so every command after that runs in <code>my-site</code>. Use <code>pwd</code> to check where you are.</p>"},{"title":"Write and read a file","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Three commands:</p>\n<ol><li>Create a file called <code>notes.txt</code> containing the word <code>Hello</code> (use <code>echo &quot;Hello&quot;</code> and the <code>&gt;</code> symbol).</li><li>List all files, including hidden ones, in long format.</li><li>Print the contents of <code>notes.txt</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"echo \"Hello\" > notes.txt\nls -la\ncat notes.txt","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*echo\\s+[\"']Hello[\"']","text":"Line 1 starts with <code>echo &quot;Hello&quot;</code>."},{"re":">\\s*notes\\.txt","text":"Send the output into the file with <code>&gt;</code>: <code>echo &quot;Hello&quot; &gt; notes.txt</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*ls\\s+-la\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>ls -la</code> (list, long format, all files)."},{"re":"^\\s*cat\\s+notes\\.txt\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>cat notes.txt</code> prints a file's contents."}],"explain":"<p><code>echo</code> prints text, and <code>&gt;</code> redirects that text into a file instead of the screen (replacing anything already in it; <code>&gt;&gt;</code> would add to the end). <code>ls -la</code> shows the new file, and <code>cat</code> reads it back.</p>"}],"text":"Why bother with a black box of text? Almost every developer tool (Git, Node, npm, Foundry, deployment tools) is used from the terminal. It is also faster than clicking once you know a dozen commands, and it lets you automate anything. Open it: on Mac press Cmd + Space and type Terminal . On Windows open Terminal (PowerShell), or Ubuntu if you installed WSL. Inside VS Code press Ctrl + ` . The dozen commands you need Task macOS / Linux / WSL Windows PowerShell Where am I? pwd pwd List files ls -la ls Go into a folder cd projects cd projects Go up one level cd .. cd .. Go home cd ~ cd ~ Make a folder mkdir my-site mkdir my-site Make an empty file touch index.html ni index.html Show a file cat notes.txt cat notes.txt Copy cp a.txt b.txt cp a.txt b.txt Move or rename mv old.txt new.txt mv old.txt new.txt Delete a file rm file.txt rm file.txt Clear the screen clear or Ctrl + L cls Paths: absolute and relative A path is an address for a file. Absolute paths start from the root: /Users/ada/projects/my-site (Mac) or C:\\Users\\ada\\projects (Windows). Relative paths start from where you are: projects/my-site . . means this folder , .. means the parent folder , ~ means my home folder . Your first session Type these one at a time, reading the output after each: cd ~ mkdir -p code/hello-terminal cd code/hello-terminal pwd echo Hello from the terminal hello.txt ls -la cat hello.txt code . echo ... file writes text into a file. code . opens the current folder in VS Code (see the Install guide if that command is missing). Commands, flags and arguments ls -la ~/code ls is the command . -la are flags that change its behaviour ( -l long format, -a include hidden files). ~/code is the argument : what to act on. Forgot how a command works? Try ls --help , man ls (press q to quit), or tldr ls if you installed tldr. rm does not use the Recycle Bin Files deleted with rm are gone. rm -rf folder deletes a folder and everything inside without asking. Never run it with a path you are not 100% sure about, and never paste commands from strangers. Speed habits from day one Press Tab to autocomplete names. Press Up to reuse the last command. Press Ctrl + R to search your history. Press Ctrl + C to stop a running program.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-install","title":"Install your developer toolkit","level":"beginner","minutes":30,"summary":"Set up VS Code, Git, Node.js and a GitHub account, then prove each one works.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-day-1-setup\">The Day 1 setup</h2>\n<p>Open the <a href=\"#toolkit\">Install everything</a> page, choose your operating system at the top, and work through the <strong>Day one</strong> section in order. It has copy-paste commands for macOS, Windows and Linux. Tick each item when it works.</p>\n<p>The minimum before your next lesson:</p>\n<ol><li>A package manager (Homebrew on Mac, winget on Windows, apt on Linux)</li><li><strong>VS Code</strong> with Prettier, Error Lens and Live Server</li><li><strong>Git</strong>, with your name and email configured</li><li>A <strong>GitHub</strong> account and the <code>gh</code> CLI, logged in</li><li><strong>Node.js LTS</strong></li></ol>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Windows users</p><p>Install WSL2 too (<code>wsl --install</code> in an administrator PowerShell). You can do web development on plain Windows, but blockchain tools like Foundry expect Linux, and most tutorials assume a Unix terminal.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"prove-it-works\">Prove it works</h2>\n<p>Close and reopen your terminal (new installs often need a fresh terminal), then run:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">git --version\nnode -v\nnpm -v\ngh auth status\ncode --version</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each should print a version number or &quot;Logged in&quot;. If one says &quot;command not found&quot;, that tool is not installed or not on your <strong>PATH</strong> (the list of folders your terminal searches for programs). Reopening the terminal fixes most PATH problems; reinstalling fixes most of the rest.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"set-up-vs-code-like-a-professional\">Set up VS Code like a professional</h2>\n<p>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd> (<kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd> on Mac), type <em>Open User Settings (JSON)</em>, and add:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\"><pre><code class=\"language-json\">{\n  &quot;editor.formatOnSave&quot;: true,\n  &quot;editor.defaultFormatter&quot;: &quot;esbenp.prettier-vscode&quot;,\n  &quot;editor.tabSize&quot;: 2,\n  &quot;editor.wordWrap&quot;: &quot;on&quot;,\n  &quot;editor.minimap.enabled&quot;: false,\n  &quot;files.autoSave&quot;: &quot;onFocusChange&quot;,\n  &quot;editor.bracketPairColorization.enabled&quot;: true\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Format-on-save means your code always looks consistent, which makes mistakes easier to spot.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"your-first-node-js-program\">Your first Node.js program</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">mkdir -p ~/code/hello-node &amp;&amp; cd ~/code/hello-node\necho 'console.log(&quot;Node is working. 2 + 2 =&quot;, 2 + 2);' &gt; hello.js\nnode hello.js</code></pre></div>\n<p>You just ran JavaScript outside a browser. That is how build tools, servers and smart-contract scripts run.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-organise-your-projects\">How to organise your projects</h2>\n<p>Keep every project in one place, one folder per project:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">~/code/\n  hello-node/\n  about-me/\n  weather-app/\n  my-first-token/</code></pre></div>\n<p>Never put code in Downloads or on the Desktop, and avoid cloud-synced folders (iCloud, OneDrive) for code: they fight with Git and <code>node_modules</code>.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"You install Node but <code>node -v</code> says &quot;command not found&quot;. What should you try first?","options":["Reinstall your operating system","Close and reopen the terminal, then try again","Install a different code editor"],"answer":1,"explain":"New installs update your PATH, but already-open terminals do not see the change."},{"q":"Why turn on format-on-save?","options":["It makes code run faster","Your code stays consistently formatted, so structure and mistakes are easier to see","GitHub requires it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Consistent formatting is a basic professional habit, and Prettier does it for free."},{"q":"Where is the best place to keep your code projects?","options":["On the Desktop","In a OneDrive or iCloud folder","A dedicated folder like ~/code with one subfolder per project"],"answer":2,"explain":"Sync services and code tooling do not get along. Keep code in a plain local folder and back it up with Git and GitHub."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Check your tools are installed","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Type the three commands that print the versions of Node.js, npm and Git, one per line, in that order. (Use the short <code>-v</code> flag for Node and npm, and <code>--version</code> for Git.)</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"node -v\nnpm -v\ngit --version","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*node\\s+-v\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>node -v</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*npm\\s+-v\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>npm -v</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+--version\\s*$","text":"Line 3: Git uses the long form with two hyphens: <code>git --version</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Each command prints a version number if the tool is installed and on your PATH. &quot;command not found&quot; means it is not installed, or your terminal needs reopening.</p>"},{"title":"Write VS Code settings","file":"settings.json","lang":"json","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a small settings file that turns on format-on-save and sets the tab size to 2. It is JSON: keys in double quotes, a colon, then the value, with a comma between entries.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\"><pre><code class=\"language-json\">{\n  &quot;editor.formatOnSave&quot;: true,\n  &quot;editor.tabSize&quot;: 2\n}</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"{\n  \"editor.formatOnSave\": true,\n  \"editor.tabSize\": 2\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*\\{","text":"Start with an opening curly brace <code>{</code> on its own line."},{"re":"\"editor\\.formatOnSave\"\\s*:\\s*true\\s*,","text":"Line 2: <code>&quot;editor.formatOnSave&quot;: true,</code> with a comma at the end, because another entry follows."},{"re":"\"editor\\.tabSize\"\\s*:\\s*2\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>&quot;editor.tabSize&quot;: 2</code> with <strong>no</strong> comma, because it is the last entry."},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Close with <code>}</code> on the last line."}],"explain":"<p>JSON is strict: double quotes only, commas between entries, no comma after the last one. A single misplaced comma makes the whole file invalid, which is why editors highlight JSON errors in red.</p>"}],"text":"The Day 1 setup Open the Install everything page, choose your operating system at the top, and work through the Day one section in order. It has copy-paste commands for macOS, Windows and Linux. Tick each item when it works. The minimum before your next lesson: A package manager (Homebrew on Mac, winget on Windows, apt on Linux) VS Code with Prettier, Error Lens and Live Server Git , with your name and email configured A GitHub account and the gh CLI, logged in Node.js LTS Windows users Install WSL2 too ( wsl --install in an administrator PowerShell). You can do web development on plain Windows, but blockchain tools like Foundry expect Linux, and most tutorials assume a Unix terminal. Prove it works Close and reopen your terminal (new installs often need a fresh terminal), then run: git --version node -v npm -v gh auth status code --version Each should print a version number or Logged in . If one says command not found , that tool is not installed or not on your PATH (the list of folders your terminal searches for programs). Reopening the terminal fixes most PATH problems; reinstalling fixes most of the rest. Set up VS Code like a professional Press Ctrl + Shift + P ( Cmd + Shift + P on Mac), type Open User Settings (JSON) , and add: { editor.formatOnSave : true, editor.defaultFormatter : esbenp.prettier-vscode , editor.tabSize : 2, editor.wordWrap : on , editor.minimap.enabled : false, files.autoSave : onFocusChange , editor.bracketPairColorization.enabled : true } Format-on-save means your code always looks consistent, which makes mistakes easier to spot. Your first Node.js program mkdir -p ~/code/hello-node cd ~/code/hello-node echo 'console.log( Node is working. 2 + 2 = , 2 + 2);' hello.js node hello.js You just ran JavaScript outside a browser. That is how build tools, servers and smart-contract scripts run. How to organise your projects Keep every project in one place, one folder per project: ~/code/ hello-node/ about-me/ weather-app/ my-first-token/ Never put code in Downloads or on the Desktop, and avoid cloud-synced folders (iCloud, OneDrive) for code: they fight with Git and node_modules .","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-git","title":"Git and GitHub basics","level":"beginner","minutes":20,"summary":"Save snapshots of your work, undo mistakes, and publish your code to GitHub.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-git-is\">What Git is</h2>\n<p>Git is a save-game system for code. Every <strong>commit</strong> is a snapshot of your whole project with a message saying what changed. You can jump back to any snapshot, see exactly what changed between two of them, and work on experiments without breaking the working version.</p>\n<p><strong>GitHub</strong> is a website that hosts Git repositories online. It is your backup, your collaboration space and your public portfolio. Employers and clients look at it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-three-places-your-changes-live\">The three places your changes live</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Working folder</strong>: the files you are editing.</li><li><strong>Staging area</strong>: changes you have chosen to include in the next commit (<code>git add</code>).</li><li><strong>Repository history</strong>: commits saved forever (<code>git commit</code>).</li></ol>\n<p>This two-step add-then-commit lets you save related changes together, even if you edited several things.</p>\n<h2 id=\"your-first-repository\">Your first repository</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">cd ~/code\nmkdir about-me &amp;&amp; cd about-me\ngit init\necho &quot;# About me&quot; &gt; README.md\ngit status\ngit add README.md\ngit commit -m &quot;Add README&quot;\ngit log --oneline</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>git status</code> is the command you will run more than any other. It tells you what changed and what is staged.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-daily-loop\">The daily loop</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">git status                # what changed?\ngit diff                  # show the exact lines\ngit add index.html        # stage one file (or: git add . for everything)\ngit commit -m &quot;Add hero section with brand photo&quot;\ngit push                  # upload to GitHub</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"publish-to-github\">Publish to GitHub</h2>\n<p>With the GitHub CLI logged in (<code>gh auth login</code>), one command creates the online repository and pushes your code:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">gh repo create about-me --public --source=. --push</code></pre></div>\n<p>Your code is now at <code>github.com/your-username/about-me</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ignore-files-that-should-never-be-committed\">Ignore files that should never be committed</h2>\n<p>Create a file called <code>.gitignore</code> in the project root:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">node_modules/\n.env\n.DS_Store\ndist/</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>node_modules/</code> can be rebuilt with <code>npm install</code>, and is huge.</li><li><code>.env</code> holds secrets like API keys. Committing it leaks them.</li><li>Build output (<code>dist/</code>) is generated from your source.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Secrets in Git are forever</p><p>If you commit an API key, deleting it in the next commit does not remove it: it stays in the history. Treat it as leaked: revoke the key and make a new one. Add <code>.env</code> to <code>.gitignore</code> before your first commit, every time.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"branches-in-one-minute\">Branches in one minute</h2>\n<p>A branch is a separate line of work. Keep <code>main</code> always working, and build new things on a branch:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">git switch -c add-contact-form    # create and switch to a new branch\n# ...edit, add, commit...\ngit switch main\ngit merge add-contact-form</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"free-hosting-with-github-pages\">Free hosting with GitHub Pages</h2>\n<p>For plain HTML/CSS/JS sites: on GitHub open your repo, then <strong>Settings, Pages</strong>, choose <strong>Deploy from a branch</strong>, pick <code>main</code> and <code>/ (root)</code>, and save. A minute later your site is live at <code>your-username.github.io/about-me</code>.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Good commit messages</p><p>Write them as commands, describing what the commit does: &quot;Add product grid&quot;, &quot;Fix mobile menu overlap&quot;, &quot;Remove unused images&quot;. Future you, reading the log at 11pm, will be grateful.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>git add</code> do?","options":["Uploads files to GitHub","Moves changes into the staging area, ready for the next commit","Creates a new repository"],"answer":1,"explain":"add stages, commit saves a snapshot, push uploads."},{"q":"You accidentally committed your .env file with an API key and pushed it. What is the right response?","options":["Delete the file in a new commit and move on","Make the repository private","Revoke the key immediately and create a new one, then remove the file and add .env to .gitignore"],"answer":2,"explain":"The key is in the history and may already have been scraped. Revoking is the only safe fix."},{"q":"Why work on a branch instead of directly on main?","options":["So main always stays working while you experiment","Branches make the code run faster","GitHub does not allow commits to main"],"answer":0,"explain":"Branches isolate unfinished work. You merge only when it is ready."},{"q":"Which command do professionals run most often?","options":["git init","git status","git clone"],"answer":1,"explain":"git status shows what changed and what is staged. Run it before every add and commit."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Your first commit","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Inside a project folder that has a <code>README.md</code>, type the three commands that: start a Git repository, stage <code>README.md</code>, and commit it with the message <code>Add README</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"git init\ngit add README.md\ngit commit -m \"Add README\"","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+init\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>git init</code> creates the repository."},{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+add\\s+README\\.md\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>git add README.md</code> stages the file (README in capitals, like the file name)."},{"re":"git\\s+commit\\s+-m\\s+","text":"Line 3 starts <code>git commit -m</code> (m for message)."},{"re":"-m\\s+[\"']Add README[\"']","text":"The message goes in quotes: <code>&quot;Add README&quot;</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>init</code> creates the hidden <code>.git</code> folder that stores history. <code>add</code> moves changes into the staging area. <code>commit</code> saves a permanent snapshot with your message. Run <code>git log --oneline</code> afterwards to see it.</p>"},{"title":"Work on a branch, then merge it","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Type the five commands for this workflow:</p>\n<ol><li>Create and switch to a new branch called <code>add-contact-form</code>.</li><li>Stage everything.</li><li>Commit with the message <code>Add contact form</code>.</li><li>Switch back to <code>main</code>.</li><li>Merge <code>add-contact-form</code> into it.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"git switch -c add-contact-form\ngit add .\ngit commit -m \"Add contact form\"\ngit switch main\ngit merge add-contact-form","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+switch\\s+-c\\s+add-contact-form\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>git switch -c add-contact-form</code> (<code>-c</code> means create)."},{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+add\\s+\\.\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>git add .</code> (the dot means &quot;everything in this folder&quot;)."},{"re":"git\\s+commit\\s+-m\\s+[\"']Add contact form[\"']","text":"Line 3: <code>git commit -m &quot;Add contact form&quot;</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+switch\\s+main\\s*$","text":"Line 4: <code>git switch main</code> (no <code>-c</code>: main already exists)."},{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+merge\\s+add-contact-form\\s*$","text":"Line 5: <code>git merge add-contact-form</code>"}],"explain":"<p>The branch let you build the form without touching <code>main</code>. Merging brings those commits into <code>main</code> once they are ready. On teams, step 5 happens through a pull request instead.</p>"}],"text":"What Git is Git is a save-game system for code. Every commit is a snapshot of your whole project with a message saying what changed. You can jump back to any snapshot, see exactly what changed between two of them, and work on experiments without breaking the working version. GitHub is a website that hosts Git repositories online. It is your backup, your collaboration space and your public portfolio. Employers and clients look at it. The three places your changes live Working folder : the files you are editing. Staging area : changes you have chosen to include in the next commit ( git add ). Repository history : commits saved forever ( git commit ). This two-step add-then-commit lets you save related changes together, even if you edited several things. Your first repository cd ~/code mkdir about-me cd about-me git init echo # About me README.md git status git add README.md git commit -m Add README git log --oneline git status is the command you will run more than any other. It tells you what changed and what is staged. The daily loop git status # what changed? git diff # show the exact lines git add index.html # stage one file (or: git add . for everything) git commit -m Add hero section with brand photo git push # upload to GitHub Publish to GitHub With the GitHub CLI logged in ( gh auth login ), one command creates the online repository and pushes your code: gh repo create about-me --public --source=. --push Your code is now at github.com/your-username/about-me . Ignore files that should never be committed Create a file called .gitignore in the project root: node_modules/ .env .DS_Store dist/ node_modules/ can be rebuilt with npm install , and is huge. .env holds secrets like API keys. Committing it leaks them. Build output ( dist/ ) is generated from your source. Secrets in Git are forever If you commit an API key, deleting it in the next commit does not remove it: it stays in the history. Treat it as leaked: revoke the key and make a new one. Add .env to .gitignore before your first commit, every time. Branches in one minute A branch is a separate line of work. Keep main always working, and build new things on a branch: git switch -c add-contact-form # create and switch to a new branch # ...edit, add, commit... git switch main git merge add-contact-form Free hosting with GitHub Pages For plain HTML/CSS/JS sites: on GitHub open your repo, then Settings, Pages , choose Deploy from a branch , pick main and / (root) , and save. A minute later your site is live at your-username.github.io/about-me . Good commit messages Write them as commands, describing what the commit does: Add product grid , Fix mobile menu overlap , Remove unused images . Future you, reading the log at 11pm, will be grateful.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-packages","title":"Packages, npm and package.json","level":"intermediate","minutes":18,"summary":"Use other people's code safely, understand package.json, versions and lockfiles, and run project scripts.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-packages-exist\">Why packages exist</h2>\n<p>Nobody writes everything from scratch. A <strong>package</strong> is reusable code someone published: a date library, React, a testing tool, the OpenZeppelin contracts. <strong>npm</strong> (Node Package Manager) installs them from the npm registry, which holds millions of packages.</p>\n<h2 id=\"start-a-project\">Start a project</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">mkdir price-tools &amp;&amp; cd price-tools\nnpm init -y\nnpm install date-fns\nnpm install -D prettier</code></pre></div>\n<p>That creates three things:</p>\n<ul><li><code>package.json</code>: the project's identity card and list of dependencies.</li><li><code>package-lock.json</code>: the exact version of every package installed, including dependencies of dependencies.</li><li><code>node_modules/</code>: the installed code. Never edit it, never commit it.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"reading-package-json\">Reading package.json</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\"><pre><code class=\"language-json\">{\n  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;price-tools&quot;,\n  &quot;version&quot;: &quot;1.0.0&quot;,\n  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;,\n  &quot;scripts&quot;: {\n    &quot;dev&quot;: &quot;node --watch index.js&quot;,\n    &quot;format&quot;: &quot;prettier --write .&quot;\n  },\n  &quot;dependencies&quot;: {\n    &quot;date-fns&quot;: &quot;^4.1.0&quot;\n  },\n  &quot;devDependencies&quot;: {\n    &quot;prettier&quot;: &quot;^3.3.3&quot;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><strong>dependencies</strong> are needed when the app runs.</li><li><strong>devDependencies</strong> (<code>-D</code>) are only needed while developing: formatters, test runners, build tools.</li><li><strong>scripts</strong> are shortcuts. <code>npm run dev</code> runs <code>node --watch index.js</code>. Every project's README should tell you which scripts to run.</li><li><code>&quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;</code> lets you use modern <code>import</code> syntax in Node.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"version-numbers-semver\">Version numbers (semver)</h2>\n<p>Versions look like <code>MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH</code>, e.g. <code>4.1.0</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Bumped when</th><th>Safe to upgrade?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PATCH (4.1.<strong>1</strong>)</td><td>Bug fixes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>MINOR (4.<strong>2</strong>.0)</td><td>New features, nothing broken</td><td>Usually</td></tr><tr><td>MAJOR (<strong>5</strong>.0.0)</td><td>Breaking changes</td><td>Read the changelog first</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>^4.1.0</code> means &quot;any 4.x.x at least 4.1.0&quot;. <code>~4.1.0</code> means &quot;any 4.1.x&quot;. The lockfile pins the exact version so everyone on the team gets identical installs. <strong>Commit the lockfile.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"useful-commands\">Useful commands</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm install            # install everything in package.json (after cloning a project)\nnpm ci                 # clean install exactly from the lockfile (used in CI)\nnpm run &lt;script&gt;       # run a script from package.json\nnpx &lt;tool&gt;             # run a package's command without installing it globally\nnpm outdated           # see what has newer versions\nnpm audit              # check for known vulnerabilities\nnpm uninstall date-fns # remove a package</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"local-versus-global-installs\">Local versus global installs</h2>\n<p>Install almost everything <strong>locally</strong> (inside the project). That way each project has its own versions and a teammate gets the same setup with one <code>npm install</code>. Global installs (<code>npm install -g</code>) are only for standalone CLI tools like <code>pnpm</code> or <code>vercel</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-a-package-like-a-senior\">Choosing a package like a senior</h2>\n<p>Before adding a dependency, check on npmjs.com and GitHub:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Weekly downloads</strong> and <strong>last publish date</strong>: is it used and maintained?</li><li><strong>Open issues</strong>: are serious bugs ignored?</li><li><strong>Size and dependencies</strong>: does a tiny feature pull in 200 other packages?</li><li><strong>Licence</strong>: MIT, Apache-2.0 and BSD are business-friendly.</li><li><strong>Do I need it at all?</strong> Five lines of your own code beat a dependency you have to trust forever.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Supply-chain attacks are real</p><p>Attackers publish look-alike packages (<code>reactt</code>, <code>ether-js</code>) and sometimes hijack real ones. Check the exact name before installing, prefer well-known packages, keep the lockfile committed, and run <code>npm audit</code>. In crypto projects this matters even more: malicious packages hunt for wallet keys.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Which file pins the exact version of every installed package so all machines match?","options":["package.json","package-lock.json","node_modules/.version"],"answer":1,"explain":"package.json lists ranges like ^4.1.0; the lockfile records exactly what was installed."},{"q":"A testing tool you only use during development should go in:","options":["dependencies","devDependencies (npm install -D)","A global install"],"answer":1,"explain":"devDependencies are not needed when the app runs in production."},{"q":"A package goes from 2.8.3 to 3.0.0. What should you expect?","options":["Only bug fixes","New features with nothing broken","Breaking changes: read the changelog before upgrading"],"answer":2,"explain":"A MAJOR bump signals breaking changes."},{"q":"Should node_modules be committed to Git?","options":["Yes, so the project works offline","No: it is rebuilt from package.json and the lockfile with npm install","Only for small projects"],"answer":1,"explain":"node_modules goes in .gitignore. The lockfile makes the rebuild exact."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Start a project and add packages","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Type three commands: create a <code>package.json</code> with the default answers, install <code>date-fns</code> as a normal dependency, and install <code>prettier</code> as a <strong>dev</strong> dependency.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"npm init -y\nnpm install date-fns\nnpm install -D prettier","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*npm\\s+init\\s+-y\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>npm init -y</code> (<code>-y</code> says yes to every question)."},{"re":"^\\s*npm\\s+install\\s+date-fns\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>npm install date-fns</code>"},{"re":"npm\\s+install\\s+-D\\s+prettier","text":"Line 3: dev dependencies use <code>-D</code>: <code>npm install -D prettier</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Add scripts to package.json","file":"package.json","lang":"json","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the <code>&quot;scripts&quot;</code> section with two scripts: <code>dev</code> runs <code>node --watch index.js</code>, and <code>format</code> runs <code>prettier --write .</code></p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"\"scripts\": {\n  \"dev\": \"node --watch index.js\",\n  \"format\": \"prettier --write .\"\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*\"scripts\"\\s*:\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>&quot;scripts&quot;: {</code>"},{"re":"\"dev\"\\s*:\\s*\"node --watch index\\.js\"\\s*,","text":"Line 2: <code>&quot;dev&quot;: &quot;node --watch index.js&quot;,</code> with a comma at the end."},{"re":"\"format\"\\s*:\\s*\"prettier --write \\.\"","text":"Line 3: <code>&quot;format&quot;: &quot;prettier --write .&quot;</code> with no comma (it is the last one)."},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Close the section with <code>}</code>."}],"explain":"<p>Now <code>npm run dev</code> starts your app and restarts it on every save, and <code>npm run format</code> tidies every file. Scripts mean nobody on a team has to remember long commands.</p>"}],"text":"Why packages exist Nobody writes everything from scratch. A package is reusable code someone published: a date library, React, a testing tool, the OpenZeppelin contracts. npm (Node Package Manager) installs them from the npm registry, which holds millions of packages. Start a project mkdir price-tools cd price-tools npm init -y npm install date-fns npm install -D prettier That creates three things: package.json : the project's identity card and list of dependencies. package-lock.json : the exact version of every package installed, including dependencies of dependencies. node_modules/ : the installed code. Never edit it, never commit it. Reading package.json { name : price-tools , version : 1.0.0 , type : module , scripts : { dev : node --watch index.js , format : prettier --write . }, dependencies : { date-fns : ^4.1.0 }, devDependencies : { prettier : ^3.3.3 } } dependencies are needed when the app runs. devDependencies ( -D ) are only needed while developing: formatters, test runners, build tools. scripts are shortcuts. npm run dev runs node --watch index.js . Every project's README should tell you which scripts to run. type : module lets you use modern import syntax in Node. Version numbers (semver) Versions look like MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH , e.g. 4.1.0 : Part Bumped when Safe to upgrade? PATCH (4.1. 1 ) Bug fixes Yes MINOR (4. 2 .0) New features, nothing broken Usually MAJOR ( 5 .0.0) Breaking changes Read the changelog first ^4.1.0 means any 4.x.x at least 4.1.0 . ~4.1.0 means any 4.1.x . The lockfile pins the exact version so everyone on the team gets identical installs. Commit the lockfile. Useful commands npm install # install everything in package.json (after cloning a project) npm ci # clean install exactly from the lockfile (used in CI) npm run script # run a script from package.json npx tool # run a package's command without installing it globally npm outdated # see what has newer versions npm audit # check for known vulnerabilities npm uninstall date-fns # remove a package Local versus global installs Install almost everything locally (inside the project). That way each project has its own versions and a teammate gets the same setup with one npm install . Global installs ( npm install -g ) are only for standalone CLI tools like pnpm or vercel . Choosing a package like a senior Before adding a dependency, check on npmjs.com and GitHub: Weekly downloads and last publish date : is it used and maintained? Open issues : are serious bugs ignored? Size and dependencies : does a tiny feature pull in 200 other packages? Licence : MIT, Apache-2.0 and BSD are business-friendly. Do I need it at all? Five lines of your own code beat a dependency you have to trust forever. Supply-chain attacks are real Attackers publish look-alike packages ( reactt , ether-js ) and sometimes hijack real ones. Check the exact name before installing, prefer well-known packages, keep the lockfile committed, and run npm audit . In crypto projects this matters even more: malicious packages hunt for wallet keys.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-debugging","title":"Debugging like a professional","level":"intermediate","minutes":20,"summary":"Read errors and stack traces, use breakpoints and the Network tab, and find any bug with a calm, repeatable method.","html":"<h2 id=\"bugs-are-normal\">Bugs are normal</h2>\n<p>Professional developers spend a large share of their time debugging. The difference is that they have a <strong>method</strong>, so bugs feel like puzzles rather than emergencies.</p>\n<h2 id=\"step-1-read-the-whole-error\">Step 1: read the whole error</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'price')\n    at getTotal (cart.js:14:22)\n    at renderCart (cart.js:31:17)\n    at HTMLButtonElement.&lt;anonymous&gt; (main.js:8:5)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Read it top to bottom:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Type</strong>: <code>TypeError</code>, something was the wrong type.</li><li><strong>Message</strong>: you tried to read <code>.price</code> from something that is <code>undefined</code>.</li><li><strong>Location</strong>: <code>cart.js</code>, line 14, column 22, inside <code>getTotal</code>.</li><li><strong>Stack trace</strong>: how we got there. <code>main.js</code> line 8 called <code>renderCart</code>, which called <code>getTotal</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>So on line 14 of cart.js, the thing before <code>.price</code> is undefined. Maybe an item is missing, or an array index is out of range.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-errors-you-will-meet-most\">The errors you will meet most</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>Usually means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>SyntaxError</code></td><td>A typo in the code: missing bracket, quote or comma</td></tr><tr><td><code>ReferenceError: x is not defined</code></td><td>Misspelt name, or used before it was declared</td></tr><tr><td><code>TypeError: x is not a function</code></td><td>Calling something that is not a function (often a typo or wrong import)</td></tr><tr><td><code>TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined</code></td><td>The object you expected is not there</td></tr><tr><td><code>Failed to fetch</code> / CORS error</td><td>Network request blocked or server unreachable</td></tr><tr><td><code>404</code> in the Network tab</td><td>Wrong file path or URL</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"step-2-look-inside-with-console-and-breakpoints\">Step 2: look inside with console and breakpoints</h2>\n<p>Label your logs so you know what you are looking at:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">console.log({ cart, index });     // shows names and values\nconsole.table(cart);              // arrays of objects as a table\nconsole.trace('how did we get here?');</code></pre></div>\n<p>Better still, <strong>pause the code</strong>. Add <code>debugger;</code> on a line, open DevTools, and reload. Execution stops there and you can hover over any variable, step line by line (<kbd>F10</kbd> step over, <kbd>F11</kbd> step into) and watch values change. You can also click a line number in the DevTools <strong>Sources</strong> panel to set a breakpoint without editing code.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"step-3-check-the-network-tab\">Step 3: check the Network tab</h2>\n<p>If data is not appearing, open DevTools, <strong>Network</strong>, and reload. Click the request and check:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Status</strong>: 200? 404? 500?</li><li><strong>Response</strong>: what did the server actually send?</li><li><strong>Payload</strong>: what did you actually send?</li></ul>\n<p>Half of &quot;my code is broken&quot; bugs are really &quot;the request failed&quot; bugs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"step-4-the-scientific-method\">Step 4: the scientific method</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Reproduce</strong> the bug reliably. If you cannot make it happen on demand, you cannot know you fixed it.</li><li><strong>Form a hypothesis</strong>: &quot;I think <code>items</code> is empty when the page first loads.&quot;</li><li><strong>Test it</strong> with a log or breakpoint.</li><li><strong>Narrow down</strong>: comment out half the code. Still broken? The bug is in the other half. Repeat.</li><li><strong>Fix one thing at a time</strong>, then retest.</li><li><strong>Prevent it</strong>: add a check, a test, or a clearer error message.</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Check the boring stuff first</p><p>Did you save the file? Is the server restarted? Is the script tag pointing at the right file? Are you editing the same folder you are running? Is it cached (try <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd>)? A surprising number of bugs are one of these.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"make-a-minimal-reproduction\">Make a minimal reproduction</h2>\n<p>When asking for help, strip the problem down to the smallest code that still shows the bug. Often you will find the bug while doing this. If not, people can now actually help you.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-try\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Practise</p><p>Open the <a href=\"#playground\">Playground</a>, load the <strong>To-do list</strong> template, and break it: rename <code>render</code> to <code>rendr</code> in one place only. Read the error in the console, find the line, fix it. Then try a missing bracket.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"&quot;TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'price')&quot;. What is undefined?","options":["The price property","The object you wrote before .price","The whole file"],"answer":1,"explain":"You tried to read .price from something that does not exist. Find out why that object is undefined."},{"q":"Your product list is empty but there are no errors in the Console. Where should you look next?","options":["The Elements tab","The Network tab, to see if the data request failed or returned something unexpected","Your CSS file"],"answer":1,"explain":"Silent data bugs are usually failed or unexpected network responses."},{"q":"What is the first step of debugging methodically?","options":["Reproduce the bug reliably","Rewrite the function from scratch","Add try/catch everywhere"],"answer":0,"explain":"If you cannot trigger the bug on demand, you cannot confirm the fix."},{"q":"What does the <code>debugger;</code> statement do with DevTools open?","options":["Deletes the bug automatically","Pauses execution on that line so you can inspect variables and step through","Prints every variable to the console"],"answer":1,"explain":"It acts as a breakpoint written in code."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Read the error, fix the typo","file":"greeting.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Press <strong>Run it</strong> first. This code throws <code>ReferenceError: greting is not defined</code>. Read the error, find the misspelt name, and fix it so it prints the greeting.</p>","starter":"const greeting = 'Hello';\nconsole.log(greting);","html":"","solution":"const greeting = 'Hello';\nconsole.log(greeting);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+greeting\\s*=\\s*['\"]Hello['\"]","text":"Keep line 1 as it is: <code>const greeting = 'Hello';</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*greeting\\s*\\)","text":"The error names the problem: <code>greting</code> is missing an &quot;e&quot;. It must match the variable: <code>greeting</code>."}],"explain":"<p>A <code>ReferenceError</code> means you used a name the program has never seen. Nine times out of ten it is a typo. The error message told you exactly which name, which is why you always read it first.</p>"},{"title":"Fix the off-by-one bug","file":"total.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>This code should print a total of <strong>70</strong> but prints <code>NaN</code> (&quot;Not a Number&quot;). Press <strong>Run it</strong>, then fix the bug. (Ask yourself: how many items are in <code>prices</code>, and what is <code>prices[3]</code>?)</p>\n<p>When it works, change the last line to the labelled log <code>console.log({ total });</code>.</p>","starter":"const prices = [20, 35, 15];\nlet total = 0;\nfor (let i = 0; i <= prices.length; i++) {\n  total = total + prices[i];\n}\nconsole.log(total);","html":"","solution":"const prices = [20, 35, 15];\nlet total = 0;\nfor (let i = 0; i < prices.length; i++) {\n  total = total + prices[i];\n}\nconsole.log({ total });","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"i\\s*<\\s*prices\\.length","text":"The loop runs once too often. With <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>i</code> reaches 3, but the last item is <code>prices[2]</code>. Use <code>&lt;</code> instead of <code>&lt;=</code>."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*\\{\\s*total\\s*\\}\\s*\\)","text":"Change the last line to <code>console.log({ total });</code> so the output shows the variable's name as well as its value."}],"explain":"<p>An array of 3 items has positions 0, 1 and 2. With <code>&lt;=</code> the loop also read <code>prices[3]</code>, which is <code>undefined</code>, and <code>70 + undefined</code> is <code>NaN</code>. This &quot;off by one&quot; mistake is one of the most common bugs in all of programming.</p>"}],"text":"Bugs are normal Professional developers spend a large share of their time debugging. The difference is that they have a method , so bugs feel like puzzles rather than emergencies. Step 1: read the whole error Uncaught TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'price') at getTotal (cart.js:14:22) at renderCart (cart.js:31:17) at HTMLButtonElement. anonymous (main.js:8:5) Read it top to bottom: Type : TypeError , something was the wrong type. Message : you tried to read .price from something that is undefined . Location : cart.js , line 14, column 22, inside getTotal . Stack trace : how we got there. main.js line 8 called renderCart , which called getTotal . So on line 14 of cart.js, the thing before .price is undefined. Maybe an item is missing, or an array index is out of range. The errors you will meet most Error Usually means SyntaxError A typo in the code: missing bracket, quote or comma ReferenceError: x is not defined Misspelt name, or used before it was declared TypeError: x is not a function Calling something that is not a function (often a typo or wrong import) TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined The object you expected is not there Failed to fetch / CORS error Network request blocked or server unreachable 404 in the Network tab Wrong file path or URL Step 2: look inside with console and breakpoints Label your logs so you know what you are looking at: console.log({ cart, index }); // shows names and values console.table(cart); // arrays of objects as a table console.trace('how did we get here?'); Better still, pause the code . Add debugger; on a line, open DevTools, and reload. Execution stops there and you can hover over any variable, step line by line ( F10 step over, F11 step into) and watch values change. You can also click a line number in the DevTools Sources panel to set a breakpoint without editing code. Step 3: check the Network tab If data is not appearing, open DevTools, Network , and reload. Click the request and check: Status : 200? 404? 500? Response : what did the server actually send? Payload : what did you actually send? Half of my code is broken bugs are really the request failed bugs. Step 4: the scientific method Reproduce the bug reliably. If you cannot make it happen on demand, you cannot know you fixed it. Form a hypothesis : I think items is empty when the page first loads. Test it with a log or breakpoint. Narrow down : comment out half the code. Still broken? The bug is in the other half. Repeat. Fix one thing at a time , then retest. Prevent it : add a check, a test, or a clearer error message. Check the boring stuff first Did you save the file? Is the server restarted? Is the script tag pointing at the right file? Are you editing the same folder you are running? Is it cached (try Ctrl + Shift + R )? A surprising number of bugs are one of these. Make a minimal reproduction When asking for help, strip the problem down to the smallest code that still shows the bug. Often you will find the bug while doing this. If not, people can now actually help you. Practise Open the Playground , load the To-do list template, and break it: rename render to rendr in one place only. Read the error in the console, find the line, fix it. Then try a missing bracket.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-pro-workflow","title":"The professional workflow","level":"advanced","minutes":25,"summary":"Pull requests, code review, conventional commits, CI with GitHub Actions, secrets management and documentation.","html":"<h2 id=\"how-teams-ship-code\">How teams ship code</h2>\n<p>On a professional team, nobody pushes straight to <code>main</code>. The flow looks like this:</p>\n<ol><li>Pull the latest <code>main</code> and create a branch: <code>git switch -c feat/checkout-validation</code>.</li><li>Make small commits as you go.</li><li>Push the branch and open a <strong>pull request</strong> (PR) describing what changed and why.</li><li><strong>Automated checks</strong> (lint, type-check, tests, build) run on the PR.</li><li>A teammate <strong>reviews</strong> the code and comments.</li><li>You address feedback, checks go green, the PR is merged.</li><li>Merging to <code>main</code> triggers a <strong>deployment</strong>.</li></ol>\n<p>Even working alone, using PRs on your own projects builds the habit and gives you a clean history.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">git push -u origin feat/checkout-validation\ngh pr create --fill\ngh pr checks          # watch the automated checks\ngh pr merge --squash</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"conventional-commits\">Conventional commits</h2>\n<p>Many teams prefix commit messages with a type. It makes history scannable and lets tools generate changelogs:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">feat: add discount code field to checkout\nfix: round VAT to the nearest penny\ndocs: explain local setup in README\nrefactor: extract price formatting helper\ntest: cover empty cart edge case\nchore: bump vite to 6.0.5</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"code-review-what-reviewers-look-for\">Code review: what reviewers look for</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Correctness</strong>: does it do what it claims? What about empty, huge or invalid inputs?</li><li><strong>Security</strong>: is input validated? Are secrets kept out? Could a user see someone else's data?</li><li><strong>Readability</strong>: would a new teammate understand it? Are names clear?</li><li><strong>Tests</strong>: is the new behaviour covered?</li><li><strong>Scope</strong>: is the PR focused on one thing? Small PRs get better reviews.</li></ul>\n<p>Review your own diff first (<code>git diff main</code>). You will catch half the issues yourself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"continuous-integration-with-github-actions\">Continuous integration with GitHub Actions</h2>\n<p>CI runs your checks on every push, so broken code never reaches <code>main</code>. Save this as <code>.github/workflows/ci.yml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\"><pre><code class=\"language-yaml\">name: CI\non:\n  pull_request:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n\njobs:\n  check:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with:\n          node-version: lts/*\n          cache: npm\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm run lint\n      - run: npm test\n      - run: npm run build</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"environment-variables-and-secrets\">Environment variables and secrets</h2>\n<p>Configuration that changes between environments (local, staging, production), and anything secret, lives outside the code:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\"># .env  (listed in .gitignore, never committed)\nDATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/shop\nSTRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li>Commit a <code>.env.example</code> with the variable <strong>names</strong> and fake values, so others know what to set.</li><li>In production, set real values in your host's dashboard (Vercel, Netlify, Railway) or a secrets manager.</li><li>In GitHub Actions, use <strong>repository secrets</strong> (<code>${{ secrets.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY }}</code>).</li><li>For smart contracts, never put a private key with real funds in a <code>.env</code> file. Use Foundry's encrypted keystore (<code>cast wallet import</code>) or a hardware wallet.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"documentation-that-pays-off\">Documentation that pays off</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>README.md</strong>: what the project is, how to run it, how to test it, how to deploy it.</li><li><strong>Decision records</strong>: a short note whenever you make a significant choice (&quot;We chose Postgres over MongoDB because...&quot;). Six months later this is gold.</li><li><strong>Comments</strong> explain <em>why</em>, not <em>what</em>. The code already says what.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"guard-rails-that-catch-mistakes-early\">Guard rails that catch mistakes early</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Prettier</strong> and <strong>ESLint</strong> in the project, run in CI.</li><li><strong>TypeScript</strong> in strict mode.</li><li><strong>Pre-commit hooks</strong> (e.g. with <code>husky</code> and <code>lint-staged</code>) that format and lint staged files before every commit.</li><li><strong>Branch protection</strong> on GitHub: require passing checks and a review before merging to <code>main</code>.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Working with AI tools professionally</p><p>AI coding assistants are now part of most teams' workflow. Treat their output exactly like a teammate's PR: read every line, run the tests, check for security issues and invented APIs. You are responsible for every line you merge, whoever or whatever typed it.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Why do teams use pull requests instead of pushing straight to main?","options":["GitHub charges for direct pushes","So changes are reviewed and automatically checked before they reach the shared, deployable branch","Pull requests make the code smaller"],"answer":1,"explain":"PRs combine human review and automated checks as a gate in front of main."},{"q":"Which commit message follows conventional commits?","options":["&quot;stuff&quot;","&quot;Fixed it!!&quot;","&quot;fix: prevent negative quantities in cart&quot;"],"answer":2,"explain":"type: short imperative description."},{"q":"What belongs in .env.example?","options":["Your real API keys, so teammates can use them","The variable names with placeholder values","Nothing: it should stay empty"],"answer":1,"explain":"It documents what configuration is needed without exposing secrets."},{"q":"What is the main job of CI?","options":["Automatically run lint, tests and build on every push or PR so broken code is caught before merging","Write code for you","Back up your laptop"],"answer":0,"explain":"Continuous integration checks every change the same way, every time."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Ship a fix the professional way","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Type the four commands to: create a branch <code>fix/vat-rounding</code>, commit with the conventional message <code>fix: round VAT to the nearest penny</code>, push the branch to <code>origin</code> (setting it as upstream), and open a pull request that fills in its title and body from your commits.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"git switch -c fix/vat-rounding\ngit commit -m \"fix: round VAT to the nearest penny\"\ngit push -u origin fix/vat-rounding\ngh pr create --fill","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+switch\\s+-c\\s+fix/vat-rounding\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>git switch -c fix/vat-rounding</code>"},{"re":"git\\s+commit\\s+-m\\s+[\"']fix: round VAT to the nearest penny[\"']","text":"Line 2: the message starts with the type <code>fix:</code> then a space."},{"re":"^\\s*git\\s+push\\s+-u\\s+origin\\s+fix/vat-rounding\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>git push -u origin fix/vat-rounding</code> (<code>-u</code> links the local branch to the remote one)."},{"re":"^\\s*gh\\s+pr\\s+create\\s+--fill\\s*$","text":"Line 4: <code>gh pr create --fill</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Write a .env.example","file":".env.example","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a <code>.env.example</code> with two variables, using placeholder values only:</p>\n<ul><li><code>DATABASE_URL</code> set to <code>postgres://localhost:5432/shop</code></li><li><code>STRIPE_SECRET_KEY</code> set to <code>sk_test_replace_me</code></li></ul>\n<p>No spaces around the <code>=</code> sign.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/shop\nSTRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_replace_me","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/shop\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/shop</code>"},{"re":"^STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_replace_me\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_replace_me</code>"}],"explain":"<p>This file is committed so teammates know which variables to set. The real <code>.env</code>, with real secrets, stays in <code>.gitignore</code>.</p>"}],"text":"How teams ship code On a professional team, nobody pushes straight to main . The flow looks like this: Pull the latest main and create a branch: git switch -c feat/checkout-validation . Make small commits as you go. Push the branch and open a pull request (PR) describing what changed and why. Automated checks (lint, type-check, tests, build) run on the PR. A teammate reviews the code and comments. You address feedback, checks go green, the PR is merged. Merging to main triggers a deployment . Even working alone, using PRs on your own projects builds the habit and gives you a clean history. git push -u origin feat/checkout-validation gh pr create --fill gh pr checks # watch the automated checks gh pr merge --squash Conventional commits Many teams prefix commit messages with a type. It makes history scannable and lets tools generate changelogs: feat: add discount code field to checkout fix: round VAT to the nearest penny docs: explain local setup in README refactor: extract price formatting helper test: cover empty cart edge case chore: bump vite to 6.0.5 Code review: what reviewers look for Correctness : does it do what it claims? What about empty, huge or invalid inputs? Security : is input validated? Are secrets kept out? Could a user see someone else's data? Readability : would a new teammate understand it? Are names clear? Tests : is the new behaviour covered? Scope : is the PR focused on one thing? Small PRs get better reviews. Review your own diff first ( git diff main ). You will catch half the issues yourself. Continuous integration with GitHub Actions CI runs your checks on every push, so broken code never reaches main . Save this as .github/workflows/ci.yml : name: CI on: pull_request: push: branches: [main] jobs: check: runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - uses: actions/setup-node@v4 with: node-version: lts/* cache: npm - run: npm ci - run: npm run lint - run: npm test - run: npm run build Environment variables and secrets Configuration that changes between environments (local, staging, production), and anything secret, lives outside the code: # .env (listed in .gitignore, never committed) DATABASE_URL=postgres://localhost:5432/shop STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_... Commit a .env.example with the variable names and fake values, so others know what to set. In production, set real values in your host's dashboard (Vercel, Netlify, Railway) or a secrets manager. In GitHub Actions, use repository secrets ( ${{ secrets.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY }} ). For smart contracts, never put a private key with real funds in a .env file. Use Foundry's encrypted keystore ( cast wallet import ) or a hardware wallet. Documentation that pays off README.md : what the project is, how to run it, how to test it, how to deploy it. Decision records : a short note whenever you make a significant choice ( We chose Postgres over MongoDB because... ). Six months later this is gold. Comments explain why , not what . The code already says what. Guard rails that catch mistakes early Prettier and ESLint in the project, run in CI. TypeScript in strict mode. Pre-commit hooks (e.g. with husky and lint-staged ) that format and lint staged files before every commit. Branch protection on GitHub: require passing checks and a review before merging to main . Working with AI tools professionally AI coding assistants are now part of most teams' workflow. Treat their output exactly like a teammate's PR: read every line, run the tests, check for security issues and invented APIs. You are responsible for every line you merge, whoever or whatever typed it.","track":"setup"},{"id":"setup-bits-bytes","title":"\"Bits, bytes and integer types: what uint256 really means\"","level":"advanced","minutes":40,"summary":"Binary and hex, bytes and encodings, fixed-size integers, overflow, BigInt and bitwise operators. The ground floor under hashing, smart contracts and token maths.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-this-matters\">Why this matters</h2>\n<p>Web developers can go years without thinking about bits. Blockchain developers cannot. Hashes are 256 bits, addresses are 20 bytes, token balances are <code>uint256</code>, and one wrong assumption about integer size or precision has drained real money from real contracts. This lesson is the foundation for all of it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"binary-counting-with-two-digits\">Binary: counting with two digits</h2>\n<p>Computers store everything as <strong>bits</strong>: 0 or 1. In binary, each position is worth double the one to its right:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Position value</th><th>128</th><th>64</th><th>32</th><th>16</th><th>8</th><th>4</th><th>2</th><th>1</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>13</code> in binary</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>8 + 4 + 1 = 13, so 13 is <code>00001101</code>. Eight bits are a <strong>byte</strong>, which holds 2^8 = <strong>256</strong> values: 0 to 255.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hexadecimal-the-developer-s-shorthand\">Hexadecimal: the developer's shorthand</h2>\n<p>Binary is long, so developers write bytes in <strong>hexadecimal</strong> (base 16), using digits 0 to 9 and letters a to f (for 10 to 15). One hex digit is exactly 4 bits, so one byte is always exactly <strong>two</strong> hex digits: <code>00</code> to <code>ff</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Decimal</th><th>Binary</th><th>Hex</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td><code>00000000</code></td><td><code>00</code></td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><code>00001010</code></td><td><code>0a</code></td></tr><tr><td>255</td><td><code>11111111</code></td><td><code>ff</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Hex is usually written with a <code>0x</code> prefix: <code>0xff</code>. Ethereum addresses (<code>0x71C7...</code>) and hashes are hex for this reason. A 256-bit hash is 64 hex characters; a 20-byte address is 40.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"text-is-bytes-too\">Text is bytes too</h2>\n<p>Strings are stored as bytes using an <strong>encoding</strong>. The web's standard is <strong>UTF-8</strong>: ASCII characters take one byte each; accented letters, other scripts and emoji take two to four.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">new TextEncoder().encode('Hi');   // Uint8Array [72, 105]\nnew TextEncoder().encode('£');    // Uint8Array [194, 163]  (two bytes)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Hash functions and signatures work on <strong>bytes</strong>, not text, which is why crypto code always encodes strings first.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"fixed-size-integers\">Fixed-size integers</h2>\n<p>Most languages store integers in a <strong>fixed number of bits</strong>. That sets a minimum and maximum:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Bits</th><th>Range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>uint8</code> (unsigned)</td><td>8</td><td>0 to 255</td></tr><tr><td><code>int8</code> (signed)</td><td>8</td><td>-128 to 127</td></tr><tr><td><code>int32</code></td><td>32</td><td>about -2.1 billion to 2.1 billion</td></tr><tr><td><code>uint64</code></td><td>64</td><td>0 to about 1.8 x 10^19</td></tr><tr><td><code>uint256</code></td><td>256</td><td>0 to about 1.16 x 10^77</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Unsigned</strong> (<code>uint</code>) types hold only zero and positives.</li><li><strong>Signed</strong> (<code>int</code>) types use one bit's worth of range for negatives (stored using a scheme called <strong>two's complement</strong>).</li><li>Solidity's default <code>uint</code> is <code>uint256</code>: big enough for any realistic token balance with 18 decimals.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"overflow-when-numbers-wrap-around\">Overflow: when numbers wrap around</h2>\n<p>What is 255 + 1 in a <code>uint8</code>? There is no room for 256, so in many languages it silently <strong>wraps</strong> to 0, like a car odometer rolling over. Subtracting 1 from 0 in a <code>uint</code> wraps to the maximum value.</p>\n<p>In older Solidity (before 0.8), this was a real attack: in 2018 an overflow bug in a token's batch-transfer function (the &quot;BatchOverflow&quot; bug) let attackers create astronomically large balances from nothing. Since Solidity 0.8, arithmetic <strong>reverts on overflow</strong> by default. Code inside an <code>unchecked { }</code> block skips that check to save gas, so it must be proven safe.</p>\n<h2 id=\"javascript-numbers-and-bigint\">JavaScript numbers and BigInt</h2>\n<p>JavaScript's <code>number</code> is a 64-bit float. It stores whole numbers exactly only up to <code>Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER</code>, which is 2^53 - 1 = 9,007,199,254,740,991. Beyond that, it silently loses precision.</p>\n<p>One ether is 10^18 wei, far beyond that limit for real balances. So blockchain JavaScript uses <strong>BigInt</strong>: integers of any size, written with an <code>n</code> suffix: <code>10n ** 18n</code>. You cannot mix BigInt and normal numbers in one calculation without converting.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"3\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"bitwise-operators\">Bitwise operators</h2>\n<p>Bitwise operators work on the individual bits of integers:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Name</th><th>Example</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>&amp;</code></td><td>AND (both bits 1)</td><td><code>0b1100 &amp; 0b1010</code></td><td><code>0b1000</code> (8)</td></tr><tr><td><code>|</code></td><td>OR (either bit 1)</td><td><code>0b1100 | 0b1010</code></td><td><code>0b1110</code> (14)</td></tr><tr><td><code>^</code></td><td>XOR (bits differ)</td><td><code>0b1100 ^ 0b1010</code></td><td><code>0b0110</code> (6)</td></tr><tr><td><code>~</code></td><td>NOT (flip bits)</td><td><code>~0</code></td><td><code>-1</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;&lt;</code></td><td>shift left</td><td><code>1 &lt;&lt; 3</code></td><td><code>8</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code></td><td>unsigned shift right</td><td><code>16 &gt;&gt;&gt; 2</code></td><td><code>4</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Uses you will actually meet: packing several true/false <strong>flags</strong> into one number (permissions, settings), hash function internals (SHA-256 is mostly rotations, XORs and ANDs; read <code>sha256</code> in this site's <code>assets/tools.js</code>), and gas-saving tricks in Solidity.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"4\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-same-types-in-solidity\">The same types in Solidity</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">uint8 small = 255;            // 0 to 255\nuint256 big = 1e18;           // one token with 18 decimals\nint256 temperature = -5;      // signed: negatives allowed\naddress owner;                // 20 bytes\nbytes32 id = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(&quot;ladder&quot;));   // 32 bytes, e.g. a hash\nbool isLive = true;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"5\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"fixed-point-maths-decimals-without-floats\">Fixed-point maths: decimals without floats</h2>\n<p>Tokens use integers plus a <strong>decimals</strong> setting (usually 18), exactly like pence with 2 decimals. Rules that prevent real bugs:</p>\n<ul><li>Multiply before you divide: <code>amount * feeBps / 10_000</code>, not <code>amount / 10_000 * feeBps</code>.</li><li>Decide which way rounding should go, and make it favour the protocol.</li><li>Tokens have different decimals (USDC uses 6). Always scale before combining amounts.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"How many values can one byte hold?","options":["8","255","256"],"answer":2,"explain":"2^8 = 256 values, from 0 to 255."},{"q":"How many hex characters represent a 32-byte hash?","options":["32","64","256"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each byte is two hex digits: 32 x 2 = 64."},{"q":"In Solidity 0.8+, what happens if a uint256 subtraction would go below zero?","options":["It wraps to the maximum value","The transaction reverts, unless the code is inside an unchecked block","It becomes negative"],"answer":1,"explain":"Checked arithmetic has been the default since 0.8."},{"q":"Why do Ethereum JavaScript libraries return balances as bigint?","options":["Wei amounts exceed the largest integer a normal JavaScript number stores exactly","bigint is faster","JSON requires it"],"answer":0,"explain":"Number is exact only up to 2^53 - 1; 18-decimal balances go far beyond that."},{"q":"What is 6 &amp; 3 (bitwise AND)?","options":["9","7","2"],"answer":2,"explain":"6 is 110 and 3 is 011. Only the middle bit is 1 in both: 010 = 2."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Convert between bases","file":"bases.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write three lines that convert the number 255:</p>\n<ol><li>Print it in binary: <code>console.log((255).toString(2));</code></li><li>Print it in hex: <code>console.log((255).toString(16));</code></li><li>Print the hex string <code>'ff'</code> back as a number: <code>console.log(parseInt('ff', 16));</code></li></ol>\n<p>(The extra brackets around <code>255</code> stop JavaScript reading the dot as a decimal point.)</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"console.log((255).toString(2));\nconsole.log((255).toString(16));\nconsole.log(parseInt('ff', 16));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"\\(\\s*255\\s*\\)\\.toString\\(\\s*2\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>(255).toString(2)</code> converts to base 2. Wrap it in <code>console.log(...)</code>."},{"re":"\\(\\s*255\\s*\\)\\.toString\\(\\s*16\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: the same, with base <code>16</code>."},{"re":"parseInt\\(\\s*['\"]ff['\"]\\s*,\\s*16\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>parseInt('ff', 16)</code>. The second argument tells it the text is base 16."}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>11111111</code>, <code>ff</code>, <code>255</code>. The same number, three spellings. <code>toString(base)</code> and <code>parseInt(text, base)</code> are your tools for moving between them.</p>"},{"title":"See the bytes inside a string","file":"bytes.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write two lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>bytes</code> equal to <code>new TextEncoder().encode('Hi')</code>.</li><li>Print it as a plain array: <code>console.log(Array.from(bytes));</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode('Hi');\nconsole.log(Array.from(bytes));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+bytes\\s*=\\s*new\\s+TextEncoder\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const bytes = new TextEncoder()</code>. <code>new</code> creates an encoder object."},{"re":"\\.encode\\(\\s*['\"]Hi['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Then call <code>.encode('Hi')</code> on it, all on the same line."},{"re":"Array\\.from\\(\\s*bytes\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(Array.from(bytes));</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>[72, 105]</code>. 'H' is 72 and 'i' is 105, the same character codes you met in Coding basics 1, now as raw bytes.</p>"},{"title":"Find the edge of safe integers","file":"safe.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li>Print <code>Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER</code>.</li><li>Print <code>2 ** 53 + 1</code> (watch for the wrong answer).</li><li>Print <code>2n ** 53n + 1n</code> (the BigInt version).</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"console.log(Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER);\nconsole.log(2 ** 53 + 1);\nconsole.log(2n ** 53n + 1n);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"Number\\.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER","text":"Line 1: <code>console.log(Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER);</code> all capitals after the dot, with underscores."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*2\\s*\\*\\*\\s*53\\s*\\+\\s*1\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(2 ** 53 + 1);</code> (<code>**</code> means &quot;to the power of&quot;)."},{"re":"2n\\s*\\*\\*\\s*53n\\s*\\+\\s*1n","text":"Line 3: every number gets an <code>n</code>: <code>2n ** 53n + 1n</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Line 2 prints <code>9007199254740992</code>, which is wrong: the true answer ends in 3. The float cannot represent it, so it rounds silently. The BigInt line prints the correct <code>9007199254740993</code>. This is exactly why viem returns balances as <code>bigint</code>.</p>"},{"title":"Work in wei, like a wallet does","file":"wei.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Represent 2.5 ether in wei using only integers:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const oneEther = 10n ** 18n;</code></li><li><code>const amount = 25n * oneEther / 10n;</code> (2.5 = 25 / 10, multiplying <strong>before</strong> dividing)</li><li><code>console.log(amount.toString());</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const oneEther = 10n ** 18n;\nconst amount = 25n * oneEther / 10n;\nconsole.log(amount.toString());","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+oneEther\\s*=\\s*10n\\s*\\*\\*\\s*18n","text":"Line 1: <code>const oneEther = 10n ** 18n;</code> (10 to the power 18, as BigInts)."},{"re":"const\\s+amount\\s*=\\s*25n\\s*\\*\\s*oneEther\\s*\\/\\s*10n","text":"Line 2: <code>const amount = 25n * oneEther / 10n;</code> Multiply first, then divide."},{"re":"amount\\.toString\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: print <code>amount.toString()</code>."}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>2500000000000000000</code>. There is no decimal point anywhere: 2.5 ether is simply 2.5 x 10^18 wei. Multiplying before dividing matters with integers: <code>25n / 10n</code> alone would round down to <code>2n</code> and lose the half.</p>"},{"title":"Permission flags","file":"flags.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Store two permissions as bits in one number, then test one:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const CAN_READ = 1;</code></li><li><code>const CAN_WRITE = 2;</code></li><li><code>const perms = CAN_READ | CAN_WRITE;</code> (combine the flags with OR)</li><li><code>console.log(perms &amp; CAN_WRITE ? 'can write' : 'read only');</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const CAN_READ = 1;\nconst CAN_WRITE = 2;\nconst perms = CAN_READ | CAN_WRITE;\nconsole.log(perms & CAN_WRITE ? 'can write' : 'read only');","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+CAN_READ\\s*=\\s*1","text":"Line 1: <code>const CAN_READ = 1;</code> (binary 01)."},{"re":"const\\s+CAN_WRITE\\s*=\\s*2","text":"Line 2: <code>const CAN_WRITE = 2;</code> (binary 10)."},{"re":"const\\s+perms\\s*=\\s*CAN_READ\\s*\\|\\s*CAN_WRITE","text":"Line 3 combines them with a single bar: <code>CAN_READ | CAN_WRITE</code> (binary 11 = 3)."},{"re":"perms\\s*&\\s*CAN_WRITE\\s*\\?","text":"Line 4 tests the bit with a single <code>&amp;</code>, then a <code>?</code> for the two possible answers."}],"explain":"<p><code>1 | 2</code> is binary <code>11</code>, which is 3. <code>3 &amp; 2</code> keeps only the write bit, giving 2, which counts as true, so it prints <strong>can write</strong>. Single <code>&amp;</code> and <code>|</code> are bitwise; double <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code> are logical. Mixing them up is a classic bug.</p>"},{"title":"Declare Solidity types","file":"Types.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write these three state-variable declarations, one per line (Solidity needs the semicolons):</p>\n<ol><li>A <code>uint8</code> called <code>small</code> set to <code>255</code>.</li><li>A <code>uint256</code> called <code>big</code> set to <code>1e18</code>.</li><li>An <code>int256</code> called <code>temperature</code> set to <code>-5</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"uint8 small = 255;\nuint256 big = 1e18;\nint256 temperature = -5;","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*uint8\\s+small\\s*=\\s*255\\s*;","text":"Line 1: type first, then name: <code>uint8 small = 255;</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*uint256\\s+big\\s*=\\s*1e18\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>uint256 big = 1e18;</code> (1e18 means 1 followed by 18 zeros)."},{"re":"^\\s*int256\\s+temperature\\s*=\\s*-5\\s*;","text":"Line 3: signed type <code>int256</code> (no u), because the value is negative: <code>int256 temperature = -5;</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Solidity puts the type before the name and requires a semicolon after every statement. Trying <code>uint8 small = 256;</code> would fail to compile, because 256 does not fit in 8 bits.</p>"}],"text":"Why this matters Web developers can go years without thinking about bits. Blockchain developers cannot. Hashes are 256 bits, addresses are 20 bytes, token balances are uint256 , and one wrong assumption about integer size or precision has drained real money from real contracts. This lesson is the foundation for all of it. Binary: counting with two digits Computers store everything as bits : 0 or 1. In binary, each position is worth double the one to its right: Position value 128 64 32 16 8 4 2 1 13 in binary 0 0 0 0 1 1 0 1 8 + 4 + 1 = 13, so 13 is 00001101 . Eight bits are a byte , which holds 2^8 = 256 values: 0 to 255. Hexadecimal: the developer's shorthand Binary is long, so developers write bytes in hexadecimal (base 16), using digits 0 to 9 and letters a to f (for 10 to 15). One hex digit is exactly 4 bits, so one byte is always exactly two hex digits: 00 to ff . Decimal Binary Hex 0 00000000 00 10 00001010 0a 255 11111111 ff Hex is usually written with a 0x prefix: 0xff . Ethereum addresses ( 0x71C7... ) and hashes are hex for this reason. A 256-bit hash is 64 hex characters; a 20-byte address is 40. Text is bytes too Strings are stored as bytes using an encoding . The web's standard is UTF-8 : ASCII characters take one byte each; accented letters, other scripts and emoji take two to four. new TextEncoder().encode('Hi'); // Uint8Array [72, 105] new TextEncoder().encode('£'); // Uint8Array [194, 163] (two bytes) Hash functions and signatures work on bytes , not text, which is why crypto code always encodes strings first. Fixed-size integers Most languages store integers in a fixed number of bits . That sets a minimum and maximum: Type Bits Range uint8 (unsigned) 8 0 to 255 int8 (signed) 8 -128 to 127 int32 32 about -2.1 billion to 2.1 billion uint64 64 0 to about 1.8 x 10^19 uint256 256 0 to about 1.16 x 10^77 Unsigned ( uint ) types hold only zero and positives. Signed ( int ) types use one bit's worth of range for negatives (stored using a scheme called two's complement ). Solidity's default uint is uint256 : big enough for any realistic token balance with 18 decimals. Overflow: when numbers wrap around What is 255 + 1 in a uint8 ? There is no room for 256, so in many languages it silently wraps to 0, like a car odometer rolling over. Subtracting 1 from 0 in a uint wraps to the maximum value. In older Solidity (before 0.8), this was a real attack: in 2018 an overflow bug in a token's batch-transfer function (the BatchOverflow bug) let attackers create astronomically large balances from nothing. Since Solidity 0.8, arithmetic reverts on overflow by default. Code inside an unchecked { } block skips that check to save gas, so it must be proven safe. JavaScript numbers and BigInt JavaScript's number is a 64-bit float. It stores whole numbers exactly only up to Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER , which is 2^53 - 1 = 9,007,199,254,740,991. Beyond that, it silently loses precision. One ether is 10^18 wei, far beyond that limit for real balances. So blockchain JavaScript uses BigInt : integers of any size, written with an n suffix: 10n ** 18n . You cannot mix BigInt and normal numbers in one calculation without converting. Bitwise operators Bitwise operators work on the individual bits of integers: Operator Name Example Result AND (both bits 1) 0b1100 0b1010 0b1000 (8) | OR (either bit 1) 0b1100 | 0b1010 0b1110 (14) ^ XOR (bits differ) 0b1100 ^ 0b1010 0b0110 (6) ~ NOT (flip bits) ~0 -1 shift left 1 3 8 unsigned shift right 16 2 4 Uses you will actually meet: packing several true/false flags into one number (permissions, settings), hash function internals (SHA-256 is mostly rotations, XORs and ANDs; read sha256 in this site's assets/tools.js ), and gas-saving tricks in Solidity. The same types in Solidity uint8 small = 255; // 0 to 255 uint256 big = 1e18; // one token with 18 decimals int256 temperature = -5; // signed: negatives allowed address owner; // 20 bytes bytes32 id = keccak256(abi.encodePacked( ladder )); // 32 bytes, e.g. a ha","track":"setup"},{"id":"web-html","title":"\"HTML: the structure of every page\"","level":"beginner","minutes":20,"summary":"Elements, attributes, semantic tags, links, images and forms. Write a real page from scratch.","html":"<h2 id=\"elements-and-tags\">Elements and tags</h2>\n<p>HTML describes <strong>what things are</strong>: this is a heading, this is a paragraph, this is a button. You wrap content in <strong>tags</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>&lt;p&gt;</code> opens, <code>&lt;/p&gt;</code> closes, and the whole thing is an <strong>element</strong>. Some elements have no content and no closing tag, like <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;br&gt;</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Attributes</strong> add information to an element:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com&quot; target=&quot;_blank&quot;&gt;My GitHub&lt;/a&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;dress.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Green linen wrap dress on a model&quot;&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-page-skeleton\">The page skeleton</h2>\n<p>Every page starts the same way. In VS Code, type <code>!</code> and press <kbd>Tab</kbd> to generate it.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;!doctype html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=&quot;utf-8&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Amara Studio | Handmade jewellery&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=&quot;description&quot; content=&quot;Handmade brass jewellery from London.&quot;&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;header&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Amara Studio&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;nav&gt;\n      &lt;a href=&quot;#shop&quot;&gt;Shop&lt;/a&gt;\n      &lt;a href=&quot;#about&quot;&gt;About&lt;/a&gt;\n    &lt;/nav&gt;\n  &lt;/header&gt;\n\n  &lt;main&gt;\n    &lt;section id=&quot;shop&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;New collection&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Hand-finished brass earrings, made in small batches.&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;ul&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;Sun hoops, £34&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;Tide drops, £28&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;/section&gt;\n\n    &lt;section id=&quot;about&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;About&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Started at a kitchen table in &lt;strong&gt;2021&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/section&gt;\n  &lt;/main&gt;\n\n  &lt;footer&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;&amp;copy; 2026 Amara Studio&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/footer&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>&lt;head&gt;</code> holds information <strong>about</strong> the page: title (shown in the tab and Google results), character set, the viewport setting that makes it work on phones.</li><li><code>&lt;body&gt;</code> holds everything you see.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"semantic-html-use-the-right-element\">Semantic HTML: use the right element</h2>\n<p>Professionals pick elements by <strong>meaning</strong>, not appearance. Screen readers, search engines and your future self all depend on it.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>&lt;header&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;footer&gt;</code></td><td>The top and bottom of a page or section</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;nav&gt;</code></td><td>A group of navigation links</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;main&gt;</code></td><td>The main content (one per page)</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;section&gt;</code></td><td>A themed group of content with a heading</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;article&gt;</code></td><td>Self-contained content: a product card, a blog post</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code></td><td>Headings in order. One <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> per page; do not skip levels</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;button&gt;</code></td><td>Anything clicked to do something</td></tr><tr><td><code>&lt;a&gt;</code></td><td>Anything clicked to go somewhere</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Avoid &quot;div soup&quot;</p><p>A page made of nothing but <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> elements works visually but is meaningless to assistive tech and search engines. Use <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> only when no meaningful element fits, usually for layout wrappers.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"images-done-right\">Images done right</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;img src=&quot;images/sun-hoops.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Brass sun hoop earrings on a linen cloth&quot; width=&quot;800&quot; height=&quot;1000&quot; loading=&quot;lazy&quot;&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>alt</code> describes the image for blind users and when the image fails to load. Describe what matters; use <code>alt=&quot;&quot;</code> for purely decorative images.</li><li><code>width</code> and <code>height</code> stop the page jumping as images load.</li><li><code>loading=&quot;lazy&quot;</code> delays off-screen images, making pages faster.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"forms\">Forms</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;form&gt;\n  &lt;label for=&quot;email&quot;&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input id=&quot;email&quot; name=&quot;email&quot; type=&quot;email&quot; required placeholder=&quot;you@example.com&quot;&gt;\n\n  &lt;label for=&quot;size&quot;&gt;Size&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;select id=&quot;size&quot; name=&quot;size&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;option&gt;S&lt;/option&gt;\n    &lt;option&gt;M&lt;/option&gt;\n    &lt;option&gt;L&lt;/option&gt;\n  &lt;/select&gt;\n\n  &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Join the waitlist&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Every input needs a <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> linked with <code>for</code> and <code>id</code>. Clicking the label focuses the input, and screen readers announce it. Input <code>type</code>s like <code>email</code>, <code>tel</code>, <code>number</code> and <code>date</code> give mobile users the right keyboard and free validation.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which element should wrap a site's main navigation links?","options":["div","nav","section"],"answer":1,"explain":"nav tells browsers, screen readers and search engines that these links are navigation."},{"q":"What is the alt attribute on an image for?","options":["The image's file name","A text description for screen-reader users and when the image fails to load","A tooltip on hover"],"answer":1,"explain":"alt text is essential for accessibility and helps SEO too."},{"q":"&quot;Add to bag&quot; adds an item without leaving the page. Which element should it be?","options":["button","a","div with a click handler"],"answer":0,"explain":"Buttons do things; links go places. A div is not keyboard-accessible by default."},{"q":"Where does the page title shown in the browser tab go?","options":["In an h1 in the body","In a title element inside head","In the footer"],"answer":1,"explain":"The title element lives in head and is also what Google shows in results."}],"exercises":[{"title":"A heading and a paragraph","file":"index.html","lang":"html","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write two elements: a main heading (<code>h1</code>) that says <code>Amara Studio</code>, and under it a paragraph (<code>p</code>) that says <code>Handmade brass jewellery.</code> Press <strong>Run it</strong> to see them rendered.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<h1>Amara Studio</h1>\n<p>Handmade brass jewellery.</p>","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"<h1>","text":"Open the heading with <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code>."},{"re":"<h1>\\s*Amara Studio\\s*</h1>","text":"Close it with <code>&lt;/h1&gt;</code> (note the slash): <code>&lt;h1&gt;Amara Studio&lt;/h1&gt;</code>"},{"re":"<p>\\s*Handmade brass jewellery\\.\\s*</p>","text":"Line 2: <code>&lt;p&gt;Handmade brass jewellery.&lt;/p&gt;</code> including the full stop."}],"explain":"<p>Every element opens with <code>&lt;name&gt;</code> and closes with <code>&lt;/name&gt;</code>. The browser shows <code>h1</code> large and bold because it is the most important heading, not because of how you typed it.</p>"},{"title":"An image with good alt text","file":"index.html","lang":"html","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Add an image of the file <code>hoops.jpg</code>. Give it the alt text <code>Brass sun hoop earrings</code> and lazy loading. Write the attributes in this order: <code>src</code>, <code>alt</code>, <code>loading</code>.</p>\n<p>Press <strong>Run it</strong>: the image file does not exist here, so you will see your alt text instead, exactly as a screen reader user would hear it.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<img src=\"hoops.jpg\" alt=\"Brass sun hoop earrings\" loading=\"lazy\">","also":["<img src=\"hoops.jpg\" alt=\"Brass sun hoop earrings\" loading=\"lazy\" />"],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*<img\\s","text":"Images use the <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> element, which has no closing tag."},{"re":"src=[\"']hoops\\.jpg[\"']","text":"First attribute: <code>src=&quot;hoops.jpg&quot;</code>"},{"re":"alt=[\"']Brass sun hoop earrings[\"']","text":"Second: <code>alt=&quot;Brass sun hoop earrings&quot;</code>"},{"re":"loading=[\"']lazy[\"']","text":"Third: <code>loading=&quot;lazy&quot;</code>, then close the tag with <code>&gt;</code>."}],"explain":""},{"title":"A labelled email field","file":"index.html","lang":"html","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write a label and an email input that are linked together:</p>\n<ol><li>A <code>label</code> with <code>for=&quot;email&quot;</code> and the text <code>Email</code>.</li><li>An <code>input</code> with these attributes in this order: <code>id=&quot;email&quot;</code>, <code>name=&quot;email&quot;</code>, <code>type=&quot;email&quot;</code>, and <code>required</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<label for=\"email\">Email</label>\n<input id=\"email\" name=\"email\" type=\"email\" required>","also":["<label for=\"email\">Email</label>\n<input id=\"email\" name=\"email\" type=\"email\" required />"],"hints":[{"re":"<label\\s+for=[\"']email[\"']\\s*>","text":"Line 1 opens with <code>&lt;label for=&quot;email&quot;&gt;</code>"},{"re":">\\s*Email\\s*</label>","text":"Then the text <code>Email</code> and the closing <code>&lt;/label&gt;</code>."},{"re":"<input\\s+id=[\"']email[\"']","text":"Line 2 starts <code>&lt;input id=&quot;email&quot;</code>. The <code>id</code> must match the label's <code>for</code>."},{"re":"name=[\"']email[\"']\\s+type=[\"']email[\"']","text":"Then <code>name=&quot;email&quot; type=&quot;email&quot;</code>."},{"re":"required\\s*/?>\\s*$","text":"End with the word <code>required</code> and close the tag with <code>&gt;</code>."}],"explain":"<p><code>for=&quot;email&quot;</code> on the label points at <code>id=&quot;email&quot;</code> on the input. Clicking the label now focuses the field, and screen readers announce &quot;Email&quot;. <code>type=&quot;email&quot;</code> gives phone users an @ keyboard and free validation.</p>"}],"text":"Elements and tags HTML describes what things are : this is a heading, this is a paragraph, this is a button. You wrap content in tags : p This is a paragraph. /p p opens, /p closes, and the whole thing is an element . Some elements have no content and no closing tag, like img and br . Attributes add information to an element: a href= https://github.com target= _blank My GitHub /a img src= dress.jpg alt= Green linen wrap dress on a model The page skeleton Every page starts the same way. In VS Code, type ! and press Tab to generate it. !doctype html html lang= en head meta charset= utf-8 meta name= viewport content= width=device-width, initial-scale=1 title Amara Studio | Handmade jewellery /title meta name= description content= Handmade brass jewellery from London. /head body header h1 Amara Studio /h1 nav a href= #shop Shop /a a href= #about About /a /nav /header main section id= shop h2 New collection /h2 p Hand-finished brass earrings, made in small batches. /p ul li Sun hoops, £34 /li li Tide drops, £28 /li /ul /section section id= about h2 About /h2 p Started at a kitchen table in strong 2021 /strong . /p /section /main footer p copy; 2026 Amara Studio /p /footer /body /html head holds information about the page: title (shown in the tab and Google results), character set, the viewport setting that makes it work on phones. body holds everything you see. Semantic HTML: use the right element Professionals pick elements by meaning , not appearance. Screen readers, search engines and your future self all depend on it. Element Use it for header , footer The top and bottom of a page or section nav A group of navigation links main The main content (one per page) section A themed group of content with a heading article Self-contained content: a product card, a blog post h1 to h6 Headings in order. One h1 per page; do not skip levels button Anything clicked to do something a Anything clicked to go somewhere Avoid div soup A page made of nothing but div elements works visually but is meaningless to assistive tech and search engines. Use div only when no meaningful element fits, usually for layout wrappers. Images done right img src= images/sun-hoops.jpg alt= Brass sun hoop earrings on a linen cloth width= 800 height= 1000 loading= lazy alt describes the image for blind users and when the image fails to load. Describe what matters; use alt= for purely decorative images. width and height stop the page jumping as images load. loading= lazy delays off-screen images, making pages faster. Forms form label for= email Email /label input id= email name= email type= email required placeholder= you@example.com label for= size Size /label select id= size name= size option S /option option M /option option L /option /select button type= submit Join the waitlist /button /form Every input needs a label linked with for and id . Clicking the label focuses the input, and screen readers announce it. Input type s like email , tel , number and date give mobile users the right keyboard and free validation.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-css","title":"\"CSS: styling and the box model\"","level":"beginner","minutes":25,"summary":"Selectors, the cascade, colours, fonts, units and the box model that controls every element's size.","html":"<h2 id=\"how-css-works\">How CSS works</h2>\n<p>CSS is a list of <strong>rules</strong>. Each rule picks elements with a <strong>selector</strong> and sets <strong>properties</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">h1 {\n  color: #1f1a17;\n  font-size: 2.5rem;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Link a stylesheet from your HTML <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;link rel=&quot;stylesheet&quot; href=&quot;styles.css&quot;&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"selectors-you-will-use-daily\">Selectors you will use daily</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Selector</th><th>Matches</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>p</code></td><td>Every paragraph</td></tr><tr><td><code>.card</code></td><td>Elements with <code>class=&quot;card&quot;</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>#hero</code></td><td>The element with <code>id=&quot;hero&quot;</code> (use sparingly)</td></tr><tr><td><code>.card h2</code></td><td>h2 elements inside a .card</td></tr><tr><td><code>.card &gt; h2</code></td><td>h2 elements that are direct children of a .card</td></tr><tr><td><code>button:hover</code></td><td>Buttons while the mouse is over them</td></tr><tr><td><code>a:focus-visible</code></td><td>Links focused with the keyboard</td></tr><tr><td><code>li:first-child</code></td><td>The first li in its list</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Style mostly with <strong>classes</strong>. They are reusable and keep specificity low.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-cascade-and-specificity\">The cascade and specificity</h2>\n<p>When two rules target the same element, the browser decides:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Specificity</strong>: <code>#id</code> beats <code>.class</code> beats <code>element</code>.</li><li>If equal, <strong>the later rule wins</strong>.</li><li><code>!important</code> overrides almost everything. Avoid it; it starts specificity wars.</li></ol>\n<p>Some properties (like <code>color</code> and <code>font-family</code>) are <strong>inherited</strong>: set them on <code>body</code> and every child gets them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-box-model\">The box model</h2>\n<p>Every element is a rectangular box with four layers:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">+------------------- margin -------------------+\n|  +---------------- border ----------------+  |\n|  |  +------------- padding -------------+ |  |\n|  |  |            content                | |  |\n|  |  +-----------------------------------+ |  |\n|  +----------------------------------------+  |\n+----------------------------------------------+</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><strong>content</strong>: the text or image</li><li><strong>padding</strong>: space inside the border</li><li><strong>border</strong>: the edge</li><li><strong>margin</strong>: space outside, pushing other elements away</li></ul>\n<p>By default, <code>width</code> only sets the content size, so padding and border make the box bigger than you asked. Every professional stylesheet starts with this fix:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">*, *::before, *::after {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Now <code>width: 300px</code> means 300px total, padding and border included.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"colours-fonts-and-units\">Colours, fonts and units</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">:root {\n  --brand: #b4532a;     /* custom property: reuse it everywhere */\n  --ink: #1f1a17;\n  --paper: #fbf7f3;\n}\n\nbody {\n  margin: 0;\n  background: var(--paper);\n  color: var(--ink);\n  font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, sans-serif;\n  font-size: 1rem;       /* rem = relative to the root font size (16px default) */\n  line-height: 1.6;\n}\n\n.button {\n  display: inline-block;\n  padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem;\n  background: var(--brand);\n  color: white;\n  border-radius: 999px;\n  text-decoration: none;\n  font-weight: 600;\n}\n\n.button:hover {\n  background: var(--ink);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>px</code></td><td>Pixels</td><td>Borders, small fixed details</td></tr><tr><td><code>rem</code></td><td>Multiples of the root font size</td><td>Font sizes, spacing</td></tr><tr><td><code>%</code></td><td>Percentage of the parent</td><td>Widths</td></tr><tr><td><code>vw</code> / <code>vh</code></td><td>1% of the viewport width / height</td><td>Full-screen sections (carefully)</td></tr><tr><td><code>ch</code></td><td>Width of the &quot;0&quot; character</td><td>Readable line lengths, e.g. <code>max-width: 65ch</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The red outline trick</p><p>When a layout misbehaves, add <code>* { outline: 1px solid red; }</code> temporarily. You will instantly see every box and what is overflowing.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which selector matches every element with class=&quot;price&quot;?","options":["#price",".price","price"],"answer":1,"explain":"A dot selects by class, a hash by id, a bare word by element name."},{"q":"With box-sizing: border-box, a box has width: 300px, padding: 20px and border: 2px. How wide is it on screen?","options":["300px","344px","320px"],"answer":0,"explain":"border-box makes width include padding and border. Without it, the box would be 344px."},{"q":"Two rules set a paragraph's colour: <code>.intro { color: red }</code> and <code>p { color: blue }</code>. Which colour wins?","options":["Red, because a class is more specific than an element selector","Blue, because it comes later","Whichever is first in the file"],"answer":0,"explain":"Specificity is checked before source order."},{"q":"Why define brand colours as custom properties like --brand?","options":["They load faster","One change updates the colour everywhere it is used","Browsers require it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Variables make design systems and rebrands easy."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Your first CSS rule","file":"styles.css","lang":"css","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write a rule that styles every <code>h1</code>: colour <code>#b4532a</code> and font size <code>2.5rem</code>. One property per line, each ending with a semicolon. Press <strong>Run it</strong> to see it applied to a sample page.</p>","starter":"","html":"<h1>Amara Studio</h1>\n<p>Handmade brass jewellery from London.</p>","solution":"h1 {\n  color: #b4532a;\n  font-size: 2.5rem;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*h1\\s*\\{","text":"Start with the selector and an opening brace: <code>h1 {</code>"},{"re":"color\\s*:\\s*#b4532a","text":"<code>color: #b4532a;</code> (American spelling: color)."},{"re":"font-size\\s*:\\s*2\\.5rem","text":"<code>font-size: 2.5rem;</code> with a hyphen in the property name."},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Close the rule with <code>}</code>."}],"explain":""},{"title":"The border-box reset","file":"styles.css","lang":"css","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the rule every professional stylesheet starts with: make every element, and every <code>::before</code> and <code>::after</code> pseudo-element, use <code>box-sizing: border-box</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"*, *::before, *::after {\n  box-sizing: border-box;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*\\*\\s*,","text":"The selector starts with <code>*</code> (every element), then a comma."},{"re":"\\*::before\\s*,\\s*\\*::after","text":"Then <code>*::before, *::after</code> (two colons each)."},{"re":"box-sizing\\s*:\\s*border-box","text":"Inside: <code>box-sizing: border-box;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Brand colours with a variable","file":"styles.css","lang":"css","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write two rules:</p>\n<ol><li>On <code>:root</code>, define a custom property <code>--brand</code> with the value <code>#b4532a</code>.</li><li>On <code>.button</code>, set <code>background</code> to <code>var(--brand)</code> and <code>color</code> to <code>white</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"<a class=\"button\" href=\"#\">Shop now</a>","solution":":root {\n  --brand: #b4532a;\n}\n.button {\n  background: var(--brand);\n  color: white;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*:root\\s*\\{","text":"Rule 1 starts <code>:root {</code>"},{"re":"--brand\\s*:\\s*#b4532a","text":"Custom properties start with two hyphens: <code>--brand: #b4532a;</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\.button\\s*\\{","text":"Rule 2 starts <code>.button {</code> (a dot, because it is a class)."},{"re":"background\\s*:\\s*var\\(\\s*--brand\\s*\\)","text":"Use the variable with <code>var(--brand)</code>: <code>background: var(--brand);</code>"},{"re":"color\\s*:\\s*white","text":"Then <code>color: white;</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"How CSS works CSS is a list of rules . Each rule picks elements with a selector and sets properties : h1 { color: #1f1a17; font-size: 2.5rem; } Link a stylesheet from your HTML head : link rel= stylesheet href= styles.css Selectors you will use daily Selector Matches p Every paragraph .card Elements with class= card #hero The element with id= hero (use sparingly) .card h2 h2 elements inside a .card .card h2 h2 elements that are direct children of a .card button:hover Buttons while the mouse is over them a:focus-visible Links focused with the keyboard li:first-child The first li in its list Style mostly with classes . They are reusable and keep specificity low. The cascade and specificity When two rules target the same element, the browser decides: Specificity : #id beats .class beats element . If equal, the later rule wins . !important overrides almost everything. Avoid it; it starts specificity wars. Some properties (like color and font-family ) are inherited : set them on body and every child gets them. The box model Every element is a rectangular box with four layers: +------------------- margin -------------------+ | +---------------- border ----------------+ | | | +------------- padding -------------+ | | | | | content | | | | | +-----------------------------------+ | | | +----------------------------------------+ | +----------------------------------------------+ content : the text or image padding : space inside the border border : the edge margin : space outside, pushing other elements away By default, width only sets the content size, so padding and border make the box bigger than you asked. Every professional stylesheet starts with this fix: *, *::before, *::after { box-sizing: border-box; } Now width: 300px means 300px total, padding and border included. Colours, fonts and units :root { --brand: #b4532a; /* custom property: reuse it everywhere */ --ink: #1f1a17; --paper: #fbf7f3; } body { margin: 0; background: var(--paper); color: var(--ink); font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, Segoe UI , sans-serif; font-size: 1rem; /* rem = relative to the root font size (16px default) */ line-height: 1.6; } .button { display: inline-block; padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem; background: var(--brand); color: white; border-radius: 999px; text-decoration: none; font-weight: 600; } .button:hover { background: var(--ink); } Unit Meaning Use for px Pixels Borders, small fixed details rem Multiples of the root font size Font sizes, spacing % Percentage of the parent Widths vw / vh 1% of the viewport width / height Full-screen sections (carefully) ch Width of the 0 character Readable line lengths, e.g. max-width: 65ch The red outline trick When a layout misbehaves, add * { outline: 1px solid red; } temporarily. You will instantly see every box and what is overflowing.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-layout","title":"Layout with Flexbox, Grid and responsive design","level":"beginner","minutes":30,"summary":"Put things side by side, build grids that reflow on phones, and design mobile-first.","html":"<h2 id=\"two-layout-tools-two-jobs\">Two layout tools, two jobs</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Flexbox</strong> arranges items in <strong>one direction</strong>: a row of nav links, a button with an icon, a card's contents.</li><li><strong>Grid</strong> arranges items in <strong>two dimensions</strong>: a product grid, a page with a sidebar.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"flexbox\">Flexbox</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">.nav {\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 1rem;                       /* space between items */\n  justify-content: space-between;  /* main axis (horizontal in a row) */\n  align-items: center;             /* cross axis (vertical in a row) */\n  flex-wrap: wrap;                 /* let items wrap on small screens */\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Common values</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>flex-direction</code></td><td><code>row</code> (default), <code>column</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>justify-content</code></td><td><code>flex-start</code>, <code>center</code>, <code>space-between</code>, <code>space-evenly</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>align-items</code></td><td><code>stretch</code> (default), <code>center</code>, <code>flex-start</code>, <code>flex-end</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>gap</code></td><td>Any length, e.g. <code>1rem</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>On a child, <code>flex: 1</code> means &quot;take up the remaining space&quot;. Load the <strong>Flexbox lab</strong> template in the <a href=\"#playground\">Playground</a> and try every value.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"grid\">Grid</h2>\n<p>The single most useful line of CSS for product grids:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">.products {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr));\n  gap: 1.5rem;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>This says: make as many columns as fit, each at least 220px wide, sharing leftover space equally. Four columns on a laptop, two on a tablet, one on a phone, with <strong>no media queries</strong>.</p>\n<p>A classic page layout:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">.page {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: 250px 1fr;   /* sidebar, then the rest */\n  grid-template-areas: &quot;side main&quot;;\n  gap: 2rem;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"responsive-design-mobile-first\">Responsive design, mobile first</h2>\n<p>Most visitors to brand and shop sites are on phones. Write the phone layout first, then add rules for larger screens with <strong>media queries</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;!doctype html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n&lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1&quot;&gt;\n&lt;style&gt;\n  * { box-sizing: border-box; }\n  body { margin: 0; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; }\n  .hero { padding: 2rem 1rem; background: #f6efe9; }\n  .hero h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3.5rem); margin: 0 0 .5rem; }\n  .grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(160px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; padding: 1rem; }\n  .card { background: #fff; border: 1px solid #eadfd6; border-radius: 12px; padding: 1rem; }\n  .card .img { aspect-ratio: 4/5; background: #e9c9b4; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: .5rem; }\n\n  /* Larger screens: more breathing room */\n  @media (min-width: 768px) {\n    .hero { padding: 4rem 3rem; }\n    .grid { padding: 2rem 3rem; gap: 1.5rem; }\n  }\n&lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;section class=&quot;hero&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Summer drop&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Breathable linen, cut for heat.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/section&gt;\n  &lt;section class=&quot;grid&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;article class=&quot;card&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;img&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;Wrap dress, £68&lt;/article&gt;\n    &lt;article class=&quot;card&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;img&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;Wide trousers, £54&lt;/article&gt;\n    &lt;article class=&quot;card&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;img&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;Camp shirt, £42&lt;/article&gt;\n    &lt;article class=&quot;card&quot;&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;img&quot;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;Bucket hat, £22&lt;/article&gt;\n  &lt;/section&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Notice <code>clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3.5rem)</code>: the heading scales with the screen but never goes below 2rem or above 3.5rem.</p>\n<p>Press <strong>Run in Playground</strong>, then make the browser window narrower to watch the grid reflow.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-viewport-meta-tag\">The viewport meta tag</h2>\n<p>Without this in your <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, phones pretend to be a desktop and shrink everything:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;meta name=&quot;viewport&quot; content=&quot;width=device-width, initial-scale=1&quot;&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Test like a pro</p><p>In Chrome DevTools press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>M</kbd> (device toolbar) and check your page at 375px wide (a common phone), 768px (tablet) and 1280px (laptop). Then check on your real phone.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"You need a row of five nav links spaced evenly across a header. Best tool?","options":["Flexbox","Grid","Absolute positioning"],"answer":0,"explain":"One direction of items: Flexbox."},{"q":"What does <code>grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr))</code> do?","options":["Always makes exactly 220 columns","Fits as many columns as possible, each at least 220px, sharing leftover space","Makes one column 220px wide"],"answer":1,"explain":"It produces a responsive grid without any media queries."},{"q":"What does &quot;mobile first&quot; mean?","options":["Only build for phones","Write base styles for small screens, then add min-width media queries for bigger ones","Build the desktop version first and hide things on mobile"],"answer":1,"explain":"Base styles serve phones; media queries enhance for larger screens."},{"q":"Your site looks tiny and zoomed out on phones. What is most likely missing?","options":["A media query","The viewport meta tag in head","box-sizing: border-box"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without the viewport tag, mobile browsers render at desktop width and scale down."}],"exercises":[{"title":"A flexbox navigation bar","file":"styles.css","lang":"css","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Style <code>.nav</code> as a flex row: <code>display: flex</code>, a <code>gap</code> of <code>1rem</code>, <code>justify-content: space-between</code> and <code>align-items: center</code>, in that order.</p>","starter":"","html":"<nav class=\"nav\"><strong>Amara</strong><a href=\"#\">Shop</a><a href=\"#\">About</a><a href=\"#\">Bag (0)</a></nav>","solution":".nav {\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 1rem;\n  justify-content: space-between;\n  align-items: center;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*\\.nav\\s*\\{","text":"Start with <code>.nav {</code>"},{"re":"display\\s*:\\s*flex","text":"<code>display: flex;</code> turns on flexbox."},{"re":"gap\\s*:\\s*1rem","text":"<code>gap: 1rem;</code>"},{"re":"justify-content\\s*:\\s*space-between","text":"<code>justify-content: space-between;</code>"},{"re":"align-items\\s*:\\s*center","text":"<code>align-items: center;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"A responsive product grid","file":"styles.css","lang":"css","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Make <code>.products</code> a grid that fits as many columns as possible, each at least <code>150px</code> wide, with a <code>1rem</code> gap. Three properties: <code>display</code>, <code>grid-template-columns</code>, <code>gap</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"<style>.p{background:#f6efe9;padding:2rem 1rem;border-radius:10px;font-family:system-ui}</style>\n<section class=\"products\"><div class=\"p\">Wrap dress</div><div class=\"p\">Camp shirt</div><div class=\"p\">Bucket hat</div><div class=\"p\">Slip dress</div><div class=\"p\">Trousers</div></section>","solution":".products {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(150px, 1fr));\n  gap: 1rem;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"display\\s*:\\s*grid","text":"<code>display: grid;</code>"},{"re":"grid-template-columns\\s*:\\s*repeat\\(","text":"<code>grid-template-columns: repeat(...)</code>"},{"re":"repeat\\(\\s*auto-fill\\s*,\\s*minmax\\(\\s*150px\\s*,\\s*1fr\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Inside: <code>repeat(auto-fill, minmax(150px, 1fr))</code>. Count the brackets: two open, two close."},{"re":"gap\\s*:\\s*1rem","text":"<code>gap: 1rem;</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>minmax(150px, 1fr)</code> means &quot;at least 150px, otherwise share the space&quot;. <code>auto-fill</code> makes as many columns as fit. Resize your window: the grid reflows with no media queries.</p>"},{"title":"Your first media query","file":"styles.css","lang":"css","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a media query for screens at least <code>768px</code> wide that gives <code>.hero</code> a padding of <code>4rem 3rem</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"@media (min-width: 768px) {\n  .hero {\n    padding: 4rem 3rem;\n  }\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*@media\\s*\\(\\s*min-width\\s*:\\s*768px\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>@media (min-width: 768px) {</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\.hero\\s*\\{","text":"Inside it, a normal rule: <code>.hero {</code>"},{"re":"padding\\s*:\\s*4rem\\s+3rem","text":"<code>padding: 4rem 3rem;</code> (top/bottom, then left/right)."},{"re":"\\}\\s*\\n\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Two closing braces: one for <code>.hero</code>, one for the media query."}],"explain":""}],"text":"Two layout tools, two jobs Flexbox arranges items in one direction : a row of nav links, a button with an icon, a card's contents. Grid arranges items in two dimensions : a product grid, a page with a sidebar. Flexbox .nav { display: flex; gap: 1rem; /* space between items */ justify-content: space-between; /* main axis (horizontal in a row) */ align-items: center; /* cross axis (vertical in a row) */ flex-wrap: wrap; /* let items wrap on small screens */ } Property Common values flex-direction row (default), column justify-content flex-start , center , space-between , space-evenly align-items stretch (default), center , flex-start , flex-end gap Any length, e.g. 1rem On a child, flex: 1 means take up the remaining space . Load the Flexbox lab template in the Playground and try every value. Grid The single most useful line of CSS for product grids: .products { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr)); gap: 1.5rem; } This says: make as many columns as fit, each at least 220px wide, sharing leftover space equally. Four columns on a laptop, two on a tablet, one on a phone, with no media queries . A classic page layout: .page { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 250px 1fr; /* sidebar, then the rest */ grid-template-areas: side main ; gap: 2rem; } Responsive design, mobile first Most visitors to brand and shop sites are on phones. Write the phone layout first, then add rules for larger screens with media queries : !doctype html html lang= en head meta name= viewport content= width=device-width, initial-scale=1 style * { box-sizing: border-box; } body { margin: 0; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; } .hero { padding: 2rem 1rem; background: #f6efe9; } .hero h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3.5rem); margin: 0 0 .5rem; } .grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(160px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; padding: 1rem; } .card { background: #fff; border: 1px solid #eadfd6; border-radius: 12px; padding: 1rem; } .card .img { aspect-ratio: 4/5; background: #e9c9b4; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: .5rem; } /* Larger screens: more breathing room */ @media (min-width: 768px) { .hero { padding: 4rem 3rem; } .grid { padding: 2rem 3rem; gap: 1.5rem; } } /style /head body section class= hero h1 Summer drop /h1 p Breathable linen, cut for heat. /p /section section class= grid article class= card div class= img /div Wrap dress, £68 /article article class= card div class= img /div Wide trousers, £54 /article article class= card div class= img /div Camp shirt, £42 /article article class= card div class= img /div Bucket hat, £22 /article /section /body /html Notice clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3.5rem) : the heading scales with the screen but never goes below 2rem or above 3.5rem. Press Run in Playground , then make the browser window narrower to watch the grid reflow. The viewport meta tag Without this in your head , phones pretend to be a desktop and shrink everything: meta name= viewport content= width=device-width, initial-scale=1 Test like a pro In Chrome DevTools press Ctrl + Shift + M (device toolbar) and check your page at 375px wide (a common phone), 768px (tablet) and 1280px (laptop). Then check on your real phone.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-js-basics","title":"\"JavaScript basics: values, variables and functions\"","level":"beginner","minutes":25,"summary":"The building blocks of every program, written and run in your browser.","html":"<h2 id=\"where-javascript-runs\">Where JavaScript runs</h2>\n<p>JavaScript runs in every browser and, with Node.js, on servers and your computer. Open any page, press <kbd>F12</kbd>, click <strong>Console</strong>, and type <code>2 + 2</code>. You are programming.</p>\n<p>For this lesson, use the <a href=\"#playground\">Playground</a> (choose the <em>JavaScript console basics</em> template) or the browser console.</p>\n<h2 id=\"values-and-types\">Values and types</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">'Linen dress'      // string: text, in quotes\n68                 // number: integers and decimals are both &quot;number&quot;\ntrue               // boolean: true or false\nnull               // &quot;deliberately nothing&quot;\nundefined          // &quot;not set yet&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Check a type with <code>typeof</code>: <code>typeof 68</code> gives <code>&quot;number&quot;</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"variables\">Variables</h2>\n<p>A variable is a named box for a value.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const brand = 'Amara Studio';   // const: cannot be reassigned\nlet stock = 12;                 // let: can be reassigned\nstock = stock - 1;              // now 11</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Use <code>const</code> by default.</strong> Switch to <code>let</code> only when the value must change. Avoid <code>var</code>; it is the old way and has confusing rules.</p>\n<p>Names are written in <strong>camelCase</strong>: <code>cartTotal</code>, <code>isLoggedIn</code>, <code>maxSupply</code>. Make them descriptive.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"operators\">Operators</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">10 + 5      // 15\n10 - 5      // 5\n10 * 5      // 50\n10 / 4      // 2.5\n10 % 3      // 1 (remainder)\n2 ** 3      // 8 (power)\n\n'Hello, ' + 'world'          // joining strings\n`Total: £${(6800 / 100).toFixed(2)}`   // template literal with backticks: &quot;Total: £68.00&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Comparisons give booleans:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">5 === 5        // true   (equal value AND type)\n5 === '5'      // false\n5 !== 3        // true\n7 &gt; 3          // true\nage &gt;= 18 &amp;&amp; hasId     // AND: both must be true\nisMember || hasCode    // OR: at least one true\n!isSoldOut             // NOT: flips true/false</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Always use === not ==</p><p><code>==</code> converts types before comparing, giving surprises like <code>'0' == false</code> being <code>true</code>. Use <code>===</code> and <code>!==</code> every time.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"making-decisions\">Making decisions</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const stock = 3;\n\nif (stock === 0) {\n  console.log('Sold out');\n} else if (stock &lt; 5) {\n  console.log(`Only ${stock} left`);\n} else {\n  console.log('In stock');\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"functions\">Functions</h2>\n<p>A function is a reusable recipe. It takes <strong>parameters</strong> and <strong>returns</strong> a result.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">// function declaration\nfunction priceWithVat(pence) {\n  return Math.round(pence * 1.2);\n}\n\n// arrow function: the same idea, shorter syntax\nconst formatGBP = (pence) =&gt; `£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`;\n\nconst dress = 5667;\nconsole.log(formatGBP(priceWithVat(dress)));   // £68.00\n\n// a function with a default parameter\nfunction greet(name = 'there') {\n  return `Hi ${name}, welcome back!`;\n}\nconsole.log(greet('Tolu'));\nconsole.log(greet());</code></pre></div>\n<p>Good functions do <strong>one thing</strong> and are named with a verb: <code>calculateTotal</code>, <code>formatDate</code>, <code>isValidEmail</code>.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Money is stored in pence</p><p><code>0.1 + 0.2</code> in JavaScript is <code>0.30000000000000004</code>, because computers store decimals in binary. Store money as whole pence (or cents) and only divide by 100 when displaying it. Blockchains do the same thing with 18 decimal places, as you will see later.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which keyword should you use by default to declare a variable?","options":["const","let","var"],"answer":0,"explain":"const prevents accidental reassignment. Use let only when the value has to change."},{"q":"What does <code>5 === '5'</code> return?","options":["true","false","An error"],"answer":1,"explain":"=== compares type as well as value. A number is not a string."},{"q":"Why store £68.00 as 6800?","options":["It uses less memory","Decimal maths in binary is imprecise; whole numbers of pence are exact","JavaScript cannot display decimals"],"answer":1,"explain":"Floating-point rounding errors cause real money bugs. Integers avoid them."},{"q":"What does this return: <code>const double = (n) =&gt; n * 2; double(21)</code>","options":["42","21","undefined"],"answer":0,"explain":"An arrow function with one expression returns that expression automatically."}],"exercises":[{"title":"const and let","file":"stock.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>brand</code> holding <code>'Amara Studio'</code>.</li><li>A variable (<code>let</code>) <code>stock</code> holding <code>12</code>.</li><li>Reduce stock by one: <code>stock = stock - 1;</code></li><li>Print both: <code>console.log(brand, stock);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const brand = 'Amara Studio';\nlet stock = 12;\nstock = stock - 1;\nconsole.log(brand, stock);","also":["const brand = 'Amara Studio';\nlet stock = 12;\nstock -= 1;\nconsole.log(brand, stock);"],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+brand\\s*=\\s*['\"]Amara Studio['\"]","text":"Line 1: <code>const brand = 'Amara Studio';</code>"},{"re":"let\\s+stock\\s*=\\s*12","text":"Line 2 uses <code>let</code>, because the value will change: <code>let stock = 12;</code>"},{"re":"stock\\s*(=\\s*stock\\s*-|-=)\\s*1","text":"Line 3: <code>stock = stock - 1;</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*brand\\s*,\\s*stock\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(brand, stock);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>Amara Studio 11</code>. Try changing <code>let</code> to <code>const</code> and run it: you get <code>TypeError: Assignment to constant variable</code>. That error is <code>const</code> protecting you.</p>"},{"title":"Format a price with a template literal","file":"price.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write two lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>pence</code> equal to <code>6800</code>.</li><li>Print the text <code>Total: £68.00</code> using a <strong>template literal</strong> (text between backticks) with <code>${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}</code> inside it.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const pence = 6800;\nconsole.log(`Total: £${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+pence\\s*=\\s*6800","text":"Line 1: <code>const pence = 6800;</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*`","text":"Line 2 starts <code>console.log(</code> and then a backtick (the key above Tab on most keyboards), not a quote."},{"re":"`Total: £\\$\\{","text":"Inside the backticks: <code>Total: £</code> then a dollar sign and an opening curly brace."},{"re":"\\(\\s*pence\\s*\\/\\s*100\\s*\\)\\.toFixed\\(\\s*2\\s*\\)","text":"Inside the braces: <code>(pence / 100).toFixed(2)</code> then close the brace and the backtick."}],"explain":"<p>Inside backticks, anything in <code>${ }</code> is calculated and dropped into the text. Output: <code>Total: £68.00</code>. Money stays in pence until the moment you display it.</p>"},{"title":"A function and an arrow function","file":"vat.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write:</p>\n<ol><li>A function <code>priceWithVat(pence)</code> that returns <code>Math.round(pence * 1.2)</code>.</li><li>An arrow function constant <code>formatGBP</code> that takes <code>pence</code> and returns <code>(pence / 100).toFixed(2)</code>.</li><li><code>console.log(formatGBP(priceWithVat(5667)));</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function priceWithVat(pence) {\n  return Math.round(pence * 1.2);\n}\nconst formatGBP = (pence) => (pence / 100).toFixed(2);\nconsole.log(formatGBP(priceWithVat(5667)));","also":["function priceWithVat(pence) {\n  return Math.round(pence * 1.2);\n}\nconst formatGBP = pence => (pence / 100).toFixed(2);\nconsole.log(formatGBP(priceWithVat(5667)));"],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+priceWithVat\\s*\\(\\s*pence\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function priceWithVat(pence) {</code>"},{"re":"return\\s+Math\\.round\\(\\s*pence\\s*\\*\\s*1\\.2\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>return Math.round(pence * 1.2);</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+formatGBP\\s*=\\s*\\(?\\s*pence\\s*\\)?\\s*=>","text":"Line 4: <code>const formatGBP = (pence) =&gt;</code> then the expression to return."},{"re":"=>\\s*\\(\\s*pence\\s*\\/\\s*100\\s*\\)\\.toFixed\\(\\s*2\\s*\\)","text":"After the arrow: <code>(pence / 100).toFixed(2);</code>"},{"re":"formatGBP\\(\\s*priceWithVat\\(\\s*5667\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Last line calls one function inside the other: <code>formatGBP(priceWithVat(5667))</code>"}],"explain":"<p>5667 x 1.2 = 6800.4, rounded to 6800 pence, formatted as <code>68.00</code>. Functions inside functions run from the inside out.</p>"}],"text":"Where JavaScript runs JavaScript runs in every browser and, with Node.js, on servers and your computer. Open any page, press F12 , click Console , and type 2 + 2 . You are programming. For this lesson, use the Playground (choose the JavaScript console basics template) or the browser console. Values and types 'Linen dress' // string: text, in quotes 68 // number: integers and decimals are both number true // boolean: true or false null // deliberately nothing undefined // not set yet Check a type with typeof : typeof 68 gives number . Variables A variable is a named box for a value. const brand = 'Amara Studio'; // const: cannot be reassigned let stock = 12; // let: can be reassigned stock = stock - 1; // now 11 Use const by default. Switch to let only when the value must change. Avoid var ; it is the old way and has confusing rules. Names are written in camelCase : cartTotal , isLoggedIn , maxSupply . Make them descriptive. Operators 10 + 5 // 15 10 - 5 // 5 10 * 5 // 50 10 / 4 // 2.5 10 % 3 // 1 (remainder) 2 ** 3 // 8 (power) 'Hello, ' + 'world' // joining strings `Total: £${(6800 / 100).toFixed(2)}` // template literal with backticks: Total: £68.00 Comparisons give booleans: 5 === 5 // true (equal value AND type) 5 === '5' // false 5 !== 3 // true 7 3 // true age = 18 hasId // AND: both must be true isMember || hasCode // OR: at least one true !isSoldOut // NOT: flips true/false Always use === not == == converts types before comparing, giving surprises like '0' == false being true . Use === and !== every time. Making decisions const stock = 3; if (stock === 0) { console.log('Sold out'); } else if (stock 5) { console.log(`Only ${stock} left`); } else { console.log('In stock'); } Functions A function is a reusable recipe. It takes parameters and returns a result. // function declaration function priceWithVat(pence) { return Math.round(pence * 1.2); } // arrow function: the same idea, shorter syntax const formatGBP = (pence) = `£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`; const dress = 5667; console.log(formatGBP(priceWithVat(dress))); // £68.00 // a function with a default parameter function greet(name = 'there') { return `Hi ${name}, welcome back!`; } console.log(greet('Tolu')); console.log(greet()); Good functions do one thing and are named with a verb: calculateTotal , formatDate , isValidEmail . Money is stored in pence 0.1 + 0.2 in JavaScript is 0.30000000000000004 , because computers store decimals in binary. Store money as whole pence (or cents) and only divide by 100 when displaying it. Blockchains do the same thing with 18 decimal places, as you will see later.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-js-data","title":"\"JavaScript data: arrays, objects and loops\"","level":"beginner","minutes":25,"summary":"Store lists and records, loop over them, and transform data with map, filter and reduce.","html":"<h2 id=\"arrays-ordered-lists\">Arrays: ordered lists</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const sizes = ['XS', 'S', 'M', 'L'];\n\nsizes[0];          // 'XS' (counting starts at 0)\nsizes.length;      // 4\nsizes.push('XL');  // add to the end\nsizes.includes('M');   // true\nsizes.indexOf('L');    // 3</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"objects-named-properties\">Objects: named properties</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const product = {\n  id: 'dr-001',\n  name: 'Linen Wrap Dress',\n  pricePence: 6800,\n  sizes: ['S', 'M', 'L'],\n  inStock: true,\n};\n\nproduct.name;            // 'Linen Wrap Dress'\nproduct['pricePence'];   // 6800 (bracket form works with variables too)\nproduct.colour = 'sage'; // add a property</code></pre></div>\n<p>Real data is usually <strong>an array of objects</strong>. That is exactly what an API sends back:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const products = [\n  { name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800, category: 'dresses' },\n  { name: 'Camp shirt', pricePence: 4200, category: 'tops' },\n  { name: 'Bucket hat', pricePence: 2200, category: 'accessories' },\n  { name: 'Slip dress', pricePence: 5400, category: 'dresses' },\n];</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"loops\">Loops</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">for (const p of products) {\n  console.log(`${p.name}: £${p.pricePence / 100}`);\n}\n\n// classic counter loop, when you need the index\nfor (let i = 0; i &lt; products.length; i++) {\n  console.log(i + 1, products[i].name);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"map-filter-reduce-the-professional-way\">map, filter, reduce: the professional way</h2>\n<p>These three methods replace most loops. They do not change the original array; they return a new value.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const products = [\n  { name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800, category: 'dresses' },\n  { name: 'Camp shirt', pricePence: 4200, category: 'tops' },\n  { name: 'Bucket hat', pricePence: 2200, category: 'accessories' },\n  { name: 'Slip dress', pricePence: 5400, category: 'dresses' },\n];\n\n// map: transform every item -&gt; new array of the same length\nconst names = products.map((p) =&gt; p.name);\n\n// filter: keep only items that pass a test\nconst dresses = products.filter((p) =&gt; p.category === 'dresses');\n\n// reduce: boil the array down to one value\nconst totalPence = products.reduce((sum, p) =&gt; sum + p.pricePence, 0);\n\n// find: the first match (or undefined)\nconst hat = products.find((p) =&gt; p.name.includes('hat'));\n\n// some / every: true or false questions\nconst anyUnder25 = products.some((p) =&gt; p.pricePence &lt; 2500);\n\n// sort (copy first: sort changes the array in place)\nconst cheapestFirst = [...products].sort((a, b) =&gt; a.pricePence - b.pricePence);\n\nconsole.log(names);\nconsole.log(dresses);\nconsole.log('Total £' + totalPence / 100);\nconsole.log(hat, anyUnder25);\nconsole.table(cheapestFirst);</code></pre></div>\n<p>You can <strong>chain</strong> them: <code>products.filter(p =&gt; p.category === 'dresses').map(p =&gt; p.name)</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"destructuring-and-spread\">Destructuring and spread</h2>\n<p>Shortcuts you will see in every modern codebase:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const { name, pricePence } = product;        // pull properties into variables\nconst [first, second] = sizes;               // pull array items into variables\n\nconst updated = { ...product, pricePence: 6000 };  // copy an object, changing one field\nconst allSizes = [...sizes, 'XXL'];                 // copy an array, adding one item</code></pre></div>\n<p>Copying instead of changing (mutating) data avoids a whole family of bugs, and it is how React expects you to work.</p>\n<h2 id=\"json\">JSON</h2>\n<p>JSON is how data travels between servers and browsers. It looks like JavaScript objects, with stricter rules (double quotes, no trailing commas, no functions).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const text = JSON.stringify(product);   // object -&gt; string (to send or store)\nconst back = JSON.parse(text);          // string -&gt; object (after receiving)</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"3\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>['a', 'b', 'c'][1]</code> return?","options":["'a'","'b'","'c'"],"answer":1,"explain":"Arrays are zero-indexed: position 0 is the first item."},{"q":"You want only the products under £30 from an array. Which method?","options":["map","filter","reduce"],"answer":1,"explain":"filter keeps items that pass a test; map transforms every item; reduce combines into one value."},{"q":"What does <code>products.reduce((sum, p) =&gt; sum + p.pricePence, 0)</code> produce?","options":["An array of prices","The total of all prices in pence","The most expensive product"],"answer":1,"explain":"reduce starts at 0 and adds each price to the running sum."},{"q":"What does <code>{ ...product, pricePence: 6000 }</code> do?","options":["Makes a new object with every property of product, but pricePence set to 6000","Changes product.pricePence to 6000","Deletes the other properties"],"answer":0,"explain":"The spread copies, then the later property overrides. The original is unchanged."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Work with an array","file":"sizes.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const sizes = ['XS', 'S', 'M', 'L'];</code></li><li>Add <code>'XL'</code> to the end with <code>push</code>.</li><li>Print how many sizes there are.</li><li>Print the first size.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const sizes = ['XS', 'S', 'M', 'L'];\nsizes.push('XL');\nconsole.log(sizes.length);\nconsole.log(sizes[0]);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+sizes\\s*=\\s*\\[","text":"Line 1: an array in square brackets: <code>const sizes = ['XS', 'S', 'M', 'L'];</code>"},{"re":"sizes\\.push\\(\\s*['\"]XL['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>sizes.push('XL');</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*sizes\\.length\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(sizes.length);</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*sizes\\[\\s*0\\s*\\]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: the first item is at position 0: <code>console.log(sizes[0]);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>5</code> then <code>XS</code>. A <code>const</code> array can still have items added: <code>const</code> only stops you replacing the whole array with a new one.</p>"},{"title":"Work with an object","file":"product.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>product</code> that is an object with <code>name: 'Wrap dress'</code> and <code>pricePence: 6800</code>.</li><li>Print <code>product.name</code>.</li><li>Print the price in pounds: <code>product.pricePence / 100</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const product = { name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800 };\nconsole.log(product.name);\nconsole.log(product.pricePence / 100);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+product\\s*=\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const product = {</code> (curly braces for objects)."},{"re":"name\\s*:\\s*['\"]Wrap dress['\"]\\s*,","text":"<code>name: 'Wrap dress',</code> then a comma before the next property."},{"re":"pricePence\\s*:\\s*6800\\s*\\}","text":"<code>pricePence: 6800 }</code> then close the object."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*product\\.name\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(product.name);</code>"},{"re":"product\\.pricePence\\s*\\/\\s*100","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(product.pricePence / 100);</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"filter and reduce","file":"prices.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const prices = [6800, 4200, 2200];</code></li><li>A constant <code>cheap</code> holding only the prices under 5000, using <code>filter</code> with an arrow function <code>(p) =&gt; p &lt; 5000</code>.</li><li>A constant <code>total</code> that adds them all up with <code>reduce((sum, p) =&gt; sum + p, 0)</code>.</li><li><code>console.log(cheap, total);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const prices = [6800, 4200, 2200];\nconst cheap = prices.filter((p) => p < 5000);\nconst total = prices.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p, 0);\nconsole.log(cheap, total);","also":["const prices = [6800, 4200, 2200];\nconst cheap = prices.filter(p => p < 5000);\nconst total = prices.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p, 0);\nconsole.log(cheap, total);"],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+prices\\s*=\\s*\\[\\s*6800\\s*,\\s*4200\\s*,\\s*2200\\s*\\]","text":"Line 1: <code>const prices = [6800, 4200, 2200];</code>"},{"re":"prices\\.filter\\(\\s*\\(?\\s*p\\s*\\)?\\s*=>\\s*p\\s*<\\s*5000\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const cheap = prices.filter((p) =&gt; p &lt; 5000);</code>"},{"re":"prices\\.reduce\\(\\s*\\(\\s*sum\\s*,\\s*p\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*sum\\s*\\+\\s*p\\s*,\\s*0\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>const total = prices.reduce((sum, p) =&gt; sum + p, 0);</code> The <code>0</code> is the starting value."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*cheap\\s*,\\s*total\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(cheap, total);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>[4200, 2200]</code> and <code>13200</code>. <code>filter</code> keeps items that pass the test; <code>reduce</code> carries a running <code>sum</code> through the array, starting from <code>0</code>.</p>"},{"title":"Copy an object with spread","file":"sale.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write three lines:</p>\n<ol><li>The same <code>product</code> object: <code>{ name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800 }</code>.</li><li>A constant <code>sale</code> that copies <code>product</code> with spread (<code>...product</code>) but sets <code>pricePence</code> to <code>5400</code>.</li><li><code>console.log(sale);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const product = { name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800 };\nconst sale = { ...product, pricePence: 5400 };\nconsole.log(sale);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+product\\s*=\\s*\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]Wrap dress['\"]\\s*,\\s*pricePence\\s*:\\s*6800\\s*\\}","text":"Line 1: <code>const product = { name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800 };</code>"},{"re":"\\{\\s*\\.\\.\\.product\\s*,","text":"Line 2 starts the new object with three dots: <code>{ ...product,</code>"},{"re":"pricePence\\s*:\\s*5400\\s*\\}","text":"Then override the price: <code>pricePence: 5400 }</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*sale\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(sale);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>{ name: &quot;Wrap dress&quot;, pricePence: 5400 }</code>. The original <code>product</code> still costs 6800. Copying instead of changing data is how React expects you to work.</p>"}],"text":"Arrays: ordered lists const sizes = ['XS', 'S', 'M', 'L']; sizes[0]; // 'XS' (counting starts at 0) sizes.length; // 4 sizes.push('XL'); // add to the end sizes.includes('M'); // true sizes.indexOf('L'); // 3 Objects: named properties const product = { id: 'dr-001', name: 'Linen Wrap Dress', pricePence: 6800, sizes: ['S', 'M', 'L'], inStock: true, }; product.name; // 'Linen Wrap Dress' product['pricePence']; // 6800 (bracket form works with variables too) product.colour = 'sage'; // add a property Real data is usually an array of objects . That is exactly what an API sends back: const products = [ { name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800, category: 'dresses' }, { name: 'Camp shirt', pricePence: 4200, category: 'tops' }, { name: 'Bucket hat', pricePence: 2200, category: 'accessories' }, { name: 'Slip dress', pricePence: 5400, category: 'dresses' }, ]; Loops for (const p of products) { console.log(`${p.name}: £${p.pricePence / 100}`); } // classic counter loop, when you need the index for (let i = 0; i products.length; i++) { console.log(i + 1, products[i].name); } map, filter, reduce: the professional way These three methods replace most loops. They do not change the original array; they return a new value. const products = [ { name: 'Wrap dress', pricePence: 6800, category: 'dresses' }, { name: 'Camp shirt', pricePence: 4200, category: 'tops' }, { name: 'Bucket hat', pricePence: 2200, category: 'accessories' }, { name: 'Slip dress', pricePence: 5400, category: 'dresses' }, ]; // map: transform every item - new array of the same length const names = products.map((p) = p.name); // filter: keep only items that pass a test const dresses = products.filter((p) = p.category === 'dresses'); // reduce: boil the array down to one value const totalPence = products.reduce((sum, p) = sum + p.pricePence, 0); // find: the first match (or undefined) const hat = products.find((p) = p.name.includes('hat')); // some / every: true or false questions const anyUnder25 = products.some((p) = p.pricePence 2500); // sort (copy first: sort changes the array in place) const cheapestFirst = [...products].sort((a, b) = a.pricePence - b.pricePence); console.log(names); console.log(dresses); console.log('Total £' + totalPence / 100); console.log(hat, anyUnder25); console.table(cheapestFirst); You can chain them: products.filter(p = p.category === 'dresses').map(p = p.name) . Destructuring and spread Shortcuts you will see in every modern codebase: const { name, pricePence } = product; // pull properties into variables const [first, second] = sizes; // pull array items into variables const updated = { ...product, pricePence: 6000 }; // copy an object, changing one field const allSizes = [...sizes, 'XXL']; // copy an array, adding one item Copying instead of changing (mutating) data avoids a whole family of bugs, and it is how React expects you to work. JSON JSON is how data travels between servers and browsers. It looks like JavaScript objects, with stricter rules (double quotes, no trailing commas, no functions). const text = JSON.stringify(product); // object - string (to send or store) const back = JSON.parse(text); // string - object (after receiving)","track":"web"},{"id":"web-dom","title":"The DOM and events","level":"beginner","minutes":30,"summary":"Find elements, change the page, respond to clicks and typing, and build an interactive to-do list.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-the-dom-is\">What the DOM is</h2>\n<p>When a browser loads HTML, it builds a live tree of objects called the <strong>Document Object Model</strong>. JavaScript can read and change that tree, and the page updates instantly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"finding-elements\">Finding elements</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const title = document.querySelector('h1');            // first match, CSS selector syntax\nconst button = document.querySelector('#add-to-bag');  // by id\nconst cards = document.querySelectorAll('.card');      // all matches (a NodeList)</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"changing-the-page\">Changing the page</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">title.textContent = 'Summer drop is live';   // change text (safe)\nbutton.classList.add('is-loading');          // add a CSS class\nbutton.classList.toggle('active');           // add if missing, remove if present\nbutton.disabled = true;                      // set a property\ntitle.style.color = '#b4532a';               // inline style (prefer classes)\n\nconst li = document.createElement('li');     // build a new element\nli.textContent = 'New item';\ndocument.querySelector('ul').append(li);     // put it on the page</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">textContent versus innerHTML</p><p><code>textContent</code> treats everything as plain text. <code>innerHTML</code> parses HTML. <strong>Never</strong> put user input into <code>innerHTML</code>: someone could type a <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> or an <code>&lt;img onerror=...&gt;</code> and run code on your page. This attack is called XSS (cross-site scripting).</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"events\">Events</h2>\n<p>An <strong>event</strong> is something that happens: a click, a key press, a form submit, the page finishing loading. You attach a <strong>listener</strong> that runs when it happens:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">button.addEventListener('click', () =&gt; {\n  console.log('Added to bag');\n});\n\nconst search = document.querySelector('#search');\nsearch.addEventListener('input', (event) =&gt; {\n  console.log('Typed:', event.target.value);\n});\n\nconst form = document.querySelector('form');\nform.addEventListener('submit', (event) =&gt; {\n  event.preventDefault();   // stop the browser reloading the page\n  // handle the form with JavaScript instead\n});</code></pre></div>\n<p>Common events: <code>click</code>, <code>input</code>, <code>change</code>, <code>submit</code>, <code>keydown</code>, <code>DOMContentLoaded</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-state-render-action-pattern\">The state, render, action pattern</h2>\n<p>The cleanest way to build interactive UI (and exactly how React thinks):</p>\n<ol><li><strong>State</strong>: one place that holds your data (<code>let todos = [...]</code>).</li><li><strong>Render</strong>: a function that rebuilds the UI from the state.</li><li><strong>Actions</strong>: event handlers change the state, then call render.</li></ol>\n<p>Never store the &quot;truth&quot; in the HTML itself. The HTML is just a picture of your state.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">let count = 0;                       // 1. state\n\nconst label = document.createElement('p');\nconst plus = document.createElement('button');\nconst reset = document.createElement('button');\nplus.textContent = '+1';\nreset.textContent = 'Reset';\ndocument.body.append(label, plus, reset);\n\nfunction render() {                  // 2. render\n  label.textContent = `Count: ${count}`;\n  reset.disabled = count === 0;\n}\n\nplus.addEventListener('click', () =&gt; { count++; render(); });   // 3. actions\nreset.addEventListener('click', () =&gt; { count = 0; render(); });\n\nrender();</code></pre></div>\n<p>Now open the <a href=\"#playground\">Playground</a> and load the <strong>To-do list (DOM)</strong> template. It uses the same pattern with an array of objects. Read it line by line, then add a &quot;Clear completed&quot; button.</p>\n<h2 id=\"saving-data-in-the-browser\">Saving data in the browser</h2>\n<p><code>localStorage</code> keeps small amounts of text after the page closes:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">localStorage.setItem('todos', JSON.stringify(todos));             // save\nconst saved = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('todos') || '[]');  // load</code></pre></div>\n<p>It only stores strings (hence <code>JSON.stringify</code>), it is per-browser, and users can clear it. Fine for preferences and drafts; not a database. (This very site saves your progress this way.)</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which method finds the first element matching a CSS selector?","options":["document.querySelector","document.getAll","document.find"],"answer":0,"explain":"querySelector takes any CSS selector and returns the first match."},{"q":"A user types their name into a field and you show it in a greeting. Which is safe?","options":["greeting.textContent = name","greeting.innerHTML = name","Both are equally safe"],"answer":0,"explain":"innerHTML would run any HTML or script the user typed. That is XSS."},{"q":"Why call event.preventDefault() in a form's submit handler?","options":["To clear the form","To stop the browser's default full page reload so JavaScript can handle it","To validate the inputs"],"answer":1,"explain":"Without it, submitting navigates or reloads the page."},{"q":"In the state, render, action pattern, what does an event handler do?","options":["Edits the HTML directly","Updates the state, then calls render","Reloads the page"],"answer":1,"explain":"Handlers change the data; render keeps the page in sync with it."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Change text on the page","file":"script.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>The page below has <code>&lt;h1 id=&quot;title&quot;&gt;Spring collection&lt;/h1&gt;</code>. Write two lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A constant <code>title</code> that finds it with <code>document.querySelector('#title')</code>.</li><li>Change its text to <code>'Summer drop is live'</code> using <code>textContent</code>.</li></ol>\n<p>Press <strong>Run it</strong> to see the heading change.</p>","starter":"","html":"<h1 id=\"title\">Spring collection</h1>\n<button id=\"buy\">Add to bag</button>","solution":"const title = document.querySelector('#title');\ntitle.textContent = 'Summer drop is live';","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+title\\s*=\\s*document\\.querySelector\\(","text":"Line 1: <code>const title = document.querySelector(</code>"},{"re":"querySelector\\(\\s*['\"]#title['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"The selector is <code>'#title'</code>: a hash because it is an id."},{"re":"title\\.textContent\\s*=\\s*['\"]Summer drop is live['\"]","text":"Line 2: <code>title.textContent = 'Summer drop is live';</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"React to a click","file":"script.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Make the button say <code>Added</code> when clicked:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const button = document.querySelector('#buy');</code></li><li><code>button.addEventListener('click', () =&gt; {</code></li><li>Inside, indented: <code>button.textContent = 'Added';</code></li><li>Close it: <code>});</code></li></ol>\n<p>Run it, then click the button in the preview.</p>","starter":"","html":"<h1 id=\"title\">Linen Wrap Dress</h1>\n<button id=\"buy\">Add to bag</button>","solution":"const button = document.querySelector('#buy');\nbutton.addEventListener('click', () => {\n  button.textContent = 'Added';\n});","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+button\\s*=\\s*document\\.querySelector\\(\\s*['\"]#buy['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>const button = document.querySelector('#buy');</code>"},{"re":"button\\.addEventListener\\(\\s*['\"]click['\"]\\s*,","text":"Line 2 starts <code>button.addEventListener('click',</code> (capital L in <code>Listener</code>)."},{"re":",\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*\\{","text":"Then an arrow function with no parameters: <code>() =&gt; {</code>"},{"re":"button\\.textContent\\s*=\\s*['\"]Added['\"]","text":"Inside: <code>button.textContent = 'Added';</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Close the function and the call: <code>});</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Turn data into text and back","file":"storage.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p><code>localStorage</code> only stores text, so you convert with JSON. Write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const todos = ['Buy fabric', 'Post photos'];</code></li><li><code>const text = JSON.stringify(todos);</code></li><li><code>const back = JSON.parse(text);</code></li><li><code>console.log(text, back.length);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const todos = ['Buy fabric', 'Post photos'];\nconst text = JSON.stringify(todos);\nconst back = JSON.parse(text);\nconsole.log(text, back.length);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+todos\\s*=\\s*\\[","text":"Line 1 is an array of two strings."},{"re":"JSON\\.stringify\\(\\s*todos\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const text = JSON.stringify(todos);</code> (JSON in capitals)."},{"re":"JSON\\.parse\\(\\s*text\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>const back = JSON.parse(text);</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*text\\s*,\\s*back\\.length\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(text, back.length);</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>stringify</code> turns the array into the text <code>[&quot;Buy fabric&quot;,&quot;Post photos&quot;]</code>, ready to save. <code>parse</code> turns it back into a real array with a <code>length</code> of 2.</p>"}],"text":"What the DOM is When a browser loads HTML, it builds a live tree of objects called the Document Object Model . JavaScript can read and change that tree, and the page updates instantly. Finding elements const title = document.querySelector('h1'); // first match, CSS selector syntax const button = document.querySelector('#add-to-bag'); // by id const cards = document.querySelectorAll('.card'); // all matches (a NodeList) Changing the page title.textContent = 'Summer drop is live'; // change text (safe) button.classList.add('is-loading'); // add a CSS class button.classList.toggle('active'); // add if missing, remove if present button.disabled = true; // set a property title.style.color = '#b4532a'; // inline style (prefer classes) const li = document.createElement('li'); // build a new element li.textContent = 'New item'; document.querySelector('ul').append(li); // put it on the page textContent versus innerHTML textContent treats everything as plain text. innerHTML parses HTML. Never put user input into innerHTML : someone could type a script or an img onerror=... and run code on your page. This attack is called XSS (cross-site scripting). Events An event is something that happens: a click, a key press, a form submit, the page finishing loading. You attach a listener that runs when it happens: button.addEventListener('click', () = { console.log('Added to bag'); }); const search = document.querySelector('#search'); search.addEventListener('input', (event) = { console.log('Typed:', event.target.value); }); const form = document.querySelector('form'); form.addEventListener('submit', (event) = { event.preventDefault(); // stop the browser reloading the page // handle the form with JavaScript instead }); Common events: click , input , change , submit , keydown , DOMContentLoaded . The state, render, action pattern The cleanest way to build interactive UI (and exactly how React thinks): State : one place that holds your data ( let todos = [...] ). Render : a function that rebuilds the UI from the state. Actions : event handlers change the state, then call render. Never store the truth in the HTML itself. The HTML is just a picture of your state. let count = 0; // 1. state const label = document.createElement('p'); const plus = document.createElement('button'); const reset = document.createElement('button'); plus.textContent = '+1'; reset.textContent = 'Reset'; document.body.append(label, plus, reset); function render() { // 2. render label.textContent = `Count: ${count}`; reset.disabled = count === 0; } plus.addEventListener('click', () = { count++; render(); }); // 3. actions reset.addEventListener('click', () = { count = 0; render(); }); render(); Now open the Playground and load the To-do list (DOM) template. It uses the same pattern with an array of objects. Read it line by line, then add a Clear completed button. Saving data in the browser localStorage keeps small amounts of text after the page closes: localStorage.setItem('todos', JSON.stringify(todos)); // save const saved = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('todos') || '[]'); // load It only stores strings (hence JSON.stringify ), it is per-browser, and users can clear it. Fine for preferences and drafts; not a database. (This very site saves your progress this way.)","track":"web"},{"id":"web-modern-js","title":"\"Modern JavaScript: modules, async and fetch\"","level":"intermediate","minutes":30,"summary":"Split code into modules, understand promises and async/await, and load real data from APIs.","html":"<h2 id=\"modules-one-job-per-file\">Modules: one job per file</h2>\n<p>Real projects split code into files. Each file is a <strong>module</strong> that <code>export</code>s what others may use and <code>import</code>s what it needs.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">// money.js\nexport function formatGBP(pence) {\n  return new Intl.NumberFormat('en-GB', { style: 'currency', currency: 'GBP' }).format(pence / 100);\n}\nexport const VAT_RATE = 0.2;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">// main.js\nimport { formatGBP, VAT_RATE } from './money.js';\nconsole.log(formatGBP(6800 * (1 + VAT_RATE)));   // £81.60</code></pre></div>\n<p>In the browser, load the entry file with <code>&lt;script type=&quot;module&quot; src=&quot;main.js&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code>. Modules need to be served over http (Live Server, Vite, or <code>npx serve</code>), not opened as a file.</p>\n<p>A file can also have one <strong>default</strong> export: <code>export default function App() {}</code>, imported without braces: <code>import App from './App.js'</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"why-asynchronous-code-exists\">Why asynchronous code exists</h2>\n<p>Some things take time: network requests, reading files, waiting for a user. JavaScript does not freeze while waiting. Instead, slow operations return a <strong>Promise</strong>: an object that will eventually hold a result (or an error).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const promise = fetch('https://api.example.com/products');   // returns immediately</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"async-await\">async / await</h2>\n<p><code>await</code> pauses <strong>this function</strong> (not the whole page) until the promise settles. It only works inside an <code>async</code> function (or at the top level of a module).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">async function loadWeather() {\n  const url = 'https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast?latitude=51.51&amp;longitude=-0.13&amp;current=temperature_2m';\n  try {\n    const response = await fetch(url);                 // wait for the response headers\n    if (!response.ok) {                                // fetch does NOT throw on 404/500\n      throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\n    }\n    const data = await response.json();                // wait for and parse the body\n    console.log(`London is ${data.current.temperature_2m}°C`);\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error('Could not load the weather:', error.message);\n  } finally {\n    console.log('Request finished (success or failure)');\n  }\n}\n\nloadWeather();</code></pre></div>\n<p>Three things beginners miss:</p>\n<ol><li><code>fetch</code> only rejects on network failure. A 404 or 500 still &quot;succeeds&quot;, so check <code>response.ok</code>.</li><li><code>response.json()</code> is also async, so it needs <code>await</code>.</li><li>Always handle errors. Networks fail all the time, especially on phones.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"running-requests-in-parallel\">Running requests in parallel</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const [products, reviews] = await Promise.all([\n  fetch('/api/products').then((r) =&gt; r.json()),\n  fetch('/api/reviews').then((r) =&gt; r.json()),\n]);</code></pre></div>\n<p>Both requests run at the same time, so this is roughly twice as fast as awaiting them one after another.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sending-data\">Sending data</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">await fetch('/api/newsletter', {\n  method: 'POST',\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n  body: JSON.stringify({ email: 'ada@example.com' }),\n});</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"loading-error-and-empty-states\">Loading, error and empty states</h2>\n<p>Every piece of UI that loads data has at least four states. Professionals design all of them:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Show</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Loading</td><td>A skeleton or spinner</td></tr><tr><td>Error</td><td>A clear message and a retry button</td></tr><tr><td>Empty</td><td>A helpful message (&quot;No orders yet&quot;)</td></tr><tr><td>Success</td><td>The data</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"other-modern-essentials\">Other modern essentials</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const city = user?.address?.city;          // optional chaining: undefined instead of crashing\nconst qty = input.value || 1;              // fallback for any falsy value ('' or 0)\nconst discount = order.discount ?? 0;      // fallback only for null/undefined (keeps 0)\nconst label = isSale ? 'Sale' : 'New';     // ternary: a one-line if/else</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">CORS errors</p><p>&quot;Blocked by CORS policy&quot; means the server you called does not allow browsers on other websites to read its responses. It is a server setting, not a bug in your fetch code. Solutions: use an API that allows it, or call it from your own back end.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"A fetch to a missing URL returns status 404. What does the fetch promise do?","options":["Rejects, so the catch block runs","Resolves normally; you must check response.ok yourself","Never settles"],"answer":1,"explain":"fetch only rejects for network failures. Always check response.ok or response.status."},{"q":"Why use Promise.all for two independent requests?","options":["They run at the same time, so the total wait is shorter","It retries failed requests","It is required for POST requests"],"answer":0,"explain":"Awaiting one after another doubles the waiting time."},{"q":"What is the difference between <code>||</code> and <code>??</code> for defaults?","options":["There is none","?? only falls back for null or undefined, so it keeps valid values like 0 and ''","?? is slower"],"answer":1,"explain":"discount ?? 0 keeps a real discount of 0; discount || 10 would replace 0 with 10."},{"q":"How do you import a named export called formatGBP from money.js?","options":["import formatGBP from './money.js'","import { formatGBP } from './money.js'","require('formatGBP')"],"answer":1,"explain":"Named exports use braces. Default exports do not."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Export a function from a module","file":"money.js","lang":"js","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a module that exports a function <code>formatGBP(pence)</code> which returns a template literal: a pound sign followed by <code>${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"export function formatGBP(pence) {\n  return `£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*export\\s+function\\s+formatGBP\\s*\\(\\s*pence\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>export function formatGBP(pence) {</code>"},{"re":"return\\s+`£\\$\\{","text":"Line 2 returns a template literal: <code>return</code> then a backtick, a pound sign, a dollar sign and an opening curly brace."},{"re":"\\(\\s*pence\\s*\\/\\s*100\\s*\\)\\.toFixed\\(\\s*2\\s*\\)\\s*\\}`","text":"Inside the braces: <code>(pence / 100).toFixed(2)</code>, then a closing brace and backtick."}],"explain":"<p>Another file can now write <code>import { formatGBP } from './money.js';</code>. One job per file keeps big projects understandable.</p>"},{"title":"Fetch real data, handling errors","file":"weather.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Fetch London's current temperature from the free Open-Meteo API. Four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const res = await fetch('https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast?latitude=51.51&amp;longitude=-0.13&amp;current=temperature_2m');</code></li><li>If the response is not OK, throw an error: <code>if (!res.ok) throw new Error(...);</code> where the message is a template literal: a backtick, the text <code>HTTP </code> and <code>${res.status}</code>, then a closing backtick.</li><li><code>const data = await res.json();</code></li><li><code>console.log(data.current.temperature_2m);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const res = await fetch('https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast?latitude=51.51&longitude=-0.13&current=temperature_2m');\nif (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);\nconst data = await res.json();\nconsole.log(data.current.temperature_2m);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+res\\s*=\\s*await\\s+fetch\\(","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const res = await fetch(</code> with the URL in quotes."},{"re":"api\\.open-meteo\\.com/v1/forecast\\?latitude=51\\.51&longitude=-0\\.13&current=temperature_2m","text":"Check the URL carefully: <code>latitude=51.51&amp;longitude=-0.13&amp;current=temperature_2m</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*!\\s*res\\.ok\\s*\\)\\s*throw\\s+new\\s+Error\\(","text":"Line 2: <code>if (!res.ok) throw new Error(...)</code>. The <code>!</code> means &quot;not&quot;."},{"re":"`HTTP \\$\\{res\\.status\\}`","text":"The error message is a template literal: backtick, <code>HTTP </code>, then the status in <code>${ }</code>, then a backtick."},{"re":"const\\s+data\\s*=\\s*await\\s+res\\.json\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>const data = await res.json();</code>"},{"re":"data\\.current\\.temperature_2m","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(data.current.temperature_2m);</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Optional chaining and a default","file":"city.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>This user has no address. Read the city safely, with <code>'Unknown'</code> as the fallback. Three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const user = { name: 'Ada' };</code></li><li><code>const city = user.address?.city ?? 'Unknown';</code></li><li><code>console.log(city);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const user = { name: 'Ada' };\nconst city = user.address?.city ?? 'Unknown';\nconsole.log(city);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+user\\s*=\\s*\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]Ada['\"]\\s*\\}","text":"Line 1: <code>const user = { name: 'Ada' };</code>"},{"re":"user\\.address\\?\\.city","text":"Use <code>?.</code> so a missing address does not crash: <code>user.address?.city</code>"},{"re":"\\?\\?\\s*['\"]Unknown['\"]","text":"Then the fallback with two question marks: <code>?? 'Unknown'</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Without <code>?.</code>, reading <code>.city</code> of <code>undefined</code> throws a TypeError and stops the page. With it, you get <code>undefined</code>, and <code>??</code> swaps in <code>'Unknown'</code>.</p>"}],"text":"Modules: one job per file Real projects split code into files. Each file is a module that export s what others may use and import s what it needs. // money.js export function formatGBP(pence) { return new Intl.NumberFormat('en-GB', { style: 'currency', currency: 'GBP' }).format(pence / 100); } export const VAT_RATE = 0.2; // main.js import { formatGBP, VAT_RATE } from './money.js'; console.log(formatGBP(6800 * (1 + VAT_RATE))); // £81.60 In the browser, load the entry file with script type= module src= main.js /script . Modules need to be served over http (Live Server, Vite, or npx serve ), not opened as a file. A file can also have one default export: export default function App() {} , imported without braces: import App from './App.js' . Why asynchronous code exists Some things take time: network requests, reading files, waiting for a user. JavaScript does not freeze while waiting. Instead, slow operations return a Promise : an object that will eventually hold a result (or an error). const promise = fetch('https://api.example.com/products'); // returns immediately async / await await pauses this function (not the whole page) until the promise settles. It only works inside an async function (or at the top level of a module). async function loadWeather() { const url = 'https://api.open-meteo.com/v1/forecast?latitude=51.51 longitude=-0.13 current=temperature_2m'; try { const response = await fetch(url); // wait for the response headers if (!response.ok) { // fetch does NOT throw on 404/500 throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`); } const data = await response.json(); // wait for and parse the body console.log(`London is ${data.current.temperature_2m}°C`); } catch (error) { console.error('Could not load the weather:', error.message); } finally { console.log('Request finished (success or failure)'); } } loadWeather(); Three things beginners miss: fetch only rejects on network failure. A 404 or 500 still succeeds , so check response.ok . response.json() is also async, so it needs await . Always handle errors. Networks fail all the time, especially on phones. Running requests in parallel const [products, reviews] = await Promise.all([ fetch('/api/products').then((r) = r.json()), fetch('/api/reviews').then((r) = r.json()), ]); Both requests run at the same time, so this is roughly twice as fast as awaiting them one after another. Sending data await fetch('/api/newsletter', { method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, body: JSON.stringify({ email: 'ada@example.com' }), }); Loading, error and empty states Every piece of UI that loads data has at least four states. Professionals design all of them: State Show Loading A skeleton or spinner Error A clear message and a retry button Empty A helpful message ( No orders yet ) Success The data Other modern essentials const city = user?.address?.city; // optional chaining: undefined instead of crashing const qty = input.value || 1; // fallback for any falsy value ('' or 0) const discount = order.discount ?? 0; // fallback only for null/undefined (keeps 0) const label = isSale ? 'Sale' : 'New'; // ternary: a one-line if/else CORS errors Blocked by CORS policy means the server you called does not allow browsers on other websites to read its responses. It is a server setting, not a bug in your fetch code. Solutions: use an API that allows it, or call it from your own back end.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-typescript","title":"TypeScript essentials","level":"intermediate","minutes":30,"summary":"Add types to JavaScript so your editor catches bugs before your users do.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-typescript\">Why TypeScript</h2>\n<p>TypeScript is JavaScript with <strong>type annotations</strong>. It compiles to plain JavaScript, so it runs anywhere JS runs. The point is the editor: it knows the shape of every value, autocompletes properties, and underlines mistakes as you type. Most professional teams use it, and every serious web3 library (viem, wagmi) is built for it.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">function formatGBP(pence: number): string {\n  return `£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`;\n}\n\nformatGBP('6800');   // error: Argument of type 'string' is not assignable to parameter of type 'number'</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"basic-types\">Basic types</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">let name: string = 'Amara';\nlet stock: number = 12;\nlet isLive: boolean = true;\nlet sizes: string[] = ['S', 'M'];\nlet maybe: string | null = null;         // union: one OR the other\nlet status: 'draft' | 'live' | 'archived' = 'draft';   // literal union: only these exact strings</code></pre></div>\n<p>You rarely need annotations on variables. TypeScript <strong>infers</strong> them: <code>let stock = 12</code> is already a <code>number</code>. Annotate function parameters, return types of exported functions, and data shapes.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"describing-objects\">Describing objects</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">type Product = {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n  pricePence: number;\n  sizes: string[];\n  salePricePence?: number;      // ? = optional\n};\n\nconst dress: Product = {\n  id: 'dr-001',\n  name: 'Linen Wrap Dress',\n  pricePence: 6800,\n  sizes: ['S', 'M', 'L'],\n};\n\nfunction displayPrice(p: Product): number {\n  return p.salePricePence ?? p.pricePence;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>interface Product { ... }</code> does almost the same as <code>type Product = { ... }</code>. Pick one style per project; <code>type</code> is more flexible.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"narrowing\">Narrowing</h2>\n<p>When a value could be several types, check it and TypeScript narrows it for you:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">function label(stock: number | 'unknown') {\n  if (stock === 'unknown') return 'Check back soon';\n  return stock &gt; 0 ? `${stock} left` : 'Sold out';   // here stock is definitely a number\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"generics-in-one-minute\">Generics in one minute</h2>\n<p>Generics let a type take a type, like a function takes an argument:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">type ApiResponse&lt;T&gt; = { data: T; error: string | null };\n\nconst res: ApiResponse&lt;Product[]&gt; = { data: [], error: null };</code></pre></div>\n<p>You will mostly use generics written by libraries (<code>useState&lt;Product[]&gt;([])</code>, <code>Promise&lt;User&gt;</code>), not write your own at first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"set-up-a-typescript-project\">Set up a TypeScript project</h2>\n<p>The easiest way is a Vite template, which is pre-configured:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm create vite@latest shop -- --template react-ts\ncd shop\nnpm install\nnpm run dev</code></pre></div>\n<p>Check types for the whole project without building:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npx tsc --noEmit</code></pre></div>\n<p>Keep <code>&quot;strict&quot;: true</code> in <code>tsconfig.json</code>. Strict mode catches the most bugs; turning it off defeats the purpose.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Types vanish at runtime</p><p>TypeScript checks your code, not your data. JSON from an API or a form can be anything, whatever type you claim. Validate untrusted data at the boundary with a library like <strong>Zod</strong>, which gives you both a runtime check and a TypeScript type from one schema.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">import { z } from 'zod';\n\nconst ProductSchema = z.object({\n  id: z.string(),\n  name: z.string().min(1),\n  pricePence: z.number().int().nonnegative(),\n});\ntype Product = z.infer&lt;typeof ProductSchema&gt;;\n\nconst product = ProductSchema.parse(await res.json());   // throws if the shape is wrong</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Avoid any</p><p><code>any</code> switches type checking off for that value, and it spreads. If you truly do not know a type yet, use <code>unknown</code>, which forces you to check before using it.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"What does TypeScript do with your type annotations when the code runs in a browser?","options":["Checks every value at runtime","Removes them: types exist only while you develop and compile","Sends them to the server"],"answer":1,"explain":"Types are erased at compile time. That is why you validate external data with something like Zod."},{"q":"What does <code>salePricePence?: number</code> mean in a type?","options":["It must be a number greater than zero","The property is optional: it may be missing, but if present it is a number","It can be any type"],"answer":1,"explain":"The question mark marks an optional property."},{"q":"Which type allows only the exact strings 'draft', 'live' or 'archived'?","options":["string","'draft' | 'live' | 'archived'","any"],"answer":1,"explain":"A union of string literals restricts values to that set, and the editor autocompletes them."},{"q":"You do not know the shape of some data yet. What is safer than any?","options":["unknown","object","never"],"answer":0,"explain":"unknown forces you to check the value before using it; any silently disables checking."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Add types to a function","file":"money.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a TypeScript function <code>formatGBP</code> that takes <code>pence</code> of type <code>number</code> and returns a <code>string</code>: a template literal with a pound sign and <code>${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function formatGBP(pence: number): string {\n  return `£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+formatGBP\\s*\\(\\s*pence\\s*:\\s*number\\s*\\)","text":"Parameter types go after a colon: <code>function formatGBP(pence: number)</code>"},{"re":"\\)\\s*:\\s*string\\s*\\{","text":"The return type goes after the closing bracket: <code>): string {</code>"},{"re":"return\\s+`£\\$\\{\\s*\\(\\s*pence\\s*\\/\\s*100\\s*\\)\\.toFixed\\(\\s*2\\s*\\)\\s*\\}`","text":"Line 2: <code>return</code> then a template literal: backtick, <code>£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}</code>, backtick."}],"explain":""},{"title":"Describe a product type","file":"product.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a <code>type</code> called <code>Product</code> with four properties: <code>id</code> (string), <code>name</code> (string), <code>pricePence</code> (number), and an <strong>optional</strong> <code>salePricePence</code> (number).</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"type Product = {\n  id: string;\n  name: string;\n  pricePence: number;\n  salePricePence?: number;\n};","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*type\\s+Product\\s*=\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>type Product = {</code>"},{"re":"id\\s*:\\s*string","text":"<code>id: string;</code>"},{"re":"name\\s*:\\s*string","text":"<code>name: string;</code>"},{"re":"pricePence\\s*:\\s*number","text":"<code>pricePence: number;</code>"},{"re":"salePricePence\\?\\s*:\\s*number","text":"Optional properties get a question mark before the colon: <code>salePricePence?: number;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"A union of allowed values","file":"status.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Two lines:</p>\n<ol><li>A type <code>Status</code> that only allows <code>'draft'</code>, <code>'live'</code> or <code>'archived'</code> (separate them with the <code>|</code> symbol).</li><li>A variable (<code>let</code>) <code>status</code> of type <code>Status</code>, set to <code>'draft'</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"type Status = 'draft' | 'live' | 'archived';\nlet status: Status = 'draft';","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"type\\s+Status\\s*=","text":"Line 1 starts <code>type Status =</code>"},{"re":"['\"]draft['\"]\\s*\\|\\s*['\"]live['\"]\\s*\\|\\s*['\"]archived['\"]","text":"List the three strings with a bar between each: <code>'draft' | 'live' | 'archived'</code>"},{"re":"let\\s+status\\s*:\\s*Status\\s*=\\s*['\"]draft['\"]","text":"Line 2: <code>let status: Status = 'draft';</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Now <code>status = 'deleted'</code> is an error in your editor before the code ever runs, and typing <code>status = '</code> offers the three valid options.</p>"}],"text":"Why TypeScript TypeScript is JavaScript with type annotations . It compiles to plain JavaScript, so it runs anywhere JS runs. The point is the editor: it knows the shape of every value, autocompletes properties, and underlines mistakes as you type. Most professional teams use it, and every serious web3 library (viem, wagmi) is built for it. function formatGBP(pence: number): string { return `£${(pence / 100).toFixed(2)}`; } formatGBP('6800'); // error: Argument of type 'string' is not assignable to parameter of type 'number' Basic types let name: string = 'Amara'; let stock: number = 12; let isLive: boolean = true; let sizes: string[] = ['S', 'M']; let maybe: string | null = null; // union: one OR the other let status: 'draft' | 'live' | 'archived' = 'draft'; // literal union: only these exact strings You rarely need annotations on variables. TypeScript infers them: let stock = 12 is already a number . Annotate function parameters, return types of exported functions, and data shapes. Describing objects type Product = { id: string; name: string; pricePence: number; sizes: string[]; salePricePence?: number; // ? = optional }; const dress: Product = { id: 'dr-001', name: 'Linen Wrap Dress', pricePence: 6800, sizes: ['S', 'M', 'L'], }; function displayPrice(p: Product): number { return p.salePricePence ?? p.pricePence; } interface Product { ... } does almost the same as type Product = { ... } . Pick one style per project; type is more flexible. Narrowing When a value could be several types, check it and TypeScript narrows it for you: function label(stock: number | 'unknown') { if (stock === 'unknown') return 'Check back soon'; return stock 0 ? `${stock} left` : 'Sold out'; // here stock is definitely a number } Generics in one minute Generics let a type take a type, like a function takes an argument: type ApiResponse T = { data: T; error: string | null }; const res: ApiResponse Product[] = { data: [], error: null }; You will mostly use generics written by libraries ( useState Product[] ([]) , Promise User ), not write your own at first. Set up a TypeScript project The easiest way is a Vite template, which is pre-configured: npm create vite@latest shop -- --template react-ts cd shop npm install npm run dev Check types for the whole project without building: npx tsc --noEmit Keep strict : true in tsconfig.json . Strict mode catches the most bugs; turning it off defeats the purpose. Types vanish at runtime TypeScript checks your code, not your data. JSON from an API or a form can be anything, whatever type you claim. Validate untrusted data at the boundary with a library like Zod , which gives you both a runtime check and a TypeScript type from one schema. import { z } from 'zod'; const ProductSchema = z.object({ id: z.string(), name: z.string().min(1), pricePence: z.number().int().nonnegative(), }); type Product = z.infer typeof ProductSchema ; const product = ProductSchema.parse(await res.json()); // throws if the shape is wrong Avoid any any switches type checking off for that value, and it spreads. If you truly do not know a type yet, use unknown , which forces you to check before using it.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-react","title":"React fundamentals","level":"intermediate","minutes":35,"summary":"Components, props, state, effects and lists. Think in components and build UIs that stay in sync with data.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-big-idea\">The big idea</h2>\n<p>In the DOM lesson you wrote state, a render function, and actions. React is that pattern, industrialised. You describe <strong>what the UI should look like for a given state</strong>, and React updates the page efficiently whenever the state changes.</p>\n<p>Start a project:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm create vite@latest shop -- --template react-ts\ncd shop &amp;&amp; npm install &amp;&amp; npm run dev</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"components-and-jsx\">Components and JSX</h2>\n<p>A component is a function that returns <strong>JSX</strong>, HTML-like syntax inside JavaScript:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">function ProductCard() {\n  return (\n    &lt;article className=&quot;card&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;Linen Wrap Dress&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;£68.00&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/article&gt;\n  );\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>JSX differences from HTML: <code>className</code> instead of <code>class</code>, <code>htmlFor</code> instead of <code>for</code>, every tag must close (<code>&lt;img /&gt;</code>), and <code>{curly braces}</code> drop in any JavaScript expression.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"props-inputs-to-a-component\">Props: inputs to a component</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">type ProductCardProps = {\n  name: string;\n  pricePence: number;\n  onAdd: () =&gt; void;\n};\n\nfunction ProductCard({ name, pricePence, onAdd }: ProductCardProps) {\n  return (\n    &lt;article className=&quot;card&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;{name}&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;£{(pricePence / 100).toFixed(2)}&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={onAdd}&gt;Add to bag&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/article&gt;\n  );\n}\n\n// used like an HTML tag:\n&lt;ProductCard name=&quot;Camp shirt&quot; pricePence={4200} onAdd={() =&gt; console.log('added')} /&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Props flow <strong>down</strong> from parent to child. Children talk back up by calling functions passed as props (<code>onAdd</code>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"state-data-that-changes\">State: data that changes</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">import { useState } from 'react';\n\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [qty, setQty] = useState(1);\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setQty(qty - 1)} disabled={qty &lt;= 1}&gt;-&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;span&gt;{qty}&lt;/span&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setQty(qty + 1)}&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>useState</code> returns the current value and a setter. Calling the setter re-renders the component with the new value. <strong>Never</strong> change state directly (<code>qty++</code> does nothing visible); always call the setter, and for arrays and objects pass a <strong>new</strong> copy:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">setCart([...cart, newItem]);                                    // add\nsetCart(cart.filter((item) =&gt; item.id !== id));                 // remove\nsetCart(cart.map((item) =&gt; item.id === id ? { ...item, qty: item.qty + 1 } : item)); // update</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"lists-and-keys\">Lists and keys</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">&lt;ul&gt;\n  {products.map((p) =&gt; (\n    &lt;li key={p.id}&gt;{p.name}&lt;/li&gt;\n  ))}\n&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each item needs a stable, unique <code>key</code> (an id, not the array index) so React can track which item is which.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"derived-data-do-not-store-what-you-can-calculate\">Derived data: do not store what you can calculate</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">const [cart, setCart] = useState&lt;CartItem[]&gt;([]);\nconst totalPence = cart.reduce((sum, i) =&gt; sum + i.pricePence * i.qty, 0);   // calculated every render</code></pre></div>\n<p>Storing <code>total</code> in its own state would let it drift out of sync with <code>cart</code>. Calculate it instead.</p>\n<h2 id=\"effects-syncing-with-the-outside-world\">Effects: syncing with the outside world</h2>\n<p><code>useEffect</code> runs code <strong>after</strong> render, for things outside React: fetching data, timers, subscriptions.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\n\nfunction Products() {\n  const [products, setProducts] = useState&lt;Product[]&gt;([]);\n  const [status, setStatus] = useState&lt;'loading' | 'error' | 'ready'&gt;('loading');\n\n  useEffect(() =&gt; {\n    let ignore = false;                       // avoid setting state after unmount\n    fetch('/products.json')\n      .then((r) =&gt; { if (!r.ok) throw new Error(); return r.json(); })\n      .then((data) =&gt; { if (!ignore) { setProducts(data); setStatus('ready'); } })\n      .catch(() =&gt; { if (!ignore) setStatus('error'); });\n    return () =&gt; { ignore = true; };          // cleanup\n  }, []);                                     // [] = run once, when first shown\n\n  if (status === 'loading') return &lt;p&gt;Loading...&lt;/p&gt;;\n  if (status === 'error') return &lt;p&gt;Could not load products.&lt;/p&gt;;\n  return &lt;ul&gt;{products.map((p) =&gt; &lt;li key={p.id}&gt;{p.name}&lt;/li&gt;)}&lt;/ul&gt;;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>The array at the end lists <strong>dependencies</strong>: the effect re-runs when any of them change. In real apps, use a data library like <strong>TanStack Query</strong> for fetching; it handles caching, retries and loading states for you.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Thinking in components</p><p>Before coding a page, sketch it and draw boxes around pieces: <code>Header</code>, <code>ProductGrid</code>, <code>ProductCard</code>, <code>CartDrawer</code>. Decide which component owns each piece of state: the closest common parent of everything that needs it. Then pass data down as props.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"How should you add an item to a cart stored in React state?","options":["cart.push(item)","setCart([...cart, item])","cart = [...cart, item]"],"answer":1,"explain":"State must be replaced with a new value through its setter, or React will not re-render."},{"q":"Why does each item in a mapped list need a key?","options":["For CSS styling","So React can track which item is which between renders","Keys make the list sorted"],"answer":1,"explain":"Use a stable id. Array indexes break when items are reordered or removed."},{"q":"The cart total can be calculated from the cart items. Where should it live?","options":["In its own useState, updated whenever the cart changes","Calculated during render from the cart state","In localStorage"],"answer":1,"explain":"Derived data should be computed, not stored, so it can never go out of sync."},{"q":"What does the empty array in useEffect(() =&gt; {...}, []) mean?","options":["Run the effect once, after the component first renders","Run the effect on every render","Never run the effect"],"answer":0,"explain":"The dependency array controls when the effect re-runs. Empty means no dependencies."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Your first component","file":"ProductCard.tsx","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a component called <code>ProductCard</code> that returns an <code>h2</code> saying <code>Linen Wrap Dress</code>. Three lines: the function line, a <code>return</code> line, and the closing brace.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function ProductCard() {\n  return <h2>Linen Wrap Dress</h2>;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+ProductCard\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function ProductCard() {</code> (components start with a capital letter)."},{"re":"return\\s+<h2>\\s*Linen Wrap Dress\\s*</h2>","text":"Line 2: <code>return &lt;h2&gt;Linen Wrap Dress&lt;/h2&gt;;</code> JSX goes straight after <code>return</code>."},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Close the function with <code>}</code>."}],"explain":""},{"title":"State with useState","file":"Counter.tsx","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a <code>Counter</code> component:</p>\n<ol><li><code>function Counter() {</code></li><li>Create state: <code>const [qty, setQty] = useState(1);</code></li><li>Return a button that adds one when clicked: <code>return &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setQty(qty + 1)}&gt;{qty}&lt;/button&gt;;</code></li><li><code>}</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function Counter() {\n  const [qty, setQty] = useState(1);\n  return <button onClick={() => setQty(qty + 1)}>{qty}</button>;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+Counter\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function Counter() {</code>"},{"re":"const\\s*\\[\\s*qty\\s*,\\s*setQty\\s*\\]\\s*=\\s*useState\\(\\s*1\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const [qty, setQty] = useState(1);</code> (square brackets on the left)."},{"re":"onClick=\\{\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*setQty\\(\\s*qty\\s*\\+\\s*1\\s*\\)\\s*\\}","text":"The click handler: <code>onClick={() =&gt; setQty(qty + 1)}</code>"},{"re":">\\s*\\{\\s*qty\\s*\\}\\s*</button>","text":"Between the tags show the value: <code>{qty}&lt;/button&gt;;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Render a list with keys","file":"ProductList.tsx","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Inside a <code>ul</code>, map over <code>products</code> and return an <code>li</code> for each, with <code>key={p.id}</code> and the product's name. Three lines: <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>, the map line, <code>&lt;/ul&gt;</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<ul>\n  {products.map((p) => <li key={p.id}>{p.name}</li>)}\n</ul>","also":["<ul>\n  {products.map(p => <li key={p.id}>{p.name}</li>)}\n</ul>"],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*<ul>\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code>"},{"re":"\\{\\s*products\\.map\\(","text":"Line 2 starts with a curly brace (JavaScript inside JSX), then <code>products.map(</code>"},{"re":"<li\\s+key=\\{\\s*p\\.id\\s*\\}\\s*>","text":"Each item: <code>&lt;li key={p.id}&gt;</code>"},{"re":"\\{\\s*p\\.name\\s*\\}\\s*</li>\\s*\\)\\s*\\}","text":"Then <code>{p.name}&lt;/li&gt;</code>, close the map with <code>)</code> and the brace with <code>}</code>."},{"re":"^\\s*</ul>\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>&lt;/ul&gt;</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"The big idea In the DOM lesson you wrote state, a render function, and actions. React is that pattern, industrialised. You describe what the UI should look like for a given state , and React updates the page efficiently whenever the state changes. Start a project: npm create vite@latest shop -- --template react-ts cd shop npm install npm run dev Components and JSX A component is a function that returns JSX , HTML-like syntax inside JavaScript: function ProductCard() { return ( article className= card h2 Linen Wrap Dress /h2 p £68.00 /p /article ); } JSX differences from HTML: className instead of class , htmlFor instead of for , every tag must close ( img / ), and {curly braces} drop in any JavaScript expression. Props: inputs to a component type ProductCardProps = { name: string; pricePence: number; onAdd: () = void; }; function ProductCard({ name, pricePence, onAdd }: ProductCardProps) { return ( article className= card h2 {name} /h2 p £{(pricePence / 100).toFixed(2)} /p button onClick={onAdd} Add to bag /button /article ); } // used like an HTML tag: ProductCard name= Camp shirt pricePence={4200} onAdd={() = console.log('added')} / Props flow down from parent to child. Children talk back up by calling functions passed as props ( onAdd ). State: data that changes import { useState } from 'react'; function Counter() { const [qty, setQty] = useState(1); return ( div button onClick={() = setQty(qty - 1)} disabled={qty = 1} - /button span {qty} /span button onClick={() = setQty(qty + 1)} + /button /div ); } useState returns the current value and a setter. Calling the setter re-renders the component with the new value. Never change state directly ( qty++ does nothing visible); always call the setter, and for arrays and objects pass a new copy: setCart([...cart, newItem]); // add setCart(cart.filter((item) = item.id !== id)); // remove setCart(cart.map((item) = item.id === id ? { ...item, qty: item.qty + 1 } : item)); // update Lists and keys ul {products.map((p) = ( li key={p.id} {p.name} /li ))} /ul Each item needs a stable, unique key (an id, not the array index) so React can track which item is which. Derived data: do not store what you can calculate const [cart, setCart] = useState CartItem[] ([]); const totalPence = cart.reduce((sum, i) = sum + i.pricePence * i.qty, 0); // calculated every render Storing total in its own state would let it drift out of sync with cart . Calculate it instead. Effects: syncing with the outside world useEffect runs code after render, for things outside React: fetching data, timers, subscriptions. import { useEffect, useState } from 'react'; function Products() { const [products, setProducts] = useState Product[] ([]); const [status, setStatus] = useState 'loading' | 'error' | 'ready' ('loading'); useEffect(() = { let ignore = false; // avoid setting state after unmount fetch('/products.json') .then((r) = { if (!r.ok) throw new Error(); return r.json(); }) .then((data) = { if (!ignore) { setProducts(data); setStatus('ready'); } }) .catch(() = { if (!ignore) setStatus('error'); }); return () = { ignore = true; }; // cleanup }, []); // [] = run once, when first shown if (status === 'loading') return p Loading... /p ; if (status === 'error') return p Could not load products. /p ; return ul {products.map((p) = li key={p.id} {p.name} /li )} /ul ; } The array at the end lists dependencies : the effect re-runs when any of them change. In real apps, use a data library like TanStack Query for fetching; it handles caching, retries and loading states for you. Thinking in components Before coding a page, sketch it and draw boxes around pieces: Header , ProductGrid , ProductCard , CartDrawer . Decide which component owns each piece of state: the closest common parent of everything that needs it. Then pass data down as props.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-tailwind","title":"Tailwind CSS and design systems","level":"intermediate","minutes":25,"summary":"Style quickly with utility classes, set up brand tokens, and build consistent, reusable components.","html":"<h2 id=\"utility-first-css\">Utility-first CSS</h2>\n<p>Instead of inventing class names and writing CSS in a separate file, Tailwind gives you small single-purpose classes you combine in your markup:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;button class=&quot;rounded-full bg-orange-700 px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white hover:bg-stone-900 focus-visible:outline-2&quot;&gt;\n  Shop the drop\n&lt;/button&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each class does one thing: <code>px-5</code> is horizontal padding, <code>bg-orange-700</code> a background colour, <code>hover:</code> applies a style on hover. You rarely leave your HTML, and unused classes are removed from the final CSS automatically.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"set-it-up-in-a-vite-project\">Set it up in a Vite project</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm install tailwindcss @tailwindcss/vite</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// vite.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vite';\nimport react from '@vitejs/plugin-react';\nimport tailwindcss from '@tailwindcss/vite';\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()],\n});</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">/* src/index.css */\n@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-classes-you-will-use-most\">The classes you will use most</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Spacing</td><td><code>p-4</code>, <code>px-6</code>, <code>mt-8</code>, <code>gap-4</code> (scale: 1 = 0.25rem)</td></tr><tr><td>Layout</td><td><code>flex</code>, <code>grid</code>, <code>grid-cols-2</code>, <code>items-center</code>, <code>justify-between</code></td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td><code>w-full</code>, <code>max-w-screen-lg</code>, <code>h-12</code>, <code>aspect-[4/5]</code></td></tr><tr><td>Type</td><td><code>text-sm</code>, <code>text-4xl</code>, <code>font-bold</code>, <code>tracking-tight</code>, <code>leading-relaxed</code></td></tr><tr><td>Colour</td><td><code>text-stone-900</code>, <code>bg-white</code>, <code>border-stone-200</code></td></tr><tr><td>Effects</td><td><code>rounded-xl</code>, <code>shadow-md</code>, <code>ring-1</code>, <code>transition</code></td></tr><tr><td>States</td><td><code>hover:</code>, <code>focus-visible:</code>, <code>disabled:</code>, <code>dark:</code></td></tr><tr><td>Breakpoints</td><td><code>sm:</code>, <code>md:</code>, <code>lg:</code> (mobile first: <code>md:grid-cols-3</code> applies from medium up)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"a-responsive-product-grid-in-tailwind\">A responsive product grid in Tailwind</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;section class=&quot;grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 p-4 md:grid-cols-3 md:gap-6 lg:grid-cols-4&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;article class=&quot;overflow-hidden rounded-2xl bg-white shadow-sm ring-1 ring-stone-200&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;img src=&quot;dress.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Sage linen wrap dress&quot; class=&quot;aspect-[4/5] w-full object-cover&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;div class=&quot;space-y-1 p-4&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h2 class=&quot;font-semibold text-stone-900&quot;&gt;Linen Wrap Dress&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;p class=&quot;text-stone-600&quot;&gt;£68.00&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/article&gt;\n&lt;/section&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"design-tokens-your-brand-in-one-place\">Design tokens: your brand in one place</h2>\n<p>A <strong>design system</strong> is a set of decisions (colours, type sizes, spacing, radius) used consistently everywhere. In Tailwind v4 you define tokens in CSS with <code>@theme</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;\n\n@theme {\n  --color-brand: #b4532a;\n  --color-ink: #1f1a17;\n  --color-paper: #fbf7f3;\n  --font-display: &quot;Fraunces&quot;, serif;\n  --radius-card: 1.25rem;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Now <code>bg-brand</code>, <code>text-ink</code> and <code>font-display</code> exist as classes. Rebranding a client's site becomes a five-line change.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"components-stop-repetition\">Components stop repetition</h2>\n<p>If you copy the same 15 classes onto every button, make a component instead:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">type ButtonProps = React.ButtonHTMLAttributes&lt;HTMLButtonElement&gt; &amp; { variant?: 'primary' | 'ghost' };\n\nexport function Button({ variant = 'primary', className = '', ...props }: ButtonProps) {\n  const styles = {\n    primary: 'bg-brand text-white hover:bg-ink',\n    ghost: 'bg-transparent text-ink ring-1 ring-ink/20 hover:bg-ink/5',\n  };\n  return &lt;button className={`rounded-full px-5 py-3 font-semibold transition ${styles[variant]} ${className}`} {...props} /&gt;;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>shadcn/ui</strong> takes this idea further: it copies accessible, well-designed components (dialogs, menus, forms) into your project so you own and restyle them.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">What makes a site look professional</p><p>Consistency beats creativity. Use one or two typefaces, a small colour palette, a spacing scale you stick to, generous white space, real high-quality photography, and aligned edges. Most &quot;amateur&quot; sites fail on inconsistency: five font sizes, six greys, random padding.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"In Tailwind, what does md:grid-cols-3 mean?","options":["Always three columns","Three columns from the medium breakpoint upwards; smaller screens use the base class","Three columns only on medium screens and below"],"answer":1,"explain":"Breakpoint prefixes are mobile first: they apply at that width and above."},{"q":"What is a design token?","options":["A crypto token for designers","A named design decision, like a brand colour or radius, reused everywhere","A Figma plug-in"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tokens keep a design consistent and make changes cheap."},{"q":"You keep pasting the same long list of classes onto every button. What should you do?","options":["Keep pasting; it is the Tailwind way","Make a reusable Button component","Switch to inline styles"],"answer":1,"explain":"Components remove duplication while keeping utility classes."}],"exercises":[{"title":"A button made of utilities","file":"Button.html","lang":"html","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a <code>button</code> with these Tailwind classes, in this order: <code>rounded-full bg-orange-700 px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white</code>. The text is <code>Shop the drop</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<button class=\"rounded-full bg-orange-700 px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white\">Shop the drop</button>","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"<button\\s+class=[\"']","text":"Start with <code>&lt;button class=&quot;</code>"},{"re":"rounded-full\\s+bg-orange-700\\s+px-5\\s+py-3\\s+font-semibold\\s+text-white","text":"The classes, separated by spaces: <code>rounded-full bg-orange-700 px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white</code>"},{"re":">\\s*Shop the drop\\s*</button>","text":"Close the quote and tag, then <code>Shop the drop&lt;/button&gt;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Brand tokens with @theme","file":"index.css","lang":"css","run":false,"prompt":"<p>After <code>@import &quot;tailwindcss&quot;;</code>, a <code>@theme</code> block defines your brand tokens. Write the block with two colours: <code>--color-brand: #b4532a;</code> and <code>--color-ink: #1f1a17;</code></p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"@theme {\n  --color-brand: #b4532a;\n  --color-ink: #1f1a17;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*@theme\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>@theme {</code>"},{"re":"--color-brand\\s*:\\s*#b4532a","text":"<code>--color-brand: #b4532a;</code>"},{"re":"--color-ink\\s*:\\s*#1f1a17","text":"<code>--color-ink: #1f1a17;</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Tailwind turns each <code>--color-*</code> token into classes: <code>bg-brand</code>, <code>text-ink</code>, <code>border-brand</code> and so on.</p>"}],"text":"Utility-first CSS Instead of inventing class names and writing CSS in a separate file, Tailwind gives you small single-purpose classes you combine in your markup: button class= rounded-full bg-orange-700 px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white hover:bg-stone-900 focus-visible:outline-2 Shop the drop /button Each class does one thing: px-5 is horizontal padding, bg-orange-700 a background colour, hover: applies a style on hover. You rarely leave your HTML, and unused classes are removed from the final CSS automatically. Set it up in a Vite project npm install tailwindcss @tailwindcss/vite // vite.config.ts import { defineConfig } from 'vite'; import react from '@vitejs/plugin-react'; import tailwindcss from '@tailwindcss/vite'; export default defineConfig({ plugins: [react(), tailwindcss()], }); /* src/index.css */ @import tailwindcss ; The classes you will use most Purpose Examples Spacing p-4 , px-6 , mt-8 , gap-4 (scale: 1 = 0.25rem) Layout flex , grid , grid-cols-2 , items-center , justify-between Size w-full , max-w-screen-lg , h-12 , aspect-[4/5] Type text-sm , text-4xl , font-bold , tracking-tight , leading-relaxed Colour text-stone-900 , bg-white , border-stone-200 Effects rounded-xl , shadow-md , ring-1 , transition States hover: , focus-visible: , disabled: , dark: Breakpoints sm: , md: , lg: (mobile first: md:grid-cols-3 applies from medium up) A responsive product grid in Tailwind section class= grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 p-4 md:grid-cols-3 md:gap-6 lg:grid-cols-4 article class= overflow-hidden rounded-2xl bg-white shadow-sm ring-1 ring-stone-200 img src= dress.jpg alt= Sage linen wrap dress class= aspect-[4/5] w-full object-cover div class= space-y-1 p-4 h2 class= font-semibold text-stone-900 Linen Wrap Dress /h2 p class= text-stone-600 £68.00 /p /div /article /section Design tokens: your brand in one place A design system is a set of decisions (colours, type sizes, spacing, radius) used consistently everywhere. In Tailwind v4 you define tokens in CSS with @theme : @import tailwindcss ; @theme { --color-brand: #b4532a; --color-ink: #1f1a17; --color-paper: #fbf7f3; --font-display: Fraunces , serif; --radius-card: 1.25rem; } Now bg-brand , text-ink and font-display exist as classes. Rebranding a client's site becomes a five-line change. Components stop repetition If you copy the same 15 classes onto every button, make a component instead: type ButtonProps = React.ButtonHTMLAttributes HTMLButtonElement { variant?: 'primary' | 'ghost' }; export function Button({ variant = 'primary', className = '', ...props }: ButtonProps) { const styles = { primary: 'bg-brand text-white hover:bg-ink', ghost: 'bg-transparent text-ink ring-1 ring-ink/20 hover:bg-ink/5', }; return button className={`rounded-full px-5 py-3 font-semibold transition ${styles[variant]} ${className}`} {...props} / ; } shadcn/ui takes this idea further: it copies accessible, well-designed components (dialogs, menus, forms) into your project so you own and restyle them. What makes a site look professional Consistency beats creativity. Use one or two typefaces, a small colour palette, a spacing scale you stick to, generous white space, real high-quality photography, and aligned edges. Most amateur sites fail on inconsistency: five font sizes, six greys, random padding.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-a11y","title":"Accessibility and forms","level":"intermediate","minutes":25,"summary":"Build sites everyone can use (including with a keyboard or screen reader) and forms that convert.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-accessibility-matters\">Why accessibility matters</h2>\n<p>Around one in five people has a disability. Many more have a temporary or situational one: a broken arm, bright sunlight, a slow connection, holding a baby. Accessible sites are easier for everyone, rank better in search, and are a legal requirement for many businesses (the UK Equality Act, the European Accessibility Act). It is also what separates professional work from amateur work.</p>\n<p>The standard is <strong>WCAG</strong> (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines). Aim for level AA.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-essentials-checklist\">The essentials checklist</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Use semantic HTML.</strong> <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, headings in order. This solves most problems for free.</li><li><strong>Every image has alt text</strong> (or <code>alt=&quot;&quot;</code> if decorative).</li><li><strong>Every input has a label.</strong></li><li><strong>Everything works with a keyboard.</strong> Press <kbd>Tab</kbd> through your site: can you reach and use every control? Can you see where focus is?</li><li><strong>Colour contrast</strong>: body text needs at least 4.5:1 against its background. Light grey on white usually fails.</li><li><strong>Do not rely on colour alone.</strong> &quot;Fields in red are required&quot; fails colour-blind users; add text or an icon.</li><li><strong>Respect user settings</strong>: zoom to 200% without breaking, and honour <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"visible-focus\">Visible focus</h2>\n<p>Never remove focus outlines without replacing them:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"css\"><pre><code class=\"language-css\">/* bad: keyboard users get lost */\nbutton:focus { outline: none; }\n\n/* good: a clear ring for keyboard users only */\n:focus-visible {\n  outline: 3px solid #2747e0;\n  outline-offset: 2px;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"a-form-done-properly\">A form done properly</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;form novalidate&gt;\n  &lt;div class=&quot;field&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;label for=&quot;email&quot;&gt;Email address&lt;/label&gt;\n    &lt;input id=&quot;email&quot; name=&quot;email&quot; type=&quot;email&quot; autocomplete=&quot;email&quot; required aria-describedby=&quot;email-hint email-error&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;p id=&quot;email-hint&quot; class=&quot;hint&quot;&gt;We will send your order confirmation here.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p id=&quot;email-error&quot; class=&quot;error&quot; hidden&gt;Enter an email address like name@example.com&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Continue to payment&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;\n&lt;style&gt;\n  body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; padding: 1rem; max-width: 420px; }\n  .field { display: grid; gap: .35rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }\n  input { padding: .7rem; font-size: 1rem; border: 1px solid #8a8a8a; border-radius: 8px; }\n  input[aria-invalid=&quot;true&quot;] { border-color: #b42318; }\n  .hint { margin: 0; color: #555; font-size: .9rem; }\n  .error { margin: 0; color: #b42318; font-size: .9rem; }\n  button { padding: .8rem 1.2rem; font-size: 1rem; border: 0; border-radius: 8px; background: #1f1a17; color: #fff; }\n&lt;/style&gt;\n&lt;script&gt;\n  const form = document.querySelector('form');\n  const email = document.querySelector('#email');\n  const error = document.querySelector('#email-error');\n  form.addEventListener('submit', (e) =&gt; {\n    e.preventDefault();\n    const ok = email.validity.valid;\n    email.setAttribute('aria-invalid', String(!ok));\n    error.hidden = ok;\n    if (!ok) email.focus();\n    else console.log('Valid! Send to the server here.');\n  });\n&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<p>What makes it good:</p>\n<ul><li>The <strong>label</strong> is always visible (placeholders disappear when you type, so they are not labels).</li><li><code>type=&quot;email&quot;</code> and <code>autocomplete=&quot;email&quot;</code> give the right mobile keyboard and let the browser autofill.</li><li>The error says <strong>how to fix it</strong>, sits next to the field, is linked with <code>aria-describedby</code>, and focus moves to the problem.</li><li>Validation runs on submit, not on every keystroke while the user is still typing.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"aria-the-last-resort\">ARIA: the last resort</h2>\n<p>ARIA attributes (<code>aria-label</code>, <code>aria-expanded</code>, <code>role</code>) add meaning for assistive technology when HTML alone cannot. The first rule of ARIA: <strong>if a native element does the job, use it</strong>. A <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> is always better than <code>&lt;div role=&quot;button&quot; tabindex=&quot;0&quot;&gt;</code> with hand-written key handling.</p>\n<p>Useful ones:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;button aria-label=&quot;Close menu&quot;&gt;x&lt;/button&gt;                     &lt;!-- icon-only buttons need a name --&gt;\n&lt;button aria-expanded=&quot;false&quot; aria-controls=&quot;menu&quot;&gt;Menu&lt;/button&gt;  &lt;!-- toggles announce their state --&gt;\n&lt;p role=&quot;status&quot;&gt;Added to bag&lt;/p&gt;                              &lt;!-- announces updates politely --&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Test it yourself in five minutes</p><p>Unplug your mouse and use your site with <kbd>Tab</kbd>, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd>, <kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Space</kbd>. Then run Lighthouse's Accessibility audit in Chrome DevTools. Then try your phone's screen reader (VoiceOver on iPhone, TalkBack on Android) on one page.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Why is a placeholder not a replacement for a label?","options":["Placeholders are not supported on phones","It disappears as soon as the user types, and is often low contrast","Labels load faster"],"answer":1,"explain":"Users forget what a field was for once they start typing. Keep a visible label."},{"q":"Minimum contrast ratio for normal body text at WCAG AA?","options":["2:1","4.5:1","10:1"],"answer":1,"explain":"4.5:1 for normal text, 3:1 for large text."},{"q":"You need a clickable control that opens a menu. Best choice?","options":["A button element with aria-expanded","A div with an onclick handler","A link with href=&quot;#&quot;"],"answer":0,"explain":"Native buttons are focusable and keyboard-operable for free. aria-expanded announces open or closed."},{"q":"What is the first rule of ARIA?","options":["Add ARIA to every element","Use a native HTML element instead whenever one exists","Only use aria-label"],"answer":1,"explain":"Native elements come with keyboard support and semantics built in."}],"exercises":[{"title":"A visible focus ring","file":"styles.css","lang":"css","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write a <code>:focus-visible</code> rule with <code>outline: 3px solid #2747e0;</code> and <code>outline-offset: 2px;</code>. Run it, click inside the preview and press Tab to see your focus ring.</p>","starter":"","html":"<p style=\"font-family:system-ui\">Click in the preview, then press Tab to move focus:</p>\n<a href=\"#\">A link</a> <button>A button</button>","solution":":focus-visible {\n  outline: 3px solid #2747e0;\n  outline-offset: 2px;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*:focus-visible\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>:focus-visible {</code> (starts with a colon)."},{"re":"outline\\s*:\\s*3px\\s+solid\\s+#2747e0","text":"<code>outline: 3px solid #2747e0;</code>"},{"re":"outline-offset\\s*:\\s*2px","text":"<code>outline-offset: 2px;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Name icon buttons for screen readers","file":"menu.html","lang":"html","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Two buttons:</p>\n<ol><li>A close button whose visible text is just <code>x</code>, with <code>aria-label=&quot;Close menu&quot;</code>.</li><li>A menu toggle with <code>aria-expanded=&quot;false&quot;</code> and <code>aria-controls=&quot;menu&quot;</code>, and the text <code>Menu</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<button aria-label=\"Close menu\">x</button>\n<button aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"menu\">Menu</button>","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"<button\\s+aria-label=[\"']Close menu[\"']\\s*>\\s*x\\s*</button>","text":"Line 1: <code>&lt;button aria-label=&quot;Close menu&quot;&gt;x&lt;/button&gt;</code>"},{"re":"aria-expanded=[\"']false[\"']","text":"Line 2 needs <code>aria-expanded=&quot;false&quot;</code>"},{"re":"aria-controls=[\"']menu[\"']\\s*>\\s*Menu\\s*</button>","text":"then <code>aria-controls=&quot;menu&quot;&gt;Menu&lt;/button&gt;</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Why accessibility matters Around one in five people has a disability. Many more have a temporary or situational one: a broken arm, bright sunlight, a slow connection, holding a baby. Accessible sites are easier for everyone, rank better in search, and are a legal requirement for many businesses (the UK Equality Act, the European Accessibility Act). It is also what separates professional work from amateur work. The standard is WCAG (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines). Aim for level AA. The essentials checklist Use semantic HTML. button , a , nav , main , headings in order. This solves most problems for free. Every image has alt text (or alt= if decorative). Every input has a label. Everything works with a keyboard. Press Tab through your site: can you reach and use every control? Can you see where focus is? Colour contrast : body text needs at least 4.5:1 against its background. Light grey on white usually fails. Do not rely on colour alone. Fields in red are required fails colour-blind users; add text or an icon. Respect user settings : zoom to 200% without breaking, and honour prefers-reduced-motion . Visible focus Never remove focus outlines without replacing them: /* bad: keyboard users get lost */ button:focus { outline: none; } /* good: a clear ring for keyboard users only */ :focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #2747e0; outline-offset: 2px; } A form done properly form novalidate div class= field label for= email Email address /label input id= email name= email type= email autocomplete= email required aria-describedby= email-hint email-error p id= email-hint class= hint We will send your order confirmation here. /p p id= email-error class= error hidden Enter an email address like name@example.com /p /div button type= submit Continue to payment /button /form style body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; padding: 1rem; max-width: 420px; } .field { display: grid; gap: .35rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; } input { padding: .7rem; font-size: 1rem; border: 1px solid #8a8a8a; border-radius: 8px; } input[aria-invalid= true ] { border-color: #b42318; } .hint { margin: 0; color: #555; font-size: .9rem; } .error { margin: 0; color: #b42318; font-size: .9rem; } button { padding: .8rem 1.2rem; font-size: 1rem; border: 0; border-radius: 8px; background: #1f1a17; color: #fff; } /style script const form = document.querySelector('form'); const email = document.querySelector('#email'); const error = document.querySelector('#email-error'); form.addEventListener('submit', (e) = { e.preventDefault(); const ok = email.validity.valid; email.setAttribute('aria-invalid', String(!ok)); error.hidden = ok; if (!ok) email.focus(); else console.log('Valid! Send to the server here.'); }); /script What makes it good: The label is always visible (placeholders disappear when you type, so they are not labels). type= email and autocomplete= email give the right mobile keyboard and let the browser autofill. The error says how to fix it , sits next to the field, is linked with aria-describedby , and focus moves to the problem. Validation runs on submit, not on every keystroke while the user is still typing. ARIA: the last resort ARIA attributes ( aria-label , aria-expanded , role ) add meaning for assistive technology when HTML alone cannot. The first rule of ARIA: if a native element does the job, use it . A button is always better than div role= button tabindex= 0 with hand-written key handling. Useful ones: button aria-label= Close menu x /button !-- icon-only buttons need a name -- button aria-expanded= false aria-controls= menu Menu /button !-- toggles announce their state -- p role= status Added to bag /p !-- announces updates politely -- Test it yourself in five minutes Unplug your mouse and use your site with Tab , Shift + Tab , Enter and Space . Then run Lighthouse's Accessibility audit in Chrome DevTools. Then try your phone's screen reader (VoiceOver on iPhone, TalkBack on Android) on one page.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-deploy","title":"Deploying your site to the internet","level":"intermediate","minutes":25,"summary":"Get a real URL with GitHub Pages, Vercel or Netlify, connect a custom domain, and ship with confidence.","html":"<h2 id=\"static-versus-dynamic-sites\">Static versus dynamic sites</h2>\n<ul><li>A <strong>static</strong> site is just files (HTML, CSS, JS, images). Any file host can serve it, usually free: GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, Cloudflare Pages.</li><li>A <strong>dynamic</strong> site runs server code per request (a Next.js app with an API, a database). It needs a platform that runs code: Vercel, Netlify Functions, Railway, Render, Fly.io, or your own server.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"option-1-github-pages-plain-html-css-js\">Option 1: GitHub Pages (plain HTML/CSS/JS)</h2>\n<ol><li>Push your project to GitHub.</li><li>Open the repository's <strong>Settings, Pages</strong>.</li><li>Choose <strong>Deploy from a branch</strong>, branch <code>main</code>, folder <code>/ (root)</code>.</li><li>Wait a minute. Your site is at <code>https://&lt;username&gt;.github.io/&lt;repo&gt;/</code>.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"option-2-vercel-or-netlify-anything-with-a-build-step\">Option 2: Vercel or Netlify (anything with a build step)</h2>\n<p>Connect your GitHub repo in their dashboard. They detect the framework (Vite, Next.js, Astro), run the build, and give you a URL. Every push to <code>main</code> deploys automatically, and every pull request gets its own <strong>preview URL</strong> you can share with a client before going live.</p>\n<p>From the terminal:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm install -g vercel\nvercel          # first deploy creates a preview\nvercel --prod   # deploy to production</code></pre></div>\n<p>Build settings for a Vite project: build command <code>npm run build</code>, output folder <code>dist</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"custom-domains\">Custom domains</h2>\n<ol><li>Buy a domain from a registrar (Cloudflare, Namecheap, Porkbun, Google-partner registrars).</li><li>In your host's dashboard, add the domain. It will tell you which <strong>DNS records</strong> to create.</li><li>In the registrar's DNS settings, add them. Typically:</li></ol>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Name</th><th>Value</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>A</code></td><td><code>@</code></td><td>An IP address from your host</td><td>Points the root domain (brand.com)</td></tr><tr><td><code>CNAME</code></td><td><code>www</code></td><td>A hostname from your host</td><td>Points www.brand.com</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol><li>Wait for DNS to propagate (minutes, occasionally hours). HTTPS certificates are issued automatically.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"environment-variables-in-production\">Environment variables in production</h2>\n<p>Secrets (API keys, database URLs) are set in the host's dashboard, never committed. In Vite, only variables starting with <code>VITE_</code> are exposed to the browser, and anything exposed to the browser is public. Keep real secrets server-side.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pre-launch-checklist\">Pre-launch checklist</h2>\n<ul><li>Title and meta description on every page; a share image (<code>og:image</code>) so links look good on social media.</li><li>A favicon.</li><li>Works at 375px wide, on a real phone.</li><li>Lighthouse scores checked (performance, accessibility, best practices, SEO).</li><li>Forms actually send somewhere, with success and error messages.</li><li>A 404 page.</li><li>Analytics installed (Plausible, Vercel Analytics or Google Analytics), with a cookie notice if required.</li><li>Images compressed (WebP or AVIF) and sized correctly.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Preview deployments sell</p><p>For client work, send a preview URL rather than screenshots. Clients click around on their own phones, give better feedback, and see you as a professional. Always deploy a preview of every change before it goes to production.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which hosting option can serve a plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript site for free with no build step?","options":["GitHub Pages","Only a paid virtual server","A database host"],"answer":0,"explain":"Static sites are just files. GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel and Cloudflare Pages all host them free."},{"q":"You want www.brandname.com to point at your Vercel site. What do you change?","options":["The HTML title","DNS records at your domain registrar","Your package.json"],"answer":1,"explain":"DNS maps names to servers. The host tells you which records to add."},{"q":"A Vite variable named VITE_STRIPE_SECRET is used in front-end code. What is the problem?","options":["Nothing, VITE_ variables are encrypted","Anything exposed to the browser is public, so the secret key is leaked","Vite will refuse to build"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only publishable keys belong in front-end code. Secret keys stay on the server."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Deploy with the Vercel CLI","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Three commands: install the Vercel CLI globally, make a preview deployment, then deploy to production.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"npm install -g vercel\nvercel\nvercel --prod","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*npm\\s+install\\s+-g\\s+vercel\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>npm install -g vercel</code> (<code>-g</code> = global)."},{"re":"^\\s*vercel\\s*$","text":"Line 2: just <code>vercel</code> makes a preview."},{"re":"^\\s*vercel\\s+--prod\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>vercel --prod</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"SEO and sharing tags","file":"head.html","lang":"html","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write three lines for a page's <code>head</code>:</p>\n<ol><li>A <code>title</code>: <code>Amara Studio | Handmade jewellery</code></li><li>A meta description: <code>name=&quot;description&quot;</code> and <code>content=&quot;Handmade brass jewellery from London.&quot;</code></li><li>A share image: <code>property=&quot;og:image&quot;</code> and <code>content=&quot;https://amarastudio.com/og.jpg&quot;</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<title>Amara Studio | Handmade jewellery</title>\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Handmade brass jewellery from London.\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://amarastudio.com/og.jpg\">","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"<title>\\s*Amara Studio \\| Handmade jewellery\\s*</title>","text":"Line 1: <code>&lt;title&gt;Amara Studio | Handmade jewellery&lt;/title&gt;</code>"},{"re":"<meta\\s+name=[\"']description[\"']\\s+content=[\"']Handmade brass jewellery from London\\.[\"']","text":"Line 2: <code>&lt;meta name=&quot;description&quot; content=&quot;Handmade brass jewellery from London.&quot;&gt;</code>"},{"re":"<meta\\s+property=[\"']og:image[\"']\\s+content=[\"']https://amarastudio\\.com/og\\.jpg[\"']","text":"Line 3: <code>&lt;meta property=&quot;og:image&quot; content=&quot;https://amarastudio.com/og.jpg&quot;&gt;</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Static versus dynamic sites A static site is just files (HTML, CSS, JS, images). Any file host can serve it, usually free: GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, Cloudflare Pages. A dynamic site runs server code per request (a Next.js app with an API, a database). It needs a platform that runs code: Vercel, Netlify Functions, Railway, Render, Fly.io, or your own server. Option 1: GitHub Pages (plain HTML/CSS/JS) Push your project to GitHub. Open the repository's Settings, Pages . Choose Deploy from a branch , branch main , folder / (root) . Wait a minute. Your site is at https:// username .github.io/ repo / . Option 2: Vercel or Netlify (anything with a build step) Connect your GitHub repo in their dashboard. They detect the framework (Vite, Next.js, Astro), run the build, and give you a URL. Every push to main deploys automatically, and every pull request gets its own preview URL you can share with a client before going live. From the terminal: npm install -g vercel vercel # first deploy creates a preview vercel --prod # deploy to production Build settings for a Vite project: build command npm run build , output folder dist . Custom domains Buy a domain from a registrar (Cloudflare, Namecheap, Porkbun, Google-partner registrars). In your host's dashboard, add the domain. It will tell you which DNS records to create. In the registrar's DNS settings, add them. Typically: Record Name Value Purpose A @ An IP address from your host Points the root domain (brand.com) CNAME www A hostname from your host Points www.brand.com Wait for DNS to propagate (minutes, occasionally hours). HTTPS certificates are issued automatically. Environment variables in production Secrets (API keys, database URLs) are set in the host's dashboard, never committed. In Vite, only variables starting with VITE_ are exposed to the browser, and anything exposed to the browser is public. Keep real secrets server-side. Pre-launch checklist Title and meta description on every page; a share image ( og:image ) so links look good on social media. A favicon. Works at 375px wide, on a real phone. Lighthouse scores checked (performance, accessibility, best practices, SEO). Forms actually send somewhere, with success and error messages. A 404 page. Analytics installed (Plausible, Vercel Analytics or Google Analytics), with a cookie notice if required. Images compressed (WebP or AVIF) and sized correctly. Preview deployments sell For client work, send a preview URL rather than screenshots. Clients click around on their own phones, give better feedback, and see you as a professional. Always deploy a preview of every change before it goes to production.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-nextjs","title":"Full-stack React with Next.js","level":"advanced","minutes":35,"summary":"File-based routing, server and client components, data fetching, server actions and route handlers.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-next-js-adds-to-react\">What Next.js adds to React</h2>\n<p>React draws UI. Next.js turns it into a full application framework:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>File-based routing</strong>: a folder is a URL.</li><li><strong>Server rendering</strong>: pages arrive as HTML, which is fast and great for SEO.</li><li><strong>Server components</strong>: components that run only on the server, so they can read databases and secrets directly.</li><li><strong>API endpoints and server actions</strong> in the same project.</li><li>Image, font and script optimisation built in.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npx create-next-app@latest shop\ncd shop &amp;&amp; npm run dev</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-app-router-folders-are-routes\">The App Router: folders are routes</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">app/\n  layout.tsx              -&gt; wraps every page (header, footer, fonts)\n  page.tsx                -&gt; /\n  products/\n    page.tsx              -&gt; /products\n    [slug]/\n      page.tsx            -&gt; /products/linen-wrap-dress  (dynamic segment)\n  api/\n    newsletter/\n      route.ts            -&gt; POST /api/newsletter</code></pre></div>\n<p>Special files: <code>loading.tsx</code> (shown while a route loads), <code>error.tsx</code> (shown when it throws), <code>not-found.tsx</code> (404).</p>\n<h2 id=\"server-components-by-default\">Server components by default</h2>\n<p>In the App Router, components run <strong>on the server</strong> unless you opt out. That means you can fetch data with plain <code>await</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">// app/products/page.tsx (a server component)\nimport { db } from '@/lib/db';\n\nexport default async function ProductsPage() {\n  const products = await db.product.findMany({ where: { live: true } });   // runs on the server only\n  return (\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      {products.map((p) =&gt; &lt;li key={p.id}&gt;{p.name}&lt;/li&gt;)}\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  );\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>The database code and credentials never reach the browser.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"client-components-for-interactivity\">Client components for interactivity</h2>\n<p>Anything that uses state, effects or browser events needs <code>'use client'</code> at the top of its file:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">'use client';\nimport { useState } from 'react';\n\nexport function AddToBag({ productId }: { productId: string }) {\n  const [added, setAdded] = useState(false);\n  return &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; setAdded(true)}&gt;{added ? 'Added' : 'Add to bag'}&lt;/button&gt;;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> keep pages and data loading on the server; push <code>'use client'</code> down to the small interactive leaves (buttons, forms, carousels). Less JavaScript ships to the browser, so pages are faster.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"dynamic-routes\">Dynamic routes</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">// app/products/[slug]/page.tsx\nimport { notFound } from 'next/navigation';\n\nexport default async function ProductPage({ params }: { params: Promise&lt;{ slug: string }&gt; }) {\n  const { slug } = await params;\n  const product = await getProductBySlug(slug);\n  if (!product) notFound();\n  return &lt;h1&gt;{product.name}&lt;/h1&gt;;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"server-actions-forms-without-writing-an-api\">Server actions: forms without writing an API</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">// app/newsletter/page.tsx\nimport { z } from 'zod';\n\nasync function subscribe(formData: FormData) {\n  'use server';                                               // this function runs on the server\n  const email = z.string().email().parse(formData.get('email'));\n  await db.subscriber.create({ data: { email } });\n}\n\nexport default function Newsletter() {\n  return (\n    &lt;form action={subscribe}&gt;\n      &lt;label htmlFor=&quot;email&quot;&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;\n      &lt;input id=&quot;email&quot; name=&quot;email&quot; type=&quot;email&quot; required /&gt;\n      &lt;button&gt;Subscribe&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/form&gt;\n  );\n}</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Server actions are public endpoints</p><p>Anyone can call a server action directly, not just your form. Always validate the input and check the user is allowed to do the action, exactly as you would for an API route.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"route-handlers-a-classic-api\">Route handlers: a classic API</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// app/api/products/route.ts\nimport { NextResponse } from 'next/server';\n\nexport async function GET() {\n  const products = await db.product.findMany();\n  return NextResponse.json(products);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use these when something other than your own pages needs the data: a mobile app, a webhook from Stripe, a partner.</p>\n<h2 id=\"seo-metadata\">SEO metadata</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">export const metadata = {\n  title: 'Linen Wrap Dress | Amara Studio',\n  description: 'Hand-finished linen wrap dress, made in London.',\n  openGraph: { images: ['/og/wrap-dress.jpg'] },\n};</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"caching-in-one-paragraph\">Caching, in one paragraph</h2>\n<p>Next.js caches aggressively for speed, which surprises people (&quot;why is my page showing old data?&quot;). Learn the current caching rules in the official docs for your version, and know how to opt a route into fresh data per request, or revalidate after a change with <code>revalidatePath('/products')</code> inside a server action.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"In the App Router, which file creates the page at /about?","options":["pages/about.html","app/about/page.tsx","app/about.tsx"],"answer":1,"explain":"Folders define the URL; page.tsx makes it a page."},{"q":"A component uses useState. What does it need?","options":["'use client' at the top of its file","'use server' at the top of its file","Nothing, all components can use state"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hooks like useState run in the browser, so the component must be a client component."},{"q":"Why is it safe to query the database inside a server component?","options":["Next.js encrypts the query","Server components only run on the server, so the code and credentials never reach the browser","Databases are public anyway"],"answer":1,"explain":"That is the main power of server components."},{"q":"What must every server action do?","options":["Return JSX","Validate its input and check permissions, because anyone can call it","Use useEffect"],"answer":1,"explain":"Server actions are HTTP endpoints under the hood. Treat them as untrusted entry points."}],"exercises":[{"title":"A server component that loads data","file":"app/products/page.tsx","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a default-exported <strong>async</strong> page component <code>ProductsPage</code> that awaits <code>getProducts()</code> and returns an <code>h1</code> showing how many products there are: <code>{products.length} products</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"export default async function ProductsPage() {\n  const products = await getProducts();\n  return <h1>{products.length} products</h1>;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"export\\s+default\\s+async\\s+function\\s+ProductsPage\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>export default async function ProductsPage() {</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+products\\s*=\\s*await\\s+getProducts\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const products = await getProducts();</code>"},{"re":"return\\s+<h1>\\s*\\{\\s*products\\.length\\s*\\}\\s*products\\s*</h1>","text":"Line 3: <code>return &lt;h1&gt;{products.length} products&lt;/h1&gt;;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"A client component","file":"AddToBag.tsx","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a client component (it needs state):</p>\n<ol><li><code>'use client';</code></li><li><code>import { useState } from 'react';</code></li><li><code>export function AddToBag() {</code></li><li><code>const [added, setAdded] = useState(false);</code></li><li>Return a button whose click sets <code>added</code> to <code>true</code> and whose text is <code>{added ? 'Added' : 'Add to bag'}</code>.</li><li><code>}</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"'use client';\nimport { useState } from 'react';\nexport function AddToBag() {\n  const [added, setAdded] = useState(false);\n  return <button onClick={() => setAdded(true)}>{added ? 'Added' : 'Add to bag'}</button>;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*['\"]use client['\"]","text":"Line 1 is the directive, in quotes: <code>'use client';</code>"},{"re":"import\\s*\\{\\s*useState\\s*\\}\\s*from\\s*['\"]react['\"]","text":"Line 2: <code>import { useState } from 'react';</code>"},{"re":"export\\s+function\\s+AddToBag\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 3: <code>export function AddToBag() {</code>"},{"re":"const\\s*\\[\\s*added\\s*,\\s*setAdded\\s*\\]\\s*=\\s*useState\\(\\s*false\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>const [added, setAdded] = useState(false);</code>"},{"re":"onClick=\\{\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*setAdded\\(\\s*true\\s*\\)\\s*\\}","text":"The button's handler: <code>onClick={() =&gt; setAdded(true)}</code>"},{"re":"\\{\\s*added\\s*\\?\\s*['\"]Added['\"]\\s*:\\s*['\"]Add to bag['\"]\\s*\\}","text":"The text uses a ternary: <code>{added ? 'Added' : 'Add to bag'}</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"What Next.js adds to React React draws UI. Next.js turns it into a full application framework: File-based routing : a folder is a URL. Server rendering : pages arrive as HTML, which is fast and great for SEO. Server components : components that run only on the server, so they can read databases and secrets directly. API endpoints and server actions in the same project. Image, font and script optimisation built in. npx create-next-app@latest shop cd shop npm run dev The App Router: folders are routes app/ layout.tsx - wraps every page (header, footer, fonts) page.tsx - / products/ page.tsx - /products [slug]/ page.tsx - /products/linen-wrap-dress (dynamic segment) api/ newsletter/ route.ts - POST /api/newsletter Special files: loading.tsx (shown while a route loads), error.tsx (shown when it throws), not-found.tsx (404). Server components by default In the App Router, components run on the server unless you opt out. That means you can fetch data with plain await : // app/products/page.tsx (a server component) import { db } from '@/lib/db'; export default async function ProductsPage() { const products = await db.product.findMany({ where: { live: true } }); // runs on the server only return ( ul {products.map((p) = li key={p.id} {p.name} /li )} /ul ); } The database code and credentials never reach the browser. Client components for interactivity Anything that uses state, effects or browser events needs 'use client' at the top of its file: 'use client'; import { useState } from 'react'; export function AddToBag({ productId }: { productId: string }) { const [added, setAdded] = useState(false); return button onClick={() = setAdded(true)} {added ? 'Added' : 'Add to bag'} /button ; } Rule of thumb: keep pages and data loading on the server; push 'use client' down to the small interactive leaves (buttons, forms, carousels). Less JavaScript ships to the browser, so pages are faster. Dynamic routes // app/products/[slug]/page.tsx import { notFound } from 'next/navigation'; export default async function ProductPage({ params }: { params: Promise { slug: string } }) { const { slug } = await params; const product = await getProductBySlug(slug); if (!product) notFound(); return h1 {product.name} /h1 ; } Server actions: forms without writing an API // app/newsletter/page.tsx import { z } from 'zod'; async function subscribe(formData: FormData) { 'use server'; // this function runs on the server const email = z.string().email().parse(formData.get('email')); await db.subscriber.create({ data: { email } }); } export default function Newsletter() { return ( form action={subscribe} label htmlFor= email Email /label input id= email name= email type= email required / button Subscribe /button /form ); } Server actions are public endpoints Anyone can call a server action directly, not just your form. Always validate the input and check the user is allowed to do the action, exactly as you would for an API route. Route handlers: a classic API // app/api/products/route.ts import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'; export async function GET() { const products = await db.product.findMany(); return NextResponse.json(products); } Use these when something other than your own pages needs the data: a mobile app, a webhook from Stripe, a partner. SEO metadata export const metadata = { title: 'Linen Wrap Dress | Amara Studio', description: 'Hand-finished linen wrap dress, made in London.', openGraph: { images: ['/og/wrap-dress.jpg'] }, }; Caching, in one paragraph Next.js caches aggressively for speed, which surprises people ( why is my page showing old data? ). Learn the current caching rules in the official docs for your version, and know how to opt a route into fresh data per request, or revalidate after a change with revalidatePath('/products') inside a server action.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-databases","title":"Databases and back ends","level":"advanced","minutes":35,"summary":"Model data in tables, write SQL, use Postgres with an ORM, and design clean REST APIs.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-a-database\">Why a database</h2>\n<p><code>localStorage</code> lives in one browser. A <strong>database</strong> lives on a server, is shared by all users, survives crashes, and can answer questions like &quot;the ten best-selling dresses this month&quot; in milliseconds.</p>\n<p><strong>PostgreSQL</strong> (Postgres) is the default choice for most new projects: free, reliable, powerful. Run it locally with Docker:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">docker run --name dev-postgres -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=devpass -p 5432:5432 -d postgres:17</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"tables-rows-and-relations\">Tables, rows and relations</h2>\n<p>A relational database stores data in <strong>tables</strong> (like spreadsheets) with <strong>rows</strong> (records) and <strong>columns</strong> (fields). Tables link to each other with <strong>keys</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">customers                orders                         order_items\n---------                ------                         -----------\nid (PK)                  id (PK)                        id (PK)\nemail (unique)           customer_id (FK -&gt; customers)  order_id (FK -&gt; orders)\nname                     status                         product_id (FK -&gt; products)\ncreated_at               total_pence                    qty\n                         created_at                     unit_price_pence</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><strong>PK</strong> (primary key): uniquely identifies a row.</li><li><strong>FK</strong> (foreign key): points to a row in another table. One customer has many orders; one order has many items.</li><li>Notice <code>unit_price_pence</code> is copied into <code>order_items</code>. Prices change; the order must remember what was actually paid.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"sql-the-language-of-databases\">SQL: the language of databases</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"sql\"><pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- read\nSELECT name, email FROM customers WHERE created_at &gt; '2026-01-01' ORDER BY name LIMIT 20;\n\n-- join tables: each order with its customer's email\nSELECT o.id, o.total_pence, c.email\nFROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.status = 'paid';\n\n-- aggregate: revenue per month\nSELECT date_trunc('month', created_at) AS month, SUM(total_pence) / 100.0 AS revenue_gbp\nFROM orders\nWHERE status = 'paid'\nGROUP BY month\nORDER BY month;\n\n-- write\nINSERT INTO customers (email, name) VALUES ('ada@example.com', 'Ada');\nUPDATE orders SET status = 'shipped' WHERE id = 42;\nDELETE FROM carts WHERE updated_at &lt; now() - interval '30 days';</code></pre></div>\n<p>Learn SQL properly. ORMs help, but every senior developer can read and write SQL, and debugging slow pages usually means reading it.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"using-an-orm\">Using an ORM</h2>\n<p>An ORM lets you query with typed code. With <strong>Prisma</strong>, you describe the schema:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">// prisma/schema.prisma\nmodel Customer {\n  id        String   @id @default(cuid())\n  email     String   @unique\n  name      String?\n  orders    Order[]\n  createdAt DateTime @default(now())\n}\n\nmodel Order {\n  id         String   @id @default(cuid())\n  customer   Customer @relation(fields: [customerId], references: [id])\n  customerId String\n  status     String   @default(&quot;pending&quot;)\n  totalPence Int\n  createdAt  DateTime @default(now())\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npx prisma migrate dev --name init     # creates the tables and a migration file</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">const paid = await prisma.order.findMany({\n  where: { status: 'paid' },\n  include: { customer: true },\n  orderBy: { createdAt: 'desc' },\n  take: 20,\n});</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Migrations</strong> are version-controlled changes to the database structure. Commit them like code, so every environment's database matches.</p>\n<h2 id=\"designing-a-rest-api\">Designing a REST API</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method and path</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>GET /api/products</code></td><td>List products (support <code>?page=2&amp;category=dresses</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /api/products/:id</code></td><td>One product</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /api/products</code></td><td>Create (admin only)</td></tr><tr><td><code>PATCH /api/products/:id</code></td><td>Update some fields</td></tr><tr><td><code>DELETE /api/products/:id</code></td><td>Delete</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Return correct status codes (201 created, 400 invalid input, 401 not logged in, 403 not allowed, 404 not found) and consistent JSON error bodies: <code>{ &quot;error&quot;: &quot;Email is required&quot; }</code>.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Never build SQL by gluing strings</p><p><code>&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '&quot; + email + &quot;'&quot;</code> lets an attacker type SQL into your form and read or delete your data. That is <strong>SQL injection</strong>. ORMs and parameterised queries (<code>WHERE email = $1</code>) prevent it. Never interpolate user input into a query string.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"other-back-end-essentials\">Other back-end essentials</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Indexes</strong> make lookups fast: add one to columns you filter or join on often (<code>email</code>, <code>customer_id</code>).</li><li><strong>Transactions</strong> group writes so they all succeed or all fail: charge the card and create the order together.</li><li><strong>Backups</strong>: managed hosts (Neon, Supabase, Railway, RDS) back up automatically. Test restoring once.</li><li><strong>Pagination</strong>: never return 50,000 rows to a browser.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"What does a foreign key do?","options":["Encrypts a column","Links a row to a row in another table, like an order to its customer","Makes a column unique"],"answer":1,"explain":"Foreign keys model relationships and protect them (you cannot point to a customer that does not exist)."},{"q":"Why copy unit_price_pence into order_items instead of reading it from products?","options":["Product prices change later, but the order must record what was actually paid","It makes the database smaller","SQL cannot join tables"],"answer":0,"explain":"Historical records must not change when current data changes."},{"q":"Which of these is vulnerable to SQL injection?","options":["prisma.user.findUnique({ where: { email } })","A query with $1 placeholders and email passed as a parameter","&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '&quot; + email + &quot;'&quot;"],"answer":2,"explain":"String-built queries let attackers inject SQL. Use parameters or an ORM."},{"q":"What is a migration?","options":["Moving to a new hosting provider","A version-controlled change to the database structure","A database backup"],"answer":1,"explain":"Migrations keep every environment's schema in sync, the same way Git does for code."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Your first SQL query","file":"query.sql","lang":"sql","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write one query (keywords in capitals): select <code>name</code> and <code>email</code> from <code>customers</code> where <code>created_at</code> is after <code>'2026-01-01'</code>, ordered by <code>name</code>, limited to <code>20</code> rows. End with a semicolon.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"SELECT name, email FROM customers WHERE created_at > '2026-01-01' ORDER BY name LIMIT 20;","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*SELECT\\s+name\\s*,\\s*email","text":"Start with <code>SELECT name, email</code>"},{"re":"FROM\\s+customers","text":"then <code>FROM customers</code>"},{"re":"WHERE\\s+created_at\\s*>\\s*['\"]2026-01-01['\"]","text":"then <code>WHERE created_at &gt; '2026-01-01'</code>"},{"re":"ORDER\\s+BY\\s+name\\s+LIMIT\\s+20\\s*;","text":"then <code>ORDER BY name LIMIT 20;</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Join two tables","file":"query.sql","lang":"sql","run":false,"prompt":"<p>List paid orders with their customer's email. Four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>SELECT o.id, o.total_pence, c.email</code></li><li><code>FROM orders o</code></li><li><code>JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id</code></li><li><code>WHERE o.status = 'paid';</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"SELECT o.id, o.total_pence, c.email\nFROM orders o\nJOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.status = 'paid';","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*SELECT\\s+o\\.id\\s*,\\s*o\\.total_pence\\s*,\\s*c\\.email\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>SELECT o.id, o.total_pence, c.email</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*FROM\\s+orders\\s+o\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>FROM orders o</code> (<code>o</code> is a short nickname for the table)."},{"re":"^\\s*JOIN\\s+customers\\s+c\\s+ON\\s+c\\.id\\s*=\\s*o\\.customer_id\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id</code>"},{"re":"WHERE\\s+o\\.status\\s*=\\s*['\"]paid['\"]\\s*;","text":"Line 4: <code>WHERE o.status = 'paid';</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>JOIN ... ON</code> matches each order to the customer whose <code>id</code> equals the order's <code>customer_id</code>. That foreign-key link is what makes relational databases powerful.</p>"},{"title":"Insert a row","file":"query.sql","lang":"sql","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Insert a customer with email <code>'ada@example.com'</code> and name <code>'Ada'</code> into <code>customers</code>, naming the columns <code>(email, name)</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"INSERT INTO customers (email, name) VALUES ('ada@example.com', 'Ada');","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*INSERT\\s+INTO\\s+customers","text":"Start with <code>INSERT INTO customers</code>"},{"re":"\\(\\s*email\\s*,\\s*name\\s*\\)\\s*VALUES","text":"then the columns and the keyword: <code>(email, name) VALUES</code>"},{"re":"VALUES\\s*\\(\\s*['\"]ada@example\\.com['\"]\\s*,\\s*['\"]Ada['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"then the values in the same order: <code>('ada@example.com', 'Ada');</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Why a database localStorage lives in one browser. A database lives on a server, is shared by all users, survives crashes, and can answer questions like the ten best-selling dresses this month in milliseconds. PostgreSQL (Postgres) is the default choice for most new projects: free, reliable, powerful. Run it locally with Docker: docker run --name dev-postgres -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=devpass -p 5432:5432 -d postgres:17 Tables, rows and relations A relational database stores data in tables (like spreadsheets) with rows (records) and columns (fields). Tables link to each other with keys . customers orders order_items --------- ------ ----------- id (PK) id (PK) id (PK) email (unique) customer_id (FK - customers) order_id (FK - orders) name status product_id (FK - products) created_at total_pence qty created_at unit_price_pence PK (primary key): uniquely identifies a row. FK (foreign key): points to a row in another table. One customer has many orders; one order has many items. Notice unit_price_pence is copied into order_items . Prices change; the order must remember what was actually paid. SQL: the language of databases -- read SELECT name, email FROM customers WHERE created_at '2026-01-01' ORDER BY name LIMIT 20; -- join tables: each order with its customer's email SELECT o.id, o.total_pence, c.email FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id WHERE o.status = 'paid'; -- aggregate: revenue per month SELECT date_trunc('month', created_at) AS month, SUM(total_pence) / 100.0 AS revenue_gbp FROM orders WHERE status = 'paid' GROUP BY month ORDER BY month; -- write INSERT INTO customers (email, name) VALUES ('ada@example.com', 'Ada'); UPDATE orders SET status = 'shipped' WHERE id = 42; DELETE FROM carts WHERE updated_at now() - interval '30 days'; Learn SQL properly. ORMs help, but every senior developer can read and write SQL, and debugging slow pages usually means reading it. Using an ORM An ORM lets you query with typed code. With Prisma , you describe the schema: // prisma/schema.prisma model Customer { id String @id @default(cuid()) email String @unique name String? orders Order[] createdAt DateTime @default(now()) } model Order { id String @id @default(cuid()) customer Customer @relation(fields: [customerId], references: [id]) customerId String status String @default( pending ) totalPence Int createdAt DateTime @default(now()) } npx prisma migrate dev --name init # creates the tables and a migration file const paid = await prisma.order.findMany({ where: { status: 'paid' }, include: { customer: true }, orderBy: { createdAt: 'desc' }, take: 20, }); Migrations are version-controlled changes to the database structure. Commit them like code, so every environment's database matches. Designing a REST API Method and path Does GET /api/products List products (support ?page=2 category=dresses ) GET /api/products/:id One product POST /api/products Create (admin only) PATCH /api/products/:id Update some fields DELETE /api/products/:id Delete Return correct status codes (201 created, 400 invalid input, 401 not logged in, 403 not allowed, 404 not found) and consistent JSON error bodies: { error : Email is required } . Never build SQL by gluing strings SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ' + email + ' lets an attacker type SQL into your form and read or delete your data. That is SQL injection . ORMs and parameterised queries ( WHERE email = $1 ) prevent it. Never interpolate user input into a query string. Other back-end essentials Indexes make lookups fast: add one to columns you filter or join on often ( email , customer_id ). Transactions group writes so they all succeed or all fail: charge the card and create the order together. Backups : managed hosts (Neon, Supabase, Railway, RDS) back up automatically. Test restoring once. Pagination : never return 50,000 rows to a browser.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-security","title":"Authentication and web security","level":"advanced","minutes":35,"summary":"The attacks that actually happen (XSS, injection, CSRF, broken access control) and the habits that stop them.","html":"<h2 id=\"think-like-an-attacker\">Think like an attacker</h2>\n<p>Every input to your system is a potential attack: form fields, URL parameters, headers, cookies, uploaded files, webhooks, even data from your own database if users wrote it. The <strong>OWASP Top 10</strong> lists the most common web risks. These are the ones you will meet first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-broken-access-control-the-most-common\">1. Broken access control (the most common)</h2>\n<p>The server must check <strong>on every request</strong> that this user is allowed to do this thing to this data.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// BAD: anyone logged in can read any order by changing the id in the URL\nconst order = await db.order.findUnique({ where: { id: params.id } });\n\n// GOOD: scope the query to the current user\nconst order = await db.order.findFirst({ where: { id: params.id, customerId: session.user.id } });\nif (!order) return notFound();</code></pre></div>\n<p>Hiding a button in the UI is not security. Attackers call your API directly.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"2-injection\">2. Injection</h2>\n<p>Untrusted input interpreted as code: SQL injection, command injection. Use parameterised queries or an ORM, never string-build commands, and validate input with a schema (Zod) at every entry point.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-cross-site-scripting-xss\">3. Cross-site scripting (XSS)</h2>\n<p>An attacker gets their script to run on your page, then steals sessions or acts as the user.</p>\n<ul><li>Use <code>textContent</code>, not <code>innerHTML</code>, for user content. React escapes text by default; avoid <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code> unless you sanitise with a library like DOMPurify.</li><li>Set a <strong>Content-Security-Policy</strong> header to restrict which scripts may run.</li><li>Never put user input into <code>href</code> without checking it is <code>https:</code> (a <code>javascript:</code> URL runs code).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"4-cross-site-request-forgery-csrf\">4. Cross-site request forgery (CSRF)</h2>\n<p>Another site tricks a logged-in user's browser into sending a request to yours. Defences: <code>SameSite=Lax</code> or <code>Strict</code> cookies (the modern default), CSRF tokens on forms, and never changing data on <code>GET</code> requests. Frameworks like Next.js server actions include protections; know what yours does.</p>\n<h2 id=\"authentication-done-right\">Authentication done right</h2>\n<p><strong>Do not build password auth from scratch</strong> for real projects. Use a well-maintained library or service: Auth.js, Better Auth, Clerk, Supabase Auth, Firebase Auth. They handle the many subtle details.</p>\n<p>If you must store passwords, understand the rules:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Never</strong> store plain passwords or encrypt them. <strong>Hash</strong> them with a slow, salted algorithm designed for passwords: <strong>Argon2id</strong> or <strong>bcrypt</strong>. (SHA-256 alone is far too fast; attackers can try billions per second.)</li><li>Rate-limit login attempts.</li><li>Offer multi-factor authentication and passkeys.</li></ul>\n<p>Sessions:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Set-Cookie: session=abc123; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/; Max-Age=604800</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>HttpOnly</code>: JavaScript cannot read it, so XSS cannot steal it.</li><li><code>Secure</code>: only sent over HTTPS.</li><li><code>SameSite</code>: limits cross-site sending (CSRF defence).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"secrets-and-dependencies\">Secrets and dependencies</h2>\n<ul><li>Secrets live in environment variables, never in Git, never in front-end code.</li><li>Rotate any key that might have leaked.</li><li>Keep dependencies updated (<code>npm audit</code>, Dependabot or Renovate on GitHub).</li><li>Give every service the least privilege it needs: a read-only database user for analytics, a restricted API key for each integration.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"security-headers-worth-setting\">Security headers worth setting</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Header</th><th>Protects against</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Content-Security-Policy</code></td><td>XSS, unwanted third-party scripts</td></tr><tr><td><code>Strict-Transport-Security</code></td><td>Downgrade to insecure HTTP</td></tr><tr><td><code>X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff</code></td><td>Browsers guessing file types</td></tr><tr><td><code>Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin</code></td><td>Leaking URLs to other sites</td></tr><tr><td><code>frame-ancestors</code> (in CSP)</td><td>Clickjacking (your site in a hidden frame)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">A security review checklist for every feature</p><p>Who can call this? What if they are not logged in? What if they change the id? What if the input is huge, empty, negative, or contains HTML? What happens if it is called 1,000 times a minute? What do the logs reveal? Ask these six questions and you will catch most vulnerabilities before they ship.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"A logged-in user changes /orders/1001 to /orders/1002 and sees someone else's order. What is this?","options":["XSS","Broken access control","CSRF"],"answer":1,"explain":"The server failed to check that the order belongs to the current user. Scope every query."},{"q":"How should user passwords be stored?","options":["Encrypted with AES","Hashed with plain SHA-256","Hashed with a slow, salted password algorithm like Argon2id or bcrypt"],"answer":2,"explain":"Password hashes must be deliberately slow so stolen databases cannot be brute-forced quickly."},{"q":"What does the HttpOnly cookie flag do?","options":["Stops JavaScript reading the cookie, so XSS cannot steal the session","Forces HTTPS","Deletes the cookie after one hour"],"answer":0,"explain":"HttpOnly protects session cookies from scripts. Secure is the HTTPS one."},{"q":"You hide the &quot;Delete product&quot; button from non-admins. Is the delete endpoint now secure?","options":["Yes, they cannot see the button","No: the server must check the user is an admin on every request","Only if the button is also disabled"],"answer":1,"explain":"Attackers call APIs directly. Authorisation lives on the server."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Scope the query to the user","file":"orders.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Fix broken access control. Write the safe version that only finds the order if it belongs to the logged-in user, and returns <code>notFound()</code> otherwise:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const order = await db.order.findFirst({</code></li><li><code>where: { id: params.id, customerId: session.user.id },</code></li><li><code>});</code></li><li><code>if (!order) return notFound();</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const order = await db.order.findFirst({\n  where: { id: params.id, customerId: session.user.id },\n});\nif (!order) return notFound();","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+order\\s*=\\s*await\\s+db\\.order\\.findFirst\\(\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>const order = await db.order.findFirst({</code>"},{"re":"where\\s*:\\s*\\{\\s*id\\s*:\\s*params\\.id\\s*,\\s*customerId\\s*:\\s*session\\.user\\.id\\s*\\}","text":"Line 2 filters by both ids: <code>where: { id: params.id, customerId: session.user.id },</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Line 3 closes the object and the call: <code>});</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*!\\s*order\\s*\\)\\s*return\\s+notFound\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>if (!order) return notFound();</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Someone changing the id in the URL now gets a 404 instead of a stranger's order. Returning &quot;not found&quot; rather than &quot;forbidden&quot; also avoids confirming the order exists.</p>"},{"title":"A secure session cookie","file":"Response header","lang":"text","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the response header that sets a <code>session</code> cookie with value <code>abc123</code>, plus these flags in this order: <code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>Secure</code>, <code>SameSite=Lax</code>, <code>Path=/</code>. Separate parts with a semicolon and a space.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"Set-Cookie: session=abc123; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*Set-Cookie\\s*:\\s*session=abc123","text":"Start: <code>Set-Cookie: session=abc123</code>"},{"re":";\\s*HttpOnly\\s*;\\s*Secure","text":"then <code>; HttpOnly; Secure</code>"},{"re":";\\s*SameSite=Lax\\s*;\\s*Path=/\\s*$","text":"then <code>; SameSite=Lax; Path=/</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Think like an attacker Every input to your system is a potential attack: form fields, URL parameters, headers, cookies, uploaded files, webhooks, even data from your own database if users wrote it. The OWASP Top 10 lists the most common web risks. These are the ones you will meet first. 1. Broken access control (the most common) The server must check on every request that this user is allowed to do this thing to this data. // BAD: anyone logged in can read any order by changing the id in the URL const order = await db.order.findUnique({ where: { id: params.id } }); // GOOD: scope the query to the current user const order = await db.order.findFirst({ where: { id: params.id, customerId: session.user.id } }); if (!order) return notFound(); Hiding a button in the UI is not security. Attackers call your API directly. 2. Injection Untrusted input interpreted as code: SQL injection, command injection. Use parameterised queries or an ORM, never string-build commands, and validate input with a schema (Zod) at every entry point. 3. Cross-site scripting (XSS) An attacker gets their script to run on your page, then steals sessions or acts as the user. Use textContent , not innerHTML , for user content. React escapes text by default; avoid dangerouslySetInnerHTML unless you sanitise with a library like DOMPurify. Set a Content-Security-Policy header to restrict which scripts may run. Never put user input into href without checking it is https: (a javascript: URL runs code). 4. Cross-site request forgery (CSRF) Another site tricks a logged-in user's browser into sending a request to yours. Defences: SameSite=Lax or Strict cookies (the modern default), CSRF tokens on forms, and never changing data on GET requests. Frameworks like Next.js server actions include protections; know what yours does. Authentication done right Do not build password auth from scratch for real projects. Use a well-maintained library or service: Auth.js, Better Auth, Clerk, Supabase Auth, Firebase Auth. They handle the many subtle details. If you must store passwords, understand the rules: Never store plain passwords or encrypt them. Hash them with a slow, salted algorithm designed for passwords: Argon2id or bcrypt . (SHA-256 alone is far too fast; attackers can try billions per second.) Rate-limit login attempts. Offer multi-factor authentication and passkeys. Sessions: Set-Cookie: session=abc123; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/; Max-Age=604800 HttpOnly : JavaScript cannot read it, so XSS cannot steal it. Secure : only sent over HTTPS. SameSite : limits cross-site sending (CSRF defence). Secrets and dependencies Secrets live in environment variables, never in Git, never in front-end code. Rotate any key that might have leaked. Keep dependencies updated ( npm audit , Dependabot or Renovate on GitHub). Give every service the least privilege it needs: a read-only database user for analytics, a restricted API key for each integration. Security headers worth setting Header Protects against Content-Security-Policy XSS, unwanted third-party scripts Strict-Transport-Security Downgrade to insecure HTTP X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff Browsers guessing file types Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin Leaking URLs to other sites frame-ancestors (in CSP) Clickjacking (your site in a hidden frame) A security review checklist for every feature Who can call this? What if they are not logged in? What if they change the id? What if the input is huge, empty, negative, or contains HTML? What happens if it is called 1,000 times a minute? What do the logs reveal? Ask these six questions and you will catch most vulnerabilities before they ship.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-testing","title":"Testing that gives you confidence","level":"advanced","minutes":30,"summary":"Unit, component and end-to-end tests with Vitest, Testing Library and Playwright, and what is worth testing.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-test\">Why test</h2>\n<p>Tests let you change code without fear. Without them, every change risks breaking something you will only hear about from an angry customer. With them, you refactor freely and ship faster. Senior developers write tests not because they are told to, but because it saves them time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-testing-pyramid\">The testing pyramid</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Tests</th><th>Speed</th><th>How many</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Unit</strong></td><td>One function in isolation</td><td>Milliseconds</td><td>Many</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Component / integration</strong></td><td>Several pieces together (a component with its logic)</td><td>Fast</td><td>Some</td></tr><tr><td><strong>End-to-end (E2E)</strong></td><td>The real app in a real browser, like a user</td><td>Seconds</td><td>A few, for critical journeys</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"unit-tests-with-vitest\">Unit tests with Vitest</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm install -D vitest</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// src/lib/cart.ts\nexport type Item = { pricePence: number; qty: number };\n\nexport function cartTotal(items: Item[], discountPercent = 0): number {\n  const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, i) =&gt; sum + i.pricePence * i.qty, 0);\n  return Math.round(subtotal * (1 - discountPercent / 100));\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// src/lib/cart.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect } from 'vitest';\nimport { cartTotal } from './cart';\n\ndescribe('cartTotal', () =&gt; {\n  it('adds up price times quantity', () =&gt; {\n    expect(cartTotal([{ pricePence: 2000, qty: 2 }, { pricePence: 1500, qty: 1 }])).toBe(5500);\n  });\n\n  it('returns 0 for an empty cart', () =&gt; {\n    expect(cartTotal([])).toBe(0);\n  });\n\n  it('applies a percentage discount and rounds to the penny', () =&gt; {\n    expect(cartTotal([{ pricePence: 999, qty: 1 }], 10)).toBe(899);\n  });\n});</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npx vitest          # watch mode: re-runs on every save</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each test follows <strong>Arrange, Act, Assert</strong>: set up the data, run the code, check the result.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"component-tests-with-testing-library\">Component tests with Testing Library</h2>\n<p>Test what the user sees and does, not implementation details:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';\nimport userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event';\nimport { Counter } from './Counter';\n\nit('increases the quantity when + is clicked', async () =&gt; {\n  render(&lt;Counter /&gt;);\n  await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: '+' }));\n  expect(screen.getByText('2')).toBeInTheDocument();\n});</code></pre></div>\n<p>Querying by <strong>role</strong> and <strong>accessible name</strong> (<code>getByRole('button', { name: '+' })</code>) also proves your UI is accessible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"end-to-end-tests-with-playwright\">End-to-end tests with Playwright</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm init playwright@latest</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// tests/checkout.spec.ts\nimport { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';\n\ntest('a shopper can add a dress to the bag', async ({ page }) =&gt; {\n  await page.goto('/products/linen-wrap-dress');\n  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to bag' }).click();\n  await page.getByRole('link', { name: /bag/i }).click();\n  await expect(page.getByText('Linen Wrap Dress')).toBeVisible();\n});</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npx playwright test\nnpx playwright test --ui     # watch it click through, step by step</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"what-is-worth-testing\">What is worth testing</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Money, maths and dates</strong>: totals, discounts, tax, time zones. Bugs here cost real money.</li><li><strong>Business rules</strong>: who can do what, stock limits, order states.</li><li><strong>Edge cases</strong>: empty, zero, negative, huge, missing, duplicated.</li><li><strong>Every bug you fix</strong>: write a test that fails because of the bug, then fix it. It can never come back silently.</li><li><strong>Critical journeys</strong> end to end: sign up, log in, checkout.</li></ul>\n<p>Skip testing that a framework works (that React renders a div). Do not chase 100% coverage; chase confidence in the parts that matter.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Test-driven development, lightly</p><p>For tricky logic, write the test first. It forces you to decide exactly what the function should do before you get lost in how. Watch it fail, make it pass, then tidy the code with the test as a safety net.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Which kind of test should you have the most of?","options":["Unit tests: fast and focused","End-to-end tests","Manual tests"],"answer":0,"explain":"Unit tests are cheap and fast. E2E tests are valuable but slow, so keep a few for critical paths."},{"q":"You fix a bug where discounts rounded the wrong way. What should you also do?","options":["Nothing more, it is fixed","Add a test that reproduces the bug, so it can never return unnoticed","Delete the old tests"],"answer":1,"explain":"Regression tests lock in every fix."},{"q":"Why query elements by role and name in component tests?","options":["It is faster","It tests what users experience and checks accessibility at the same time","CSS classes are not allowed in tests"],"answer":1,"explain":"If a screen reader cannot find the button by role and name, neither can your test."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write a unit test","file":"cart.test.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a Vitest test named <code>'returns 0 for an empty cart'</code> that expects <code>cartTotal([])</code> to be <code>0</code>. Three lines.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"it('returns 0 for an empty cart', () => {\n  expect(cartTotal([])).toBe(0);\n});","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*it\\(\\s*['\"]returns 0 for an empty cart['\"]\\s*,","text":"Line 1 starts <code>it('returns 0 for an empty cart',</code>"},{"re":",\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*\\{","text":"then an arrow function: <code>() =&gt; {</code>"},{"re":"expect\\(\\s*cartTotal\\(\\s*\\[\\s*\\]\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\.toBe\\(\\s*0\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>expect(cartTotal([])).toBe(0);</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*\\}\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>});</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"An end-to-end step","file":"checkout.spec.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Two Playwright lines: click the button whose accessible name is <code>'Add to bag'</code>, then expect the text <code>'Linen Wrap Dress'</code> to be visible.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to bag' }).click();\nawait expect(page.getByText('Linen Wrap Dress')).toBeVisible();","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"await\\s+page\\.getByRole\\(\\s*['\"]button['\"]\\s*,","text":"Line 1 starts <code>await page.getByRole('button',</code>"},{"re":"\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]Add to bag['\"]\\s*\\}\\s*\\)\\.click\\(\\s*\\)","text":"then <code>{ name: 'Add to bag' }).click();</code>"},{"re":"await\\s+expect\\(\\s*page\\.getByText\\(\\s*['\"]Linen Wrap Dress['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\.toBeVisible\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>await expect(page.getByText('Linen Wrap Dress')).toBeVisible();</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Why test Tests let you change code without fear. Without them, every change risks breaking something you will only hear about from an angry customer. With them, you refactor freely and ship faster. Senior developers write tests not because they are told to, but because it saves them time. The testing pyramid Type Tests Speed How many Unit One function in isolation Milliseconds Many Component / integration Several pieces together (a component with its logic) Fast Some End-to-end (E2E) The real app in a real browser, like a user Seconds A few, for critical journeys Unit tests with Vitest npm install -D vitest // src/lib/cart.ts export type Item = { pricePence: number; qty: number }; export function cartTotal(items: Item[], discountPercent = 0): number { const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, i) = sum + i.pricePence * i.qty, 0); return Math.round(subtotal * (1 - discountPercent / 100)); } // src/lib/cart.test.ts import { describe, it, expect } from 'vitest'; import { cartTotal } from './cart'; describe('cartTotal', () = { it('adds up price times quantity', () = { expect(cartTotal([{ pricePence: 2000, qty: 2 }, { pricePence: 1500, qty: 1 }])).toBe(5500); }); it('returns 0 for an empty cart', () = { expect(cartTotal([])).toBe(0); }); it('applies a percentage discount and rounds to the penny', () = { expect(cartTotal([{ pricePence: 999, qty: 1 }], 10)).toBe(899); }); }); npx vitest # watch mode: re-runs on every save Each test follows Arrange, Act, Assert : set up the data, run the code, check the result. Component tests with Testing Library Test what the user sees and does, not implementation details: import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react'; import userEvent from '@testing-library/user-event'; import { Counter } from './Counter'; it('increases the quantity when + is clicked', async () = { render( Counter / ); await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: '+' })); expect(screen.getByText('2')).toBeInTheDocument(); }); Querying by role and accessible name ( getByRole('button', { name: '+' }) ) also proves your UI is accessible. End-to-end tests with Playwright npm init playwright@latest // tests/checkout.spec.ts import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test'; test('a shopper can add a dress to the bag', async ({ page }) = { await page.goto('/products/linen-wrap-dress'); await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Add to bag' }).click(); await page.getByRole('link', { name: /bag/i }).click(); await expect(page.getByText('Linen Wrap Dress')).toBeVisible(); }); npx playwright test npx playwright test --ui # watch it click through, step by step What is worth testing Money, maths and dates : totals, discounts, tax, time zones. Bugs here cost real money. Business rules : who can do what, stock limits, order states. Edge cases : empty, zero, negative, huge, missing, duplicated. Every bug you fix : write a test that fails because of the bug, then fix it. It can never come back silently. Critical journeys end to end: sign up, log in, checkout. Skip testing that a framework works (that React renders a div). Do not chase 100% coverage; chase confidence in the parts that matter. Test-driven development, lightly For tricky logic, write the test first. It forces you to decide exactly what the function should do before you get lost in how. Watch it fail, make it pass, then tidy the code with the test as a safety net.","track":"web"},{"id":"web-performance","title":"Performance and Core Web Vitals","level":"advanced","minutes":30,"summary":"Measure what users feel, then fix images, fonts, JavaScript and loading order to make sites fast.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-speed-sells\">Why speed sells</h2>\n<p>Slow sites lose visitors and sales. Shoppers abandon pages that take more than a few seconds to load, and Google uses page experience signals in rankings. For brand and e-commerce clients, performance work has a direct, measurable return, which makes it an excellent service to sell.</p>\n<h2 id=\"measure-first-core-web-vitals\">Measure first: Core Web Vitals</h2>\n<p>Google's three user-centred metrics, measured on real visitors:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Measures</th><th>Good</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>LCP</strong> (Largest Contentful Paint)</td><td>When the main content (often the hero image) appears</td><td>Under 2.5 s</td></tr><tr><td><strong>INP</strong> (Interaction to Next Paint)</td><td>How quickly the page responds to taps and clicks</td><td>Under 200 ms</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CLS</strong> (Cumulative Layout Shift)</td><td>How much things jump around while loading</td><td>Under 0.1</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Tools: Chrome DevTools <strong>Lighthouse</strong> and <strong>Performance</strong> panels (lab data), and <strong>PageSpeed Insights</strong> (pagespeed.web.dev), which also shows real-user data for popular sites. Always test with mobile throttling: your users are not on your laptop's Wi-Fi.</p>\n<h2 id=\"fix-1-images-usually-the-biggest-win\">Fix 1: images (usually the biggest win)</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"html\"><pre><code class=\"language-html\">&lt;!-- the hero: load it early, at high priority --&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;/hero-1600.avif&quot;\n     srcset=&quot;/hero-800.avif 800w, /hero-1600.avif 1600w&quot;\n     sizes=&quot;100vw&quot;\n     width=&quot;1600&quot; height=&quot;900&quot;\n     alt=&quot;Model in the sage wrap dress on a London street&quot;\n     fetchpriority=&quot;high&quot;&gt;\n\n&lt;!-- everything below the fold: lazy --&gt;\n&lt;img src=&quot;/look-2.webp&quot; width=&quot;800&quot; height=&quot;1000&quot; alt=&quot;...&quot; loading=&quot;lazy&quot; decoding=&quot;async&quot;&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li>Use modern formats: <strong>AVIF</strong> or <strong>WebP</strong> are typically far smaller than JPEG or PNG.</li><li>Serve the right size with <code>srcset</code> and <code>sizes</code>. A phone does not need a 4000px image.</li><li>Always set <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> (or <code>aspect-ratio</code>) to prevent layout shift.</li><li>Never lazy-load the LCP image; give it <code>fetchpriority=&quot;high&quot;</code>.</li><li>Next.js <code>&lt;Image&gt;</code> and services like Cloudinary or Shopify's CDN do most of this automatically.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"fix-2-fonts\">Fix 2: fonts</h2>\n<ul><li>Use one or two families, and only the weights you need.</li><li>Self-host or use <code>next/font</code>, and set <code>font-display: swap</code> so text shows immediately.</li><li>Preload the main font file.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"fix-3-javascript\">Fix 3: JavaScript</h2>\n<p>JavaScript is the most expensive byte: it must be downloaded, parsed and executed, which blocks interactions (hurting INP).</p>\n<ul><li>Ship less: question every dependency (<code>npx vite-bundle-visualizer</code> shows what is big).</li><li>Split code: load heavy components only when needed with <code>import()</code> or <code>React.lazy</code>.</li><li>Keep work off the main thread: break long tasks up, debounce input handlers.</li><li>Audit third-party scripts (chat widgets, trackers, review widgets). They are often the slowest thing on a shop site; load them late or remove them.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"fix-4-loading-order-and-caching\">Fix 4: loading order and caching</h2>\n<ul><li>Put scripts at the end or use <code>defer</code> / <code>type=&quot;module&quot;</code>.</li><li>Inline small critical CSS; avoid huge render-blocking stylesheets.</li><li>Use a CDN (Vercel, Netlify and Cloudflare do this by default).</li><li>Cache static assets for a long time with hashed file names (<code>app.3f9a1c.js</code>), which build tools generate.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"fix-5-layout-stability\">Fix 5: layout stability</h2>\n<ul><li>Reserve space for images, embeds and ads.</li><li>Do not insert banners above existing content after load.</li><li>Use <code>transform</code> for animations, not properties like <code>top</code> or <code>height</code>.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The performance audit as a service</p><p>Run PageSpeed Insights on a prospect's site, screenshot the mobile score, then list three specific fixes with estimated impact (&quot;Hero image is 2.4 MB; converting to AVIF at the right size should cut LCP by about 2 seconds&quot;). Concrete numbers win clients far more often than vague promises.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Which metric measures when the main content, often the hero image, becomes visible?","options":["LCP","CLS","INP"],"answer":0,"explain":"Largest Contentful Paint. Good is under 2.5 seconds."},{"q":"Products jump down the page when a banner loads in. Which metric suffers?","options":["LCP","CLS","INP"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cumulative Layout Shift measures unexpected movement. Reserve space for late content."},{"q":"Should the hero image use loading=&quot;lazy&quot;?","options":["Yes, lazy loading is always faster","No: the LCP image should load immediately with fetchpriority=&quot;high&quot;","Only on mobile"],"answer":1,"explain":"Lazy-loading the main image delays LCP. Lazy-load only below-the-fold images."},{"q":"What usually gives the biggest performance win on image-heavy shop sites?","options":["Minifying HTML","Serving correctly sized, modern-format images","Removing CSS comments"],"answer":1,"explain":"Images are typically most of the page weight."}],"exercises":[{"title":"A fast hero image","file":"hero.html","lang":"html","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the hero <code>img</code> with attributes in this order: <code>src=&quot;/hero.avif&quot;</code>, <code>width=&quot;1600&quot;</code>, <code>height=&quot;900&quot;</code>, <code>alt=&quot;Summer collection on a London street&quot;</code>, <code>fetchpriority=&quot;high&quot;</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"<img src=\"/hero.avif\" width=\"1600\" height=\"900\" alt=\"Summer collection on a London street\" fetchpriority=\"high\">","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"<img\\s+src=[\"']/hero\\.avif[\"']","text":"Start <code>&lt;img src=&quot;/hero.avif&quot;</code>"},{"re":"width=[\"']1600[\"']\\s+height=[\"']900[\"']","text":"then <code>width=&quot;1600&quot; height=&quot;900&quot;</code> (stops layout shift)."},{"re":"alt=[\"']Summer collection on a London street[\"']","text":"then the alt text."},{"re":"fetchpriority=[\"']high[\"']\\s*/?>\\s*$","text":"finally <code>fetchpriority=&quot;high&quot;&gt;</code> (the main image loads first, never lazily)."}],"explain":""},{"title":"Fonts that never block text","file":"fonts.css","lang":"css","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write an <code>@font-face</code> rule: <code>font-family: 'Brand';</code>, <code>src: url('/fonts/brand.woff2') format('woff2');</code> and <code>font-display: swap;</code></p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"@font-face {\n  font-family: 'Brand';\n  src: url('/fonts/brand.woff2') format('woff2');\n  font-display: swap;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*@font-face\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>@font-face {</code>"},{"re":"font-family\\s*:\\s*['\"]Brand['\"]","text":"<code>font-family: 'Brand';</code>"},{"re":"src\\s*:\\s*url\\(\\s*['\"]/fonts/brand\\.woff2['\"]\\s*\\)\\s*format\\(\\s*['\"]woff2['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"<code>src: url('/fonts/brand.woff2') format('woff2');</code>"},{"re":"font-display\\s*:\\s*swap","text":"<code>font-display: swap;</code> shows fallback text immediately, then swaps in the font."}],"explain":""}],"text":"Why speed sells Slow sites lose visitors and sales. Shoppers abandon pages that take more than a few seconds to load, and Google uses page experience signals in rankings. For brand and e-commerce clients, performance work has a direct, measurable return, which makes it an excellent service to sell. Measure first: Core Web Vitals Google's three user-centred metrics, measured on real visitors: Metric Measures Good LCP (Largest Contentful Paint) When the main content (often the hero image) appears Under 2.5 s INP (Interaction to Next Paint) How quickly the page responds to taps and clicks Under 200 ms CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift) How much things jump around while loading Under 0.1 Tools: Chrome DevTools Lighthouse and Performance panels (lab data), and PageSpeed Insights (pagespeed.web.dev), which also shows real-user data for popular sites. Always test with mobile throttling: your users are not on your laptop's Wi-Fi. Fix 1: images (usually the biggest win) !-- the hero: load it early, at high priority -- img src= /hero-1600.avif srcset= /hero-800.avif 800w, /hero-1600.avif 1600w sizes= 100vw width= 1600 height= 900 alt= Model in the sage wrap dress on a London street fetchpriority= high !-- everything below the fold: lazy -- img src= /look-2.webp width= 800 height= 1000 alt= ... loading= lazy decoding= async Use modern formats: AVIF or WebP are typically far smaller than JPEG or PNG. Serve the right size with srcset and sizes . A phone does not need a 4000px image. Always set width and height (or aspect-ratio ) to prevent layout shift. Never lazy-load the LCP image; give it fetchpriority= high . Next.js Image and services like Cloudinary or Shopify's CDN do most of this automatically. Fix 2: fonts Use one or two families, and only the weights you need. Self-host or use next/font , and set font-display: swap so text shows immediately. Preload the main font file. Fix 3: JavaScript JavaScript is the most expensive byte: it must be downloaded, parsed and executed, which blocks interactions (hurting INP). Ship less: question every dependency ( npx vite-bundle-visualizer shows what is big). Split code: load heavy components only when needed with import() or React.lazy . Keep work off the main thread: break long tasks up, debounce input handlers. Audit third-party scripts (chat widgets, trackers, review widgets). They are often the slowest thing on a shop site; load them late or remove them. Fix 4: loading order and caching Put scripts at the end or use defer / type= module . Inline small critical CSS; avoid huge render-blocking stylesheets. Use a CDN (Vercel, Netlify and Cloudflare do this by default). Cache static assets for a long time with hashed file names ( app.3f9a1c.js ), which build tools generate. Fix 5: layout stability Reserve space for images, embeds and ads. Do not insert banners above existing content after load. Use transform for animations, not properties like top or height . The performance audit as a service Run PageSpeed Insights on a prospect's site, screenshot the mobile score, then list three specific fixes with estimated impact ( Hero image is 2.4 MB; converting to AVIF at the right size should cut LCP by about 2 seconds ). Concrete numbers win clients far more often than vague promises.","track":"web"},{"id":"crypto-intro","title":"Cryptography 101","level":"beginner","minutes":20,"summary":"What cryptography protects, the four building blocks behind HTTPS, WhatsApp and every blockchain, and the one rule you must never break.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-cryptography-is-for\">What cryptography is for</h2>\n<p>Cryptography uses maths to protect information from people who should not read it or change it. It gives you four guarantees:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Everyday example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Confidentiality</strong></td><td>Can anyone else read this?</td><td>Your card number over HTTPS</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Integrity</strong></td><td>Has this been changed?</td><td>Checking a downloaded file is not tampered with</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Authenticity</strong></td><td>Who really sent this?</td><td>Your bank's website certificate</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Non-repudiation</strong></td><td>Can the sender deny it later?</td><td>Signing a blockchain transaction</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-four-building-blocks\">The four building blocks</h2>\n<h3 id=\"1-hash-functions\">1. Hash functions</h3>\n<p>Turn any data into a short, fixed-size fingerprint. One-way: you cannot get the data back. Used for integrity, passwords and linking blocks in a blockchain. Next lesson, plus the <a href=\"#hash-lab\">Hash Lab</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-symmetric-encryption\">2. Symmetric encryption</h3>\n<p>One shared secret key locks and unlocks. Fast, used for bulk data. The standard is <strong>AES</strong> (in GCM mode, which also detects tampering). The hard part: how do two people agree a secret key over the internet without anyone overhearing?</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-asymmetric-public-key-cryptography\">3. Asymmetric (public-key) cryptography</h3>\n<p>Each person has a <strong>key pair</strong>: a <strong>private key</strong> they keep secret and a <strong>public key</strong> they share. What one key does, only the other can undo. This solves the key-sharing problem and enables signatures.</p>\n<h3 id=\"4-digital-signatures\">4. Digital signatures</h3>\n<p>Sign with your private key; anyone verifies with your public key. Proves who sent a message and that it was not altered. <strong>Every blockchain transaction is a digital signature.</strong></p>\n<p>Real systems combine them. HTTPS uses public-key crypto to agree a shared key, symmetric encryption for the data, hashes for integrity, and signed certificates for authenticity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"try-encryption-in-your-browser\">Try encryption in your browser</h2>\n<p>The Web Crypto API is built into every modern browser. This encrypts and decrypts a message with AES-GCM:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">const enc = new TextEncoder();\nconst dec = new TextDecoder();\n\n// 1. Make a random 256-bit key (in real life, store or derive this safely)\nconst key = await crypto.subtle.generateKey({ name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 }, true, ['encrypt', 'decrypt']);\n\n// 2. A fresh random &quot;nonce&quot; (iv) for every message. Never reuse one with the same key.\nconst iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));\n\n// 3. Encrypt\nconst secret = enc.encode('Launch date: 14 March');\nconst ciphertext = await crypto.subtle.encrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, secret);\nconsole.log('Encrypted bytes:', [...new Uint8Array(ciphertext)].slice(0, 16).join(','), '...');\n\n// 4. Decrypt with the same key and iv\nconst plain = await crypto.subtle.decrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, ciphertext);\nconsole.log('Decrypted:', dec.decode(plain));</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"randomness-matters\">Randomness matters</h2>\n<p>Keys must be unpredictable. Use <code>crypto.getRandomValues()</code> or <code>crypto.randomUUID()</code>. <strong>Never</strong> use <code>Math.random()</code> for anything security-related; it is predictable.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"kerckhoffs-s-principle\">Kerckhoffs's principle</h2>\n<p>A system must be secure <strong>even if everything about it except the key is public</strong>. Good algorithms (AES, SHA-256, Ed25519) are published and studied by thousands of experts. Secret, home-made algorithms are almost always broken.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The one rule: do not roll your own crypto</p><p>Never invent your own algorithm, and avoid implementing standard ones yourself for real use. Subtle mistakes (reused nonces, timing leaks, bad randomness) break systems completely. Use well-reviewed libraries: the Web Crypto API, libsodium, @noble/hashes and @noble/curves, OpenZeppelin for contracts. (The SHA-256 in this site's Hash Lab is hand-written for learning only.)</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Which building block lets anyone verify that a message really came from you?","options":["Symmetric encryption","Digital signatures","Hash functions alone"],"answer":1,"explain":"You sign with your private key; anyone checks with your public key."},{"q":"What is the main weakness of symmetric encryption on its own?","options":["It is too slow","Both sides need the same secret key, and sharing it safely is hard","It cannot encrypt text"],"answer":1,"explain":"Public-key cryptography solves key exchange; symmetric encryption then does the fast bulk work."},{"q":"Which should you use to generate a secret key in JavaScript?","options":["Math.random()","crypto.getRandomValues() or crypto.subtle.generateKey()","Date.now()"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only cryptographically secure randomness is unpredictable enough for keys."},{"q":"Why are published algorithms like AES trusted more than secret ones?","options":["Thousands of experts have tried to break them and failed","Secret algorithms are illegal","Published algorithms are faster"],"answer":0,"explain":"Kerckhoffs's principle: security should rest on the key, not on hiding the method."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Encrypt and decrypt with AES-GCM","file":"aes.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Using the example above as your guide, write five lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const key = await crypto.subtle.generateKey({ name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 }, true, ['encrypt', 'decrypt']);</code></li><li><code>const iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));</code></li><li><code>const secret = new TextEncoder().encode('Launch date: 14 March');</code></li><li><code>const box = await crypto.subtle.encrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, secret);</code></li><li><code>console.log(new TextDecoder().decode(await crypto.subtle.decrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, box)));</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const key = await crypto.subtle.generateKey({ name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 }, true, ['encrypt', 'decrypt']);\nconst iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));\nconst secret = new TextEncoder().encode('Launch date: 14 March');\nconst box = await crypto.subtle.encrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, secret);\nconsole.log(new TextDecoder().decode(await crypto.subtle.decrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, box)));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"crypto\\.subtle\\.generateKey\\(\\s*\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]AES-GCM['\"]\\s*,\\s*length\\s*:\\s*256\\s*\\}","text":"Line 1 calls <code>crypto.subtle.generateKey({ name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 }, ...)</code>"},{"re":"\\[\\s*['\"]encrypt['\"]\\s*,\\s*['\"]decrypt['\"]\\s*\\]","text":"The key's allowed uses go in an array: <code>['encrypt', 'decrypt']</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+iv\\s*=\\s*crypto\\.getRandomValues\\(\\s*new\\s+Uint8Array\\(\\s*12\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: a fresh 12-byte nonce: <code>const iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12));</code>"},{"re":"new\\s+TextEncoder\\(\\s*\\)\\.encode\\(\\s*['\"]Launch date: 14 March['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3 turns text into bytes with <code>new TextEncoder().encode(...)</code>."},{"re":"const\\s+box\\s*=\\s*await\\s+crypto\\.subtle\\.encrypt\\(\\s*\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]AES-GCM['\"]\\s*,\\s*iv\\s*\\}\\s*,\\s*key\\s*,\\s*secret\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>const box = await crypto.subtle.encrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, secret);</code>"},{"re":"crypto\\.subtle\\.decrypt\\(\\s*\\{\\s*name\\s*:\\s*['\"]AES-GCM['\"]\\s*,\\s*iv\\s*\\}\\s*,\\s*key\\s*,\\s*box\\s*\\)","text":"Line 5 decrypts with the same key and iv: <code>crypto.subtle.decrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, box)</code>, wrapped in <code>new TextDecoder().decode(await ...)</code>."}],"explain":"<p><code>box</code> is unreadable bytes. Only the same key and nonce turn it back into the message. Change one byte of <code>box</code> and decryption throws an error instead: GCM mode detects tampering, giving you integrity as well as confidentiality.</p>"},{"title":"Generate secure random values","file":"random.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Make a 16-byte random key and a random ID the safe way. Three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const key = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(16));</code></li><li><code>console.log(Array.from(key));</code></li><li><code>console.log(crypto.randomUUID());</code></li></ol>\n<p>Run it a few times: every result is different and unpredictable.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const key = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(16));\nconsole.log(Array.from(key));\nconsole.log(crypto.randomUUID());","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+key\\s*=\\s*crypto\\.getRandomValues\\(","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const key = crypto.getRandomValues(</code>"},{"re":"new\\s+Uint8Array\\(\\s*16\\s*\\)","text":"Inside: <code>new Uint8Array(16)</code>: an empty box of 16 bytes for it to fill."},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*Array\\.from\\(\\s*key\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(Array.from(key));</code>"},{"re":"crypto\\.randomUUID\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(crypto.randomUUID());</code> (UUID in capitals)."}],"explain":"<p>16 bytes is 128 bits of randomness, the size of an AES-128 key. <code>Math.random()</code> would look random but is predictable; these functions use the operating system's cryptographically secure generator.</p>"}],"text":"What cryptography is for Cryptography uses maths to protect information from people who should not read it or change it. It gives you four guarantees: Goal Question it answers Everyday example Confidentiality Can anyone else read this? Your card number over HTTPS Integrity Has this been changed? Checking a downloaded file is not tampered with Authenticity Who really sent this? Your bank's website certificate Non-repudiation Can the sender deny it later? Signing a blockchain transaction The four building blocks 1. Hash functions Turn any data into a short, fixed-size fingerprint. One-way: you cannot get the data back. Used for integrity, passwords and linking blocks in a blockchain. Next lesson, plus the Hash Lab . 2. Symmetric encryption One shared secret key locks and unlocks. Fast, used for bulk data. The standard is AES (in GCM mode, which also detects tampering). The hard part: how do two people agree a secret key over the internet without anyone overhearing? 3. Asymmetric (public-key) cryptography Each person has a key pair : a private key they keep secret and a public key they share. What one key does, only the other can undo. This solves the key-sharing problem and enables signatures. 4. Digital signatures Sign with your private key; anyone verifies with your public key. Proves who sent a message and that it was not altered. Every blockchain transaction is a digital signature. Real systems combine them. HTTPS uses public-key crypto to agree a shared key, symmetric encryption for the data, hashes for integrity, and signed certificates for authenticity. Try encryption in your browser The Web Crypto API is built into every modern browser. This encrypts and decrypts a message with AES-GCM: const enc = new TextEncoder(); const dec = new TextDecoder(); // 1. Make a random 256-bit key (in real life, store or derive this safely) const key = await crypto.subtle.generateKey({ name: 'AES-GCM', length: 256 }, true, ['encrypt', 'decrypt']); // 2. A fresh random nonce (iv) for every message. Never reuse one with the same key. const iv = crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(12)); // 3. Encrypt const secret = enc.encode('Launch date: 14 March'); const ciphertext = await crypto.subtle.encrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, secret); console.log('Encrypted bytes:', [...new Uint8Array(ciphertext)].slice(0, 16).join(','), '...'); // 4. Decrypt with the same key and iv const plain = await crypto.subtle.decrypt({ name: 'AES-GCM', iv }, key, ciphertext); console.log('Decrypted:', dec.decode(plain)); Randomness matters Keys must be unpredictable. Use crypto.getRandomValues() or crypto.randomUUID() . Never use Math.random() for anything security-related; it is predictable. Kerckhoffs's principle A system must be secure even if everything about it except the key is public . Good algorithms (AES, SHA-256, Ed25519) are published and studied by thousands of experts. Secret, home-made algorithms are almost always broken. The one rule: do not roll your own crypto Never invent your own algorithm, and avoid implementing standard ones yourself for real use. Subtle mistakes (reused nonces, timing leaks, bad randomness) break systems completely. Use well-reviewed libraries: the Web Crypto API, libsodium, @noble/hashes and @noble/curves, OpenZeppelin for contracts. (The SHA-256 in this site's Hash Lab is hand-written for learning only.)","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-hashing","title":"Hash functions hands-on","level":"beginner","minutes":20,"summary":"The properties that make SHA-256 and Keccak-256 secure fingerprints, and where hashes are used everywhere.","html":"<h2 id=\"a-digital-fingerprint\">A digital fingerprint</h2>\n<p>A <strong>hash function</strong> takes input of any size and returns a fixed-size output called a <strong>hash</strong> or <strong>digest</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">SHA-256(&quot;hello&quot;)  = 2cf24dba5fb0a30e26e83b2ac5b9e29e1b161e5c1fa7425e73043362938b9824\nSHA-256(&quot;Hello&quot;)  = 185f8db32271fe25f561a6fc938b2e264306ec304eda518007d1764826381969</code></pre></div>\n<p>Always 256 bits, shown as 64 hexadecimal characters (each hex character is 4 bits), whether the input is one letter or a whole film.</p>\n<p>Open the <a href=\"#hash-lab\">Hash Lab</a> in another tab and type as you read.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-properties-that-matter\">The properties that matter</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Deterministic</strong></td><td>Same input, same hash, every time</td><td>Anyone can re-check it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fast to compute</strong></td><td>Milliseconds for normal data</td><td>Practical to use everywhere</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pre-image resistant</strong></td><td>Given a hash, you cannot find an input that produces it</td><td>One-way: hashes do not reveal data</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Collision resistant</strong></td><td>You cannot find two different inputs with the same hash</td><td>A hash uniquely identifies data in practice</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Avalanche effect</strong></td><td>Change one bit of input and about half the output bits flip</td><td>No patterns to exploit</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"where-hashes-are-used\">Where hashes are used</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>File integrity</strong>: download pages publish a SHA-256. Hash your download; if it matches, nobody tampered with it.</li><li><strong>Git</strong>: every commit is identified by a hash of its contents and its parent's hash.</li><li><strong>Blockchains</strong>: each block contains the hash of the previous block. Change history and every later hash breaks.</li><li><strong>Proof of work</strong>: miners search for an input whose hash starts with many zeros.</li><li><strong>Merkle trees</strong>: summarise thousands of items in one hash, with tiny proofs that an item is included.</li><li><strong>Commitments</strong>: publish a hash of your answer now, reveal the answer later; everyone can check you did not change it.</li><li><strong>Ethereum</strong> uses <strong>Keccak-256</strong> for addresses, function selectors and storage locations.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"hashing-in-code\">Hashing in code</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">async function sha256Hex(text) {\n  const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode(text);\n  const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes);\n  return Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest), (b) =&gt; b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');\n}\n\nconsole.log(await sha256Hex('hello'));\nconsole.log(await sha256Hex('hello '));   // one extra space: a completely different hash</code></pre></div>\n<p>In Solidity:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">bytes32 h = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(&quot;hello&quot;));</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"hashes-are-not-encryption\">Hashes are not encryption</h2>\n<p>There is no key and no way back. That makes hashes perfect for checking, not for hiding data you need to read again.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Passwords need slow hashes</p><p>SHA-256 is designed to be fast, which is exactly wrong for passwords: attackers can try billions of guesses per second. Passwords need deliberately slow, salted algorithms like <strong>Argon2id</strong> or <strong>bcrypt</strong>.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"broken-hashes\">Broken hashes</h2>\n<p><strong>MD5</strong> and <strong>SHA-1</strong> have practical collision attacks. Do not use them for security. Modern choices: SHA-256, SHA-3 / Keccak-256, BLAKE2 and BLAKE3.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"You change one letter of a 10-page document. What happens to its SHA-256 hash?","options":["One character of the hash changes","It becomes completely different, with about half the bits flipped","It stays the same"],"answer":1,"explain":"The avalanche effect makes every change produce an unpredictable new hash."},{"q":"What does pre-image resistance mean?","options":["Given a hash, you cannot work out an input that produces it","Two inputs can never have the same hash","Hashes are always 256 bits"],"answer":0,"explain":"That one-way property is why hashes can be published safely."},{"q":"Which hash function does Ethereum use for addresses and function selectors?","options":["MD5","SHA-1","Keccak-256"],"answer":2,"explain":"Ethereum uses Keccak-256, a close relative of the SHA-3 standard."},{"q":"Why is plain SHA-256 a bad choice for storing passwords?","options":["It is too slow","It is so fast that attackers can try billions of guesses per second","It produces collisions"],"answer":1,"explain":"Password storage needs slow, salted algorithms like Argon2id or bcrypt."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Hash text with SHA-256","file":"hash.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode('hello');</code></li><li><code>const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes);</code></li><li><code>const hex = Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest), (b) =&gt; b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');</code></li><li><code>console.log(hex);</code></li></ol>\n<p>The output should start with <code>2cf24dba</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode('hello');\nconst digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes);\nconst hex = Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest), (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');\nconsole.log(hex);","also":["const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode('hello');\nconst digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes);\nconst hex = Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest), b => b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');\nconsole.log(hex);"],"hints":[{"re":"new\\s+TextEncoder\\(\\s*\\)\\.encode\\(\\s*['\"]hello['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode('hello');</code>"},{"re":"await\\s+crypto\\.subtle\\.digest\\(\\s*['\"]SHA-256['\"]\\s*,\\s*bytes\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes);</code>"},{"re":"Array\\.from\\(\\s*new\\s+Uint8Array\\(\\s*digest\\s*\\)\\s*,","text":"Line 3 starts <code>const hex = Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest),</code>"},{"re":"b\\.toString\\(\\s*16\\s*\\)\\.padStart\\(\\s*2\\s*,\\s*['\"]0['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Each byte becomes two hex characters: <code>b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')</code>"},{"re":"\\)\\.join\\(\\s*['\"]['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Join them with an empty string: <code>.join('')</code>"}],"explain":"<p><code>padStart(2, '0')</code> matters: the byte 10 is <code>a</code> in hex, but it must be written <code>0a</code>. Without padding, some hashes would come out shorter than 64 characters and would never match.</p>"},{"title":"Hash in Solidity","file":"Hash.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write one Solidity line: a <code>bytes32</code> called <code>h</code> equal to <code>keccak256(abi.encodePacked(&quot;hello&quot;))</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"bytes32 h = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(\"hello\"));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*bytes32\\s+h\\s*=","text":"Start with the type and name: <code>bytes32 h =</code>"},{"re":"keccak256\\(\\s*abi\\.encodePacked\\(\\s*[\"']hello[\"']\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"<code>keccak256(abi.encodePacked(&quot;hello&quot;))</code>"},{"re":"\\)\\s*;\\s*$","text":"Solidity needs a semicolon at the end."}],"explain":""}],"text":"A digital fingerprint A hash function takes input of any size and returns a fixed-size output called a hash or digest . SHA-256( hello ) = 2cf24dba5fb0a30e26e83b2ac5b9e29e1b161e5c1fa7425e73043362938b9824 SHA-256( Hello ) = 185f8db32271fe25f561a6fc938b2e264306ec304eda518007d1764826381969 Always 256 bits, shown as 64 hexadecimal characters (each hex character is 4 bits), whether the input is one letter or a whole film. Open the Hash Lab in another tab and type as you read. The properties that matter Property Meaning Why it matters Deterministic Same input, same hash, every time Anyone can re-check it Fast to compute Milliseconds for normal data Practical to use everywhere Pre-image resistant Given a hash, you cannot find an input that produces it One-way: hashes do not reveal data Collision resistant You cannot find two different inputs with the same hash A hash uniquely identifies data in practice Avalanche effect Change one bit of input and about half the output bits flip No patterns to exploit Where hashes are used File integrity : download pages publish a SHA-256. Hash your download; if it matches, nobody tampered with it. Git : every commit is identified by a hash of its contents and its parent's hash. Blockchains : each block contains the hash of the previous block. Change history and every later hash breaks. Proof of work : miners search for an input whose hash starts with many zeros. Merkle trees : summarise thousands of items in one hash, with tiny proofs that an item is included. Commitments : publish a hash of your answer now, reveal the answer later; everyone can check you did not change it. Ethereum uses Keccak-256 for addresses, function selectors and storage locations. Hashing in code async function sha256Hex(text) { const bytes = new TextEncoder().encode(text); const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes); return Array.from(new Uint8Array(digest), (b) = b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join(''); } console.log(await sha256Hex('hello')); console.log(await sha256Hex('hello ')); // one extra space: a completely different hash In Solidity: bytes32 h = keccak256(abi.encodePacked( hello )); Hashes are not encryption There is no key and no way back. That makes hashes perfect for checking, not for hiding data you need to read again. Passwords need slow hashes SHA-256 is designed to be fast, which is exactly wrong for passwords: attackers can try billions of guesses per second. Passwords need deliberately slow, salted algorithms like Argon2id or bcrypt . Broken hashes MD5 and SHA-1 have practical collision attacks. Do not use them for security. Modern choices: SHA-256, SHA-3 / Keccak-256, BLAKE2 and BLAKE3.","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-keys","title":"Keys, signatures and wallets","level":"beginner","minutes":25,"summary":"How a private key becomes an address, how signing works, what seed phrases really are, and how to keep funds safe.","html":"<h2 id=\"from-random-number-to-address\">From random number to address</h2>\n<p>On Ethereum (and Bitcoin), your identity is a key pair on the <strong>secp256k1</strong> elliptic curve:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Private key</strong>: a random 256-bit number. About 10^77 possibilities, so guessing one is hopeless if it is truly random.</li><li><strong>Public key</strong>: calculated from the private key with elliptic-curve multiplication. Easy forwards, practically impossible backwards.</li><li><strong>Address</strong>: the last 20 bytes of the Keccak-256 hash of the public key, written in hex: <code>0x71C7656EC7ab88b098defB751B7401B5f6d8976F</code>.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">private key  --(curve maths, one-way)--&gt;  public key  --(keccak256, last 20 bytes)--&gt;  address</code></pre></div>\n<p>Anyone can know your address. Only the private key can spend from it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"signatures\">Signatures</h2>\n<p>To send a transaction, your wallet <strong>signs</strong> it with the private key using <strong>ECDSA</strong>. Every node then checks the signature against your address. Nobody needs your private key to verify; nobody can forge a signature without it.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// npm install viem\nimport { generatePrivateKey, privateKeyToAccount } from 'viem/accounts';\nimport { verifyMessage } from 'viem';\n\nconst privateKey = generatePrivateKey();          // a throwaway key for learning only\nconst account = privateKeyToAccount(privateKey);\nconsole.log('Address:', account.address);\n\nconst message = 'I agree to the Code Ladder terms';\nconst signature = await account.signMessage({ message });\n\nconst valid = await verifyMessage({ address: account.address, message, signature });\nconsole.log('Signature valid?', valid);                                  // true\nconsole.log(await verifyMessage({ address: account.address, message: message + '!', signature })); // false</code></pre></div>\n<p>Change one character of the message and verification fails. That is integrity plus authenticity in one step.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"seed-phrases\">Seed phrases</h2>\n<p>Managing raw private keys is error-prone, so wallets use a <strong>seed phrase</strong> (also called a recovery phrase or mnemonic): 12 or 24 words from a standard list of 2,048 (BIP-39). From that one phrase, a wallet deterministically derives a tree of private keys (BIP-32 / BIP-44). The first Ethereum account uses the path <code>m/44'/60'/0'/0/0</code>.</p>\n<p>That is why restoring your 12 words in any compatible wallet brings back every account.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Your seed phrase IS your money</p><p>Anyone who sees your seed phrase controls every account derived from it, forever. Write it on paper (or metal), store it offline, never photograph it, never type it into a website, chat, email, cloud note or code file. No legitimate support person, airdrop or &quot;wallet sync&quot; will ever ask for it. If one does, it is a scam.</p></aside>\n<h2 id=\"types-of-wallet\">Types of wallet</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it is</th><th>Good for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Browser or mobile wallet</strong> (MetaMask, Rabby, Rainbow)</td><td>Keys stored, encrypted, on your device</td><td>Daily use, development</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hardware wallet</strong> (Ledger, Trezor)</td><td>Keys never leave a separate device; you confirm on its screen</td><td>Holding meaningful amounts</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Smart contract wallet</strong> (Safe, ERC-4337 wallets)</td><td>A contract controls funds, with rules like multi-signature or spending limits</td><td>Teams, treasuries, better recovery</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Custodial</strong> (exchange accounts)</td><td>Someone else holds the keys</td><td>Convenience, with counterparty risk (&quot;not your keys, not your coins&quot;)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"developer-key-hygiene\">Developer key hygiene</h2>\n<ul><li>Make a <strong>separate development wallet</strong> that only ever holds testnet tokens.</li><li>Never paste a private key with real funds into code, a <code>.env</code> file, or a terminal command.</li><li>For deployments, use Foundry's encrypted keystore (<code>cast wallet import</code>) or a hardware wallet.</li><li>Read what you are signing. Malicious sites ask for signatures that approve transfers of your tokens.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"What can someone do with only your Ethereum address?","options":["Send you tokens and see your public balance and history","Spend your tokens","Work out your private key"],"answer":0,"explain":"Addresses are public by design. Only the private key can authorise spending."},{"q":"How does a node know a transaction really came from your account?","options":["You email it your private key","It verifies the transaction's signature against your address","The wallet company confirms it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Signatures prove control of the private key without revealing it."},{"q":"A &quot;support agent&quot; in a Discord DM asks for your 12-word phrase to fix a stuck transaction. What should you do?","options":["Send the first six words only","Send it, then change it afterwards","Refuse and report it: it is a scam"],"answer":2,"explain":"Nobody legitimate ever needs your seed phrase. Six words also massively weakens it."},{"q":"Why does restoring a seed phrase bring back all your accounts?","options":["All private keys are derived deterministically from the seed","The blockchain stores your accounts by seed phrase","Wallets back up keys to the cloud"],"answer":0,"explain":"BIP-32/39/44 derive a whole tree of keys from one seed."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Sign and verify a message","file":"sign.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>With viem's functions already imported, write four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const account = privateKeyToAccount(generatePrivateKey());</code></li><li><code>const signature = await account.signMessage({ message: 'I agree' });</code></li><li><code>const valid = await verifyMessage({ address: account.address, message: 'I agree', signature });</code></li><li><code>console.log(valid);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const account = privateKeyToAccount(generatePrivateKey());\nconst signature = await account.signMessage({ message: 'I agree' });\nconst valid = await verifyMessage({ address: account.address, message: 'I agree', signature });\nconsole.log(valid);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"privateKeyToAccount\\(\\s*generatePrivateKey\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1: <code>const account = privateKeyToAccount(generatePrivateKey());</code>"},{"re":"account\\.signMessage\\(\\s*\\{\\s*message\\s*:\\s*['\"]I agree['\"]\\s*\\}\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>await account.signMessage({ message: 'I agree' })</code>"},{"re":"verifyMessage\\(\\s*\\{\\s*address\\s*:\\s*account\\.address\\s*,\\s*message\\s*:\\s*['\"]I agree['\"]\\s*,\\s*signature\\s*\\}\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>verifyMessage({ address: account.address, message: 'I agree', signature })</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*valid\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(valid);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>This needs viem installed (<code>npm install viem</code>), so there is no Run button here. It prints <code>true</code>. Change the message in line 3 and it prints <code>false</code>: the signature only matches the exact message it signed.</p>"}],"text":"From random number to address On Ethereum (and Bitcoin), your identity is a key pair on the secp256k1 elliptic curve: Private key : a random 256-bit number. About 10^77 possibilities, so guessing one is hopeless if it is truly random. Public key : calculated from the private key with elliptic-curve multiplication. Easy forwards, practically impossible backwards. Address : the last 20 bytes of the Keccak-256 hash of the public key, written in hex: 0x71C7656EC7ab88b098defB751B7401B5f6d8976F . private key --(curve maths, one-way)-- public key --(keccak256, last 20 bytes)-- address Anyone can know your address. Only the private key can spend from it. Signatures To send a transaction, your wallet signs it with the private key using ECDSA . Every node then checks the signature against your address. Nobody needs your private key to verify; nobody can forge a signature without it. // npm install viem import { generatePrivateKey, privateKeyToAccount } from 'viem/accounts'; import { verifyMessage } from 'viem'; const privateKey = generatePrivateKey(); // a throwaway key for learning only const account = privateKeyToAccount(privateKey); console.log('Address:', account.address); const message = 'I agree to the Code Ladder terms'; const signature = await account.signMessage({ message }); const valid = await verifyMessage({ address: account.address, message, signature }); console.log('Signature valid?', valid); // true console.log(await verifyMessage({ address: account.address, message: message + '!', signature })); // false Change one character of the message and verification fails. That is integrity plus authenticity in one step. Seed phrases Managing raw private keys is error-prone, so wallets use a seed phrase (also called a recovery phrase or mnemonic): 12 or 24 words from a standard list of 2,048 (BIP-39). From that one phrase, a wallet deterministically derives a tree of private keys (BIP-32 / BIP-44). The first Ethereum account uses the path m/44'/60'/0'/0/0 . That is why restoring your 12 words in any compatible wallet brings back every account. Your seed phrase IS your money Anyone who sees your seed phrase controls every account derived from it, forever. Write it on paper (or metal), store it offline, never photograph it, never type it into a website, chat, email, cloud note or code file. No legitimate support person, airdrop or wallet sync will ever ask for it. If one does, it is a scam. Types of wallet Type What it is Good for Browser or mobile wallet (MetaMask, Rabby, Rainbow) Keys stored, encrypted, on your device Daily use, development Hardware wallet (Ledger, Trezor) Keys never leave a separate device; you confirm on its screen Holding meaningful amounts Smart contract wallet (Safe, ERC-4337 wallets) A contract controls funds, with rules like multi-signature or spending limits Teams, treasuries, better recovery Custodial (exchange accounts) Someone else holds the keys Convenience, with counterparty risk ( not your keys, not your coins ) Developer key hygiene Make a separate development wallet that only ever holds testnet tokens. Never paste a private key with real funds into code, a .env file, or a terminal command. For deployments, use Foundry's encrypted keystore ( cast wallet import ) or a hardware wallet. Read what you are signing. Malicious sites ask for signatures that approve transfers of your tokens.","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-blockchain","title":"How blockchains work","level":"beginner","minutes":25,"summary":"Blocks, transactions, consensus, gas and accounts, explained with Ethereum, plus your first look at a real transaction.","html":"<h2 id=\"a-shared-ledger-nobody-owns\">A shared ledger nobody owns</h2>\n<p>A blockchain is a database that thousands of independent computers (<strong>nodes</strong>) keep identical copies of. Nobody can change past entries, and anyone can verify everything. It is useful when people who do not trust each other need to agree on who owns what, without a bank or company in the middle.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blocks-chained-by-hashes\">Blocks chained by hashes</h2>\n<p>Transactions are grouped into <strong>blocks</strong>. Each block includes the hash of the previous block:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">[Block 100]          [Block 101]               [Block 102]\n prev: 0x9a..   &lt;--   prev: hash(Block 100)  &lt;--  prev: hash(Block 101)\n txs: ...             txs: ...                     txs: ...</code></pre></div>\n<p>Change one transaction in block 100 and its hash changes, which breaks block 101's link, and 102's, all the way up. Everyone would see the forgery immediately.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-life-of-a-transaction\">The life of a transaction</h2>\n<ol><li>You build a transaction (send 0.1 ETH to Bob) and your wallet <strong>signs</strong> it.</li><li>It is broadcast to the network and waits in the <strong>mempool</strong> (pending transactions).</li><li>A <strong>validator</strong> includes it in a block, choosing transactions that pay higher fees first.</li><li>The block is shared, every node checks every signature and rule, and adds it to their copy.</li><li>After more blocks build on top, it becomes practically irreversible (<strong>finality</strong>).</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"consensus-agreeing-without-a-boss\">Consensus: agreeing without a boss</h2>\n<p>How do thousands of strangers agree on the next block?</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Proof of Work</strong> (Bitcoin): miners burn electricity searching for a hash below a target (try mining in the <a href=\"#hash-lab\">Hash Lab</a>). Rewriting history would need more computing power than the honest majority.</li><li><strong>Proof of Stake</strong> (Ethereum since 2022): validators lock up ETH as collateral (32 ETH per validator). They are randomly chosen to propose blocks and lose stake (&quot;slashing&quot;) if they cheat. It uses vastly less energy than proof of work.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"ethereum-accounts\">Ethereum accounts</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Controlled by</th><th>Has code?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Externally owned account (EOA)</strong></td><td>A private key</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Contract account</strong></td><td>Its code</td><td>Yes: a smart contract</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Every EOA has a <strong>nonce</strong>: a counter of transactions sent. It stops the same signed transaction being replayed twice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"units-and-gas\">Units and gas</h2>\n<p>ETH has 18 decimal places. The smallest unit is the <strong>wei</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Wei</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>wei</td><td>1</td><td>Contract maths</td></tr><tr><td>gwei</td><td>1,000,000,000 (10^9)</td><td>Gas prices</td></tr><tr><td>ether</td><td>1,000,000,000,000,000,000 (10^18)</td><td>Human amounts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Gas</strong> measures computation. A plain ETH transfer costs 21,000 gas; complex contract calls cost more. You pay <code>gas used x gas price</code>. Since EIP-1559, each block has a <strong>base fee</strong> (burned, destroyed forever) plus an optional <strong>priority fee</strong> (tip to the validator).</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Fee = 21,000 gas x (base 8 gwei + tip 1 gwei) = 189,000 gwei = 0.000189 ETH</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"layer-1-and-layer-2\">Layer 1 and Layer 2</h2>\n<p>Ethereum mainnet (Layer 1) is secure but limited in throughput, so fees rise when busy. <strong>Layer 2 rollups</strong> (Arbitrum, Optimism, Base, zkSync, Starknet and others) execute transactions cheaply, then post compressed data and proofs back to Ethereum. Most everyday activity now happens on L2s. Same Solidity code, same tools.</p>\n<h2 id=\"testnets\">Testnets</h2>\n<p><strong>Sepolia</strong> is Ethereum's main test network. Its ETH is free (from <strong>faucets</strong>) and worthless, so you can make every mistake there. Always deploy to a testnet first.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-try\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Read a real transaction</p><p>Go to sepolia.etherscan.io (or etherscan.io for mainnet), open any recent block, click a transaction, and find: from, to, value, gas used, gas price, the block number, and the status. Every number in this lesson is on that page.</p></aside>\n<p>You can also query the chain from your terminal once Foundry is installed:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">cast block-number --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL\ncast balance 0xYourAddress --ether --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL\ncast to-wei 0.1\ncast from-wei 189000000000000</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Why can't someone quietly edit a transaction in an old block?","options":["Old blocks are encrypted","Its hash would change and break the link in every later block, which every node checks","Only the original sender can read old blocks"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hash links make history tamper-evident."},{"q":"Ethereum's consensus mechanism since 2022 is:","options":["Proof of Work","Proof of Stake","Proof of Authority"],"answer":1,"explain":"The Merge moved Ethereum to proof of stake, cutting its energy use dramatically."},{"q":"How many wei are in 1 ether?","options":["1,000","10^9","10^18"],"answer":2,"explain":"18 decimals. Gwei (10^9 wei) is the unit used for gas prices."},{"q":"What happens to the base fee under EIP-1559?","options":["It goes to the validator","It is burned (destroyed)","It is refunded to the sender"],"answer":1,"explain":"The base fee is burned; only the priority fee (tip) goes to the validator."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Calculate a gas fee in wei","file":"fee.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Work out the fee for a plain transfer (21,000 gas) at 9 gwei, using BigInt (numbers ending in <code>n</code>). Four lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const gasUsed = 21000n;</code></li><li><code>const gasPriceWei = 9n * 10n ** 9n;</code> (9 gwei)</li><li><code>const feeWei = gasUsed * gasPriceWei;</code></li><li><code>console.log(feeWei.toString());</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const gasUsed = 21000n;\nconst gasPriceWei = 9n * 10n ** 9n;\nconst feeWei = gasUsed * gasPriceWei;\nconsole.log(feeWei.toString());","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+gasUsed\\s*=\\s*21000n","text":"Line 1: <code>const gasUsed = 21000n;</code> (the <code>n</code> makes it a BigInt)."},{"re":"const\\s+gasPriceWei\\s*=\\s*9n\\s*\\*\\s*10n\\s*\\*\\*\\s*9n","text":"Line 2: <code>const gasPriceWei = 9n * 10n ** 9n;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+feeWei\\s*=\\s*gasUsed\\s*\\*\\s*gasPriceWei","text":"Line 3: <code>const feeWei = gasUsed * gasPriceWei;</code>"},{"re":"feeWei\\.toString\\(\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(feeWei.toString());</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>189000000000000</code> wei, which is 0.000189 ETH. Wallets do exactly this maths with integers, then format it for display.</p>"},{"title":"Query the chain with cast","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Two Foundry commands: get the latest block number using the RPC URL in <code>$SEPOLIA_RPC_URL</code>, then convert <code>0.1</code> ether to wei.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"cast block-number --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL\ncast to-wei 0.1","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*cast\\s+block-number\\s+--rpc-url\\s+\\$SEPOLIA_RPC_URL\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>cast block-number --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*cast\\s+to-wei\\s+0\\.1\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>cast to-wei 0.1</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"A shared ledger nobody owns A blockchain is a database that thousands of independent computers ( nodes ) keep identical copies of. Nobody can change past entries, and anyone can verify everything. It is useful when people who do not trust each other need to agree on who owns what, without a bank or company in the middle. Blocks chained by hashes Transactions are grouped into blocks . Each block includes the hash of the previous block: [Block 100] [Block 101] [Block 102] prev: 0x9a.. -- prev: hash(Block 100) -- prev: hash(Block 101) txs: ... txs: ... txs: ... Change one transaction in block 100 and its hash changes, which breaks block 101's link, and 102's, all the way up. Everyone would see the forgery immediately. The life of a transaction You build a transaction (send 0.1 ETH to Bob) and your wallet signs it. It is broadcast to the network and waits in the mempool (pending transactions). A validator includes it in a block, choosing transactions that pay higher fees first. The block is shared, every node checks every signature and rule, and adds it to their copy. After more blocks build on top, it becomes practically irreversible ( finality ). Consensus: agreeing without a boss How do thousands of strangers agree on the next block? Proof of Work (Bitcoin): miners burn electricity searching for a hash below a target (try mining in the Hash Lab ). Rewriting history would need more computing power than the honest majority. Proof of Stake (Ethereum since 2022): validators lock up ETH as collateral (32 ETH per validator). They are randomly chosen to propose blocks and lose stake ( slashing ) if they cheat. It uses vastly less energy than proof of work. Ethereum accounts Type Controlled by Has code? Externally owned account (EOA) A private key No Contract account Its code Yes: a smart contract Every EOA has a nonce : a counter of transactions sent. It stops the same signed transaction being replayed twice. Units and gas ETH has 18 decimal places. The smallest unit is the wei : Unit Wei Used for wei 1 Contract maths gwei 1,000,000,000 (10^9) Gas prices ether 1,000,000,000,000,000,000 (10^18) Human amounts Gas measures computation. A plain ETH transfer costs 21,000 gas; complex contract calls cost more. You pay gas used x gas price . Since EIP-1559, each block has a base fee (burned, destroyed forever) plus an optional priority fee (tip to the validator). Fee = 21,000 gas x (base 8 gwei + tip 1 gwei) = 189,000 gwei = 0.000189 ETH Layer 1 and Layer 2 Ethereum mainnet (Layer 1) is secure but limited in throughput, so fees rise when busy. Layer 2 rollups (Arbitrum, Optimism, Base, zkSync, Starknet and others) execute transactions cheaply, then post compressed data and proofs back to Ethereum. Most everyday activity now happens on L2s. Same Solidity code, same tools. Testnets Sepolia is Ethereum's main test network. Its ETH is free (from faucets ) and worthless, so you can make every mistake there. Always deploy to a testnet first. Read a real transaction Go to sepolia.etherscan.io (or etherscan.io for mainnet), open any recent block, click a transaction, and find: from, to, value, gas used, gas price, the block number, and the status. Every number in this lesson is on that page. You can also query the chain from your terminal once Foundry is installed: cast block-number --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL cast balance 0xYourAddress --ether --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL cast to-wei 0.1 cast from-wei 189000000000000","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-solidity","title":"Solidity basics","level":"intermediate","minutes":35,"summary":"Write, compile and test your first smart contract with Foundry. Types, functions, errors, events and modifiers.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-a-smart-contract-is\">What a smart contract is</h2>\n<p>A smart contract is a program deployed to a blockchain. Once deployed, its code cannot be changed, it runs exactly as written for anyone who calls it, and its data (state) is public. Every write costs gas, so contracts are small and careful.</p>\n<p><strong>Solidity</strong> is the main language for Ethereum and every EVM chain (Arbitrum, Base, Polygon and many more).</p>\n<h2 id=\"set-up-a-foundry-project\">Set up a Foundry project</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">forge init tip-jar\ncd tip-jar\nforge build\nforge test</code></pre></div>\n<p>You get <code>src/</code> (contracts), <code>test/</code> (tests, also written in Solidity), <code>script/</code> (deployment scripts) and <code>foundry.toml</code> (config). For quick experiments with no install, remix.ethereum.org runs in the browser.</p>\n<h2 id=\"your-first-contract-a-tip-jar\">Your first contract: a tip jar</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\n/// @title A tip jar that only its owner can empty\ncontract TipJar {\n    // ---- state (stored on-chain, costs gas to write) ----\n    address public immutable owner;      // set once in the constructor\n    uint256 public totalTips;\n    mapping(address =&gt; uint256) public tipsFrom;\n\n    // ---- events (cheap logs that apps can listen to) ----\n    event Tipped(address indexed from, uint256 amount, string message);\n    event Withdrawn(uint256 amount);\n\n    // ---- custom errors (cheaper and clearer than strings) ----\n    error NotOwner();\n    error NoTipSent();\n    error TransferFailed();\n\n    constructor() {\n        owner = msg.sender;              // whoever deploys is the owner\n    }\n\n    modifier onlyOwner() {\n        if (msg.sender != owner) revert NotOwner();\n        _;                               // run the rest of the function here\n    }\n\n    function tip(string calldata message) external payable {\n        if (msg.value == 0) revert NoTipSent();\n        tipsFrom[msg.sender] += msg.value;\n        totalTips += msg.value;\n        emit Tipped(msg.sender, msg.value, message);\n    }\n\n    function balance() external view returns (uint256) {\n        return address(this).balance;\n    }\n\n    function withdraw() external onlyOwner {\n        uint256 amount = address(this).balance;\n        emit Withdrawn(amount);\n        (bool ok, ) = owner.call{value: amount}(&quot;&quot;);\n        if (!ok) revert TransferFailed();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-pieces-explained\">The pieces, explained</h2>\n<ul><li><code>pragma solidity ^0.8.24;</code> pins the compiler version. Since 0.8, arithmetic overflow reverts automatically.</li><li><strong>Types</strong>: <code>uint256</code> (whole numbers from 0 to 2^256 - 1; there are no decimals), <code>int256</code>, <code>address</code>, <code>bool</code>, <code>bytes32</code>, <code>string</code>, arrays and <code>struct</code>s.</li><li><code>mapping(address =&gt; uint256)</code> is a key-value lookup table. Every possible key exists and defaults to 0; you cannot loop over a mapping.</li><li><strong>Visibility</strong>: <code>public</code> (anyone, and auto-creates a getter for variables), <code>external</code> (only from outside), <code>internal</code> (this contract and children), <code>private</code> (this contract only). Note: private data is still <strong>readable by anyone</strong> on-chain. Nothing on a public chain is secret.</li><li><strong>State mutability</strong>: <code>view</code> reads state, <code>pure</code> reads nothing, <code>payable</code> can receive ETH. View and pure calls are free when called from outside.</li><li><strong>Globals</strong>: <code>msg.sender</code> (who called), <code>msg.value</code> (ETH sent, in wei), <code>block.timestamp</code> (current block time).</li><li><strong>Errors</strong>: <code>revert</code> undoes everything the transaction did. <code>require(condition, &quot;message&quot;)</code> also works; custom errors are cheaper.</li><li><strong>Events</strong>: logs that front ends and indexers read. Indexed parameters can be filtered.</li><li><strong>Data locations</strong>: <code>storage</code> (permanent, expensive), <code>memory</code> (temporary), <code>calldata</code> (read-only input, cheapest).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"test-it\">Test it</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// test/TipJar.t.sol\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\nimport {Test} from &quot;forge-std/Test.sol&quot;;\nimport {TipJar} from &quot;../src/TipJar.sol&quot;;\n\ncontract TipJarTest is Test {\n    TipJar jar;\n    address alice = makeAddr(&quot;alice&quot;);\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        jar = new TipJar();               // this test contract is the owner\n        vm.deal(alice, 10 ether);         // give alice some test ETH\n    }\n\n    receive() external payable {}         // let this test contract receive ETH on withdraw\n\n    function test_TipIsRecorded() public {\n        vm.prank(alice);                  // next call comes from alice\n        jar.tip{value: 1 ether}(&quot;great work&quot;);\n        assertEq(jar.totalTips(), 1 ether);\n        assertEq(jar.tipsFrom(alice), 1 ether);\n    }\n\n    function test_RevertWhen_TipIsZero() public {\n        vm.expectRevert(TipJar.NoTipSent.selector);\n        jar.tip(&quot;free&quot;);\n    }\n\n    function test_RevertWhen_NonOwnerWithdraws() public {\n        vm.prank(alice);\n        vm.expectRevert(TipJar.NotOwner.selector);\n        jar.withdraw();\n    }\n\n    function test_OwnerCanWithdraw() public {\n        vm.prank(alice);\n        jar.tip{value: 2 ether}(&quot;&quot;);\n        uint256 before = address(this).balance;\n        jar.withdraw();\n        assertEq(address(this).balance, before + 2 ether);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">forge test -vv</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>vm.prank</code>, <code>vm.deal</code> and <code>vm.expectRevert</code> are Foundry <strong>cheatcodes</strong> that let tests act as any address, set balances and assert failures.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Make the compiler your teacher</p><p>Run <code>forge build</code> often and read the warnings, not just the errors. Install the Solidity extension in VS Code for inline errors. When something confuses you, <code>chisel</code> (installed with Foundry) is a Solidity REPL where you can try expressions instantly.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Your contract stores a password in a private variable. Who can read it?","options":["Only the contract owner","Nobody","Anyone, by reading the contract's storage on-chain"],"answer":2,"explain":"private only stops other contracts reading it through code. All on-chain data is public."},{"q":"What does msg.value hold?","options":["The amount of ETH (in wei) sent with the call","The caller's balance","The gas price"],"answer":0,"explain":"msg.value is only non-zero for payable functions that were sent ETH."},{"q":"What happens when a function hits revert?","options":["Only the rest of that function is skipped","Every state change in the whole transaction is undone","The contract is deleted"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reverts are all-or-nothing, though the sender still pays for gas used."},{"q":"What does vm.prank(alice) do in a Foundry test?","options":["Gives alice ETH","Makes the next call appear to come from alice","Deploys a contract as alice"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cheatcodes let tests impersonate any address. vm.deal sets balances."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write the tip function","file":"TipJar.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>From memory if you can, write the <code>tip</code> function: it is <code>external payable</code>, takes <code>string calldata message</code>, reverts with <code>NoTipSent()</code> if no ETH was sent, adds <code>msg.value</code> to <code>tipsFrom[msg.sender]</code>, and emits <code>Tipped(msg.sender, msg.value, message)</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function tip(string calldata message) external payable {\n    if (msg.value == 0) revert NoTipSent();\n    tipsFrom[msg.sender] += msg.value;\n    emit Tipped(msg.sender, msg.value, message);\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+tip\\s*\\(\\s*string\\s+calldata\\s+message\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1 starts <code>function tip(string calldata message)</code>"},{"re":"\\)\\s*external\\s+payable\\s*\\{","text":"then <code>external payable {</code>. Without <code>payable</code> the function cannot receive ETH."},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*msg\\.value\\s*==\\s*0\\s*\\)\\s*revert\\s+NoTipSent\\(\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>if (msg.value == 0) revert NoTipSent();</code>"},{"re":"tipsFrom\\[\\s*msg\\.sender\\s*\\]\\s*\\+=\\s*msg\\.value\\s*;","text":"Line 3: <code>tipsFrom[msg.sender] += msg.value;</code>"},{"re":"emit\\s+Tipped\\(\\s*msg\\.sender\\s*,\\s*msg\\.value\\s*,\\s*message\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 4: <code>emit Tipped(msg.sender, msg.value, message);</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Write a modifier","file":"TipJar.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the <code>onlyOwner</code> modifier: if <code>msg.sender</code> is not <code>owner</code>, revert with <code>NotOwner()</code>; otherwise continue with <code>_;</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"modifier onlyOwner() {\n    if (msg.sender != owner) revert NotOwner();\n    _;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*modifier\\s+onlyOwner\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>modifier onlyOwner() {</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*msg\\.sender\\s*!=\\s*owner\\s*\\)\\s*revert\\s+NotOwner\\(\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>if (msg.sender != owner) revert NotOwner();</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*_\\s*;\\s*$","text":"Line 3 is just an underscore and a semicolon: <code>_;</code> It means &quot;now run the function&quot;."}],"explain":""},{"title":"Write a Foundry test","file":"TipJar.t.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write <code>test_TipIsRecorded</code>: as <code>alice</code> (<code>vm.prank</code>), tip 1 ether with the message <code>&quot;great work&quot;</code>, then assert <code>jar.totalTips()</code> equals <code>1 ether</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function test_TipIsRecorded() public {\n    vm.prank(alice);\n    jar.tip{value: 1 ether}(\"great work\");\n    assertEq(jar.totalTips(), 1 ether);\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+test_TipIsRecorded\\s*\\(\\s*\\)\\s*public\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function test_TipIsRecorded() public {</code>"},{"re":"vm\\.prank\\(\\s*alice\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>vm.prank(alice);</code>"},{"re":"jar\\.tip\\{\\s*value\\s*:\\s*1\\s+ether\\s*\\}\\(\\s*[\"']great work[\"']\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 3 sends ETH with curly braces before the arguments: <code>jar.tip{value: 1 ether}(&quot;great work&quot;);</code>"},{"re":"assertEq\\(\\s*jar\\.totalTips\\(\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*1\\s+ether\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 4: <code>assertEq(jar.totalTips(), 1 ether);</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"What a smart contract is A smart contract is a program deployed to a blockchain. Once deployed, its code cannot be changed, it runs exactly as written for anyone who calls it, and its data (state) is public. Every write costs gas, so contracts are small and careful. Solidity is the main language for Ethereum and every EVM chain (Arbitrum, Base, Polygon and many more). Set up a Foundry project forge init tip-jar cd tip-jar forge build forge test You get src/ (contracts), test/ (tests, also written in Solidity), script/ (deployment scripts) and foundry.toml (config). For quick experiments with no install, remix.ethereum.org runs in the browser. Your first contract: a tip jar // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT pragma solidity ^0.8.24; /// @title A tip jar that only its owner can empty contract TipJar { // ---- state (stored on-chain, costs gas to write) ---- address public immutable owner; // set once in the constructor uint256 public totalTips; mapping(address = uint256) public tipsFrom; // ---- events (cheap logs that apps can listen to) ---- event Tipped(address indexed from, uint256 amount, string message); event Withdrawn(uint256 amount); // ---- custom errors (cheaper and clearer than strings) ---- error NotOwner(); error NoTipSent(); error TransferFailed(); constructor() { owner = msg.sender; // whoever deploys is the owner } modifier onlyOwner() { if (msg.sender != owner) revert NotOwner(); _; // run the rest of the function here } function tip(string calldata message) external payable { if (msg.value == 0) revert NoTipSent(); tipsFrom[msg.sender] += msg.value; totalTips += msg.value; emit Tipped(msg.sender, msg.value, message); } function balance() external view returns (uint256) { return address(this).balance; } function withdraw() external onlyOwner { uint256 amount = address(this).balance; emit Withdrawn(amount); (bool ok, ) = owner.call{value: amount}( ); if (!ok) revert TransferFailed(); } } The pieces, explained pragma solidity ^0.8.24; pins the compiler version. Since 0.8, arithmetic overflow reverts automatically. Types : uint256 (whole numbers from 0 to 2^256 - 1; there are no decimals), int256 , address , bool , bytes32 , string , arrays and struct s. mapping(address = uint256) is a key-value lookup table. Every possible key exists and defaults to 0; you cannot loop over a mapping. Visibility : public (anyone, and auto-creates a getter for variables), external (only from outside), internal (this contract and children), private (this contract only). Note: private data is still readable by anyone on-chain. Nothing on a public chain is secret. State mutability : view reads state, pure reads nothing, payable can receive ETH. View and pure calls are free when called from outside. Globals : msg.sender (who called), msg.value (ETH sent, in wei), block.timestamp (current block time). Errors : revert undoes everything the transaction did. require(condition, message ) also works; custom errors are cheaper. Events : logs that front ends and indexers read. Indexed parameters can be filtered. Data locations : storage (permanent, expensive), memory (temporary), calldata (read-only input, cheapest). Test it // test/TipJar.t.sol // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT pragma solidity ^0.8.24; import {Test} from forge-std/Test.sol ; import {TipJar} from ../src/TipJar.sol ; contract TipJarTest is Test { TipJar jar; address alice = makeAddr( alice ); function setUp() public { jar = new TipJar(); // this test contract is the owner vm.deal(alice, 10 ether); // give alice some test ETH } receive() external payable {} // let this test contract receive ETH on withdraw function test_TipIsRecorded() public { vm.prank(alice); // next call comes from alice jar.tip{value: 1 ether}( great work ); assertEq(jar.totalTips(), 1 ether); assertEq(jar.tipsFrom(alice), 1 ether); } function test_RevertWhen_TipIsZero() public { vm.expectRevert(TipJar.NoTipSent.selector); jar.tip( free ); } function test_RevertWhen_NonOwnerWithdraws() public { vm.prank(alice); v","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-erc20","title":"Build and deploy an ERC-20 token","level":"intermediate","minutes":45,"summary":"Create a token with OpenZeppelin, test it with Foundry, deploy it to Sepolia with an encrypted key and verify it on Etherscan.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-erc-20-standard\">The ERC-20 standard</h2>\n<p>ERC-20 is the interface every fungible token on Ethereum implements, so wallets, exchanges and other contracts can work with any token the same way:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>totalSupply()</code></td><td>Total tokens in existence</td></tr><tr><td><code>balanceOf(account)</code></td><td>Tokens held by an account</td></tr><tr><td><code>transfer(to, amount)</code></td><td>Send your tokens</td></tr><tr><td><code>approve(spender, amount)</code></td><td>Allow another address (like a DEX) to spend up to <code>amount</code> of yours</td></tr><tr><td><code>allowance(owner, spender)</code></td><td>How much a spender may still spend</td></tr><tr><td><code>transferFrom(from, to, amount)</code></td><td>A spender moves tokens it was approved for</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Plus <code>Transfer</code> and <code>Approval</code> events, and <code>name</code>, <code>symbol</code> and <code>decimals</code> (almost always 18).</p>\n<p><strong>Decimals</strong> work like pence: balances are whole numbers. With 18 decimals, &quot;1 token&quot; is stored as <code>1 * 10**18</code>. Displaying it as 1.0 is the front end's job.</p>\n<h2 id=\"create-the-project\">Create the project</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">forge init ladder-token\ncd ladder-token\nforge install OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts\necho '@openzeppelin/contracts/=lib/openzeppelin-contracts/contracts/' &gt; remappings.txt</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-contract\">The contract</h2>\n<p>Never write ERC-20 logic from scratch for real use. Inherit OpenZeppelin's audited implementation and add only your rules:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// src/LadderToken.sol\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\nimport {ERC20} from &quot;@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/ERC20.sol&quot;;\nimport {Ownable} from &quot;@openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol&quot;;\n\ncontract LadderToken is ERC20, Ownable {\n    uint256 public constant MAX_SUPPLY = 1_000_000_000 ether;   // 1 billion tokens (ether = 10**18)\n\n    error MaxSupplyExceeded();\n\n    constructor(address initialOwner) ERC20(&quot;Ladder&quot;, &quot;LDR&quot;) Ownable(initialOwner) {\n        _mint(initialOwner, 100_000_000 ether);   // 10% minted at launch\n    }\n\n    function mint(address to, uint256 amount) external onlyOwner {\n        if (totalSupply() + amount &gt; MAX_SUPPLY) revert MaxSupplyExceeded();\n        _mint(to, amount);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"test-it\">Test it</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// test/LadderToken.t.sol\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\nimport {Test} from &quot;forge-std/Test.sol&quot;;\nimport {Ownable} from &quot;@openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol&quot;;\nimport {LadderToken} from &quot;../src/LadderToken.sol&quot;;\n\ncontract LadderTokenTest is Test {\n    LadderToken token;\n    address owner = makeAddr(&quot;owner&quot;);\n    address alice = makeAddr(&quot;alice&quot;);\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        token = new LadderToken(owner);\n    }\n\n    function test_InitialSupplyGoesToOwner() public view {\n        assertEq(token.balanceOf(owner), 100_000_000 ether);\n        assertEq(token.decimals(), 18);\n    }\n\n    function test_Transfer() public {\n        vm.prank(owner);\n        token.transfer(alice, 50 ether);\n        assertEq(token.balanceOf(alice), 50 ether);\n    }\n\n    function test_RevertWhen_StrangerMints() public {\n        vm.prank(alice);\n        vm.expectRevert(abi.encodeWithSelector(Ownable.OwnableUnauthorizedAccount.selector, alice));\n        token.mint(alice, 1 ether);\n    }\n\n    function test_RevertWhen_MintingPastMax() public {\n        vm.prank(owner);\n        vm.expectRevert(LadderToken.MaxSupplyExceeded.selector);\n        token.mint(owner, 900_000_000 ether + 1);\n    }\n\n    function testFuzz_TransferNeverCreatesTokens(uint256 amount) public {\n        amount = bound(amount, 0, token.balanceOf(owner));\n        uint256 supplyBefore = token.totalSupply();\n        vm.prank(owner);\n        token.transfer(alice, amount);\n        assertEq(token.totalSupply(), supplyBefore);\n        assertEq(token.balanceOf(owner) + token.balanceOf(alice), supplyBefore);\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">forge test -vv\nforge test --gas-report</code></pre></div>\n<p>The last test is a <strong>fuzz test</strong>: Foundry runs it with hundreds of random amounts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"deploy-to-sepolia\">Deploy to Sepolia</h2>\n<ol><li>Create a <strong>development wallet</strong> and store it encrypted (you choose a password):</li></ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">cast wallet new                       # prints a new address and private key (testnet use only)\ncast wallet import dev --interactive  # paste that private key; it is saved encrypted as &quot;dev&quot;\ncast wallet address --account dev</code></pre></div>\n<ol><li>Get free Sepolia ETH from a faucet (search &quot;Sepolia faucet&quot;; Alchemy and Google Cloud run reliable ones).</li><li>Get an RPC URL (free Alchemy or Infura account) and an Etherscan API key. Put them in <code>.env</code> (and make sure <code>.env</code> is in <code>.gitignore</code>):</li></ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">SEPOLIA_RPC_URL=https://eth-sepolia.g.alchemy.com/v2/your-key\nETHERSCAN_API_KEY=your-etherscan-key\nTOKEN_OWNER=0xYourDevAddress</code></pre></div>\n<ol><li>Write a deploy script:</li></ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// script/DeployLadder.s.sol\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\nimport {Script} from &quot;forge-std/Script.sol&quot;;\nimport {LadderToken} from &quot;../src/LadderToken.sol&quot;;\n\ncontract DeployLadder is Script {\n    function run() external returns (LadderToken token) {\n        address owner = vm.envAddress(&quot;TOKEN_OWNER&quot;);\n        vm.startBroadcast();\n        token = new LadderToken(owner);\n        vm.stopBroadcast();\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<ol><li>Deploy and verify:</li></ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">source .env\nforge script script/DeployLadder.s.sol --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL --account dev --broadcast --verify</code></pre></div>\n<p>Find the contract address in the output, open it on sepolia.etherscan.io, and import the token into your wallet. You made a token.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Testnet first, real money much later</p><p>Deploying to mainnet costs real money and is permanent. Many tokens also raise legal questions (see the Tokenomics track's launch lesson). Use testnets until you have written tests, run Slither, and understand every line.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"2\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"A token has 18 decimals. How is a balance of 2.5 tokens stored on-chain?","options":["2.5","2500000000000000000 (2.5 x 10^18)","25"],"answer":1,"explain":"On-chain amounts are integers. The front end divides by 10^decimals for display."},{"q":"Why does a DEX ask you to approve before swapping your tokens?","options":["So it can call transferFrom to move your tokens on your behalf, up to the approved amount","To check your identity","To pay gas for you"],"answer":0,"explain":"approve plus transferFrom lets contracts pull tokens you authorised."},{"q":"Why inherit from OpenZeppelin's ERC20 instead of writing your own?","options":["It is required by Ethereum","It is audited and battle-tested, so subtle bugs are far less likely","It uses less storage"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reusing audited code is the single biggest security win in Solidity."},{"q":"What does <code>cast wallet import dev --interactive</code> do?","options":["Uploads your key to Etherscan","Stores a private key encrypted on your machine under the name &quot;dev&quot;","Creates a new testnet"],"answer":1,"explain":"Keystores avoid having raw private keys in .env files or shell history."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Create the project","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Three commands: create a Foundry project called <code>ladder-token</code>, move into it, and install the OpenZeppelin contracts library.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"forge init ladder-token\ncd ladder-token\nforge install OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*forge\\s+init\\s+ladder-token\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>forge init ladder-token</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*cd\\s+ladder-token\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>cd ladder-token</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*forge\\s+install\\s+OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>forge install OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts</code> (GitHub owner/repo)."}],"explain":""},{"title":"Write the token contract","file":"LadderToken.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the core of the token (imports and pragma omitted): a contract <code>LadderToken</code> that inherits <code>ERC20</code> and <code>Ownable</code>, with a constructor taking <code>address initialOwner</code> that sets the name <code>&quot;Ladder&quot;</code>, symbol <code>&quot;LDR&quot;</code>, owner <code>initialOwner</code>, and mints <code>100_000_000 ether</code> to <code>initialOwner</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"contract LadderToken is ERC20, Ownable {\n    constructor(address initialOwner) ERC20(\"Ladder\", \"LDR\") Ownable(initialOwner) {\n        _mint(initialOwner, 100_000_000 ether);\n    }\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*contract\\s+LadderToken\\s+is\\s+ERC20\\s*,\\s*Ownable\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>contract LadderToken is ERC20, Ownable {</code>"},{"re":"constructor\\(\\s*address\\s+initialOwner\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2 starts <code>constructor(address initialOwner)</code>"},{"re":"ERC20\\(\\s*[\"']Ladder[\"']\\s*,\\s*[\"']LDR[\"']\\s*\\)\\s*Ownable\\(\\s*initialOwner\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"then call both parent constructors: <code>ERC20(&quot;Ladder&quot;, &quot;LDR&quot;) Ownable(initialOwner) {</code>"},{"re":"_mint\\(\\s*initialOwner\\s*,\\s*100_000_000\\s+ether\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 3: <code>_mint(initialOwner, 100_000_000 ether);</code>"},{"re":"\\}\\s*\\n\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"Close the constructor and the contract: two <code>}</code>."}],"explain":""},{"title":"Deploy and verify","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the single command that runs <code>script/DeployLadder.s.sol</code> against <code>$SEPOLIA_RPC_URL</code>, signs with the keystore account <code>dev</code>, broadcasts the transaction, and verifies the contract. Flags in this order: <code>--rpc-url</code>, <code>--account</code>, <code>--broadcast</code>, <code>--verify</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"forge script script/DeployLadder.s.sol --rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL --account dev --broadcast --verify","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*forge\\s+script\\s+script/DeployLadder\\.s\\.sol","text":"Start with <code>forge script script/DeployLadder.s.sol</code>"},{"re":"--rpc-url\\s+\\$SEPOLIA_RPC_URL","text":"then <code>--rpc-url $SEPOLIA_RPC_URL</code>"},{"re":"--account\\s+dev","text":"then <code>--account dev</code>"},{"re":"--broadcast\\s+--verify\\s*$","text":"finish with <code>--broadcast --verify</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"The ERC-20 standard ERC-20 is the interface every fungible token on Ethereum implements, so wallets, exchanges and other contracts can work with any token the same way: Function Does totalSupply() Total tokens in existence balanceOf(account) Tokens held by an account transfer(to, amount) Send your tokens approve(spender, amount) Allow another address (like a DEX) to spend up to amount of yours allowance(owner, spender) How much a spender may still spend transferFrom(from, to, amount) A spender moves tokens it was approved for Plus Transfer and Approval events, and name , symbol and decimals (almost always 18). Decimals work like pence: balances are whole numbers. With 18 decimals, 1 token is stored as 1 * 10**18 . Displaying it as 1.0 is the front end's job. Create the project forge init ladder-token cd ladder-token forge install OpenZeppelin/openzeppelin-contracts echo '@openzeppelin/contracts/=lib/openzeppelin-contracts/contracts/' remappings.txt The contract Never write ERC-20 logic from scratch for real use. Inherit OpenZeppelin's audited implementation and add only your rules: // src/LadderToken.sol // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT pragma solidity ^0.8.24; import {ERC20} from @openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/ERC20.sol ; import {Ownable} from @openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol ; contract LadderToken is ERC20, Ownable { uint256 public constant MAX_SUPPLY = 1_000_000_000 ether; // 1 billion tokens (ether = 10**18) error MaxSupplyExceeded(); constructor(address initialOwner) ERC20( Ladder , LDR ) Ownable(initialOwner) { _mint(initialOwner, 100_000_000 ether); // 10% minted at launch } function mint(address to, uint256 amount) external onlyOwner { if (totalSupply() + amount MAX_SUPPLY) revert MaxSupplyExceeded(); _mint(to, amount); } } Test it // test/LadderToken.t.sol // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT pragma solidity ^0.8.24; import {Test} from forge-std/Test.sol ; import {Ownable} from @openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol ; import {LadderToken} from ../src/LadderToken.sol ; contract LadderTokenTest is Test { LadderToken token; address owner = makeAddr( owner ); address alice = makeAddr( alice ); function setUp() public { token = new LadderToken(owner); } function test_InitialSupplyGoesToOwner() public view { assertEq(token.balanceOf(owner), 100_000_000 ether); assertEq(token.decimals(), 18); } function test_Transfer() public { vm.prank(owner); token.transfer(alice, 50 ether); assertEq(token.balanceOf(alice), 50 ether); } function test_RevertWhen_StrangerMints() public { vm.prank(alice); vm.expectRevert(abi.encodeWithSelector(Ownable.OwnableUnauthorizedAccount.selector, alice)); token.mint(alice, 1 ether); } function test_RevertWhen_MintingPastMax() public { vm.prank(owner); vm.expectRevert(LadderToken.MaxSupplyExceeded.selector); token.mint(owner, 900_000_000 ether + 1); } function testFuzz_TransferNeverCreatesTokens(uint256 amount) public { amount = bound(amount, 0, token.balanceOf(owner)); uint256 supplyBefore = token.totalSupply(); vm.prank(owner); token.transfer(alice, amount); assertEq(token.totalSupply(), supplyBefore); assertEq(token.balanceOf(owner) + token.balanceOf(alice), supplyBefore); } } forge test -vv forge test --gas-report The last test is a fuzz test : Foundry runs it with hundreds of random amounts. Deploy to Sepolia Create a development wallet and store it encrypted (you choose a password): cast wallet new # prints a new address and private key (testnet use only) cast wallet import dev --interactive # paste that private key; it is saved encrypted as dev cast wallet address --account dev Get free Sepolia ETH from a faucet (search Sepolia faucet ; Alchemy and Google Cloud run reliable ones). Get an RPC URL (free Alchemy or Infura account) and an Etherscan API key. Put them in .env (and make sure .env is in .gitignore ): SEPOLIA_RPC_URL=https://eth-sepolia.g.alchemy.com/v2/your-key ETHERSCAN_API_KEY=your-etherscan-key TOKEN_OWNER=0xYourDevAddress Write a deploy script: // script/DeployLadder.s.","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-dapp","title":"Talking to the chain from JavaScript","level":"intermediate","minutes":35,"summary":"Read contract data with viem, connect wallets with wagmi and RainbowKit, send transactions and handle every state.","html":"<h2 id=\"how-a-dapp-is-wired\">How a dApp is wired</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Your React app  --reads--&gt;   RPC provider (Alchemy, Infura, public node)  --&gt;  blockchain\n       |\n       +--writes--&gt; user's wallet (MetaMask, Rabby...) --signs and sends--&gt;  blockchain</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Reading</strong> (balances, prices, contract state) is free and needs no wallet. You call an RPC endpoint.</li><li><strong>Writing</strong> (transfers, mints, swaps) changes state, costs gas, and must be signed by the user's wallet. Your app never sees their private key.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"reading-with-viem\">Reading with viem</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// npm install viem\nimport { createPublicClient, http, erc20Abi, formatUnits } from 'viem';\nimport { sepolia } from 'viem/chains';\n\nconst client = createPublicClient({ chain: sepolia, transport: http(import.meta.env.VITE_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL) });\n\nconst token = '0xYourTokenAddress';\nconst holder = '0xSomeHolderAddress';\n\nconst [symbol, decimals, raw] = await Promise.all([\n  client.readContract({ address: token, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'symbol' }),\n  client.readContract({ address: token, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'decimals' }),\n  client.readContract({ address: token, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'balanceOf', args: [holder] }),\n]);\n\nconsole.log(`${formatUnits(raw, decimals)} ${symbol}`);   // e.g. &quot;50 LDR&quot;\nconsole.log('Latest block:', await client.getBlockNumber());</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li>An <strong>ABI</strong> (Application Binary Interface) is the JSON description of a contract's functions. Foundry writes it to <code>out/YourContract.sol/YourContract.json</code>. viem ships <code>erc20Abi</code> for standard tokens.</li><li>Balances come back as <code>bigint</code>. Use <code>formatUnits</code> to display and <code>parseUnits('2.5', 18)</code> to convert user input. Never use floating-point numbers for token amounts.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">RPC URLs in front-end code</p><p>An RPC key in front-end code is visible to everyone. Providers expect this: restrict the key to your domain in their dashboard. Never put a <strong>private key</strong> anywhere near front-end code.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"connecting-wallets-with-wagmi-and-rainbowkit\">Connecting wallets with wagmi and RainbowKit</h2>\n<p>wagmi gives React hooks for accounts, reads and writes; RainbowKit adds a polished &quot;Connect wallet&quot; button and modal.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">npm install @rainbow-me/rainbowkit wagmi viem @tanstack/react-query</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">// main.tsx\nimport '@rainbow-me/rainbowkit/styles.css';\nimport { getDefaultConfig, RainbowKitProvider } from '@rainbow-me/rainbowkit';\nimport { WagmiProvider } from 'wagmi';\nimport { sepolia } from 'wagmi/chains';\nimport { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/react-query';\n\nconst config = getDefaultConfig({\n  appName: 'Ladder dApp',\n  projectId: 'YOUR_WALLETCONNECT_PROJECT_ID',   // free from WalletConnect / Reown dashboard\n  chains: [sepolia],\n});\nconst queryClient = new QueryClient();\n\nexport function Providers({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {\n  return (\n    &lt;WagmiProvider config={config}&gt;\n      &lt;QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}&gt;\n        &lt;RainbowKitProvider&gt;{children}&lt;/RainbowKitProvider&gt;\n      &lt;/QueryClientProvider&gt;\n    &lt;/WagmiProvider&gt;\n  );\n}</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"reading-and-writing-with-hooks\">Reading and writing with hooks</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"tsx\"><pre><code class=\"language-tsx\">import { ConnectButton } from '@rainbow-me/rainbowkit';\nimport { useAccount, useReadContract, useWriteContract, useWaitForTransactionReceipt } from 'wagmi';\nimport { erc20Abi, formatUnits, parseUnits } from 'viem';\n\nconst TOKEN = '0xYourTokenAddress' as const;\n\nexport function TokenPanel() {\n  const { address, isConnected } = useAccount();\n\n  const { data: balance, refetch } = useReadContract({\n    address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'balanceOf',\n    args: address ? [address] : undefined,\n    query: { enabled: Boolean(address) },\n  });\n\n  const { writeContract, data: hash, isPending, error } = useWriteContract();\n  const { isLoading: confirming, isSuccess } = useWaitForTransactionReceipt({ hash });\n\n  const send = () =&gt;\n    writeContract({ address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'transfer', args: ['0xFriendAddress', parseUnits('1', 18)] });\n\n  return (\n    &lt;section&gt;\n      &lt;ConnectButton /&gt;\n      {isConnected &amp;&amp; &lt;p&gt;Balance: {balance !== undefined ? formatUnits(balance, 18) : '...'} LDR&lt;/p&gt;}\n      &lt;button onClick={send} disabled={!isConnected || isPending || confirming}&gt;\n        {isPending ? 'Confirm in your wallet...' : confirming ? 'Waiting for the block...' : 'Send 1 LDR'}\n      &lt;/button&gt;\n      {hash &amp;&amp; &lt;a href={`https://sepolia.etherscan.io/tx/${hash}`} target=&quot;_blank&quot; rel=&quot;noreferrer&quot;&gt;View transaction&lt;/a&gt;}\n      {isSuccess &amp;&amp; &lt;p&gt;Sent. &lt;button onClick={() =&gt; refetch()}&gt;Refresh balance&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/p&gt;}\n      {error &amp;&amp; &lt;p role=&quot;alert&quot;&gt;{error.message.split('\\n')[0]}&lt;/p&gt;}\n    &lt;/section&gt;\n  );\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"every-state-a-transaction-goes-through\">Every state a transaction goes through</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>What the user should see</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Not connected</td><td>A clear Connect button</td></tr><tr><td>Wrong network</td><td>&quot;Switch to Sepolia&quot; button</td></tr><tr><td>Waiting for signature</td><td>&quot;Confirm in your wallet&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Rejected by user</td><td>A calm message, not a scary error</td></tr><tr><td>Pending (in mempool)</td><td>Progress, plus a link to the explorer</td></tr><tr><td>Confirmed</td><td>Success, and refreshed balances</td></tr><tr><td>Reverted</td><td>The reason, in plain English</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Good dApps feel trustworthy because they handle all seven. Most bad ones only handle &quot;confirmed&quot;.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Simulate before you send</p><p>viem's <code>simulateContract</code> (and wagmi's <code>useSimulateContract</code>) runs the transaction against current state without sending it. If it would revert, you can show the reason before the user pays gas.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"Which operation needs the user's wallet to sign?","options":["Reading a token balance","Getting the latest block number","Transferring tokens"],"answer":2,"explain":"Reads are free calls to an RPC node. State changes must be signed and paid for."},{"q":"A balance comes back as 2500000000000000000n with 18 decimals. How do you display it?","options":["formatUnits(balance, 18), giving &quot;2.5&quot;","Number(balance) / 18","balance.toString()"],"answer":0,"explain":"formatUnits divides by 10^decimals safely using bigint maths."},{"q":"What is an ABI?","options":["The contract's source code","A JSON description of a contract's functions and events, so code knows how to call it","An API key"],"answer":1,"explain":"Front ends and scripts need the ABI to encode calls and decode results."},{"q":"Why simulate a transaction before sending it?","options":["It makes it cheaper","To catch reverts and show the reason before the user pays gas","Wallets require it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Failed transactions still cost gas. Simulating saves users money and confusion."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Read a token balance with viem","file":"balance.ts","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Using <code>client</code>, <code>token</code>, <code>holder</code>, <code>erc20Abi</code> and <code>formatUnits</code> from the example above, read <code>balanceOf(holder)</code> and print it with 18 decimals. Write it over several lines, one property per line, exactly like this shape:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ts\"><pre><code class=\"language-ts\">const balance = await client.readContract({\n  address: token,\n  abi: erc20Abi,\n  functionName: 'balanceOf',\n  args: [holder],\n});\nconsole.log(formatUnits(balance, 18));</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const balance = await client.readContract({\n  address: token,\n  abi: erc20Abi,\n  functionName: 'balanceOf',\n  args: [holder],\n});\nconsole.log(formatUnits(balance, 18));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+balance\\s*=\\s*await\\s+client\\.readContract\\(\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>const balance = await client.readContract({</code>"},{"re":"address\\s*:\\s*token\\s*,","text":"<code>address: token,</code>"},{"re":"abi\\s*:\\s*erc20Abi\\s*,","text":"<code>abi: erc20Abi,</code>"},{"re":"functionName\\s*:\\s*['\"]balanceOf['\"]\\s*,","text":"<code>functionName: 'balanceOf',</code>"},{"re":"args\\s*:\\s*\\[\\s*holder\\s*\\]\\s*,","text":"<code>args: [holder],</code> (arguments always go in an array)."},{"re":"formatUnits\\(\\s*balance\\s*,\\s*18\\s*\\)","text":"Last line: <code>console.log(formatUnits(balance, 18));</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Send a transfer from React","file":"TokenPanel.tsx","lang":"ts","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the single <code>writeContract</code> call that transfers 1 token (18 decimals) to <code>to</code>: address <code>TOKEN</code>, abi <code>erc20Abi</code>, functionName <code>'transfer'</code>, args <code>[to, parseUnits('1', 18)]</code>. All on one line.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"writeContract({ address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'transfer', args: [to, parseUnits('1', 18)] });","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*writeContract\\(\\s*\\{","text":"Start <code>writeContract({</code>"},{"re":"address\\s*:\\s*TOKEN\\s*,\\s*abi\\s*:\\s*erc20Abi\\s*,","text":"then <code>address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi,</code>"},{"re":"functionName\\s*:\\s*['\"]transfer['\"]\\s*,","text":"then <code>functionName: 'transfer',</code>"},{"re":"args\\s*:\\s*\\[\\s*to\\s*,\\s*parseUnits\\(\\s*['\"]1['\"]\\s*,\\s*18\\s*\\)\\s*\\]","text":"then <code>args: [to, parseUnits('1', 18)]</code>"},{"re":"\\}\\s*\\)\\s*;?\\s*$","text":"Close with <code>});</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"How a dApp is wired Your React app --reads-- RPC provider (Alchemy, Infura, public node) -- blockchain | +--writes-- user's wallet (MetaMask, Rabby...) --signs and sends-- blockchain Reading (balances, prices, contract state) is free and needs no wallet. You call an RPC endpoint. Writing (transfers, mints, swaps) changes state, costs gas, and must be signed by the user's wallet. Your app never sees their private key. Reading with viem // npm install viem import { createPublicClient, http, erc20Abi, formatUnits } from 'viem'; import { sepolia } from 'viem/chains'; const client = createPublicClient({ chain: sepolia, transport: http(import.meta.env.VITE_SEPOLIA_RPC_URL) }); const token = '0xYourTokenAddress'; const holder = '0xSomeHolderAddress'; const [symbol, decimals, raw] = await Promise.all([ client.readContract({ address: token, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'symbol' }), client.readContract({ address: token, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'decimals' }), client.readContract({ address: token, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'balanceOf', args: [holder] }), ]); console.log(`${formatUnits(raw, decimals)} ${symbol}`); // e.g. 50 LDR console.log('Latest block:', await client.getBlockNumber()); An ABI (Application Binary Interface) is the JSON description of a contract's functions. Foundry writes it to out/YourContract.sol/YourContract.json . viem ships erc20Abi for standard tokens. Balances come back as bigint . Use formatUnits to display and parseUnits('2.5', 18) to convert user input. Never use floating-point numbers for token amounts. RPC URLs in front-end code An RPC key in front-end code is visible to everyone. Providers expect this: restrict the key to your domain in their dashboard. Never put a private key anywhere near front-end code. Connecting wallets with wagmi and RainbowKit wagmi gives React hooks for accounts, reads and writes; RainbowKit adds a polished Connect wallet button and modal. npm install @rainbow-me/rainbowkit wagmi viem @tanstack/react-query // main.tsx import '@rainbow-me/rainbowkit/styles.css'; import { getDefaultConfig, RainbowKitProvider } from '@rainbow-me/rainbowkit'; import { WagmiProvider } from 'wagmi'; import { sepolia } from 'wagmi/chains'; import { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/react-query'; const config = getDefaultConfig({ appName: 'Ladder dApp', projectId: 'YOUR_WALLETCONNECT_PROJECT_ID', // free from WalletConnect / Reown dashboard chains: [sepolia], }); const queryClient = new QueryClient(); export function Providers({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) { return ( WagmiProvider config={config} QueryClientProvider client={queryClient} RainbowKitProvider {children} /RainbowKitProvider /QueryClientProvider /WagmiProvider ); } Reading and writing with hooks import { ConnectButton } from '@rainbow-me/rainbowkit'; import { useAccount, useReadContract, useWriteContract, useWaitForTransactionReceipt } from 'wagmi'; import { erc20Abi, formatUnits, parseUnits } from 'viem'; const TOKEN = '0xYourTokenAddress' as const; export function TokenPanel() { const { address, isConnected } = useAccount(); const { data: balance, refetch } = useReadContract({ address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'balanceOf', args: address ? [address] : undefined, query: { enabled: Boolean(address) }, }); const { writeContract, data: hash, isPending, error } = useWriteContract(); const { isLoading: confirming, isSuccess } = useWaitForTransactionReceipt({ hash }); const send = () = writeContract({ address: TOKEN, abi: erc20Abi, functionName: 'transfer', args: ['0xFriendAddress', parseUnits('1', 18)] }); return ( section ConnectButton / {isConnected p Balance: {balance !== undefined ? formatUnits(balance, 18) : '...'} LDR /p } button onClick={send} disabled={!isConnected || isPending || confirming} {isPending ? 'Confirm in your wallet...' : confirming ? 'Waiting for the block...' : 'Send 1 LDR'} /button {hash a href={`https://sepolia.etherscan.io/tx/${hash}`} target= _blank rel= noreferrer View trans","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-standards","title":"\"Token standards: ERC-20, 721, 1155 and friends\"","level":"intermediate","minutes":25,"summary":"Fungible tokens, NFTs, multi-tokens, vaults and signed approvals, and the risks in each.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-standards-matter\">Why standards matter</h2>\n<p>A standard (an <strong>ERC</strong>, Ethereum Request for Comments) is an agreed interface. Because every ERC-20 token has <code>transfer</code> and <code>balanceOf</code>, any wallet or exchange supports any token without custom code. Use standards, and use OpenZeppelin's implementations of them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-ones-you-will-meet\">The ones you will meet</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Standard</th><th>What it is</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>ERC-20</strong></td><td>Fungible tokens: every unit is identical</td><td>Stablecoins, governance tokens</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ERC-721</strong></td><td>Non-fungible tokens (NFTs): each token ID is unique</td><td>Art, tickets, memberships, domain names</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ERC-1155</strong></td><td>Many token types in one contract, fungible or not</td><td>Game items, editions, event passes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ERC-4626</strong></td><td>Tokenised vaults: deposit an asset, receive shares</td><td>Yield vaults, staking wrappers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>EIP-2612</strong></td><td><code>permit</code>: approve with a signature instead of a transaction</td><td>Gasless approvals</td></tr><tr><td><strong>EIP-712</strong></td><td>A standard for signing structured data readably</td><td>Orders, permits, off-chain votes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ERC-4337</strong></td><td>Account abstraction: smart contract wallets with sponsored gas, batching and recovery</td><td>Modern wallets</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"nfts-erc-721-in-brief\">NFTs (ERC-721) in brief</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.24;\n\nimport {ERC721} from &quot;@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC721/ERC721.sol&quot;;\nimport {Ownable} from &quot;@openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol&quot;;\n\ncontract StudioPass is ERC721, Ownable {\n    uint256 private _nextId = 1;\n\n    constructor(address initialOwner) ERC721(&quot;Studio Pass&quot;, &quot;PASS&quot;) Ownable(initialOwner) {}\n\n    function mint(address to) external onlyOwner returns (uint256 id) {\n        id = _nextId++;\n        _safeMint(to, id);\n    }\n\n    function _baseURI() internal pure override returns (string memory) {\n        return &quot;ipfs://YOUR_METADATA_FOLDER_CID/&quot;;\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each token's <code>tokenURI</code> points to <strong>metadata</strong> JSON (name, description, image). The image is usually on IPFS or Arweave, not on-chain, because storage on-chain is very expensive. If metadata lives on a normal web server, the NFT's appearance depends on that server staying up.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"approvals-powerful-and-dangerous\">Approvals: powerful and dangerous</h2>\n<p>ERC-20's <code>approve</code> lets a contract spend your tokens. Many apps ask for an <strong>unlimited</strong> approval so you never have to approve again. Convenient, but if that contract is later exploited, or was malicious from the start, it can drain every approved token.</p>\n<ul><li>As a user: approve only what you need, and revoke old approvals regularly (revoke.cash, or your wallet's approval manager).</li><li>As a builder: request exact amounts where practical, and support <code>permit</code> signatures.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Signatures can move money too</p><p>&quot;Just sign this message, it is free&quot; can be a <code>permit</code> signature that approves a transfer of your tokens. EIP-712 makes signatures readable in wallets: read them. Scam sites rely on people signing without reading.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"erc-4626-vaults\">ERC-4626 vaults</h2>\n<p>A vault takes deposits of one asset (say USDC) and mints <strong>shares</strong>. As the vault earns yield, each share is worth more of the asset. A standard interface (<code>deposit</code>, <code>withdraw</code>, <code>convertToAssets</code>) means any app can integrate any vault. The classic bug: rounding and &quot;inflation attacks&quot; on nearly empty vaults. OpenZeppelin's implementation includes protections; read its docs before building one.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"Which standard fits 500 identical concert tickets plus 20 unique backstage passes in one contract?","options":["ERC-20","ERC-721","ERC-1155"],"answer":2,"explain":"ERC-1155 handles many token types, fungible and non-fungible, in a single contract."},{"q":"Why is an unlimited token approval risky?","options":["If the approved contract is malicious or hacked, it can take all of that token from your wallet","It costs more gas every time","It expires after one day"],"answer":0,"explain":"Approvals persist until revoked. Approve only what you need."},{"q":"Where is an NFT's image usually stored?","options":["Inside the token contract's storage","Off-chain (often IPFS or Arweave), referenced by the token's metadata URI","In the buyer's wallet app"],"answer":1,"explain":"On-chain storage is expensive, so contracts store a pointer to metadata."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Mint an NFT","file":"StudioPass.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the <code>mint</code> function: <code>external onlyOwner</code>, takes <code>address to</code>, <code>returns (uint256 id)</code>, sets <code>id = _nextId++;</code> and calls <code>_safeMint(to, id);</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function mint(address to) external onlyOwner returns (uint256 id) {\n    id = _nextId++;\n    _safeMint(to, id);\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+mint\\s*\\(\\s*address\\s+to\\s*\\)","text":"Line 1 starts <code>function mint(address to)</code>"},{"re":"external\\s+onlyOwner\\s+returns\\s*\\(\\s*uint256\\s+id\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"then <code>external onlyOwner returns (uint256 id) {</code>"},{"re":"id\\s*=\\s*_nextId\\+\\+\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>id = _nextId++;</code> (use the current value, then add one)."},{"re":"_safeMint\\(\\s*to\\s*,\\s*id\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 3: <code>_safeMint(to, id);</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Approve exactly what you need","file":"Approve.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Two Solidity lines: approve <code>address(dex)</code> to spend <code>100 ether</code> of <code>token</code>, then read the remaining allowance from <code>address(this)</code> to <code>address(dex)</code> into a <code>uint256 left</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"token.approve(address(dex), 100 ether);\nuint256 left = token.allowance(address(this), address(dex));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"token\\.approve\\(\\s*address\\(\\s*dex\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*100\\s+ether\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 1: <code>token.approve(address(dex), 100 ether);</code>"},{"re":"uint256\\s+left\\s*=\\s*token\\.allowance\\(","text":"Line 2 starts <code>uint256 left = token.allowance(</code>"},{"re":"allowance\\(\\s*address\\(\\s*this\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*address\\(\\s*dex\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Arguments: owner first, then spender: <code>address(this), address(dex)</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Why standards matter A standard (an ERC , Ethereum Request for Comments) is an agreed interface. Because every ERC-20 token has transfer and balanceOf , any wallet or exchange supports any token without custom code. Use standards, and use OpenZeppelin's implementations of them. The ones you will meet Standard What it is Examples ERC-20 Fungible tokens: every unit is identical Stablecoins, governance tokens ERC-721 Non-fungible tokens (NFTs): each token ID is unique Art, tickets, memberships, domain names ERC-1155 Many token types in one contract, fungible or not Game items, editions, event passes ERC-4626 Tokenised vaults: deposit an asset, receive shares Yield vaults, staking wrappers EIP-2612 permit : approve with a signature instead of a transaction Gasless approvals EIP-712 A standard for signing structured data readably Orders, permits, off-chain votes ERC-4337 Account abstraction: smart contract wallets with sponsored gas, batching and recovery Modern wallets NFTs (ERC-721) in brief // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT pragma solidity ^0.8.24; import {ERC721} from @openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC721/ERC721.sol ; import {Ownable} from @openzeppelin/contracts/access/Ownable.sol ; contract StudioPass is ERC721, Ownable { uint256 private _nextId = 1; constructor(address initialOwner) ERC721( Studio Pass , PASS ) Ownable(initialOwner) {} function mint(address to) external onlyOwner returns (uint256 id) { id = _nextId++; _safeMint(to, id); } function _baseURI() internal pure override returns (string memory) { return ipfs://YOUR_METADATA_FOLDER_CID/ ; } } Each token's tokenURI points to metadata JSON (name, description, image). The image is usually on IPFS or Arweave, not on-chain, because storage on-chain is very expensive. If metadata lives on a normal web server, the NFT's appearance depends on that server staying up. Approvals: powerful and dangerous ERC-20's approve lets a contract spend your tokens. Many apps ask for an unlimited approval so you never have to approve again. Convenient, but if that contract is later exploited, or was malicious from the start, it can drain every approved token. As a user: approve only what you need, and revoke old approvals regularly (revoke.cash, or your wallet's approval manager). As a builder: request exact amounts where practical, and support permit signatures. Signatures can move money too Just sign this message, it is free can be a permit signature that approves a transfer of your tokens. EIP-712 makes signatures readable in wallets: read them. Scam sites rely on people signing without reading. ERC-4626 vaults A vault takes deposits of one asset (say USDC) and mints shares . As the vault earns yield, each share is worth more of the asset. A standard interface ( deposit , withdraw , convertToAssets ) means any app can integrate any vault. The classic bug: rounding and inflation attacks on nearly empty vaults. OpenZeppelin's implementation includes protections; read its docs before building one.","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-security","title":"Smart contract security","level":"advanced","minutes":45,"summary":"The vulnerability classes behind billions in losses (reentrancy, access control, oracle manipulation, replay and more) and how to prevent them.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-security-is-different-here\">Why security is different here</h2>\n<p>Web bugs can be patched in minutes. Smart contracts are usually immutable, hold real money, run in public, and can be attacked by anyone, instantly, with borrowed millions (flash loans). Billions of dollars have been lost to contract bugs and key compromises. Security is not a final step; it is the job.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-reentrancy\">1. Reentrancy</h2>\n<p>A contract sends ETH (or calls an unknown contract) <strong>before</strong> updating its own state. The receiver's code calls back in and repeats the withdrawal while the balance still looks full. This drained The DAO in 2016 and still appears today.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// VULNERABLE\nfunction withdraw() external {\n    uint256 amount = balances[msg.sender];\n    (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}(&quot;&quot;);   // attacker re-enters here...\n    require(ok);\n    balances[msg.sender] = 0;                             // ...before this runs\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// SAFE: checks, effects, interactions\nfunction withdraw() external nonReentrant {\n    uint256 amount = balances[msg.sender];\n    if (amount == 0) revert NothingToWithdraw();   // checks\n    balances[msg.sender] = 0;                       // effects (update state first)\n    (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}(&quot;&quot;); // interactions (external calls last)\n    if (!ok) revert TransferFailed();\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Follow <strong>checks-effects-interactions</strong> everywhere and add OpenZeppelin's <code>ReentrancyGuard</code> (<code>nonReentrant</code>) to functions that move value. Watch for <strong>read-only reentrancy</strong> too: other protocols reading your state mid-update.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"2-access-control\">2. Access control</h2>\n<p>Missing <code>onlyOwner</code> on a mint or upgrade function. An <code>initialize</code> function anyone can call first. Using <code>tx.origin</code> (the original signer) instead of <code>msg.sender</code> for auth, which lets a malicious contract act on a user's behalf.</p>\n<ul><li>Use OpenZeppelin <code>Ownable2Step</code> or <code>AccessControl</code> with named roles.</li><li>Put privileged roles behind a <strong>multisig</strong> (Safe) and a <strong>timelock</strong> so users can see changes coming.</li><li>Write a test for every privileged function proving strangers are rejected.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"3-oracle-and-price-manipulation\">3. Oracle and price manipulation</h2>\n<p>If your protocol reads a token's price from a single DEX pool's current reserves, an attacker can borrow millions in a <strong>flash loan</strong>, skew that pool, exploit your protocol at the fake price, and repay the loan, all in one transaction.</p>\n<ul><li>Use robust oracles (Chainlink price feeds) or time-weighted average prices (TWAPs), never spot prices from one pool.</li><li>Check oracle data is fresh and within sane bounds.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"4-arithmetic-and-precision\">4. Arithmetic and precision</h2>\n<p>Solidity 0.8+ reverts on overflow, but precision bugs remain:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">uint256 fee = amount / 1000 * 3;   // BAD: divides first, small amounts round to zero\nuint256 fee = amount * 3 / 1000;   // better: multiply before dividing</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li>Decide who rounding should favour (almost always the protocol, never the user who can repeat it).</li><li>Be careful with <code>unchecked</code> blocks and with tokens that have different decimals (USDC has 6, most have 18).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"5-front-running-and-mev\">5. Front-running and MEV</h2>\n<p>Pending transactions are public. Bots can see your large swap and trade before and after it (&quot;sandwich&quot;), profiting from the price movement they caused.</p>\n<ul><li>Swaps need a <strong>slippage limit</strong> (<code>minAmountOut</code>) and a <strong>deadline</strong>.</li><li>Commit-reveal schemes hide sensitive values until it is too late to front-run.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"6-signature-replay\">6. Signature replay</h2>\n<p>A signed message (a permit, an off-chain order) must be usable once, on one chain, for one contract. Include a <strong>nonce</strong>, the <strong>chain ID</strong>, the <strong>contract address</strong> and an <strong>expiry</strong>: exactly what EIP-712 domains and OpenZeppelin's <code>EIP712</code> and <code>Nonces</code> give you. Use <code>ECDSA.recover</code> from OpenZeppelin, which rejects malleable signatures.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-denial-of-service\">7. Denial of service</h2>\n<ul><li>Looping over an array that users can grow will eventually exceed the block gas limit and brick the function.</li><li>Sending ETH to a list of addresses in one loop fails entirely if one recipient reverts. Prefer <strong>pull payments</strong>: record what each user is owed and let them withdraw.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"8-unsafe-token-interactions\">8. Unsafe token interactions</h2>\n<p>Some tokens do not return <code>true</code> from <code>transfer</code>, charge fees on transfer, or rebase balances. Use OpenZeppelin's <code>SafeERC20</code> (<code>safeTransfer</code>, <code>safeTransferFrom</code>), and measure balances before and after if fee-on-transfer tokens are possible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-upgrade-and-initialisation-bugs\">9. Upgrade and initialisation bugs</h2>\n<p>Proxies separate storage from logic. Mistakes in storage layout or unprotected initialisers have caused major losses. See the upgradeability lesson before using proxies.</p>\n<h2 id=\"10-keys-and-operations\">10. Keys and operations</h2>\n<p>Many of the largest losses were not code bugs at all but compromised private keys (bridges, exchanges, deployer keys). Use multisigs, hardware wallets, separate deployer and admin keys, monitoring and an incident plan.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-pre-deployment-checklist\">The pre-deployment checklist</h2>\n<ul><li>Every external function: who can call it, and with what inputs?</li><li>Checks-effects-interactions everywhere; <code>nonReentrant</code> where value moves.</li><li>No <code>tx.origin</code> auth. No unbounded loops. Pull over push payments.</li><li>Oracles are robust and freshness-checked. Slippage and deadlines on swaps.</li><li>Signatures include nonce, chain ID, contract and expiry.</li><li>100% of business logic has unit, fuzz and invariant tests.</li><li>Slither and at least one other analyser run clean (or every finding explained).</li><li>An independent audit or competitive audit for anything holding significant value.</li><li>Admin keys in a multisig behind a timelock. A pause switch and an incident plan.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Learn by breaking things</p><p>Play <strong>Ethernaut</strong> (OpenZeppelin), then <strong>Damn Vulnerable DeFi</strong>. Read post-mortems on rekt.news and audit reports from firms like Trail of Bits, OpenZeppelin, Spearbit and Cyfrin. Auditing contests (Code4rena, Sherlock, Cantina) pay for found bugs and are a recognised path into a security career.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"What is the core fix for reentrancy?","options":["Use tx.origin instead of msg.sender","Update state before making external calls (checks-effects-interactions), plus a reentrancy guard","Use a newer compiler version"],"answer":1,"explain":"If balances are zeroed first, re-entering finds nothing left to take."},{"q":"A lending protocol prices collateral using one DEX pool's current reserves. What attack does this invite?","options":["Signature replay","Flash-loan price manipulation","Denial of service"],"answer":1,"explain":"Spot prices can be moved within one transaction. Use robust oracles or TWAPs."},{"q":"Which fee calculation loses the least precision?","options":["amount / 1000 * 3","amount * 3 / 1000","They are identical"],"answer":1,"explain":"Integer division truncates, so divide last."},{"q":"A signed permit can be reused on another chain where the same contract is deployed. What is missing?","options":["A higher gas price","The chain ID (and contract address) in the signed data, as EIP-712 domains provide","An event"],"answer":1,"explain":"Domain separation and nonces make each signature valid exactly once, in one place."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Fix the reentrancy bug","file":"Vault.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>This <code>withdraw</code> is vulnerable: it sends ETH <strong>before</strong> zeroing the balance. Fix it: add the <code>nonReentrant</code> modifier after <code>external</code>, and move the line that zeroes the balance so it runs <strong>before</strong> the external call (checks, effects, interactions).</p>","starter":"function withdraw() external {\n    uint256 amount = balances[msg.sender];\n    (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}(\"\");\n    require(ok);\n    balances[msg.sender] = 0;\n}","html":"","solution":"function withdraw() external nonReentrant {\n    uint256 amount = balances[msg.sender];\n    balances[msg.sender] = 0;\n    (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}(\"\");\n    require(ok);\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"external\\s+nonReentrant\\s*\\{","text":"Add the guard to line 1: <code>function withdraw() external nonReentrant {</code>"},{"re":"balances\\[\\s*msg\\.sender\\s*\\]\\s*=\\s*0\\s*;[\\s\\S]*msg\\.sender\\.call","text":"Effects before interactions: <code>balances[msg.sender] = 0;</code> must come <strong>before</strong> the <code>.call{value: amount}</code> line."},{"re":"require\\(\\s*ok\\s*\\)\\s*;\\s*\\n\\s*\\}\\s*$","text":"After the call, <code>require(ok);</code> is the last line before the closing brace."}],"explain":"<p>Now, if the receiver re-enters <code>withdraw</code>, its balance is already 0, so there is nothing more to take, and <code>nonReentrant</code> blocks the second call anyway. Two independent defences: that is defence in depth.</p>"},{"title":"Fix the precision bug","file":"Fees.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>This fee calculation divides first, so small amounts round down to a fee of zero. Rewrite it to multiply before dividing.</p>","starter":"uint256 fee = amount / 1000 * 3;","html":"","solution":"uint256 fee = amount * 3 / 1000;","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"uint256\\s+fee\\s*=\\s*amount\\s*\\*\\s*3","text":"Multiply first: <code>amount * 3</code>"},{"re":"\\*\\s*3\\s*\\/\\s*1000\\s*;","text":"then divide last: <code>/ 1000;</code>"}],"explain":"<p>With <code>amount = 999</code>, the old version computes <code>999 / 1000 = 0</code>, then <code>0 * 3 = 0</code>: no fee at all, repeatable forever. The fixed version gives <code>2997 / 1000 = 2</code>.</p>"}],"text":"Why security is different here Web bugs can be patched in minutes. Smart contracts are usually immutable, hold real money, run in public, and can be attacked by anyone, instantly, with borrowed millions (flash loans). Billions of dollars have been lost to contract bugs and key compromises. Security is not a final step; it is the job. 1. Reentrancy A contract sends ETH (or calls an unknown contract) before updating its own state. The receiver's code calls back in and repeats the withdrawal while the balance still looks full. This drained The DAO in 2016 and still appears today. // VULNERABLE function withdraw() external { uint256 amount = balances[msg.sender]; (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}( ); // attacker re-enters here... require(ok); balances[msg.sender] = 0; // ...before this runs } // SAFE: checks, effects, interactions function withdraw() external nonReentrant { uint256 amount = balances[msg.sender]; if (amount == 0) revert NothingToWithdraw(); // checks balances[msg.sender] = 0; // effects (update state first) (bool ok, ) = msg.sender.call{value: amount}( ); // interactions (external calls last) if (!ok) revert TransferFailed(); } Follow checks-effects-interactions everywhere and add OpenZeppelin's ReentrancyGuard ( nonReentrant ) to functions that move value. Watch for read-only reentrancy too: other protocols reading your state mid-update. 2. Access control Missing onlyOwner on a mint or upgrade function. An initialize function anyone can call first. Using tx.origin (the original signer) instead of msg.sender for auth, which lets a malicious contract act on a user's behalf. Use OpenZeppelin Ownable2Step or AccessControl with named roles. Put privileged roles behind a multisig (Safe) and a timelock so users can see changes coming. Write a test for every privileged function proving strangers are rejected. 3. Oracle and price manipulation If your protocol reads a token's price from a single DEX pool's current reserves, an attacker can borrow millions in a flash loan , skew that pool, exploit your protocol at the fake price, and repay the loan, all in one transaction. Use robust oracles (Chainlink price feeds) or time-weighted average prices (TWAPs), never spot prices from one pool. Check oracle data is fresh and within sane bounds. 4. Arithmetic and precision Solidity 0.8+ reverts on overflow, but precision bugs remain: uint256 fee = amount / 1000 * 3; // BAD: divides first, small amounts round to zero uint256 fee = amount * 3 / 1000; // better: multiply before dividing Decide who rounding should favour (almost always the protocol, never the user who can repeat it). Be careful with unchecked blocks and with tokens that have different decimals (USDC has 6, most have 18). 5. Front-running and MEV Pending transactions are public. Bots can see your large swap and trade before and after it ( sandwich ), profiting from the price movement they caused. Swaps need a slippage limit ( minAmountOut ) and a deadline . Commit-reveal schemes hide sensitive values until it is too late to front-run. 6. Signature replay A signed message (a permit, an off-chain order) must be usable once, on one chain, for one contract. Include a nonce , the chain ID , the contract address and an expiry : exactly what EIP-712 domains and OpenZeppelin's EIP712 and Nonces give you. Use ECDSA.recover from OpenZeppelin, which rejects malleable signatures. 7. Denial of service Looping over an array that users can grow will eventually exceed the block gas limit and brick the function. Sending ETH to a list of addresses in one loop fails entirely if one recipient reverts. Prefer pull payments : record what each user is owed and let them withdraw. 8. Unsafe token interactions Some tokens do not return true from transfer , charge fees on transfer, or rebase balances. Use OpenZeppelin's SafeERC20 ( safeTransfer , safeTransferFrom ), and measure balances before and after if fee-on-transfer tokens are possible. 9. Upgrade and initialisation bugs Proxies s","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-testing","title":"Fuzzing, invariants and auditing tools","level":"advanced","minutes":40,"summary":"Go beyond unit tests with fuzz, invariant and fork tests in Foundry, then use Slither and an audit mindset.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-testing-ladder-for-contracts\">The testing ladder for contracts</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Foundry feature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unit</td><td>Does this function do what I intended for these inputs?</td><td><code>test_...</code> functions</td></tr><tr><td>Fuzz</td><td>Does it hold for thousands of random inputs?</td><td>Test functions with parameters</td></tr><tr><td>Invariant</td><td>Do core truths hold after any sequence of calls?</td><td><code>invariant_...</code> functions</td></tr><tr><td>Fork</td><td>Does it work against real deployed protocols?</td><td><code>--fork-url</code></td></tr><tr><td>Static analysis</td><td>Are there known bug patterns?</td><td>Slither, Aderyn</td></tr><tr><td>Formal verification</td><td>Can we prove it for all inputs?</td><td>Halmos, Certora, Kontrol</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"fuzz-testing\">Fuzz testing</h2>\n<p>Any test function with parameters is fuzzed: Foundry calls it with many random values, and shrinks any failure to a minimal example.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">function testFuzz_WithdrawNeverExceedsDeposit(uint96 deposit, uint96 withdrawAmount) public {\n    vm.assume(deposit &gt; 0);                       // skip inputs you do not care about\n    withdrawAmount = uint96(bound(withdrawAmount, 0, deposit)); // keep values in a valid range\n    vm.deal(alice, deposit);\n\n    vm.startPrank(alice);\n    vault.deposit{value: deposit}();\n    vault.withdraw(withdrawAmount);\n    vm.stopPrank();\n\n    assertEq(vault.balanceOf(alice), deposit - withdrawAmount);\n    assertEq(address(vault).balance, deposit - withdrawAmount);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Prefer <code>bound</code> over <code>vm.assume</code> when possible: <code>assume</code> throws inputs away, <code>bound</code> maps them into range.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"invariant-testing\">Invariant testing</h2>\n<p>An <strong>invariant</strong> is something that must always be true, whatever anyone does. For example:</p>\n<ul><li>The sum of all user balances equals the contract's ETH balance.</li><li>Total token supply never exceeds the cap.</li><li>A vault's share price never decreases (absent losses).</li></ul>\n<p>Foundry calls random sequences of functions on a <strong>handler</strong> contract, then checks your invariants after each call.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// test/invariant/VaultInvariants.t.sol\ncontract Handler is Test {\n    Vault vault;\n    address[] public actors;\n    uint256 public ghostDeposited;      // &quot;ghost variables&quot; track what the totals should be\n    uint256 public ghostWithdrawn;\n\n    constructor(Vault _vault) {\n        vault = _vault;\n        actors.push(makeAddr(&quot;a&quot;));\n        actors.push(makeAddr(&quot;b&quot;));\n    }\n\n    function deposit(uint256 actorSeed, uint96 amount) external {\n        address actor = actors[actorSeed % actors.length];\n        amount = uint96(bound(amount, 1, 100 ether));\n        vm.deal(actor, amount);\n        vm.prank(actor);\n        vault.deposit{value: amount}();\n        ghostDeposited += amount;\n    }\n\n    function withdraw(uint256 actorSeed, uint96 amount) external {\n        address actor = actors[actorSeed % actors.length];\n        amount = uint96(bound(amount, 0, vault.balanceOf(actor)));\n        vm.prank(actor);\n        vault.withdraw(amount);\n        ghostWithdrawn += amount;\n    }\n}\n\ncontract VaultInvariants is Test {\n    Vault vault;\n    Handler handler;\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        vault = new Vault();\n        handler = new Handler(vault);\n        targetContract(address(handler));    // only call the handler's functions\n    }\n\n    function invariant_SolventAtAllTimes() public view {\n        assertEq(address(vault).balance, handler.ghostDeposited() - handler.ghostWithdrawn());\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Invariant tests find the bugs nobody thought to write a unit test for: strange orderings, repeated calls, rounding drift.</p>\n<h2 id=\"fork-testing\">Fork testing</h2>\n<p>Test against real mainnet state (real USDC, real Uniswap pools) on a local copy:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">forge test --fork-url $MAINNET_RPC_URL --match-contract ForkTest -vvv</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"coverage-and-gas\">Coverage and gas</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">forge coverage            # which lines and branches your tests hit\nforge test --gas-report   # gas per function\nforge snapshot            # save gas usage; compare after optimising</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"static-analysis\">Static analysis</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">uv tool install slither-analyzer\nslither .</code></pre></div>\n<p>Slither flags reentrancy, unprotected functions, shadowing, dangerous calls and dozens more patterns in seconds. Treat each finding as a question: fix it, or write down why it is safe. Aderyn is another fast analyser worth running.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"how-auditors-think\">How auditors think</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Understand intent first.</strong> Read the docs and specs. What must never happen?</li><li><strong>List the actors and their powers.</strong> Users, admins, keepers, oracles, other contracts.</li><li><strong>Follow the money.</strong> Every path where value enters or leaves.</li><li><strong>Question every assumption.</strong> What if this token charges fees? What if the oracle is stale? What if this is called twice, in the same block, with zero, with the maximum?</li><li><strong>Write a proof-of-concept test</strong> for every suspected bug. If you cannot exploit it in a test, keep digging or move on.</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Your security learning path</p><p>Ethernaut, then Damn Vulnerable DeFi, then read real audit reports, then join a competitive audit as a learner. Cyfrin Updraft's free security course covers this whole path.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"What is the advantage of bound() over vm.assume() in fuzz tests?","options":["bound maps inputs into range instead of discarding them, so fewer runs are wasted","bound makes tests deterministic","assume does not work with uint256"],"answer":0,"explain":"Too many assume rejections can make a fuzz run fail to find enough valid inputs."},{"q":"Which is a good invariant for a simple ETH vault?","options":["Every deposit is exactly 1 ETH","The contract's ETH balance equals total deposits minus total withdrawals","Users can only withdraw once"],"answer":1,"explain":"Invariants are truths that must hold after any sequence of actions."},{"q":"What does a fork test let you do?","options":["Deploy to mainnet for free","Run tests against a local copy of real chain state, including real deployed protocols","Split a contract into two"],"answer":1,"explain":"Forks let you integrate with real tokens and pools safely."},{"q":"Slither reports a possible reentrancy. What is the professional response?","options":["Ignore it: static tools have false positives","Investigate; fix it or document exactly why it is safe, ideally with a test","Delete the function"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every finding gets an explicit decision."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write a fuzz test","file":"LadderToken.t.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write <code>testFuzz_Transfer(uint256 amount)</code>: bound <code>amount</code> between 0 and the owner's balance, prank as <code>owner</code>, transfer <code>amount</code> to <code>alice</code>, and assert alice's balance equals <code>amount</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function testFuzz_Transfer(uint256 amount) public {\n    amount = bound(amount, 0, token.balanceOf(owner));\n    vm.prank(owner);\n    token.transfer(alice, amount);\n    assertEq(token.balanceOf(alice), amount);\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+testFuzz_Transfer\\s*\\(\\s*uint256\\s+amount\\s*\\)\\s*public\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function testFuzz_Transfer(uint256 amount) public {</code> (the parameter makes it a fuzz test)."},{"re":"amount\\s*=\\s*bound\\(\\s*amount\\s*,\\s*0\\s*,\\s*token\\.balanceOf\\(\\s*owner\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>amount = bound(amount, 0, token.balanceOf(owner));</code>"},{"re":"vm\\.prank\\(\\s*owner\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 3: <code>vm.prank(owner);</code>"},{"re":"token\\.transfer\\(\\s*alice\\s*,\\s*amount\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 4: <code>token.transfer(alice, amount);</code>"},{"re":"assertEq\\(\\s*token\\.balanceOf\\(\\s*alice\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*amount\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 5: <code>assertEq(token.balanceOf(alice), amount);</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Run the safety checks","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Three commands: measure test coverage, run the tests with a gas report, and run Slither on the current folder.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"forge coverage\nforge test --gas-report\nslither .","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*forge\\s+coverage\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>forge coverage</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*forge\\s+test\\s+--gas-report\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>forge test --gas-report</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*slither\\s+\\.\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>slither .</code> (the dot means this folder)."}],"explain":""}],"text":"The testing ladder for contracts Level Question it answers Foundry feature Unit Does this function do what I intended for these inputs? test_... functions Fuzz Does it hold for thousands of random inputs? Test functions with parameters Invariant Do core truths hold after any sequence of calls? invariant_... functions Fork Does it work against real deployed protocols? --fork-url Static analysis Are there known bug patterns? Slither, Aderyn Formal verification Can we prove it for all inputs? Halmos, Certora, Kontrol Fuzz testing Any test function with parameters is fuzzed: Foundry calls it with many random values, and shrinks any failure to a minimal example. function testFuzz_WithdrawNeverExceedsDeposit(uint96 deposit, uint96 withdrawAmount) public { vm.assume(deposit 0); // skip inputs you do not care about withdrawAmount = uint96(bound(withdrawAmount, 0, deposit)); // keep values in a valid range vm.deal(alice, deposit); vm.startPrank(alice); vault.deposit{value: deposit}(); vault.withdraw(withdrawAmount); vm.stopPrank(); assertEq(vault.balanceOf(alice), deposit - withdrawAmount); assertEq(address(vault).balance, deposit - withdrawAmount); } Prefer bound over vm.assume when possible: assume throws inputs away, bound maps them into range. Invariant testing An invariant is something that must always be true, whatever anyone does. For example: The sum of all user balances equals the contract's ETH balance. Total token supply never exceeds the cap. A vault's share price never decreases (absent losses). Foundry calls random sequences of functions on a handler contract, then checks your invariants after each call. // test/invariant/VaultInvariants.t.sol contract Handler is Test { Vault vault; address[] public actors; uint256 public ghostDeposited; // ghost variables track what the totals should be uint256 public ghostWithdrawn; constructor(Vault _vault) { vault = _vault; actors.push(makeAddr( a )); actors.push(makeAddr( b )); } function deposit(uint256 actorSeed, uint96 amount) external { address actor = actors[actorSeed % actors.length]; amount = uint96(bound(amount, 1, 100 ether)); vm.deal(actor, amount); vm.prank(actor); vault.deposit{value: amount}(); ghostDeposited += amount; } function withdraw(uint256 actorSeed, uint96 amount) external { address actor = actors[actorSeed % actors.length]; amount = uint96(bound(amount, 0, vault.balanceOf(actor))); vm.prank(actor); vault.withdraw(amount); ghostWithdrawn += amount; } } contract VaultInvariants is Test { Vault vault; Handler handler; function setUp() public { vault = new Vault(); handler = new Handler(vault); targetContract(address(handler)); // only call the handler's functions } function invariant_SolventAtAllTimes() public view { assertEq(address(vault).balance, handler.ghostDeposited() - handler.ghostWithdrawn()); } } Invariant tests find the bugs nobody thought to write a unit test for: strange orderings, repeated calls, rounding drift. Fork testing Test against real mainnet state (real USDC, real Uniswap pools) on a local copy: forge test --fork-url $MAINNET_RPC_URL --match-contract ForkTest -vvv Coverage and gas forge coverage # which lines and branches your tests hit forge test --gas-report # gas per function forge snapshot # save gas usage; compare after optimising Static analysis uv tool install slither-analyzer slither . Slither flags reentrancy, unprotected functions, shadowing, dangerous calls and dozens more patterns in seconds. Treat each finding as a question: fix it, or write down why it is safe. Aderyn is another fast analyser worth running. How auditors think Understand intent first. Read the docs and specs. What must never happen? List the actors and their powers. Users, admins, keepers, oracles, other contracts. Follow the money. Every path where value enters or leaves. Question every assumption. What if this token charges fees? What if the oracle is stale? What if this is called twice, in the same block, with zero, with the maximum? Write a proof-of-concept ","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-advanced-eng","title":"Upgradeability, gas optimisation and Layer 2s","level":"advanced","minutes":35,"summary":"When and how to use proxies safely, where gas really goes, and how rollups and account abstraction change deployment.","html":"<h2 id=\"upgradeability-a-trade-off-not-a-default\">Upgradeability: a trade-off, not a default</h2>\n<p>Deployed bytecode cannot change. <strong>Proxy patterns</strong> work around that: users call a proxy contract that holds the storage and forwards every call (with <code>delegatecall</code>) to a separate <strong>implementation</strong> contract. To upgrade, the admin points the proxy at a new implementation.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Upgrade logic lives in</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Transparent proxy</strong></td><td>The proxy (with a separate admin)</td><td>Simple mental model, slightly more gas</td></tr><tr><td><strong>UUPS</strong></td><td>The implementation</td><td>Cheaper; you must keep the upgrade function in every version</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Beacon</strong></td><td>A shared beacon contract</td><td>Upgrade many proxies at once</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Rules that prevent disasters:</p>\n<ul><li>Use OpenZeppelin's upgradeable contracts and their <strong>Foundry Upgrades</strong> plugin, which checks storage layout between versions.</li><li><strong>Never reorder, remove or change the type of existing storage variables.</strong> Only append new ones. Otherwise the new code reads old data in the wrong slots.</li><li>Constructors do not run for proxies. Use an <code>initializer</code> function, protect it, and call <code>_disableInitializers()</code> in the implementation's constructor.</li><li>Put upgrade rights behind a multisig and a timelock.</li></ul>\n<p>The honest trade-off: an upgradeable contract is only as trustworthy as whoever can upgrade it. Many protocols launch upgradeable, then renounce or restrict upgrades once mature. Immutability is a feature users value.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-gas-actually-goes\">Where gas actually goes</h2>\n<p>Storage is by far the most expensive thing a contract does. Writing a new non-zero storage slot costs about 20,000 gas; reading a slot for the first time in a transaction costs about 2,100; memory and arithmetic cost a few gas each.</p>\n<p>Optimisations that matter, in order:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Touch storage less.</strong> Read a storage variable into a local variable once, use it, write it back once.</li><li><strong>Pack variables.</strong> Several small types declared next to each other share one 32-byte slot.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"solidity\"><pre><code class=\"language-solidity\">// 3 slots\nuint256 amount;\nuint64 start;\nuint256 total;\n\n// 2 slots: start and duration are packed together\nuint256 amount;\nuint64 start;\nuint64 duration;</code></pre></div>\n<ol><li><strong>Use <code>constant</code> and <code>immutable</code></strong> for values that never change; they live in the bytecode, not storage.</li><li><strong>Use <code>calldata</code></strong> for read-only array and string parameters.</li><li><strong>Custom errors</strong> instead of revert strings.</li><li><strong>Emit events</strong> for data only off-chain apps need, instead of storing it.</li></ol>\n<p>Then measure with <code>forge test --gas-report</code> and <code>forge snapshot</code>. Never sacrifice readability or safety for tiny savings; a gas optimisation that introduces a bug is the most expensive kind.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"layer-2-rollups\">Layer 2 rollups</h2>\n<p>Rollups run transactions off Ethereum mainnet, then post the data (and proofs) back to it, inheriting much of its security at a fraction of the cost.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>How correctness is ensured</th><th>Withdrawal to L1</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Optimistic</strong></td><td>Assumed valid; anyone can submit a fraud proof during a challenge window</td><td>About 7 days via the native bridge</td><td>Arbitrum, Optimism, Base</td></tr><tr><td><strong>ZK (validity)</strong></td><td>A cryptographic proof is verified on L1 for every batch</td><td>Faster, once the proof is verified</td><td>zkSync, Starknet, Scroll, Linea</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>For developers: most EVM rollups run the same Solidity, the same Foundry and the same viem, with a different chain ID and RPC URL. Watch for differences in <code>block.number</code> and timestamps, precompiles, and gas pricing (L2 fees include the cost of posting data to L1). Bridges between chains are a major source of hacks; use canonical bridges and well-audited messaging.</p>\n<h2 id=\"account-abstraction\">Account abstraction</h2>\n<p>Traditional accounts (EOAs) are a single private key: lose it and everything is lost; every action needs ETH for gas.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>ERC-4337</strong> smart accounts are contracts: they can have multiple signers, spending limits, social recovery, session keys, batched actions, and <strong>paymasters</strong> that sponsor gas (users can pay in USDC or not at all).</li><li><strong>EIP-7702</strong> (live since Ethereum's 2025 Pectra upgrade) lets an existing EOA delegate to smart-account code, bringing batching and sponsorship to normal wallets.</li></ul>\n<p>For dApp builders this means far better onboarding: users sign in with a passkey and never touch a seed phrase or buy ETH for gas first.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"Version 2 of an upgradeable contract inserts a new variable above existing ones. What goes wrong?","options":["Nothing, the compiler fixes it","Storage slots shift, so the new code reads existing data from the wrong places","Gas costs double"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only append new storage variables. Use tooling that validates layouts."},{"q":"Which change usually saves the most gas?","options":["Reducing storage reads and writes","Shortening variable names","Removing comments"],"answer":0,"explain":"Storage operations cost thousands of gas; names and comments cost nothing at runtime."},{"q":"Why do withdrawals from optimistic rollups to Ethereum take about a week via the native bridge?","options":["Validators are slow","There is a challenge window for fraud proofs before the withdrawal is final","Ethereum limits withdrawals"],"answer":1,"explain":"Optimistic rollups assume validity unless challenged within the window."},{"q":"What can an ERC-4337 paymaster do?","options":["Mine blocks","Sponsor users' gas fees, or let them pay in another token","Recover lost seed phrases"],"answer":1,"explain":"Paymasters enable gasless and token-paid transactions for smart accounts."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Values that cost no storage","file":"Token.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Two declarations that live in bytecode, not storage: a <code>public constant</code> <code>uint256</code> called <code>MAX_SUPPLY</code> equal to <code>1_000_000_000 ether</code>, and a <code>public immutable</code> <code>address</code> called <code>owner</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"uint256 public constant MAX_SUPPLY = 1_000_000_000 ether;\naddress public immutable owner;","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"uint256\\s+public\\s+constant\\s+MAX_SUPPLY\\s*=\\s*1_000_000_000\\s+ether\\s*;","text":"Line 1: <code>uint256 public constant MAX_SUPPLY = 1_000_000_000 ether;</code>"},{"re":"address\\s+public\\s+immutable\\s+owner\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>address public immutable owner;</code> (set once, in the constructor)."}],"explain":""},{"title":"Pack a struct into one slot","file":"Vesting.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write a struct <code>Grant</code> with three fields that pack into a single 32-byte storage slot: <code>uint128 amount</code>, <code>uint64 start</code>, <code>uint64 duration</code> (128 + 64 + 64 = 256 bits).</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"struct Grant {\n    uint128 amount;\n    uint64 start;\n    uint64 duration;\n}","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*struct\\s+Grant\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>struct Grant {</code>"},{"re":"uint128\\s+amount\\s*;","text":"<code>uint128 amount;</code>"},{"re":"uint64\\s+start\\s*;","text":"<code>uint64 start;</code>"},{"re":"uint64\\s+duration\\s*;","text":"<code>uint64 duration;</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Three <code>uint256</code> fields would use three slots: about 60,000 gas to write fresh. Packed, it is one slot. <code>uint128</code> still holds amounts up to about 3.4 x 10^38, plenty for 18-decimal tokens.</p>"}],"text":"Upgradeability: a trade-off, not a default Deployed bytecode cannot change. Proxy patterns work around that: users call a proxy contract that holds the storage and forwards every call (with delegatecall ) to a separate implementation contract. To upgrade, the admin points the proxy at a new implementation. Pattern Upgrade logic lives in Notes Transparent proxy The proxy (with a separate admin) Simple mental model, slightly more gas UUPS The implementation Cheaper; you must keep the upgrade function in every version Beacon A shared beacon contract Upgrade many proxies at once Rules that prevent disasters: Use OpenZeppelin's upgradeable contracts and their Foundry Upgrades plugin, which checks storage layout between versions. Never reorder, remove or change the type of existing storage variables. Only append new ones. Otherwise the new code reads old data in the wrong slots. Constructors do not run for proxies. Use an initializer function, protect it, and call _disableInitializers() in the implementation's constructor. Put upgrade rights behind a multisig and a timelock. The honest trade-off: an upgradeable contract is only as trustworthy as whoever can upgrade it. Many protocols launch upgradeable, then renounce or restrict upgrades once mature. Immutability is a feature users value. Where gas actually goes Storage is by far the most expensive thing a contract does. Writing a new non-zero storage slot costs about 20,000 gas; reading a slot for the first time in a transaction costs about 2,100; memory and arithmetic cost a few gas each. Optimisations that matter, in order: Touch storage less. Read a storage variable into a local variable once, use it, write it back once. Pack variables. Several small types declared next to each other share one 32-byte slot. // 3 slots uint256 amount; uint64 start; uint256 total; // 2 slots: start and duration are packed together uint256 amount; uint64 start; uint64 duration; Use constant and immutable for values that never change; they live in the bytecode, not storage. Use calldata for read-only array and string parameters. Custom errors instead of revert strings. Emit events for data only off-chain apps need, instead of storing it. Then measure with forge test --gas-report and forge snapshot . Never sacrifice readability or safety for tiny savings; a gas optimisation that introduces a bug is the most expensive kind. Layer 2 rollups Rollups run transactions off Ethereum mainnet, then post the data (and proofs) back to it, inheriting much of its security at a fraction of the cost. Type How correctness is ensured Withdrawal to L1 Examples Optimistic Assumed valid; anyone can submit a fraud proof during a challenge window About 7 days via the native bridge Arbitrum, Optimism, Base ZK (validity) A cryptographic proof is verified on L1 for every batch Faster, once the proof is verified zkSync, Starknet, Scroll, Linea For developers: most EVM rollups run the same Solidity, the same Foundry and the same viem, with a different chain ID and RPC URL. Watch for differences in block.number and timestamps, precompiles, and gas pricing (L2 fees include the cost of posting data to L1). Bridges between chains are a major source of hacks; use canonical bridges and well-audited messaging. Account abstraction Traditional accounts (EOAs) are a single private key: lose it and everything is lost; every action needs ETH for gas. ERC-4337 smart accounts are contracts: they can have multiple signers, spending limits, social recovery, session keys, batched actions, and paymasters that sponsor gas (users can pay in USDC or not at all). EIP-7702 (live since Ethereum's 2025 Pectra upgrade) lets an existing EOA delegate to smart-account code, bringing batching and sponsorship to normal wallets. For dApp builders this means far better onboarding: users sign in with a passkey and never touch a seed phrase or buy ETH for gas first.","track":"crypto"},{"id":"crypto-advanced-crypto","title":"\"Advanced cryptography: curves, Merkle proofs and zero knowledge\"","level":"advanced","minutes":40,"summary":"How elliptic curves and ECDSA work, build and verify Merkle proofs, and an honest introduction to zero-knowledge proofs.","html":"<h2 id=\"elliptic-curves-in-one-page\">Elliptic curves in one page</h2>\n<p>An elliptic curve is a set of points satisfying an equation like <code>y² = x³ + 7</code> (Bitcoin and Ethereum's <strong>secp256k1</strong>), computed over a huge prime field so all numbers wrap around.</p>\n<p>You can &quot;add&quot; two points on the curve to get a third. Adding a starting point <strong>G</strong> to itself <code>k</code> times is called <strong>scalar multiplication</strong>: <code>P = k × G</code>.</p>\n<ul><li>Computing <code>P</code> from <code>k</code> is fast (using doubling, about 256 steps).</li><li>Recovering <code>k</code> from <code>P</code> and <code>G</code> is the <strong>elliptic curve discrete logarithm problem</strong>: no known method does it in any practical time for well-chosen curves.</li></ul>\n<p>That one-way function is the whole trick: your <strong>private key</strong> is <code>k</code>, your <strong>public key</strong> is <code>P = k × G</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ecdsa-and-why-nonces-matter\">ECDSA and why nonces matter</h2>\n<p>To sign a message hash <code>z</code>, the signer picks a secret one-time number <code>k</code> (the <strong>nonce</strong>), computes a point from it, and produces a signature <code>(r, s)</code> from <code>z</code>, <code>k</code> and the private key. Anyone can verify <code>(r, s)</code> against the public key.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Never reuse an ECDSA nonce</p><p>If the same <code>k</code> is used for two different messages, simple algebra reveals the private key. This famously broke the PlayStation 3's code-signing key in 2010, and weak random number generators have emptied Bitcoin wallets. Modern libraries derive <code>k</code> deterministically (RFC 6979). Another reason never to implement signatures yourself.</p></aside>\n<p><strong>Ed25519</strong> (an Edwards curve signature scheme) is designed to avoid these pitfalls and is the default for SSH keys, Solana and many modern systems.</p>\n<h2 id=\"merkle-trees-and-proofs\">Merkle trees and proofs</h2>\n<p>A Merkle tree hashes items in pairs, then hashes those hashes in pairs, until one <strong>root</strong> remains.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">                 root = H(H12 + H34)\n               /                     \\\n        H12 = H(H1+H2)          H34 = H(H3+H4)\n         /        \\               /        \\\n     H1=H(A)   H2=H(B)       H3=H(C)    H4=H(D)</code></pre></div>\n<p>To prove <code>C</code> is in the set, you only need <code>H4</code> and <code>H12</code>: hash <code>C</code>, combine with <code>H4</code>, combine with <code>H12</code>, and compare with the root. For a million items, a proof is only about 20 hashes.</p>\n<p>This is how airdrops work cheaply: store only the 32-byte root on-chain; each user submits their own proof.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">// A tiny Merkle tree with SHA-256 and sorted pairs (the convention OpenZeppelin uses, with keccak256)\nconst enc = new TextEncoder();\nconst toHex = (buf) =&gt; [...new Uint8Array(buf)].map((b) =&gt; b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join('');\nconst hash = async (s) =&gt; toHex(await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', enc.encode(s)));\nconst pair = (a, b) =&gt; hash(a &lt; b ? a + b : b + a);\n\nconst items = ['alice:100', 'bob:250', 'carol:75', 'dave:300'];\nconst leaves = await Promise.all(items.map(hash));\n\n// build the tree level by level\nconst levels = [leaves];\nwhile (levels.at(-1).length &gt; 1) {\n  const prev = levels.at(-1), next = [];\n  for (let i = 0; i &lt; prev.length; i += 2) next.push(await pair(prev[i], prev[i + 1] ?? prev[i]));\n  levels.push(next);\n}\nconst root = levels.at(-1)[0];\nconsole.log('root', root);\n\n// proof for carol: the sibling at each level\nfunction proofFor(index) {\n  const proof = [];\n  for (const level of levels.slice(0, -1)) {\n    const sibling = index % 2 ? level[index - 1] : level[index + 1] ?? level[index];\n    proof.push(sibling);\n    index = Math.floor(index / 2);\n  }\n  return proof;\n}\nconst proof = proofFor(2);\n\nasync function verify(item, proof, root) {\n  let h = await hash(item);\n  for (const sib of proof) h = await pair(h, sib);\n  return h === root;\n}\nconsole.log('carol:75 included?', await verify('carol:75', proof, root));   // true\nconsole.log('carol:999 included?', await verify('carol:999', proof, root)); // false</code></pre></div>\n<p>In Solidity, OpenZeppelin's <code>MerkleProof.verify(proof, root, leaf)</code> does the checking. Off-chain, <code>@openzeppelin/merkle-tree</code> builds compatible trees. Hash leaves twice (or with a distinct prefix) to prevent a known second-preimage trick where an inner node is passed off as a leaf.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"zero-knowledge-proofs\">Zero-knowledge proofs</h2>\n<p>A <strong>zero-knowledge proof</strong> convinces a verifier that a statement is true without revealing why:</p>\n<ul><li>&quot;I know a password that hashes to this value&quot; without revealing the password.</li><li>&quot;I am over 18&quot; without revealing a birthdate.</li><li>&quot;These 10,000 transactions were all executed correctly&quot; with one short proof (this is what ZK rollups do).</li></ul>\n<p>In practice you write the statement as a <strong>circuit</strong> (a program with arithmetic constraints), and a proving system produces a proof that verifies quickly.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Proof size</th><th>Trusted setup?</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>SNARKs</strong> (Groth16, PLONK)</td><td>Tiny, cheap to verify on-chain</td><td>Often (or universal)</td><td>Most common on Ethereum</td></tr><tr><td><strong>STARKs</strong></td><td>Larger</td><td>No</td><td>Transparent, based on hashes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Tools to explore later: <strong>Circom</strong> with snarkjs, <strong>Noir</strong> (a Rust-like ZK language), and zkVMs like RISC Zero and SP1 that prove ordinary Rust programs. Start with Noir's tutorials once you are solid in Solidity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"looking-ahead-post-quantum-cryptography\">Looking ahead: post-quantum cryptography</h2>\n<p>Large quantum computers would break elliptic-curve and RSA cryptography (not hash functions or AES-256 to the same degree). In 2024, NIST published the first post-quantum standards, including ML-KEM for key exchange and ML-DSA for signatures, and browsers and messaging apps have begun adopting hybrid schemes. Blockchains are researching migration paths. You do not need to act now, but you should know why &quot;harvest now, decrypt later&quot; matters for long-lived secrets.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"What makes scalar multiplication on an elliptic curve useful for keys?","options":["k x G is easy to compute, but recovering k from the result is infeasible","It is reversible with the right password","It produces 12-word phrases"],"answer":0,"explain":"That one-way function turns a private key into a public key safely."},{"q":"An ECDSA signer reuses the same nonce for two different messages. What happens?","options":["Both signatures are invalid","Anyone can compute the private key from the two signatures","Nothing, nonces are optional"],"answer":1,"explain":"Nonce reuse is catastrophic for ECDSA."},{"q":"A Merkle tree has about one million leaves. Roughly how many hashes are in an inclusion proof?","options":["1,000,000","1,000","About 20"],"answer":2,"explain":"Proof size grows with log2 of the number of leaves."},{"q":"What do ZK rollups use zero-knowledge proofs for?","options":["Hiding all transactions forever","Proving to Ethereum that a large batch of transactions was executed correctly","Generating seed phrases"],"answer":1,"explain":"One small validity proof replaces re-executing every transaction on L1."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Verify a Merkle proof on-chain","file":"Airdrop.sol","lang":"solidity","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Two lines inside a <code>claim(uint256 amount, bytes32[] calldata proof)</code> function:</p>\n<ol><li>Build the leaf the way OpenZeppelin's Merkle tree library does, hashing twice: <code>bytes32 leaf = keccak256(bytes.concat(keccak256(abi.encode(msg.sender, amount))));</code></li><li>Require that <code>MerkleProof.verify(proof, root, leaf)</code> is true, with the message <code>&quot;Not eligible&quot;</code>.</li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"bytes32 leaf = keccak256(bytes.concat(keccak256(abi.encode(msg.sender, amount))));\nrequire(MerkleProof.verify(proof, root, leaf), \"Not eligible\");","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"bytes32\\s+leaf\\s*=\\s*keccak256\\(\\s*bytes\\.concat\\(","text":"Line 1 starts <code>bytes32 leaf = keccak256(bytes.concat(</code>"},{"re":"keccak256\\(\\s*abi\\.encode\\(\\s*msg\\.sender\\s*,\\s*amount\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"then the inner hash <code>keccak256(abi.encode(msg.sender, amount))</code> and three closing brackets plus a semicolon."},{"re":"require\\(\\s*MerkleProof\\.verify\\(\\s*proof\\s*,\\s*root\\s*,\\s*leaf\\s*\\)\\s*,\\s*[\"']Not eligible[\"']\\s*\\)\\s*;","text":"Line 2: <code>require(MerkleProof.verify(proof, root, leaf), &quot;Not eligible&quot;);</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Elliptic curves in one page An elliptic curve is a set of points satisfying an equation like y² = x³ + 7 (Bitcoin and Ethereum's secp256k1 ), computed over a huge prime field so all numbers wrap around. You can add two points on the curve to get a third. Adding a starting point G to itself k times is called scalar multiplication : P = k × G . Computing P from k is fast (using doubling, about 256 steps). Recovering k from P and G is the elliptic curve discrete logarithm problem : no known method does it in any practical time for well-chosen curves. That one-way function is the whole trick: your private key is k , your public key is P = k × G . ECDSA and why nonces matter To sign a message hash z , the signer picks a secret one-time number k (the nonce ), computes a point from it, and produces a signature (r, s) from z , k and the private key. Anyone can verify (r, s) against the public key. Never reuse an ECDSA nonce If the same k is used for two different messages, simple algebra reveals the private key. This famously broke the PlayStation 3's code-signing key in 2010, and weak random number generators have emptied Bitcoin wallets. Modern libraries derive k deterministically (RFC 6979). Another reason never to implement signatures yourself. Ed25519 (an Edwards curve signature scheme) is designed to avoid these pitfalls and is the default for SSH keys, Solana and many modern systems. Merkle trees and proofs A Merkle tree hashes items in pairs, then hashes those hashes in pairs, until one root remains. root = H(H12 + H34) / \\ H12 = H(H1+H2) H34 = H(H3+H4) / \\ / \\ H1=H(A) H2=H(B) H3=H(C) H4=H(D) To prove C is in the set, you only need H4 and H12 : hash C , combine with H4 , combine with H12 , and compare with the root. For a million items, a proof is only about 20 hashes. This is how airdrops work cheaply: store only the 32-byte root on-chain; each user submits their own proof. // A tiny Merkle tree with SHA-256 and sorted pairs (the convention OpenZeppelin uses, with keccak256) const enc = new TextEncoder(); const toHex = (buf) = [...new Uint8Array(buf)].map((b) = b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')).join(''); const hash = async (s) = toHex(await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', enc.encode(s))); const pair = (a, b) = hash(a b ? a + b : b + a); const items = ['alice:100', 'bob:250', 'carol:75', 'dave:300']; const leaves = await Promise.all(items.map(hash)); // build the tree level by level const levels = [leaves]; while (levels.at(-1).length 1) { const prev = levels.at(-1), next = []; for (let i = 0; i prev.length; i += 2) next.push(await pair(prev[i], prev[i + 1] ?? prev[i])); levels.push(next); } const root = levels.at(-1)[0]; console.log('root', root); // proof for carol: the sibling at each level function proofFor(index) { const proof = []; for (const level of levels.slice(0, -1)) { const sibling = index % 2 ? level[index - 1] : level[index + 1] ?? level[index]; proof.push(sibling); index = Math.floor(index / 2); } return proof; } const proof = proofFor(2); async function verify(item, proof, root) { let h = await hash(item); for (const sib of proof) h = await pair(h, sib); return h === root; } console.log('carol:75 included?', await verify('carol:75', proof, root)); // true console.log('carol:999 included?', await verify('carol:999', proof, root)); // false In Solidity, OpenZeppelin's MerkleProof.verify(proof, root, leaf) does the checking. Off-chain, @openzeppelin/merkle-tree builds compatible trees. Hash leaves twice (or with a distinct prefix) to prevent a known second-preimage trick where an inner node is passed off as a leaf. Zero-knowledge proofs A zero-knowledge proof convinces a verifier that a statement is true without revealing why: I know a password that hashes to this value without revealing the password. I am over 18 without revealing a birthdate. These 10,000 transactions were all executed correctly with one short proof (this is what ZK rollups do). In practice you write the statement as a circuit (a program with ari","track":"crypto"},{"id":"tok-basics","title":"\"Tokenomics 101: supply, market cap and FDV\"","level":"beginner","minutes":20,"summary":"The numbers every token has, how to calculate them, and why a \"cheap\" token often is not.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-tokenomics-is\">What tokenomics is</h2>\n<p><strong>Tokenomics</strong> (token economics) is the design of a token's supply, distribution and incentives: how many exist, who gets them and when, what they are used for, and what makes people want to hold or use them. Good tokenomics cannot save a useless product, but bad tokenomics can sink a good one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-three-supply-numbers\">The three supply numbers</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Max supply</strong></td><td>The most that can ever exist. Bitcoin: 21 million. Some tokens have no cap.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Total supply</strong></td><td>Tokens created so far (minus any burned), including locked ones.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Circulating supply</strong></td><td>Tokens actually unlocked and able to trade today.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"market-cap-and-fdv\">Market cap and FDV</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">Market cap = price x circulating supply\nFDV (fully diluted valuation) = price x max supply</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Example.</strong> A token trades at $0.50. Max supply is 1,000,000,000. Only 100,000,000 (10%) circulate.</p>\n<ul><li>Market cap = $0.50 x 100M = <strong>$50 million</strong></li><li>FDV = $0.50 x 1B = <strong>$500 million</strong></li><li>FDV is <strong>10x</strong> the market cap.</li></ul>\n<p>That gap is the supply still to come. If the other 900 million tokens unlock over four years, demand must grow roughly tenfold just to keep the price at $0.50. This <strong>low float, high FDV</strong> pattern has burned many buyers.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"price-is-not-value\">Price is not value</h2>\n<p>A token at $0.0001 is not &quot;cheap&quot; and a token at $3,000 is not &quot;expensive&quot;. Price on its own means nothing without supply.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Price</th><th>Circulating supply</th><th>Market cap</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>$0.0001</td><td>900 trillion</td><td>$90 billion</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>$40.00</td><td>2 million</td><td>$80 million</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Token A &quot;only&quot; needs to reach $1 to make you rich? That would be a market cap of $900 trillion, many times the value of every company on Earth. This trap is called <strong>unit bias</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"supply-over-time\">Supply over time</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Fixed supply</strong> (Bitcoin): new coins are issued on a schedule that halves roughly every four years, approaching 21 million.</li><li><strong>Inflationary</strong>: new tokens keep being created (often as staking rewards). Holders who do not earn the new tokens are diluted.</li><li><strong>Deflationary pressure</strong>: tokens are <strong>burned</strong> (destroyed). Ethereum burns the base fee of every transaction, so its supply can shrink when the network is busy.</li><li><strong>Elastic / stable</strong>: supply expands and contracts to hold a target price (stablecoins). Fiat-backed stablecoins like USDC instead mint and redeem against real reserves.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"questions-to-ask-about-any-token\">Questions to ask about any token</h2>\n<ol><li>What is the max supply, and how much circulates today?</li><li>What is the FDV to market cap ratio?</li><li>When do the locked tokens unlock, and who gets them?</li><li>Is new supply being created? By whom, and why?</li><li>What is the token <strong>used for</strong>, and would anyone want it if the price stopped going up?</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-try\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Try it</p><p>Open the <a href=\"#token-sim\">Tokenomics Simulator</a> and load the <strong>Low float, high FDV</strong> preset. Look at the FDV to market cap ratio, then the circulating supply curve. That gap is what this lesson is about.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Price $2, circulating supply 50 million, max supply 500 million. What is the market cap?","options":["$100 million","$1 billion","$250 million"],"answer":0,"explain":"Market cap uses circulating supply: 2 x 50M = $100M. FDV would be $1B."},{"q":"In the same example, what does the 10x gap between FDV and market cap suggest?","options":["The token is undervalued by 10x","90% of the supply is still to arrive, which will dilute holders unless demand grows a lot","The token has no max supply"],"answer":1,"explain":"The gap is future supply. It has to be absorbed by future demand."},{"q":"Token X costs $0.00001. Why is it not necessarily cheap?","options":["Low-priced tokens are always scams","Value depends on price times supply; with a huge supply, the market cap may already be enormous","Prices below one cent are not allowed on exchanges"],"answer":1,"explain":"Unit bias: always compare market caps, never prices."},{"q":"What does burning tokens do?","options":["Permanently removes them from supply","Locks them for a year","Sends them to the team"],"answer":0,"explain":"Burned tokens are destroyed. Whether that supports price depends on how burning compares with new issuance and demand."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Market cap and FDV","file":"valuation.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Five lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const price = 0.5;</code></li><li><code>const circulating = 100_000_000;</code></li><li><code>const maxSupply = 1_000_000_000;</code></li><li>Print the market cap: <code>price * circulating</code></li><li>Print the FDV: <code>price * maxSupply</code></li></ol>\n<p>(Underscores in numbers are allowed and just make them readable.)</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const price = 0.5;\nconst circulating = 100_000_000;\nconst maxSupply = 1_000_000_000;\nconsole.log(price * circulating);\nconsole.log(price * maxSupply);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+price\\s*=\\s*0\\.5","text":"Line 1: <code>const price = 0.5;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+circulating\\s*=\\s*100_000_000","text":"Line 2: <code>const circulating = 100_000_000;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+maxSupply\\s*=\\s*1_000_000_000","text":"Line 3: <code>const maxSupply = 1_000_000_000;</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*price\\s*\\*\\s*circulating\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(price * circulating);</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*price\\s*\\*\\s*maxSupply\\s*\\)","text":"Line 5: <code>console.log(price * maxSupply);</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>50000000</code> (a $50M market cap) and <code>500000000</code> (a $500M FDV). Nine-tenths of the valuation is supply that does not exist on the market yet.</p>"},{"title":"The FDV gap","file":"ratio.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Three lines: <code>const marketCap = 50_000_000;</code>, <code>const fdv = 500_000_000;</code>, then print <code>fdv / marketCap</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const marketCap = 50_000_000;\nconst fdv = 500_000_000;\nconsole.log(fdv / marketCap);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+marketCap\\s*=\\s*50_000_000","text":"Line 1: <code>const marketCap = 50_000_000;</code> (capital C)."},{"re":"const\\s+fdv\\s*=\\s*500_000_000","text":"Line 2: <code>const fdv = 500_000_000;</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*fdv\\s*\\/\\s*marketCap\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(fdv / marketCap);</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"What tokenomics is Tokenomics (token economics) is the design of a token's supply, distribution and incentives: how many exist, who gets them and when, what they are used for, and what makes people want to hold or use them. Good tokenomics cannot save a useless product, but bad tokenomics can sink a good one. The three supply numbers Metric Meaning Max supply The most that can ever exist. Bitcoin: 21 million. Some tokens have no cap. Total supply Tokens created so far (minus any burned), including locked ones. Circulating supply Tokens actually unlocked and able to trade today. Market cap and FDV Market cap = price x circulating supply FDV (fully diluted valuation) = price x max supply Example. A token trades at $0.50. Max supply is 1,000,000,000. Only 100,000,000 (10%) circulate. Market cap = $0.50 x 100M = $50 million FDV = $0.50 x 1B = $500 million FDV is 10x the market cap. That gap is the supply still to come. If the other 900 million tokens unlock over four years, demand must grow roughly tenfold just to keep the price at $0.50. This low float, high FDV pattern has burned many buyers. Price is not value A token at $0.0001 is not cheap and a token at $3,000 is not expensive . Price on its own means nothing without supply. Token Price Circulating supply Market cap A $0.0001 900 trillion $90 billion B $40.00 2 million $80 million Token A only needs to reach $1 to make you rich? That would be a market cap of $900 trillion, many times the value of every company on Earth. This trap is called unit bias . Supply over time Fixed supply (Bitcoin): new coins are issued on a schedule that halves roughly every four years, approaching 21 million. Inflationary : new tokens keep being created (often as staking rewards). Holders who do not earn the new tokens are diluted. Deflationary pressure : tokens are burned (destroyed). Ethereum burns the base fee of every transaction, so its supply can shrink when the network is busy. Elastic / stable : supply expands and contracts to hold a target price (stablecoins). Fiat-backed stablecoins like USDC instead mint and redeem against real reserves. Questions to ask about any token What is the max supply, and how much circulates today? What is the FDV to market cap ratio? When do the locked tokens unlock, and who gets them? Is new supply being created? By whom, and why? What is the token used for , and would anyone want it if the price stopped going up? Try it Open the Tokenomics Simulator and load the Low float, high FDV preset. Look at the FDV to market cap ratio, then the circulating supply curve. That gap is what this lesson is about.","track":"tokenomics"},{"id":"tok-allocation","title":"Allocation and utility","level":"beginner","minutes":25,"summary":"Who receives tokens and why, what a token is actually for, and how value flows (or does not) to holders.","html":"<h2 id=\"who-gets-the-tokens\">Who gets the tokens</h2>\n<p>Every token's supply is split between groups. A typical launch includes some of these:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Purpose</th><th>What to look for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Team</strong></td><td>Pay and motivate builders</td><td>Long vesting (3 to 4 years) with a 12-month cliff</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Investors</strong></td><td>Funding before launch</td><td>Vesting; their entry price versus yours</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Advisors</strong></td><td>Expertise and network</td><td>Small share, vested</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Community / ecosystem</strong></td><td>Grants, rewards, incentives, partnerships</td><td>Who controls it, and how it is released</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Treasury</strong></td><td>Long-term operations, run by the project or a DAO</td><td>Transparency, multisig or governance control</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Liquidity</strong></td><td>Seeding trading pools at launch</td><td>Locked or protocol-owned</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Airdrop</strong></td><td>Reward early users, decentralise ownership</td><td>Sybil resistance (one person, many wallets)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>There is no perfect split. Combined team, investor and advisor (&quot;insider&quot;) shares are frequently somewhere in the 30 to 50% range at launch; much higher than that tells you who the project is really built for. Context matters: what matters most is that the split is justified, disclosed and vested.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-is-the-token-for\">What is the token for?</h2>\n<p>A token needs a job. Common <strong>utilities</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Utility</th><th>Example</th><th>Creates demand because</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Gas / fees</strong></td><td>ETH pays for every Ethereum transaction</td><td>Using the network requires it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Staking / security</strong></td><td>Validators stake ETH to secure the chain</td><td>Locked tokens earn rewards and secure the system</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Governance</strong></td><td>Holders vote on protocol upgrades and treasury spend</td><td>Control over something valuable</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Collateral</strong></td><td>Deposited to borrow against</td><td>Unlocks liquidity for the holder</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Access</strong></td><td>Hold or spend to use features, content or communities</td><td>The product requires it</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rewards / points</strong></td><td>Earned for activity</td><td>Drives usage (but is also new supply to sell)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fee share / buybacks</strong></td><td>Protocol revenue flows to stakers or buys tokens</td><td>Links token value to real business performance</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-two-questions-that-matter-most\">The two questions that matter most</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Would the product work just as well without the token?</strong> If yes, the token is probably a fundraising tool, and its price depends on speculation alone.</p>\n<p><strong>2. How does value flow to holders?</strong> A protocol can make lots of money while its token captures none of it. Look for a clear, sustainable link: fees used to buy back and burn, real yield to stakers, or demand created by using the product.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-sinks\">Sources and sinks</h2>\n<p>Think of a token economy as a bathtub:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Sources (taps)</strong>: unlocks, emissions, rewards, airdrops. Each adds supply that someone might sell.</li><li><strong>Sinks (drains)</strong>: fees burned, tokens locked for staking or governance, tokens spent on in-app items, buybacks.</li></ul>\n<p>If the taps run faster than the drains and demand is not growing, the price falls. Most failed tokens simply had more taps than drains.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Red flags in an allocation</p><p>Insiders above about 40%. Short or no vesting for team and investors. A large &quot;marketing&quot; or &quot;ecosystem&quot; bucket controlled by one wallet with no schedule. Undisclosed allocations. A token whose only use is &quot;staking to earn more of the same token&quot;.</p></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">For marketers and founders</p><p>If you help brands launch tokens or token-gated communities, lead with utility and fairness, not price. Projects that publish clear allocations, on-chain vesting contracts and a real reason to hold the token build trust that hype cannot.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"What is the main risk if team and investors hold 60% of supply with short vesting?","options":["Insiders can sell large amounts early and control governance votes","The token becomes deflationary","Gas fees rise"],"answer":0,"explain":"Concentrated, quickly-unlocking insider supply is one of the clearest red flags."},{"q":"A protocol earns millions in fees, but none of it reaches token holders and the token has no use in the product. What does that mean?","options":["The token price must rise with revenue","The token captures little or none of the protocol's value, so its price relies on speculation","The protocol is illegal"],"answer":1,"explain":"Revenue and token value are separate unless there is a mechanism linking them."},{"q":"Which of these is a token sink?","options":["A monthly team unlock","Staking rewards paid in new tokens","A fee that is burned every time the product is used"],"answer":2,"explain":"Sinks remove or lock supply. Unlocks and emissions are sources."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Check an allocation adds up","file":"allocation.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Three lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const allocation = { team: 18, investors: 17, community: 45, treasury: 20 };</code></li><li><code>const total = Object.values(allocation).reduce((sum, pct) =&gt; sum + pct, 0);</code></li><li><code>console.log(total === 100 ? 'Adds up' : 'Check the numbers');</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const allocation = { team: 18, investors: 17, community: 45, treasury: 20 };\nconst total = Object.values(allocation).reduce((sum, pct) => sum + pct, 0);\nconsole.log(total === 100 ? 'Adds up' : 'Check the numbers');","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+allocation\\s*=\\s*\\{\\s*team\\s*:\\s*18\\s*,\\s*investors\\s*:\\s*17\\s*,\\s*community\\s*:\\s*45\\s*,\\s*treasury\\s*:\\s*20\\s*\\}","text":"Line 1 is an object: <code>{ team: 18, investors: 17, community: 45, treasury: 20 }</code>"},{"re":"Object\\.values\\(\\s*allocation\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2 starts from <code>Object.values(allocation)</code>, which gives <code>[18, 17, 45, 20]</code>."},{"re":"\\.reduce\\(\\s*\\(\\s*sum\\s*,\\s*pct\\s*\\)\\s*=>\\s*sum\\s*\\+\\s*pct\\s*,\\s*0\\s*\\)","text":"then <code>.reduce((sum, pct) =&gt; sum + pct, 0)</code>"},{"re":"total\\s*===\\s*100\\s*\\?\\s*['\"]Adds up['\"]\\s*:\\s*['\"]Check the numbers['\"]","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(total === 100 ? 'Adds up' : 'Check the numbers');</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Flag insider-heavy allocations","file":"insiders.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Four lines: <code>const team = 18;</code>, <code>const investors = 17;</code>, <code>const insiders = team + investors;</code>, then print <code>insiders &gt; 40 ? 'Red flag' : 'OK'</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const team = 18;\nconst investors = 17;\nconst insiders = team + investors;\nconsole.log(insiders > 40 ? 'Red flag' : 'OK');","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+team\\s*=\\s*18","text":"Line 1: <code>const team = 18;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+investors\\s*=\\s*17","text":"Line 2: <code>const investors = 17;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+insiders\\s*=\\s*team\\s*\\+\\s*investors","text":"Line 3: <code>const insiders = team + investors;</code>"},{"re":"insiders\\s*>\\s*40\\s*\\?\\s*['\"]Red flag['\"]\\s*:\\s*['\"]OK['\"]","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(insiders &gt; 40 ? 'Red flag' : 'OK');</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Who gets the tokens Every token's supply is split between groups. A typical launch includes some of these: Group Purpose What to look for Team Pay and motivate builders Long vesting (3 to 4 years) with a 12-month cliff Investors Funding before launch Vesting; their entry price versus yours Advisors Expertise and network Small share, vested Community / ecosystem Grants, rewards, incentives, partnerships Who controls it, and how it is released Treasury Long-term operations, run by the project or a DAO Transparency, multisig or governance control Liquidity Seeding trading pools at launch Locked or protocol-owned Airdrop Reward early users, decentralise ownership Sybil resistance (one person, many wallets) There is no perfect split. Combined team, investor and advisor ( insider ) shares are frequently somewhere in the 30 to 50% range at launch; much higher than that tells you who the project is really built for. Context matters: what matters most is that the split is justified, disclosed and vested. What is the token for? A token needs a job. Common utilities : Utility Example Creates demand because Gas / fees ETH pays for every Ethereum transaction Using the network requires it Staking / security Validators stake ETH to secure the chain Locked tokens earn rewards and secure the system Governance Holders vote on protocol upgrades and treasury spend Control over something valuable Collateral Deposited to borrow against Unlocks liquidity for the holder Access Hold or spend to use features, content or communities The product requires it Rewards / points Earned for activity Drives usage (but is also new supply to sell) Fee share / buybacks Protocol revenue flows to stakers or buys tokens Links token value to real business performance The two questions that matter most 1. Would the product work just as well without the token? If yes, the token is probably a fundraising tool, and its price depends on speculation alone. 2. How does value flow to holders? A protocol can make lots of money while its token captures none of it. Look for a clear, sustainable link: fees used to buy back and burn, real yield to stakers, or demand created by using the product. Sources and sinks Think of a token economy as a bathtub: Sources (taps) : unlocks, emissions, rewards, airdrops. Each adds supply that someone might sell. Sinks (drains) : fees burned, tokens locked for staking or governance, tokens spent on in-app items, buybacks. If the taps run faster than the drains and demand is not growing, the price falls. Most failed tokens simply had more taps than drains. Red flags in an allocation Insiders above about 40%. Short or no vesting for team and investors. A large marketing or ecosystem bucket controlled by one wallet with no schedule. Undisclosed allocations. A token whose only use is staking to earn more of the same token . For marketers and founders If you help brands launch tokens or token-gated communities, lead with utility and fairness, not price. Projects that publish clear allocations, on-chain vesting contracts and a real reason to hold the token build trust that hype cannot.","track":"tokenomics"},{"id":"tok-vesting","title":"Vesting, cliffs and unlock schedules","level":"intermediate","minutes":30,"summary":"Calculate exactly how many tokens are unlocked on any date, read unlock charts, and spot dangerous cliffs.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-vocabulary\">The vocabulary</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>TGE (token generation event)</strong>: launch day, when trading starts.</li><li><strong>TGE unlock</strong>: the percentage of an allocation released immediately.</li><li><strong>Cliff</strong>: a waiting period with no further unlocks.</li><li><strong>Vesting period</strong>: the time over which the rest unlocks, usually monthly or continuously (every block).</li><li><strong>Linear vesting</strong>: equal amounts per period.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"two-common-cliff-styles\">Two common cliff styles</h2>\n<p><strong>Style A: linear from the cliff.</strong> &quot;12-month cliff, then 24 months linear.&quot; Nothing until month 12, then an equal slice every month until month 36.</p>\n<p><strong>Style B: lump at the cliff.</strong> &quot;36-month vesting with a 12-month cliff.&quot; Tokens accrue from day one, but none can be claimed until month 12, when everything accrued so far (12/36 = 33%) unlocks at once. Then the rest vests monthly. This is common in employment-style deals and creates a big one-day <strong>unlock wall</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"#token-sim\">Tokenomics Simulator</a> has a switch for each style. Try both with the same numbers and watch the bars.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-maths\">The maths</h2>\n<p>For one allocation, the fraction unlocked at month <code>m</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">function unlockedFraction(m, { tge = 0, cliff = 0, vest = 0, lumpAtCliff = false }) {\n  if (m &lt; cliff) return tge;                          // only the TGE unlock so far\n  if (vest === 0) return 1;                           // everything at the cliff\n  const vested = lumpAtCliff\n    ? Math.min(1, m / (cliff + vest))                 // accrued since launch\n    : Math.min(1, (m - cliff) / vest);                // linear starting at the cliff\n  return tge + (1 - tge) * vested;\n}\n\nconst team = { tge: 0, cliff: 12, vest: 24 };\nfor (const m of [0, 6, 11, 12, 13, 24, 36, 48]) {\n  console.log(`month ${m}: ${(unlockedFraction(m, team) * 100).toFixed(1)}% of team tokens unlocked`);\n}\n\nconst teamLump = { ...team, lumpAtCliff: true };\nconsole.log('lump style at month 12:', (unlockedFraction(12, teamLump) * 100).toFixed(1) + '%');</code></pre></div>\n<p>Add every allocation together, multiply by max supply, and you have the circulating supply curve.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"why-vesting-matters-to-price\">Why vesting matters to price</h2>\n<p>Every unlock gives someone tokens they may sell. Investors who bought at $0.02 are very happy to sell at $0.30. What matters is not only how much unlocks, but:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Relative size</strong>: a 2% unlock when 10% circulates grows supply by 20% in a day.</li><li><strong>Holder cost basis</strong>: early investors are deep in profit; airdrop recipients paid nothing.</li><li><strong>Holder intent</strong>: a treasury unlock that goes to a grants programme is different from a seed investor unlock.</li></ul>\n<p>Markets tend to anticipate large unlocks, so price pressure often appears in the weeks before, not just on the day.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reading-an-unlock-chart\">Reading an unlock chart</h2>\n<p>Sites like Tokenomist (formerly Token Unlocks) show schedules for live tokens. Look for:</p>\n<ol><li>The <strong>circulating supply today</strong> versus max supply.</li><li><strong>Steps</strong> in the curve: cliff dates for insiders.</li><li>The <strong>slope</strong> after the cliffs: steady monthly pressure.</li><li><strong>When the curve flattens</strong>: after that, new supply comes only from emissions.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"vesting-on-chain\">Vesting on-chain</h2>\n<p>Promises in a PDF are not vesting. Trustworthy projects lock allocations in <strong>vesting contracts</strong> anyone can inspect. OpenZeppelin's <code>VestingWallet</code> releases tokens linearly between a start time and an end time to a beneficiary, and its cliff variant adds a cliff. Projects often use audited vesting platforms too. Either way, verify on a block explorer that the tokens are really locked.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Design guidelines that build trust</p><p>Team and investors: a 12-month cliff and 2 to 4 years of vesting. Avoid one-day walls by vesting monthly (or per block) after the cliff. Publish the schedule and contract addresses. Avoid tiny launch floats that let insiders print a huge paper valuation.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Team allocation: 0% at TGE, 12-month cliff, then 24 months linear (style A). What fraction is unlocked at month 24?","options":["100%","50%","33%"],"answer":1,"explain":"(24 - 12) / 24 = 0.5. Fully unlocked at month 36."},{"q":"Same numbers, but style B (lump at cliff, 36 months total). What unlocks at month 12?","options":["Nothing","About 33% in one go","50%"],"answer":1,"explain":"12 of 36 months have accrued, all released together when the cliff ends."},{"q":"10% of supply circulates. A 3% unlock happens next week. By how much does circulating supply grow?","options":["3%","30%","13%"],"answer":1,"explain":"3 / 10 = 30% more circulating tokens. Relative size matters more than the headline number."},{"q":"What makes a vesting schedule trustworthy?","options":["It is described in the whitepaper","The team promises on social media","The tokens are locked in a public vesting contract you can verify on-chain"],"answer":2,"explain":"Code anyone can read beats promises anyone can break."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write a vesting function","file":"vesting.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write <code>unlocked(month, cliff, vest)</code>: return <code>0</code> before the cliff, otherwise <code>Math.min(1, (month - cliff) / vest)</code>. Then print <code>unlocked(24, 12, 24)</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function unlocked(month, cliff, vest) {\n  if (month < cliff) return 0;\n  return Math.min(1, (month - cliff) / vest);\n}\nconsole.log(unlocked(24, 12, 24));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+unlocked\\s*\\(\\s*month\\s*,\\s*cliff\\s*,\\s*vest\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function unlocked(month, cliff, vest) {</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*month\\s*<\\s*cliff\\s*\\)\\s*return\\s+0","text":"Line 2: <code>if (month &lt; cliff) return 0;</code>"},{"re":"return\\s+Math\\.min\\(\\s*1\\s*,\\s*\\(\\s*month\\s*-\\s*cliff\\s*\\)\\s*\\/\\s*vest\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>return Math.min(1, (month - cliff) / vest);</code>"},{"re":"unlocked\\(\\s*24\\s*,\\s*12\\s*,\\s*24\\s*\\)","text":"After the function: <code>console.log(unlocked(24, 12, 24));</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>0.5</code>. Twelve months after a 12-month cliff, half of a 24-month vest has unlocked. <code>Math.min(1, ...)</code> caps it at 100% after the vest ends.</p>"},{"title":"How big is that unlock, really?","file":"unlock.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Circulating supply is 10% and 3% unlocks next week. Three lines: <code>const circulating = 10;</code>, <code>const unlock = 3;</code>, then print <code>(unlock / circulating) * 100</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const circulating = 10;\nconst unlock = 3;\nconsole.log((unlock / circulating) * 100);","also":["const circulating = 10;\nconst unlock = 3;\nconsole.log(unlock / circulating * 100);"],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+circulating\\s*=\\s*10","text":"Line 1: <code>const circulating = 10;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+unlock\\s*=\\s*3","text":"Line 2: <code>const unlock = 3;</code>"},{"re":"unlock\\s*\\/\\s*circulating\\s*\\)?\\s*\\*\\s*100","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log((unlock / circulating) * 100);</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"The vocabulary TGE (token generation event) : launch day, when trading starts. TGE unlock : the percentage of an allocation released immediately. Cliff : a waiting period with no further unlocks. Vesting period : the time over which the rest unlocks, usually monthly or continuously (every block). Linear vesting : equal amounts per period. Two common cliff styles Style A: linear from the cliff. 12-month cliff, then 24 months linear. Nothing until month 12, then an equal slice every month until month 36. Style B: lump at the cliff. 36-month vesting with a 12-month cliff. Tokens accrue from day one, but none can be claimed until month 12, when everything accrued so far (12/36 = 33%) unlocks at once. Then the rest vests monthly. This is common in employment-style deals and creates a big one-day unlock wall . The Tokenomics Simulator has a switch for each style. Try both with the same numbers and watch the bars. The maths For one allocation, the fraction unlocked at month m : function unlockedFraction(m, { tge = 0, cliff = 0, vest = 0, lumpAtCliff = false }) { if (m cliff) return tge; // only the TGE unlock so far if (vest === 0) return 1; // everything at the cliff const vested = lumpAtCliff ? Math.min(1, m / (cliff + vest)) // accrued since launch : Math.min(1, (m - cliff) / vest); // linear starting at the cliff return tge + (1 - tge) * vested; } const team = { tge: 0, cliff: 12, vest: 24 }; for (const m of [0, 6, 11, 12, 13, 24, 36, 48]) { console.log(`month ${m}: ${(unlockedFraction(m, team) * 100).toFixed(1)}% of team tokens unlocked`); } const teamLump = { ...team, lumpAtCliff: true }; console.log('lump style at month 12:', (unlockedFraction(12, teamLump) * 100).toFixed(1) + '%'); Add every allocation together, multiply by max supply, and you have the circulating supply curve. Why vesting matters to price Every unlock gives someone tokens they may sell. Investors who bought at $0.02 are very happy to sell at $0.30. What matters is not only how much unlocks, but: Relative size : a 2% unlock when 10% circulates grows supply by 20% in a day. Holder cost basis : early investors are deep in profit; airdrop recipients paid nothing. Holder intent : a treasury unlock that goes to a grants programme is different from a seed investor unlock. Markets tend to anticipate large unlocks, so price pressure often appears in the weeks before, not just on the day. Reading an unlock chart Sites like Tokenomist (formerly Token Unlocks) show schedules for live tokens. Look for: The circulating supply today versus max supply. Steps in the curve: cliff dates for insiders. The slope after the cliffs: steady monthly pressure. When the curve flattens : after that, new supply comes only from emissions. Vesting on-chain Promises in a PDF are not vesting. Trustworthy projects lock allocations in vesting contracts anyone can inspect. OpenZeppelin's VestingWallet releases tokens linearly between a start time and an end time to a beneficiary, and its cliff variant adds a cliff. Projects often use audited vesting platforms too. Either way, verify on a block explorer that the tokens are really locked. Design guidelines that build trust Team and investors: a 12-month cliff and 2 to 4 years of vesting. Avoid one-day walls by vesting monthly (or per block) after the cliff. Publish the schedule and contract addresses. Avoid tiny launch floats that let insiders print a huge paper valuation.","track":"tokenomics"},{"id":"tok-amm","title":"AMMs, liquidity and price impact","level":"intermediate","minutes":35,"summary":"How x*y=k sets prices, why big trades move them, what liquidity providers earn and risk, and how launches use pools.","html":"<h2 id=\"from-order-books-to-formulas\">From order books to formulas</h2>\n<p>Traditional exchanges match buyers and sellers in an <strong>order book</strong>. An <strong>Automated Market Maker (AMM)</strong> replaces that with a pool of two tokens and a formula. Anyone can trade against the pool at any time, and anyone can add liquidity to it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-constant-product-formula\">The constant product formula</h2>\n<p>Uniswap v2 popularised the simplest design. A pool holds <code>x</code> of TOKEN and <code>y</code> of USDC, and trades must keep:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">x * y = k   (constant)\nprice of TOKEN in USDC = y / x</code></pre></div>\n<p>When you buy TOKEN, you add USDC (y goes up) and remove TOKEN (x goes down). To keep <code>k</code> constant, each extra token costs more than the last. The pool can never run out: the price just rises towards infinity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-worked-trade\">A worked trade</h2>\n<p>Pool: 1,000,000 TOKEN and 250,000 USDC, so the price is <strong>$0.25</strong>. Fee: 0.3%. You buy with 10,000 USDC.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">USDC in after fee = 10,000 x 0.997                = 9,970\nTOKEN out         = x * in / (y + in)\n                  = 1,000,000 x 9,970 / 259,970   = 38,350.6 TOKEN\nYour average price = 10,000 / 38,350.6            = $0.2608\nPool price after   = 260,000 / 961,649.4           = $0.2704</code></pre></div>\n<p>You paid an average of 4.3% more than the starting price: about 4% from your own trade moving the price along the curve (<strong>price impact</strong>), plus the 0.3% fee. Bigger trades and smaller pools mean more impact. Run the numbers yourself in the <a href=\"#amm-lab\">AMM Lab</a>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">function quote({ x, y, fee = 0.003 }, usdcIn) {\n  const inAfterFee = usdcIn * (1 - fee);\n  const out = (x * inAfterFee) / (y + inAfterFee);\n  const avg = usdcIn / out;\n  return { out, avg, impact: avg / (y / x) - 1, newPrice: (y + usdcIn) / (x - out) };\n}\n\nconst pool = { x: 1_000_000, y: 250_000 };\nfor (const size of [100, 1_000, 10_000, 50_000]) {\n  const q = quote(pool, size);\n  console.log(`Spend ${size} USDC: get ${q.out.toFixed(0)} TOKEN, impact ${(q.impact * 100).toFixed(2)}%`);\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"slippage-protection\">Slippage protection</h2>\n<p>Between signing your swap and it landing in a block, other trades can move the price. Your <strong>slippage tolerance</strong> sets the worst price you accept (as a minimum amount out). Too tight and the transaction fails; too loose and bots can sandwich you. A deadline stops a stale transaction executing hours later.</p>\n<h2 id=\"liquidity-providers\">Liquidity providers</h2>\n<p>Anyone can deposit both tokens (at the current ratio) and receive <strong>LP tokens</strong> representing their share of the pool. Every trade's fee stays in the pool, so LP shares grow in value with trading volume.</p>\n<p>The catch is <strong>impermanent loss</strong>: when the price moves, the pool automatically sells the rising token and buys the falling one. Compared with simply holding both, an LP ends up with less.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">IL = 2 * sqrt(r) / (1 + r) - 1      where r = new price / old price\n\nprice x2   -&gt; about -5.7%\nprice x4   -&gt; -20%\nprice x0.5 -&gt; about -5.7%</code></pre></div>\n<p>LPs profit only when fees earned exceed impermanent loss. Pools of two assets that move together (like two stablecoins) have little IL.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"concentrated-liquidity\">Concentrated liquidity</h2>\n<p>Uniswap v3 and later designs let LPs provide liquidity only within a price range. Capital is far more efficient (deeper liquidity near the current price), but positions need active management: if the price leaves your range, you earn no fees and hold only one token.</p>\n<h2 id=\"liquidity-at-launch\">Liquidity at launch</h2>\n<p>New tokens usually launch by seeding a pool. The ratio chosen sets the launch price, and the size sets how much price impact traders suffer.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Thin liquidity</strong> means wild price swings; a few buyers can pump it and a few sellers can crash it.</li><li>If the team holds the LP tokens, they can withdraw all the liquidity at any time. That is the classic <strong>rug pull</strong>. Locked or burned LP tokens, or protocol-owned liquidity, are signs of good faith.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Check before you trade a new token</p><p>Is the LP locked, and until when? Can the owner mint, pause or blacklist? Are buy and sell taxes adjustable? Who holds the top wallets? Block explorers' holder tabs and token scanners help, but reading the verified contract is best.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"A pool holds 500,000 TOKEN and 100,000 USDC. What is TOKEN's price?","options":["$5.00","$0.20","$0.50"],"answer":1,"explain":"Price = y / x = 100,000 / 500,000."},{"q":"Why does a large buy get a worse average price than a small one in the same pool?","options":["Each unit bought moves the price along the x*y=k curve, so later units cost more","Large traders pay a higher fee percentage","Pools refuse large orders"],"answer":0,"explain":"That is price impact. Deeper pools reduce it."},{"q":"TOKEN's price doubles after you provide liquidity. Ignoring fees, compared with holding, you have:","options":["About 5.7% more","About 5.7% less","Exactly the same"],"answer":1,"explain":"Impermanent loss at 2x is about -5.7%. Fees have to make up the difference."},{"q":"What makes a &quot;rug pull&quot; possible after a launch?","options":["Locked LP tokens","The team holding unlocked LP tokens and withdrawing all the liquidity","Using a 0.3% fee"],"answer":1,"explain":"Whoever holds the LP tokens controls the liquidity."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Write the swap formula","file":"amm.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write <code>getAmountOut(amountIn, reserveIn, reserveOut)</code> with a 0.3% fee: <code>inWithFee</code> is <code>amountIn * 0.997</code>, and the function returns <code>(reserveOut * inWithFee) / (reserveIn + inWithFee)</code>. Then print <code>getAmountOut(10000, 250000, 1000000)</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"function getAmountOut(amountIn, reserveIn, reserveOut) {\n  const inWithFee = amountIn * 0.997;\n  return (reserveOut * inWithFee) / (reserveIn + inWithFee);\n}\nconsole.log(getAmountOut(10000, 250000, 1000000));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"function\\s+getAmountOut\\s*\\(\\s*amountIn\\s*,\\s*reserveIn\\s*,\\s*reserveOut\\s*\\)\\s*\\{","text":"Line 1: <code>function getAmountOut(amountIn, reserveIn, reserveOut) {</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+inWithFee\\s*=\\s*amountIn\\s*\\*\\s*0\\.997","text":"Line 2: <code>const inWithFee = amountIn * 0.997;</code>"},{"re":"return\\s*\\(\\s*reserveOut\\s*\\*\\s*inWithFee\\s*\\)\\s*\\/\\s*\\(\\s*reserveIn\\s*\\+\\s*inWithFee\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>return (reserveOut * inWithFee) / (reserveIn + inWithFee);</code>"},{"re":"getAmountOut\\(\\s*10000\\s*,\\s*250000\\s*,\\s*1000000\\s*\\)","text":"Last line: <code>console.log(getAmountOut(10000, 250000, 1000000));</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: about <code>38350.58</code> tokens, matching the worked example. This is the same maths Uniswap v2's contracts run, written with integers on-chain.</p>"},{"title":"Impermanent loss in one line","file":"il.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write an arrow function <code>il</code> that takes <code>r</code> and returns <code>(2 * Math.sqrt(r)) / (1 + r) - 1</code>, then print <code>il(2)</code> and <code>il(4)</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const il = (r) => (2 * Math.sqrt(r)) / (1 + r) - 1;\nconsole.log(il(2));\nconsole.log(il(4));","also":["const il = r => (2 * Math.sqrt(r)) / (1 + r) - 1;\nconsole.log(il(2));\nconsole.log(il(4));"],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+il\\s*=\\s*\\(?\\s*r\\s*\\)?\\s*=>","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const il = (r) =&gt;</code>"},{"re":"\\(\\s*2\\s*\\*\\s*Math\\.sqrt\\(\\s*r\\s*\\)\\s*\\)\\s*\\/\\s*\\(\\s*1\\s*\\+\\s*r\\s*\\)\\s*-\\s*1","text":"The formula: <code>(2 * Math.sqrt(r)) / (1 + r) - 1</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*il\\(\\s*2\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"<code>console.log(il(2));</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*il\\(\\s*4\\s*\\)\\s*\\)","text":"<code>console.log(il(4));</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: about <code>-0.057</code> (a 5.7% loss when the price doubles) and <code>-0.2</code> (20% when it quadruples), compared with just holding.</p>"}],"text":"From order books to formulas Traditional exchanges match buyers and sellers in an order book . An Automated Market Maker (AMM) replaces that with a pool of two tokens and a formula. Anyone can trade against the pool at any time, and anyone can add liquidity to it. The constant product formula Uniswap v2 popularised the simplest design. A pool holds x of TOKEN and y of USDC, and trades must keep: x * y = k (constant) price of TOKEN in USDC = y / x When you buy TOKEN, you add USDC (y goes up) and remove TOKEN (x goes down). To keep k constant, each extra token costs more than the last. The pool can never run out: the price just rises towards infinity. A worked trade Pool: 1,000,000 TOKEN and 250,000 USDC, so the price is $0.25 . Fee: 0.3%. You buy with 10,000 USDC. USDC in after fee = 10,000 x 0.997 = 9,970 TOKEN out = x * in / (y + in) = 1,000,000 x 9,970 / 259,970 = 38,350.6 TOKEN Your average price = 10,000 / 38,350.6 = $0.2608 Pool price after = 260,000 / 961,649.4 = $0.2704 You paid an average of 4.3% more than the starting price: about 4% from your own trade moving the price along the curve ( price impact ), plus the 0.3% fee. Bigger trades and smaller pools mean more impact. Run the numbers yourself in the AMM Lab . function quote({ x, y, fee = 0.003 }, usdcIn) { const inAfterFee = usdcIn * (1 - fee); const out = (x * inAfterFee) / (y + inAfterFee); const avg = usdcIn / out; return { out, avg, impact: avg / (y / x) - 1, newPrice: (y + usdcIn) / (x - out) }; } const pool = { x: 1_000_000, y: 250_000 }; for (const size of [100, 1_000, 10_000, 50_000]) { const q = quote(pool, size); console.log(`Spend ${size} USDC: get ${q.out.toFixed(0)} TOKEN, impact ${(q.impact * 100).toFixed(2)}%`); } Slippage protection Between signing your swap and it landing in a block, other trades can move the price. Your slippage tolerance sets the worst price you accept (as a minimum amount out). Too tight and the transaction fails; too loose and bots can sandwich you. A deadline stops a stale transaction executing hours later. Liquidity providers Anyone can deposit both tokens (at the current ratio) and receive LP tokens representing their share of the pool. Every trade's fee stays in the pool, so LP shares grow in value with trading volume. The catch is impermanent loss : when the price moves, the pool automatically sells the rising token and buys the falling one. Compared with simply holding both, an LP ends up with less. IL = 2 * sqrt(r) / (1 + r) - 1 where r = new price / old price price x2 - about -5.7% price x4 - -20% price x0.5 - about -5.7% LPs profit only when fees earned exceed impermanent loss. Pools of two assets that move together (like two stablecoins) have little IL. Concentrated liquidity Uniswap v3 and later designs let LPs provide liquidity only within a price range. Capital is far more efficient (deeper liquidity near the current price), but positions need active management: if the price leaves your range, you earn no fees and hold only one token. Liquidity at launch New tokens usually launch by seeding a pool. The ratio chosen sets the launch price, and the size sets how much price impact traders suffer. Thin liquidity means wild price swings; a few buyers can pump it and a few sellers can crash it. If the team holds the LP tokens, they can withdraw all the liquidity at any time. That is the classic rug pull . Locked or burned LP tokens, or protocol-owned liquidity, are signs of good faith. Check before you trade a new token Is the LP locked, and until when? Can the owner mint, pause or blacklist? Are buy and sell taxes adjustable? Who holds the top wallets? Block explorers' holder tabs and token scanners help, but reading the verified contract is best.","track":"tokenomics"},{"id":"tok-incentives","title":"Staking, emissions, burns and real yield","level":"intermediate","minutes":30,"summary":"Separate real returns from printed ones, design emission schedules, and understand when burns actually matter.","html":"<h2 id=\"emissions-the-printing-press\">Emissions: the printing press</h2>\n<p><strong>Emissions</strong> are new tokens created over time, usually to reward stakers, validators or liquidity providers. They are a cost paid by every holder through dilution. Done well, they buy something valuable: network security, liquidity, early users. Done badly, they pay people to farm and dump.</p>\n<p>Common schedules:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Schedule</th><th>Shape</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fixed per block</td><td>Same amount forever, so inflation % slowly falls</td><td>Many early chains</td></tr><tr><td>Halving</td><td>Cut in half on a schedule</td><td>Bitcoin, about every 4 years</td></tr><tr><td>Exponential decay</td><td>A % less each month</td><td>Many DeFi liquidity programmes</td></tr><tr><td>Dynamic</td><td>Depends on how much is staked or on usage</td><td>Ethereum's issuance under proof of stake</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"nominal-versus-real-yield\">Nominal versus real yield</h2>\n<p>&quot;Stake and earn 40% APY!&quot; Paid in what?</p>\n<p>If you earn 40% more tokens per year, and total supply also grows 40% per year from those same emissions, your <strong>share of the network is unchanged</strong>. You only avoided being diluted; non-stakers paid for it. And if the price falls because farmers sell their rewards, you can lose money while your token balance grows.</p>\n<p><strong>Real yield</strong> comes from actual revenue: trading fees, borrowing interest, protocol income, paid in ETH or stablecoins or used to buy tokens from the market. Ask every time: <em>where does this yield come from?</em> If the answer is &quot;new tokens&quot;, it is dilution with extra steps. If nobody can answer, be very careful.</p>\n<h2 id=\"burns\">Burns</h2>\n<p>Burning removes tokens permanently. It is often marketed as automatically good. It only helps when:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">net supply change = new issuance - tokens burned</code></pre></div>\n<p>is low or negative <strong>and</strong> demand holds up. Ethereum's EIP-1559 burns the base fee of every transaction, so when network usage is high, more ETH can be burned than issued. A &quot;burn&quot; of tokens the team never intended to sell, or a tiny burn alongside big emissions, changes little.</p>\n<p><strong>Buybacks</strong> use protocol revenue to buy tokens on the market (then burn them or send them to stakers or the treasury). They link token demand to business performance, which is why many mature protocols favour them.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"mercenary-capital\">Mercenary capital</h2>\n<p>In 2020's &quot;DeFi summer&quot;, protocols handed out huge token emissions to attract liquidity. Capital flooded in, farmed the rewards, sold them, and left the moment rewards dropped. Lessons the industry learned:</p>\n<ul><li>Reward <strong>behaviour you want to last</strong> (long-term locking, real usage) rather than just parked capital.</li><li>Taper emissions and announce schedules clearly.</li><li>Measure <strong>retention after rewards end</strong>, not total value locked during them.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Points programmes</strong> (track activity now, convert to tokens later) are the modern version. Same economics, delayed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"model-it\">Model it</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">// 5 years of supply: 100M initial, 20M emitted in year 1 decaying 30% a year, plus fee burns\nlet supply = 100_000_000;\nlet emission = 20_000_000;\nconst yearlyBurn = 4_000_000; // assume steady usage burns 4M tokens a year\n\nfor (let year = 1; year &lt;= 5; year++) {\n  const before = supply;\n  supply = supply + emission - yearlyBurn;\n  const inflation = (supply / before - 1) * 100;\n  console.log(`Year ${year}: emitted ${(emission / 1e6).toFixed(1)}M, supply ${(supply / 1e6).toFixed(1)}M, net inflation ${inflation.toFixed(1)}%`);\n  emission *= 0.7;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Try changing the burn to 10M and the decay to 0.5. When does net inflation turn negative?</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The sustainability test</p><p>Imagine the token price drops 80% and stays there for a year. Do validators or stakers still have a reason to stay? Does the treasury still cover two years of costs? Do users still use the product? Designs that pass this test survive bear markets.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Staking pays 25% APY in new tokens, and supply inflates 25% a year from those rewards. What happens to a staker's share of total supply?","options":["It grows 25% a year","It stays about the same; non-stakers are diluted instead","It shrinks"],"answer":1,"explain":"Nominal yield from emissions mostly redistributes value from non-stakers to stakers."},{"q":"Which is real yield?","options":["Rewards paid in newly minted governance tokens","Stakers receiving a share of trading fees paid in ETH or stablecoins","A bonus for referring friends, paid in new tokens"],"answer":1,"explain":"Real yield comes from revenue that users actually paid."},{"q":"A protocol emits 10M tokens a year and burns 1M a year. What is net issuance?","options":["9M tokens a year of new supply","Deflationary, because it burns","11M tokens a year"],"answer":0,"explain":"Net change is issuance minus burn. A small burn does not offset large emissions."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Inflationary or deflationary?","file":"net.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Four lines: <code>const emitted = 10_000_000;</code>, <code>const burned = 1_000_000;</code>, <code>const net = emitted - burned;</code>, then print <code>net &gt; 0 ? 'Inflationary' : 'Deflationary'</code> and <code>net</code> in the same <code>console.log</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const emitted = 10_000_000;\nconst burned = 1_000_000;\nconst net = emitted - burned;\nconsole.log(net > 0 ? 'Inflationary' : 'Deflationary', net);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+emitted\\s*=\\s*10_000_000","text":"Line 1: <code>const emitted = 10_000_000;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+burned\\s*=\\s*1_000_000","text":"Line 2: <code>const burned = 1_000_000;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+net\\s*=\\s*emitted\\s*-\\s*burned","text":"Line 3: <code>const net = emitted - burned;</code>"},{"re":"net\\s*>\\s*0\\s*\\?\\s*['\"]Inflationary['\"]\\s*:\\s*['\"]Deflationary['\"]\\s*,\\s*net","text":"Line 4: <code>console.log(net &gt; 0 ? 'Inflationary' : 'Deflationary', net);</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Real return after dilution","file":"real.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Three lines: <code>const apy = 0.25;</code>, <code>const inflation = 0.25;</code>, then print <code>((1 + apy) / (1 + inflation) - 1) * 100</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const apy = 0.25;\nconst inflation = 0.25;\nconsole.log(((1 + apy) / (1 + inflation) - 1) * 100);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+apy\\s*=\\s*0\\.25","text":"Line 1: <code>const apy = 0.25;</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+inflation\\s*=\\s*0\\.25","text":"Line 2: <code>const inflation = 0.25;</code>"},{"re":"\\(\\s*\\(\\s*1\\s*\\+\\s*apy\\s*\\)\\s*\\/\\s*\\(\\s*1\\s*\\+\\s*inflation\\s*\\)\\s*-\\s*1\\s*\\)\\s*\\*\\s*100","text":"Line 3: <code>console.log(((1 + apy) / (1 + inflation) - 1) * 100);</code> Count the brackets."}],"explain":"<p>Output: <code>0</code>. A 25% yield paid in new tokens, while supply grows 25%, leaves your real share exactly where it was.</p>"}],"text":"Emissions: the printing press Emissions are new tokens created over time, usually to reward stakers, validators or liquidity providers. They are a cost paid by every holder through dilution. Done well, they buy something valuable: network security, liquidity, early users. Done badly, they pay people to farm and dump. Common schedules: Schedule Shape Example Fixed per block Same amount forever, so inflation % slowly falls Many early chains Halving Cut in half on a schedule Bitcoin, about every 4 years Exponential decay A % less each month Many DeFi liquidity programmes Dynamic Depends on how much is staked or on usage Ethereum's issuance under proof of stake Nominal versus real yield Stake and earn 40% APY! Paid in what? If you earn 40% more tokens per year, and total supply also grows 40% per year from those same emissions, your share of the network is unchanged . You only avoided being diluted; non-stakers paid for it. And if the price falls because farmers sell their rewards, you can lose money while your token balance grows. Real yield comes from actual revenue: trading fees, borrowing interest, protocol income, paid in ETH or stablecoins or used to buy tokens from the market. Ask every time: where does this yield come from? If the answer is new tokens , it is dilution with extra steps. If nobody can answer, be very careful. Burns Burning removes tokens permanently. It is often marketed as automatically good. It only helps when: net supply change = new issuance - tokens burned is low or negative and demand holds up. Ethereum's EIP-1559 burns the base fee of every transaction, so when network usage is high, more ETH can be burned than issued. A burn of tokens the team never intended to sell, or a tiny burn alongside big emissions, changes little. Buybacks use protocol revenue to buy tokens on the market (then burn them or send them to stakers or the treasury). They link token demand to business performance, which is why many mature protocols favour them. Mercenary capital In 2020's DeFi summer , protocols handed out huge token emissions to attract liquidity. Capital flooded in, farmed the rewards, sold them, and left the moment rewards dropped. Lessons the industry learned: Reward behaviour you want to last (long-term locking, real usage) rather than just parked capital. Taper emissions and announce schedules clearly. Measure retention after rewards end , not total value locked during them. Points programmes (track activity now, convert to tokens later) are the modern version. Same economics, delayed. Model it // 5 years of supply: 100M initial, 20M emitted in year 1 decaying 30% a year, plus fee burns let supply = 100_000_000; let emission = 20_000_000; const yearlyBurn = 4_000_000; // assume steady usage burns 4M tokens a year for (let year = 1; year = 5; year++) { const before = supply; supply = supply + emission - yearlyBurn; const inflation = (supply / before - 1) * 100; console.log(`Year ${year}: emitted ${(emission / 1e6).toFixed(1)}M, supply ${(supply / 1e6).toFixed(1)}M, net inflation ${inflation.toFixed(1)}%`); emission *= 0.7; } Try changing the burn to 10M and the decay to 0.5. When does net inflation turn negative? The sustainability test Imagine the token price drops 80% and stays there for a year. Do validators or stakers still have a reason to stay? Does the treasury still cover two years of costs? Do users still use the product? Designs that pass this test survive bear markets.","track":"tokenomics"},{"id":"tok-mechanisms","title":"\"Mechanism design: ve-tokens, bonding curves and game theory\"","level":"advanced","minutes":40,"summary":"The designs advanced protocols use to align incentives, the maths behind them, and how they fail.","html":"<h2 id=\"thinking-in-incentives\">Thinking in incentives</h2>\n<p><strong>Mechanism design</strong> is designing the rules of a game so that people acting in their own interest produce the outcome you want. Every token system is a game: holders, stakers, traders, LPs, insiders, attackers. For each design decision, ask: <em>what is the most profitable thing a selfish, well-funded, clever person could do here?</em> Then make sure that thing is good for the system, or impossible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"game-theory-you-will-use\">Game theory you will use</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Nash equilibrium</strong>: a situation where nobody gains by changing strategy alone. Your design's equilibrium is what will actually happen, whatever the whitepaper hopes.</li><li><strong>Prisoner's dilemma</strong>: everyone would be better off if all held, but each individual is better off selling first. Unlock events often play out this way.</li><li><strong>Coordination and Schelling points</strong>: people converge on obvious focal points (round-number prices, unlock dates, snapshot rumours).</li><li><strong>Reflexivity</strong>: price affects fundamentals, which affect price. Rising price raises collateral values and staking APY in dollar terms, attracting more buyers, until it reverses and the loop runs backwards.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"vote-escrow-ve-tokenomics\">Vote-escrow (ve) tokenomics</h2>\n<p>Popularised by Curve: you lock tokens for a chosen time (up to 4 years) and receive non-transferable <strong>veTokens</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">voting power = tokens locked x (time remaining / maximum lock time)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Lock 1,000 tokens for 4 years: 1,000 veTokens. For 1 year: 250. Power decays linearly as the unlock date approaches, unless you extend the lock.</p>\n<p>veToken holders typically get:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Governance votes</strong>, including <strong>gauge votes</strong> that direct where new emissions go.</li><li><strong>Boosted rewards</strong> on their own liquidity.</li><li>A <strong>share of protocol fees</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>Why it works: it rewards long-term commitment and removes supply from the market. Side effects: protocols compete to direct emissions, creating <strong>bribe markets</strong> where projects pay veToken holders to vote for their pools, and meta-protocols that pool locks. Liquidity of locked positions becomes a problem of its own.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"bonding-curves\">Bonding curves</h2>\n<p>A <strong>bonding curve</strong> is a contract that mints tokens when you buy and burns them when you sell, at a price set by a formula of the current supply:</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\"><pre><code class=\"language-text\">linear curve:  price(s) = m * s + b\ncost to buy from supply s1 to s2 = area under the curve\n                                 = m/2 * (s2^2 - s1^2) + b * (s2 - s1)</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"js\" data-try=\"1\"><pre><code class=\"language-js\">// Linear bonding curve: price rises 0.000001 USDC per token minted, starting at 0.01\nconst m = 0.000001, b = 0.01;\nconst price = (s) =&gt; m * s + b;\nconst cost = (s1, s2) =&gt; (m / 2) * (s2 ** 2 - s1 ** 2) + b * (s2 - s1);\n\nlet supply = 0;\nfor (const buy of [10_000, 10_000, 10_000]) {\n  const paid = cost(supply, supply + buy);\n  supply += buy;\n  console.log(`bought ${buy}, paid ${paid.toFixed(2)} USDC, price now ${price(supply).toFixed(4)}`);\n}\nconsole.log('reserve held by the curve:', cost(0, supply).toFixed(2), 'USDC');</code></pre></div>\n<p>Properties: always liquid (the contract is the counterparty), price discovery without an order book, and the reserve always covers selling everyone back down the curve. Risks: early buyers profit directly from later buyers, which drives speculation; front-running on each purchase; the shape of the curve hard-codes who wins. Many token launchpads use curves to bootstrap a token before moving it to an AMM.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"governance-attacks\">Governance attacks</h2>\n<p>If voting power is just token balance at the time of voting, an attacker can <strong>borrow</strong> tokens with a flash loan, vote, and repay in one transaction. In 2022 the Beanstalk protocol lost around $180 million this way: the attacker borrowed enough to pass a proposal sending the treasury to themselves.</p>\n<p>Defences:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Snapshot</strong> voting power at a past block, before the proposal.</li><li><strong>Timelocks</strong> between a vote passing and execution, so users can react.</li><li><strong>Quorums</strong>, vote delays, and guardians who can veto clearly malicious proposals.</li><li>Locked voting (ve-tokens) where power cannot be borrowed.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"when-designs-fail-stablecoin-death-spirals\">When designs fail: stablecoin death spirals</h2>\n<p>Algorithmic stablecoins that rely on a sister token to absorb volatility are the textbook reflexivity failure. In May 2022, TerraUSD lost its peg; redemptions minted ever more LUNA, LUNA's price collapsed, which made the peg less credible, which caused more redemptions. Tens of billions of dollars of value disappeared within days. The lesson: a mechanism that only works while confidence holds will eventually be tested by a loss of confidence.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Stress-test every mechanism</p><p>For each design, write down: the intended equilibrium; what a whale with $50 million and a flash loan does; what happens if the price falls 90% in a week; what happens if the team disappears. If any answer is &quot;it collapses&quot;, redesign before launch.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"You lock 2,000 tokens for 2 years in a ve system with a 4-year maximum. What is your initial voting power?","options":["2,000","1,000","4,000"],"answer":1,"explain":"2,000 x (2 / 4) = 1,000, decaying to zero as the unlock approaches."},{"q":"How did the 2022 Beanstalk governance attack work?","options":["The attacker flash-borrowed voting power, passed a malicious proposal and repaid within one transaction","The attacker guessed the admin's private key","A bug in an AMM pool"],"answer":0,"explain":"Voting power based on current balance can be borrowed. Snapshots and timelocks prevent it."},{"q":"On a linear bonding curve, who profits most if lots of people buy later?","options":["Early buyers, because later buys raise the price they can sell at","Late buyers","Nobody, prices are fixed"],"answer":0,"explain":"The curve guarantees rising prices with supply, transferring value from later to earlier buyers."},{"q":"What is reflexivity in token economies?","options":["Tokens that reflect light","A feedback loop where price changes alter fundamentals, which push price further the same way","Mirroring a token on another chain"],"answer":1,"explain":"Reflexive loops amplify booms and busts. Algorithmic stablecoins are the classic example."}],"exercises":[{"title":"Vote-escrow voting power","file":"ve.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write an arrow function <code>votingPower</code> that takes <code>tokens</code> and <code>yearsLocked</code> and returns <code>tokens * (yearsLocked / 4)</code>. Print <code>votingPower(2000, 2)</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const votingPower = (tokens, yearsLocked) => tokens * (yearsLocked / 4);\nconsole.log(votingPower(2000, 2));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+votingPower\\s*=\\s*\\(\\s*tokens\\s*,\\s*yearsLocked\\s*\\)\\s*=>","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const votingPower = (tokens, yearsLocked) =&gt;</code>"},{"re":"tokens\\s*\\*\\s*\\(\\s*yearsLocked\\s*\\/\\s*4\\s*\\)","text":"then <code>tokens * (yearsLocked / 4);</code>"},{"re":"votingPower\\(\\s*2000\\s*,\\s*2\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(votingPower(2000, 2));</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Cost to buy on a bonding curve","file":"curve.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Write <code>cost</code> as an arrow function of <code>s1</code> and <code>s2</code>: <code>(0.000001 / 2) * (s2 ** 2 - s1 ** 2) + 0.01 * (s2 - s1)</code>. Print <code>cost(0, 10000)</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const cost = (s1, s2) => (0.000001 / 2) * (s2 ** 2 - s1 ** 2) + 0.01 * (s2 - s1);\nconsole.log(cost(0, 10000));","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+cost\\s*=\\s*\\(\\s*s1\\s*,\\s*s2\\s*\\)\\s*=>","text":"Line 1 starts <code>const cost = (s1, s2) =&gt;</code>"},{"re":"\\(\\s*0\\.000001\\s*\\/\\s*2\\s*\\)\\s*\\*\\s*\\(\\s*s2\\s*\\*\\*\\s*2\\s*-\\s*s1\\s*\\*\\*\\s*2\\s*\\)","text":"first term: <code>(0.000001 / 2) * (s2 ** 2 - s1 ** 2)</code>"},{"re":"\\+\\s*0\\.01\\s*\\*\\s*\\(\\s*s2\\s*-\\s*s1\\s*\\)","text":"second term: <code>+ 0.01 * (s2 - s1);</code>"},{"re":"cost\\(\\s*0\\s*,\\s*10000\\s*\\)","text":"Line 2: <code>console.log(cost(0, 10000));</code>"}],"explain":"<p>Output: about <code>150</code>: the first 10,000 tokens cost 150 USDC in total, matching the lesson's example (floating-point maths may show a tiny rounding tail).</p>"}],"text":"Thinking in incentives Mechanism design is designing the rules of a game so that people acting in their own interest produce the outcome you want. Every token system is a game: holders, stakers, traders, LPs, insiders, attackers. For each design decision, ask: what is the most profitable thing a selfish, well-funded, clever person could do here? Then make sure that thing is good for the system, or impossible. Game theory you will use Nash equilibrium : a situation where nobody gains by changing strategy alone. Your design's equilibrium is what will actually happen, whatever the whitepaper hopes. Prisoner's dilemma : everyone would be better off if all held, but each individual is better off selling first. Unlock events often play out this way. Coordination and Schelling points : people converge on obvious focal points (round-number prices, unlock dates, snapshot rumours). Reflexivity : price affects fundamentals, which affect price. Rising price raises collateral values and staking APY in dollar terms, attracting more buyers, until it reverses and the loop runs backwards. Vote-escrow (ve) tokenomics Popularised by Curve: you lock tokens for a chosen time (up to 4 years) and receive non-transferable veTokens . voting power = tokens locked x (time remaining / maximum lock time) Lock 1,000 tokens for 4 years: 1,000 veTokens. For 1 year: 250. Power decays linearly as the unlock date approaches, unless you extend the lock. veToken holders typically get: Governance votes , including gauge votes that direct where new emissions go. Boosted rewards on their own liquidity. A share of protocol fees . Why it works: it rewards long-term commitment and removes supply from the market. Side effects: protocols compete to direct emissions, creating bribe markets where projects pay veToken holders to vote for their pools, and meta-protocols that pool locks. Liquidity of locked positions becomes a problem of its own. Bonding curves A bonding curve is a contract that mints tokens when you buy and burns them when you sell, at a price set by a formula of the current supply: linear curve: price(s) = m * s + b cost to buy from supply s1 to s2 = area under the curve = m/2 * (s2^2 - s1^2) + b * (s2 - s1) // Linear bonding curve: price rises 0.000001 USDC per token minted, starting at 0.01 const m = 0.000001, b = 0.01; const price = (s) = m * s + b; const cost = (s1, s2) = (m / 2) * (s2 ** 2 - s1 ** 2) + b * (s2 - s1); let supply = 0; for (const buy of [10_000, 10_000, 10_000]) { const paid = cost(supply, supply + buy); supply += buy; console.log(`bought ${buy}, paid ${paid.toFixed(2)} USDC, price now ${price(supply).toFixed(4)}`); } console.log('reserve held by the curve:', cost(0, supply).toFixed(2), 'USDC'); Properties: always liquid (the contract is the counterparty), price discovery without an order book, and the reserve always covers selling everyone back down the curve. Risks: early buyers profit directly from later buyers, which drives speculation; front-running on each purchase; the shape of the curve hard-codes who wins. Many token launchpads use curves to bootstrap a token before moving it to an AMM. Governance attacks If voting power is just token balance at the time of voting, an attacker can borrow tokens with a flash loan, vote, and repay in one transaction. In 2022 the Beanstalk protocol lost around $180 million this way: the attacker borrowed enough to pass a proposal sending the treasury to themselves. Defences: Snapshot voting power at a past block, before the proposal. Timelocks between a vote passing and execution, so users can react. Quorums , vote delays, and guardians who can veto clearly malicious proposals. Locked voting (ve-tokens) where power cannot be borrowed. When designs fail: stablecoin death spirals Algorithmic stablecoins that rely on a sister token to absorb volatility are the textbook reflexivity failure. In May 2022, TerraUSD lost its peg; redemptions minted ever more LUNA, LUNA's price collapsed, which made the peg ","track":"tokenomics"},{"id":"tok-launch","title":"Launch playbook, red flags and the rules","level":"advanced","minutes":35,"summary":"How serious teams launch tokens, a checklist for judging any token, and the UK, EU and US rules you must know before marketing one.","html":"<h2 id=\"a-responsible-launch-step-by-step\">A responsible launch, step by step</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Product first.</strong> A live product with real users, and a clear answer to &quot;why does this need a token?&quot;</li><li><strong>Design and model</strong> supply, allocation, vesting and incentives. Stress-test them (see the modelling lesson).</li><li><strong>Legal review</strong> in every country where you will market or sell. Do this before you announce anything.</li><li><strong>Build and audit</strong> the token, vesting, staking and any distribution contracts. Publish the audit.</li><li><strong>Distribution plan</strong>: who gets tokens and how. Airdrops need sybil filtering (one person, one allocation).</li><li><strong>Liquidity plan</strong>: how the first pools are seeded and whether LP tokens are locked or protocol-owned. If market makers are involved, publish the terms.</li><li><strong>Transparency</strong>: publish the full allocation, the vesting schedule and every contract address before launch.</li><li><strong>Launch day</strong>: monitor contracts, pools and social channels; have an incident plan.</li><li><strong>After launch</strong>: report regularly on treasury, unlocks and emissions. Trust is earned over years.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"distribution-methods\">Distribution methods</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>How it works</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Airdrop</strong></td><td>Free tokens to past users or a community</td><td>Farmers with thousands of wallets; instant sell pressure</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Points then airdrop</strong></td><td>Track activity, convert to tokens later</td><td>Users feel misled if conversion disappoints</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fair launch</strong></td><td>No pre-sale; everyone buys at the same terms</td><td>Bots and snipers at launch</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Liquidity bootstrapping pool (LBP)</strong></td><td>Price starts high and falls over days so buyers can find a fair price</td><td>Complex for newcomers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Public sale (ICO / IDO)</strong></td><td>Sell tokens for funds</td><td>Heavy securities-law implications</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bonding curve launchpad</strong></td><td>Price rises with each mint, then migrates to an AMM</td><td>Highly speculative; early-buyer advantage</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"red-flag-checklist-for-any-token\">Red-flag checklist for any token</h2>\n<p>Use this before buying, promoting or advising on a token:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Contract</strong>: unverified source code; owner can mint without limit, pause trading, blacklist wallets, or change fees and taxes; a proxy that the team can upgrade instantly.</li><li><strong>Liquidity</strong>: LP tokens unlocked or held by one wallet; tiny liquidity relative to market cap.</li><li><strong>Holders</strong>: a handful of wallets hold most of the supply (check the explorer's holders tab).</li><li><strong>Tokenomics</strong>: low float with a huge FDV; insiders above about 40%; short or no vesting; undisclosed allocations.</li><li><strong>Yield</strong>: high APY with no revenue source.</li><li><strong>Team and marketing</strong>: anonymous team plus pressure to buy now; paid promotions without disclosure; guaranteed returns.</li><li><strong>Honeypot behaviour</strong>: you can buy but cannot sell (test with a tiny amount, or use a scanner, before anything else).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"the-rules-regulation-you-must-know\">The rules: regulation you must know</h2>\n<p>This is general awareness, <strong>not legal advice</strong>. Rules change, and penalties are serious. Speak to a qualified lawyer before launching or promoting a token.</p>\n<h3 id=\"united-kingdom\">United Kingdom</h3>\n<ul><li>Since October 2023, <strong>cryptoasset marketing to UK consumers is a regulated financial promotion</strong> under the FCA's rules. Promotions must be made or approved by an authorised firm (or by a firm registered with the FCA for crypto), must be fair, clear and not misleading, carry prominent risk warnings, and first-time investors face a 24-hour cooling-off period and appropriateness checks.</li><li>This applies to <strong>social media posts, influencers and agencies</strong>, not just exchanges. The FCA has taken action against unlawful crypto promotions, including by influencers.</li><li>If you run marketing for brands, never promote a token to UK audiences without confirming the promotion has been properly approved.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"european-union\">European Union</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>MiCA</strong> (Markets in Crypto-Assets Regulation) has applied in full since the end of 2024. Most token issuers offering to the public must publish a crypto-asset <strong>white paper</strong> with required disclosures and are responsible for its accuracy; service providers need authorisation; stablecoins have their own strict regime.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"united-states\">United States</h3>\n<ul><li>The SEC has long applied the <strong>Howey test</strong>: an investment of money, in a common enterprise, with an expectation of profit from the efforts of others, is an investment contract (a security), whatever it is called. Many token sales have been treated as securities offerings. The US approach has been changing, with new legislation and agency guidance; check the current position with a lawyer.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"everywhere\">Everywhere</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Tax</strong>: token grants to teams, airdrops, staking rewards and trades usually create tax events.</li><li><strong>Sanctions and AML</strong>: you must not deal with sanctioned addresses or persons; regulated activities require KYC.</li><li><strong>Consumer protection</strong>: do not promise returns or price targets. Ever.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Never promise price or returns</p><p>&quot;This will 10x&quot;, &quot;guaranteed APY&quot;, &quot;get in before the pump&quot;: statements like these can turn marketing into an unlawful financial promotion or misrepresentation, and they destroy trust when they fail. Talk about the product, the utility and the risks.</p></aside>","quiz":[{"q":"A token's contract owner can change the sell tax to 99% at any time. What is this?","options":["A normal feature","A major red flag: the owner could stop holders selling profitably (a honeypot)","A way to reduce gas"],"answer":1,"explain":"Adjustable taxes, blacklists and unlimited minting give the owner power over your money."},{"q":"You run social media for a brand that wants you to post about its new token to UK followers. What do the UK rules require?","options":["Nothing, social posts are exempt","Only a hashtag like #ad","The promotion must comply with the FCA's financial promotion rules, including approval by an appropriately authorised or registered firm and proper risk warnings"],"answer":2,"explain":"UK crypto promotions are regulated, including those by influencers and agencies."},{"q":"Under the US Howey test, what makes a token sale look like a securities offering?","options":["People invest money expecting profits from the efforts of the team","The token uses the ERC-20 standard","The token has a fixed supply"],"answer":0,"explain":"The economic reality matters, not the label."},{"q":"What is a sensible first step before launching a token?","options":["Announce the launch date to build hype","Make sure there is a real product and a clear reason for the token, then get legal advice","List it on as many exchanges as possible"],"answer":1,"explain":"A token amplifies a product; it cannot replace one. Legal review comes before any announcement."}],"exercises":[{"title":"An automated red-flag checker","file":"redflags.js","lang":"js","run":true,"prompt":"<p>Six lines:</p>\n<ol><li><code>const token = { insiderShare: 0.55, lpLocked: false, ownerCanMint: true };</code></li><li><code>const flags = [];</code></li><li><code>if (token.insiderShare &gt; 0.4) flags.push('Insiders above 40%');</code></li><li><code>if (!token.lpLocked) flags.push('Liquidity not locked');</code></li><li><code>if (token.ownerCanMint) flags.push('Owner can mint');</code></li><li><code>console.log(flags);</code></li></ol>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"const token = { insiderShare: 0.55, lpLocked: false, ownerCanMint: true };\nconst flags = [];\nif (token.insiderShare > 0.4) flags.push('Insiders above 40%');\nif (!token.lpLocked) flags.push('Liquidity not locked');\nif (token.ownerCanMint) flags.push('Owner can mint');\nconsole.log(flags);","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"const\\s+token\\s*=\\s*\\{\\s*insiderShare\\s*:\\s*0\\.55\\s*,\\s*lpLocked\\s*:\\s*false\\s*,\\s*ownerCanMint\\s*:\\s*true\\s*\\}","text":"Line 1: <code>const token = { insiderShare: 0.55, lpLocked: false, ownerCanMint: true };</code>"},{"re":"const\\s+flags\\s*=\\s*\\[\\s*\\]","text":"Line 2: an empty array: <code>const flags = [];</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*token\\.insiderShare\\s*>\\s*0\\.4\\s*\\)\\s*flags\\.push\\(\\s*['\"]Insiders above 40%['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 3: <code>if (token.insiderShare &gt; 0.4) flags.push('Insiders above 40%');</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*!\\s*token\\.lpLocked\\s*\\)\\s*flags\\.push\\(\\s*['\"]Liquidity not locked['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 4 uses <code>!</code> for &quot;not&quot;: <code>if (!token.lpLocked) flags.push('Liquidity not locked');</code>"},{"re":"if\\s*\\(\\s*token\\.ownerCanMint\\s*\\)\\s*flags\\.push\\(\\s*['\"]Owner can mint['\"]\\s*\\)","text":"Line 5: <code>if (token.ownerCanMint) flags.push('Owner can mint');</code>"},{"re":"console\\.log\\(\\s*flags\\s*\\)","text":"Line 6: <code>console.log(flags);</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"A responsible launch, step by step Product first. A live product with real users, and a clear answer to why does this need a token? Design and model supply, allocation, vesting and incentives. Stress-test them (see the modelling lesson). Legal review in every country where you will market or sell. Do this before you announce anything. Build and audit the token, vesting, staking and any distribution contracts. Publish the audit. Distribution plan : who gets tokens and how. Airdrops need sybil filtering (one person, one allocation). Liquidity plan : how the first pools are seeded and whether LP tokens are locked or protocol-owned. If market makers are involved, publish the terms. Transparency : publish the full allocation, the vesting schedule and every contract address before launch. Launch day : monitor contracts, pools and social channels; have an incident plan. After launch : report regularly on treasury, unlocks and emissions. Trust is earned over years. Distribution methods Method How it works Watch out for Airdrop Free tokens to past users or a community Farmers with thousands of wallets; instant sell pressure Points then airdrop Track activity, convert to tokens later Users feel misled if conversion disappoints Fair launch No pre-sale; everyone buys at the same terms Bots and snipers at launch Liquidity bootstrapping pool (LBP) Price starts high and falls over days so buyers can find a fair price Complex for newcomers Public sale (ICO / IDO) Sell tokens for funds Heavy securities-law implications Bonding curve launchpad Price rises with each mint, then migrates to an AMM Highly speculative; early-buyer advantage Red-flag checklist for any token Use this before buying, promoting or advising on a token: Contract : unverified source code; owner can mint without limit, pause trading, blacklist wallets, or change fees and taxes; a proxy that the team can upgrade instantly. Liquidity : LP tokens unlocked or held by one wallet; tiny liquidity relative to market cap. Holders : a handful of wallets hold most of the supply (check the explorer's holders tab). Tokenomics : low float with a huge FDV; insiders above about 40%; short or no vesting; undisclosed allocations. Yield : high APY with no revenue source. Team and marketing : anonymous team plus pressure to buy now; paid promotions without disclosure; guaranteed returns. Honeypot behaviour : you can buy but cannot sell (test with a tiny amount, or use a scanner, before anything else). The rules: regulation you must know This is general awareness, not legal advice . Rules change, and penalties are serious. Speak to a qualified lawyer before launching or promoting a token. United Kingdom Since October 2023, cryptoasset marketing to UK consumers is a regulated financial promotion under the FCA's rules. Promotions must be made or approved by an authorised firm (or by a firm registered with the FCA for crypto), must be fair, clear and not misleading, carry prominent risk warnings, and first-time investors face a 24-hour cooling-off period and appropriateness checks. This applies to social media posts, influencers and agencies , not just exchanges. The FCA has taken action against unlawful crypto promotions, including by influencers. If you run marketing for brands, never promote a token to UK audiences without confirming the promotion has been properly approved. European Union MiCA (Markets in Crypto-Assets Regulation) has applied in full since the end of 2024. Most token issuers offering to the public must publish a crypto-asset white paper with required disclosures and are responsible for its accuracy; service providers need authorisation; stablecoins have their own strict regime. United States The SEC has long applied the Howey test : an investment of money, in a common enterprise, with an expectation of profit from the efforts of others, is an investment contract (a security), whatever it is called. Many token sales have been treated as securities offerings. The US approach has ","track":"tokenomics"},{"id":"tok-modelling","title":"Modelling a token economy in code","level":"advanced","minutes":40,"summary":"Turn a design into a model, run scenarios and stress tests in Python, and present results honestly.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-model\">Why model</h2>\n<p>A model forces vague ideas (&quot;emissions will reward the community&quot;) into numbers you can question (&quot;emissions add 18% supply in year one while projected fee burn removes 2%&quot;). Its job is to <strong>compare designs and find failure modes</strong>, not to predict a price. Anyone who claims their model predicts token prices is selling something.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-modelling-process\">The modelling process</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Goals</strong>: what must the design achieve? (Secure the network, reward long-term users, fund development for five years.)</li><li><strong>Actors</strong>: who participates, and what do they want? (Team, investors, stakers, LPs, users, speculators.)</li><li><strong>Flows</strong>: every source of new supply and every sink, as a function of time and behaviour.</li><li><strong>Assumptions</strong>: user growth, fees per user, what share of unlocked tokens get sold. Write them down explicitly.</li><li><strong>Scenarios</strong>: bear, base and bull versions of the assumptions.</li><li><strong>Stress tests</strong>: extreme but plausible shocks.</li><li><strong>Decide</strong>: change the design until it survives the bad scenarios.</li></ol>\n<p>Start in a spreadsheet; move to code when you need many scenarios, randomness or agent behaviour. Use the <a href=\"#token-sim\">Tokenomics Simulator</a> for the supply side first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-supply-and-sell-pressure-model-in-python\">A supply and sell-pressure model in Python</h2>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">uv init token-model &amp;&amp; cd token-model\nuv add pandas matplotlib</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\"># model.py\nimport pandas as pd\n\nMAX_SUPPLY = 1_000_000_000\nALLOCATIONS = {\n    # name: (share, tge_unlock, cliff_months, vest_months, sell_ratio_of_unlocks)\n    &quot;team&quot;:       (0.18, 0.00, 12, 36, 0.20),\n    &quot;investors&quot;:  (0.17, 0.00, 12, 24, 0.50),\n    &quot;ecosystem&quot;:  (0.25, 0.05,  0, 48, 0.30),\n    &quot;community&quot;:  (0.15, 0.00,  0, 48, 0.40),\n    &quot;treasury&quot;:   (0.12, 0.10,  6, 36, 0.10),\n    &quot;liquidity&quot;:  (0.07, 1.00,  0,  0, 0.00),\n    &quot;airdrop&quot;:    (0.06, 0.50,  0,  6, 0.60),\n}\n\ndef unlocked(month, tge, cliff, vest):\n    if month &lt; cliff:\n        return tge\n    if vest == 0:\n        return 1.0\n    return tge + (1 - tge) * min(1.0, (month - cliff) / vest)\n\ndef run(months=48, monthly_buy_usd=1_500_000, price=0.10):\n    rows, prev = [], {name: 0.0 for name in ALLOCATIONS}\n    for m in range(months + 1):\n        row = {&quot;month&quot;: m, &quot;circulating&quot;: 0.0, &quot;sell_tokens&quot;: 0.0}\n        for name, (share, tge, cliff, vest, sell_ratio) in ALLOCATIONS.items():\n            amount = share * MAX_SUPPLY * unlocked(m, tge, cliff, vest)\n            new = amount - prev[name]\n            prev[name] = amount\n            row[&quot;circulating&quot;] += amount\n            row[&quot;sell_tokens&quot;] += new * sell_ratio\n        row[&quot;sell_usd&quot;] = row[&quot;sell_tokens&quot;] * price\n        row[&quot;buy_usd&quot;] = monthly_buy_usd\n        row[&quot;pressure_ratio&quot;] = row[&quot;sell_usd&quot;] / row[&quot;buy_usd&quot;]   # above 1 means more sellers than buyers at this price\n        rows.append(row)\n    return pd.DataFrame(rows)\n\nif __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:\n    for name, buy in {&quot;bear&quot;: 500_000, &quot;base&quot;: 1_500_000, &quot;bull&quot;: 4_000_000}.items():\n        df = run(monthly_buy_usd=buy)\n        worst = df.loc[df[&quot;pressure_ratio&quot;].idxmax()]\n        months_over = int((df[&quot;pressure_ratio&quot;] &gt; 1).sum())\n        print(f&quot;{name:&gt;4}: worst month {int(worst.month)} (ratio {worst.pressure_ratio:.1f}), &quot;\n              f&quot;{months_over} months with more selling than buying&quot;)</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\"><pre><code class=\"language-bash\">uv run model.py</code></pre></div>\n<p>This is deliberately simple. It already answers useful questions: which months need the most demand, how sensitive the design is to investor selling, and whether moving the investor cliff from 12 to 18 months helps.</p>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"0\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"going-further\">Going further</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Monte Carlo</strong>: draw assumptions (growth, sell ratios) from ranges instead of single values, run thousands of times, and report the spread of outcomes, not one line.</li><li><strong>Agent-based models</strong>: simulate individual holders with simple rules (&quot;stake if APY &gt; 8%, sell 30% of unlocks if price fell this month&quot;) and watch emergent behaviour. Frameworks: <strong>radCAD</strong> or <strong>cadCAD</strong> in Python.</li><li><strong>Real data</strong>: calibrate assumptions against similar live tokens using Dune queries (actual staking rates, holder behaviour around unlocks, fee revenue).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"stress-tests-to-always-run\">Stress tests to always run</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Shock</th><th>Question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Price falls 80% for 12 months</td><td>Is the treasury still funded? Do stakers stay?</td></tr><tr><td>Demand stays flat for two years</td><td>How bad is unlock pressure?</td></tr><tr><td>Largest holder sells everything</td><td>What happens to pool prices and governance?</td></tr><tr><td>Fee revenue falls 90%</td><td>Does real yield vanish and stakers leave?</td></tr><tr><td>Emissions doubled by a governance vote</td><td>Can the design survive its own governance?</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"presenting-results-honestly\">Presenting results honestly</h2>\n<ul><li>Lead with the assumptions and the scenario ranges.</li><li>Show charts of supply, unlocks and pressure, not price predictions.</li><li>State what the model does not capture (sentiment, market-wide crashes, regulation).</li><li>Keep the code and data public so others can check it. That transparency is itself a trust signal.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-pro\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Portfolio project</p><p>Pick a real token with a published schedule. Rebuild its unlock model, compare it with on-chain data, run the three scenarios, and write a two-page memo with charts and recommendations. That one piece of work demonstrates more tokenomics skill than any certificate.</p></aside>\n<div class=\"exercise\" data-ex=\"1\"></div>","quiz":[{"q":"What is a tokenomics model best used for?","options":["Predicting the exact future token price","Comparing designs and finding failure modes under different assumptions","Replacing a legal review"],"answer":1,"explain":"Models test designs. They cannot predict markets."},{"q":"Why run bear, base and bull scenarios instead of one forecast?","options":["Outcomes depend heavily on uncertain assumptions; a design should survive the bad cases, not just the expected one","Investors require exactly three scenarios","It makes the charts look better"],"answer":0,"explain":"Robust designs are judged by their worst plausible outcomes."},{"q":"In the Python model, what does a pressure_ratio above 1 mean?","options":["The token is undervalued","In that month, the dollar value of expected selling from unlocks exceeds assumed buying demand at that price","Unlocks have finished"],"answer":1,"explain":"It flags months where the design needs more demand than assumed to hold the price."}],"exercises":[{"title":"The unlock function in Python","file":"model.py","lang":"python","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Write the <code>unlocked</code> function from the model above in Python. Indent with 4 spaces (Tab does it for you):</p>\n<div class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\"><pre><code class=\"language-python\">def unlocked(month, tge, cliff, vest):\n    if month &lt; cliff:\n        return tge\n    if vest == 0:\n        return 1.0\n    return tge + (1 - tge) * min(1.0, (month - cliff) / vest)</code></pre></div>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"def unlocked(month, tge, cliff, vest):\n    if month < cliff:\n        return tge\n    if vest == 0:\n        return 1.0\n    return tge + (1 - tge) * min(1.0, (month - cliff) / vest)","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^def\\s+unlocked\\s*\\(\\s*month\\s*,\\s*tge\\s*,\\s*cliff\\s*,\\s*vest\\s*\\)\\s*:","text":"Line 1: <code>def unlocked(month, tge, cliff, vest):</code> ending with a colon."},{"re":"^ {4}if\\s+month\\s*<\\s*cliff\\s*:","text":"Line 2, indented 4 spaces: <code>if month &lt; cliff:</code>"},{"re":"^ {8}return\\s+tge\\s*$","text":"Line 3, indented 8 spaces: <code>return tge</code>"},{"re":"^ {4}if\\s+vest\\s*==\\s*0\\s*:","text":"Line 4, back to 4 spaces: <code>if vest == 0:</code>"},{"re":"^ {8}return\\s+1\\.0\\s*$","text":"Line 5, 8 spaces: <code>return 1.0</code>"},{"re":"^ {4}return\\s+tge\\s*\\+\\s*\\(\\s*1\\s*-\\s*tge\\s*\\)\\s*\\*\\s*min\\(\\s*1\\.0\\s*,\\s*\\(\\s*month\\s*-\\s*cliff\\s*\\)\\s*\\/\\s*vest\\s*\\)","text":"Line 6, 4 spaces: <code>return tge + (1 - tge) * min(1.0, (month - cliff) / vest)</code>"}],"explain":""},{"title":"Set up and run the model","file":"Terminal","lang":"bash","run":false,"prompt":"<p>Four commands: create a uv project called <code>token-model</code>, move into it, add <code>pandas</code> and <code>matplotlib</code>, then run <code>model.py</code>.</p>","starter":"","html":"","solution":"uv init token-model\ncd token-model\nuv add pandas matplotlib\nuv run model.py","also":[],"hints":[{"re":"^\\s*uv\\s+init\\s+token-model\\s*$","text":"Line 1: <code>uv init token-model</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*cd\\s+token-model\\s*$","text":"Line 2: <code>cd token-model</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*uv\\s+add\\s+pandas\\s+matplotlib\\s*$","text":"Line 3: <code>uv add pandas matplotlib</code>"},{"re":"^\\s*uv\\s+run\\s+model\\.py\\s*$","text":"Line 4: <code>uv run model.py</code>"}],"explain":""}],"text":"Why model A model forces vague ideas ( emissions will reward the community ) into numbers you can question ( emissions add 18% supply in year one while projected fee burn removes 2% ). Its job is to compare designs and find failure modes , not to predict a price. Anyone who claims their model predicts token prices is selling something. The modelling process Goals : what must the design achieve? (Secure the network, reward long-term users, fund development for five years.) Actors : who participates, and what do they want? (Team, investors, stakers, LPs, users, speculators.) Flows : every source of new supply and every sink, as a function of time and behaviour. Assumptions : user growth, fees per user, what share of unlocked tokens get sold. Write them down explicitly. Scenarios : bear, base and bull versions of the assumptions. Stress tests : extreme but plausible shocks. Decide : change the design until it survives the bad scenarios. Start in a spreadsheet; move to code when you need many scenarios, randomness or agent behaviour. Use the Tokenomics Simulator for the supply side first. A supply and sell-pressure model in Python uv init token-model cd token-model uv add pandas matplotlib # model.py import pandas as pd MAX_SUPPLY = 1_000_000_000 ALLOCATIONS = { # name: (share, tge_unlock, cliff_months, vest_months, sell_ratio_of_unlocks) team : (0.18, 0.00, 12, 36, 0.20), investors : (0.17, 0.00, 12, 24, 0.50), ecosystem : (0.25, 0.05, 0, 48, 0.30), community : (0.15, 0.00, 0, 48, 0.40), treasury : (0.12, 0.10, 6, 36, 0.10), liquidity : (0.07, 1.00, 0, 0, 0.00), airdrop : (0.06, 0.50, 0, 6, 0.60), } def unlocked(month, tge, cliff, vest): if month cliff: return tge if vest == 0: return 1.0 return tge + (1 - tge) * min(1.0, (month - cliff) / vest) def run(months=48, monthly_buy_usd=1_500_000, price=0.10): rows, prev = [], {name: 0.0 for name in ALLOCATIONS} for m in range(months + 1): row = { month : m, circulating : 0.0, sell_tokens : 0.0} for name, (share, tge, cliff, vest, sell_ratio) in ALLOCATIONS.items(): amount = share * MAX_SUPPLY * unlocked(m, tge, cliff, vest) new = amount - prev[name] prev[name] = amount row[ circulating ] += amount row[ sell_tokens ] += new * sell_ratio row[ sell_usd ] = row[ sell_tokens ] * price row[ buy_usd ] = monthly_buy_usd row[ pressure_ratio ] = row[ sell_usd ] / row[ buy_usd ] # above 1 means more sellers than buyers at this price rows.append(row) return pd.DataFrame(rows) if __name__ == __main__ : for name, buy in { bear : 500_000, base : 1_500_000, bull : 4_000_000}.items(): df = run(monthly_buy_usd=buy) worst = df.loc[df[ pressure_ratio ].idxmax()] months_over = int((df[ pressure_ratio ] 1).sum()) print(f {name: 4}: worst month {int(worst.month)} (ratio {worst.pressure_ratio:.1f}), f {months_over} months with more selling than buying ) uv run model.py This is deliberately simple. It already answers useful questions: which months need the most demand, how sensitive the design is to investor selling, and whether moving the investor cliff from 12 to 18 months helps. Going further Monte Carlo : draw assumptions (growth, sell ratios) from ranges instead of single values, run thousands of times, and report the spread of outcomes, not one line. Agent-based models : simulate individual holders with simple rules ( stake if APY 8%, sell 30% of unlocks if price fell this month ) and watch emergent behaviour. Frameworks: radCAD or cadCAD in Python. Real data : calibrate assumptions against similar live tokens using Dune queries (actual staking rates, holder behaviour around unlocks, fee revenue). Stress tests to always run Shock Question Price falls 80% for 12 months Is the treasury still funded? Do stakers stay? Demand stays flat for two years How bad is unlock pressure? Largest holder sells everything What happens to pool prices and governance? Fee revenue falls 90% Does real yield vanish and stakers leave? Emissions doubled by a governance vote Can the design survive its own governance? Presenting re","track":"tokenomics"}];
